{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "I'm trying to write a Bash script that will overwrite an existing directory. I have a directory ```foo/``` and I am trying to overwrite ```bar/``` with it. But when I do this:\n```cp -Rf foo/ bar/\n```\na new ```bar/foo/``` directory is created. I don't want that. There are two files in ```foo/```; ```a``` and ```b```. There are files with same names in ```bar/``` as well. I want the ```foo/a``` and ```foo/b``` to replace ```bar/a``` and ```bar/b```."}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user wants to overwrite an existing directory with contents of another directory, not create subdirectory. They used cp -Rf foo/ bar/. That copies foo/ recursively into bar, creating bar/foo. To overwrite contents, they need to copy contents of foo into bar: cp -a foo/. bar/ or cp -a foo/* bar/ (but hidden files?). Or use rsync -a --delete foo/ bar/. Or use mv to replace: rm -rf bar && mv foo bar, but they want replace files while preserving others? Based on description: both directories have same structure. Want to replace files inside bar with those from foo, effectively update. Use cp -a foo/. bar/. The dot copies hidden files too. Or use rsync -av --delete foo/ bar/. Might need to ensure trailing slash. The key is the trailing slash after source. They already have it. However need to copy contents, not folder. Use cp -a foo/. bar/. Additionally note that -r is redundant if -a includes -R.\n\nAnswer: Provide explanation and examples.\n\nWrap answer in <answer> tags.</think>\n<answer>\n`cp` copies the *source* you give it, so when you write\n\n```bash\ncp -Rf foo/ bar/\n```\n\nthe directory *foo* itself is copied into *bar*, creating `bar/foo/`.\n\nTo copy **the contents** of `foo/` into `bar/` (over\u2011writing files that already exist there) you need to tell `cp` to copy everything *inside* `foo/`, not the directory itself. The usual way is to use the `.` (or `*`) after the source directory:\n\n```bash\n# Copy everything (including hidden files) from foo/ into bar/\ncp -a foo/. bar/\n# or equivalently\ncp -a foo/* bar/\n```\n\nExplanation of the options:\n\n* `-a` \u2013 \u201carchive\u201d mode; it is equivalent to `-dR --preserve=all`.  \n  It copies directories recursively, preserves symlinks, permissions, timestamps, etc.\n* `-R` / `-r` \u2013 recursive copy (included in `-a` already).\n* `-f` \u2013 force overwriting of existing files (also implied by `-a`).\n\nIf you also want to **delete files in `bar/` that no longer exist in `foo/`**, `rsync` is often a better tool:\n\n```bash\nrsync -a --delete foo/ bar/\n```\n\n`rsync` will:\n\n* copy all files and sub\u2011directories from `foo/` into `bar/`,\n* overwrite existing files with the newer versions from `foo/`,\n* remove any files in `bar/` that are not present in `foo/` (`--delete`).\n\nBoth of these commands will give you the result you described:\n\n```\nbar/\n\u251c\u2500 a   # replaced by foo/a\n\u2514\u2500 b   # replaced by foo/b\n```\n\nNo extra `bar/foo/` directory will be created."}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I'm trying to write a Bash script that will overwrite an existing directory. I have a directory ```foo/``` and I am trying to overwrite ```bar/``` with it. But when I do this:\n```cp -Rf foo/ bar/\n```\na new ```bar/foo/``` directory is created. I don't want that. There are two files in ```foo/```; ```a``` and ```b```. There are files with same names in ```bar/``` as well. I want the ```foo/a``` and ```foo/b``` to replace ```bar/a``` and ```bar/b```."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user wants to overwrite an existing directory with contents of another directory, not create subdirectory. They used cp -Rf foo/ bar/. That copies foo/ recursively into bar, creating bar/foo. To overwrite contents, they need to copy contents of foo into bar: cp -a foo/. bar/ or cp -a foo/* bar/ (but hidden files?). Or use rsync -a --delete foo/ bar/. Or use mv to replace: rm -rf bar && mv foo bar, but they want replace files while preserving others? Based on description: both directories have same structure. Want to replace files inside bar with those from foo, effectively update. Use cp -a foo/. bar/. The dot copies hidden files too. Or use rsync -av --delete foo/ bar/. Might need to ensure trailing slash. The key is the trailing slash after source. They already have it. However need to copy contents, not folder. Use cp -a foo/. bar/. Additionally note that -r is redundant if -a includes -R.\n\nAnswer: Provide explanation and examples.\n\nWrap answer in <answer> tags.</think>\n<answer>\n`cp` copies the *source* you give it, so when you write\n\n```bash\ncp -Rf foo/ bar/\n```\n\nthe directory *foo* itself is copied into *bar*, creating `bar/foo/`.\n\nTo copy **the contents** of `foo/` into `bar/` (over\u2011writing files that already exist there) you need to tell `cp` to copy everything *inside* `foo/`, not the directory itself. The usual way is to use the `.` (or `*`) after the source directory:\n\n```bash\n# Copy everything (including hidden files) from foo/ into bar/\ncp -a foo/. bar/\n# or equivalently\ncp -a foo/* bar/\n```\n\nExplanation of the options:\n\n* `-a` \u2013 \u201carchive\u201d mode; it is equivalent to `-dR --preserve=all`.  \n  It copies directories recursively, preserves symlinks, permissions, timestamps, etc.\n* `-R` / `-r` \u2013 recursive copy (included in `-a` already).\n* `-f` \u2013 force overwriting of existing files (also implied by `-a`).\n\nIf you also want to **delete files in `bar/` that no longer exist in `foo/`**, `rsync` is often a better tool:\n\n```bash\nrsync -a --delete foo/ bar/\n```\n\n`rsync` will:\n\n* copy all files and sub\u2011directories from `foo/` into `bar/`,\n* overwrite existing files with the newer versions from `foo/`,\n* remove any files in `bar/` that are not present in `foo/` (`--delete`).\n\nBoth of these commands will give you the result you described:\n\n```\nbar/\n\u251c\u2500 a   # replaced by foo/a\n\u2514\u2500 b   # replaced by foo/b\n```\n\nNo extra `bar/foo/` directory will be created."}], "q_num": 109, "originalconversations": ["I'm trying to write a Bash script that will overwrite an existing directory. I have a directory ```foo/``` and I am trying to overwrite ```bar/``` with it. But when I do this:\n```cp -Rf foo/ bar/\n```\na new ```bar/foo/``` directory is created. I don't want that. There are two files in ```foo/```; ```a``` and ```b```. There are files with same names in ```bar/``` as well. I want the ```foo/a``` and ```foo/b``` to replace ```bar/a``` and ```bar/b```.", "If you want to ensure ```bar/``` ends up identical to ```foo/```, use ```rsync``` instead:\n```rsync -a --delete foo/ bar/\n```\nIf just a few things have changed, this will execute much faster than removing and re-copying the whole directory.\n\n* ```-a``` is 'archive mode', which copies faithfully files in ```foo/``` to ```bar/```\n* ```--delete``` removes extra files not in ```foo/``` from ```bar/``` as well, ensuring ```bar/``` ends up identical\n* If you want to see what it's doing, add ```-vh``` for verbose and human-readable\n* Note: the slash after ```foo``` is required, otherwise ```rsync``` will copy ```foo/``` to ```bar/foo/``` rather than overwriting ```bar/``` itself.\n\n* (Slashes after directories in rsync are confusing; if you're interested, here's the scoop. They tell rsync to refer to the contents of the directory, rather than the directory itself. So to overwrite from the contents of ```foo/``` onto the contents of ```bar/```, we use a slash on both. It's confusing because it won't work as expected with a slash on neither, though; rsync sneakily always interprets the destination path as though it has a slash, even though it honors an absence of a slash on the source path. So we need a slash on the source path to make it match the auto-added slash on the destination path, if we want to copy the contents of ```foo/``` into ```bar/```, rather than the directory ```foo/``` itself landing into ```bar/``` as ```bar/foo```.)\n\n```rsync``` is very powerful and useful, if you're curious look around for what else it can do (such as copying over ssh)."], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "I'm trying to write a Bash script that will overwrite an existing directory. I have a directory ```foo/``` and I am trying to overwrite ```bar/``` with it. But when I do this:\n```cp -Rf foo/ bar/\n```\na new ```bar/foo/``` directory is created. I don't want that. There are two files in ```foo/```; ```a``` and ```b```. There are files with same names in ```bar/``` as well. I want the ```foo/a``` and ```foo/b``` to replace ```bar/a``` and ```bar/b```."}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "How can I copy a string (e.g \"hello\") to the System Clipboard in C#, so next time I press CTRL+V I'll get \"hello\"?"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer the question: copy a string to clipboard in C#. Provide method using System.Windows.Forms.Clipboard.SetText, need to add STA thread, etc. Possibly mention .NET Core, using TextCopy library, or using Windows API. Provide code snippet. Also need to wrap answer in <answer></answer> tags (as per system). Should be concise but thorough. Also mention need reference to System.Windows.Forms.dll. Ensure to mention using System.Windows; for WPF. Provide full example.\n\nLet's produce final answer.</think>\n<answer>\nYou can put any text on the Windows clipboard from C# by calling the **Clipboard** class.  \nThe class lives in **System.Windows.Forms** (WinForms) or **System.Windows** (WPF).  \nBecause the clipboard API requires a single\u2011threaded apartment (STA) thread, make sure the calling thread is marked as STA (e.g., a Windows Forms/WPF UI thread or a manually\u2011created STA thread).\n\n---\n\n### 1. Using WinForms (`System.Windows.Forms`)\n\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing System.Windows.Forms;   // Add reference to System.Windows.Forms.dll\n\nclass Program\n{\n    [STAThread]                     // <-- Required for clipboard access\n    static void Main()\n    {\n        string text = \"hello\";\n\n        // Copies the text to the clipboard\n        Clipboard.SetText(text);\n\n        Console.WriteLine(\"Text copied to clipboard.\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n*If you call `Clipboard.SetText` from a background thread, create an STA thread yourself:*\n\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing System.Threading;\nusing System.Windows.Forms;\n\nclass ClipboardHelper\n{\n    public static void SetClipboardText(string text)\n    {\n        Thread t = new Thread(() => Clipboard.SetText(text));\n        t.SetApartmentState(ApartmentState.STA); // Must be STA\n        t.Start();\n        t.Join(); // optional \u2013 wait until it finishes\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Using WPF (`System.Windows`)\n\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing System.Windows;   // Add reference to PresentationCore.dll, WindowsBase.dll\n\nclass Program\n{\n    [STAThread]\n    static void Main()\n    {\n        Clipboard.SetDataObject(\"hello\", true);\n        Console.WriteLine(\"Text copied to clipboard.\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. .NET\u202fCore / .NET\u202f5+ (no UI reference)\n\nIf you are writing a console app and don\u2019t want to reference WinForms/WPF, you can use a small, cross\u2011platform NuGet package like **TextCopy**:\n\n```bash\ndotnet add package TextCopy\n```\n\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing TextCopy;   // Namespace from the TextCopy package\n\nclass Program\n{\n    static void Main()\n    {\n        ClipboardService.SetText(\"hello\");\n        Console.WriteLine(\"Text copied to clipboard.\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`TextCopy` works on Windows, macOS and Linux (using the appropriate platform clipboard).\n\n---\n\n## Quick checklist\n\n| Requirement | How to satisfy |\n|-------------|----------------|\n| **Reference** | Add `System.Windows.Forms.dll` (WinForms) **or** `PresentationCore.dll` + `WindowsBase.dll` (WPF) |\n| **STA thread** | Mark `Main` with `[STAThread]` **or** run the code on an STA background thread |\n| **Copy operation** | `Clipboard.SetText(\"hello\")` (WinForms) <br> `Clipboard.SetDataObject(\"hello\", true)` (WPF) <br> `ClipboardService.SetText(\"hello\")` (TextCopy) |\n| **Paste later** | Press **Ctrl\u202f+\u202fV** in any application \u2013 the string \u201chello\u201d will appear |\n\nThat\u2019s all you need to place a string on the system clipboard so that a subsequent **Ctrl\u202f+\u202fV** paste yields the same text. Happy coding!"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How can I copy a string (e.g \"hello\") to the System Clipboard in C#, so next time I press CTRL+V I'll get \"hello\"?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer the question: copy a string to clipboard in C#. Provide method using System.Windows.Forms.Clipboard.SetText, need to add STA thread, etc. Possibly mention .NET Core, using TextCopy library, or using Windows API. Provide code snippet. Also need to wrap answer in <answer></answer> tags (as per system). Should be concise but thorough. Also mention need reference to System.Windows.Forms.dll. Ensure to mention using System.Windows; for WPF. Provide full example.\n\nLet's produce final answer.</think>\n<answer>\nYou can put any text on the Windows clipboard from C# by calling the **Clipboard** class.  \nThe class lives in **System.Windows.Forms** (WinForms) or **System.Windows** (WPF).  \nBecause the clipboard API requires a single\u2011threaded apartment (STA) thread, make sure the calling thread is marked as STA (e.g., a Windows Forms/WPF UI thread or a manually\u2011created STA thread).\n\n---\n\n### 1. Using WinForms (`System.Windows.Forms`)\n\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing System.Windows.Forms;   // Add reference to System.Windows.Forms.dll\n\nclass Program\n{\n    [STAThread]                     // <-- Required for clipboard access\n    static void Main()\n    {\n        string text = \"hello\";\n\n        // Copies the text to the clipboard\n        Clipboard.SetText(text);\n\n        Console.WriteLine(\"Text copied to clipboard.\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n*If you call `Clipboard.SetText` from a background thread, create an STA thread yourself:*\n\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing System.Threading;\nusing System.Windows.Forms;\n\nclass ClipboardHelper\n{\n    public static void SetClipboardText(string text)\n    {\n        Thread t = new Thread(() => Clipboard.SetText(text));\n        t.SetApartmentState(ApartmentState.STA); // Must be STA\n        t.Start();\n        t.Join(); // optional \u2013 wait until it finishes\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Using WPF (`System.Windows`)\n\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing System.Windows;   // Add reference to PresentationCore.dll, WindowsBase.dll\n\nclass Program\n{\n    [STAThread]\n    static void Main()\n    {\n        Clipboard.SetDataObject(\"hello\", true);\n        Console.WriteLine(\"Text copied to clipboard.\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. .NET\u202fCore / .NET\u202f5+ (no UI reference)\n\nIf you are writing a console app and don\u2019t want to reference WinForms/WPF, you can use a small, cross\u2011platform NuGet package like **TextCopy**:\n\n```bash\ndotnet add package TextCopy\n```\n\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing TextCopy;   // Namespace from the TextCopy package\n\nclass Program\n{\n    static void Main()\n    {\n        ClipboardService.SetText(\"hello\");\n        Console.WriteLine(\"Text copied to clipboard.\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`TextCopy` works on Windows, macOS and Linux (using the appropriate platform clipboard).\n\n---\n\n## Quick checklist\n\n| Requirement | How to satisfy |\n|-------------|----------------|\n| **Reference** | Add `System.Windows.Forms.dll` (WinForms) **or** `PresentationCore.dll` + `WindowsBase.dll` (WPF) |\n| **STA thread** | Mark `Main` with `[STAThread]` **or** run the code on an STA background thread |\n| **Copy operation** | `Clipboard.SetText(\"hello\")` (WinForms) <br> `Clipboard.SetDataObject(\"hello\", true)` (WPF) <br> `ClipboardService.SetText(\"hello\")` (TextCopy) |\n| **Paste later** | Press **Ctrl\u202f+\u202fV** in any application \u2013 the string \u201chello\u201d will appear |\n\nThat\u2019s all you need to place a string on the system clipboard so that a subsequent **Ctrl\u202f+\u202fV** paste yields the same text. Happy coding!"}], "q_num": 16, "originalconversations": ["How can I copy a string (e.g \"hello\") to the System Clipboard in C#, so next time I press CTRL+V I'll get \"hello\"?", "There are two classes that lives in different assemblies and different namespaces.\n\n* WinForms: use following namespace declaration, make sure ```Main``` is marked with ```[STAThread]``` attribute:\n```using System.Windows.Forms;\n```\n* WPF: use following namespace declaration\n```using System.Windows;\n```\n* console: add reference to ```System.Windows.Forms```, use following namespace declaration, make sure ```Main``` is marked with ```[STAThread]``` attribute. Step-by-step guide in another answer\n```using System.Windows.Forms;\n```\n\nTo copy an exact string (literal in this case):\n```Clipboard.SetText(\"Hello, clipboard\");\n```\nTo copy the contents of a textbox either use TextBox.Copy() or get text first and then set clipboard value:\n```Clipboard.SetText(txtClipboard.Text);\n```\nSee here for an example.\nOr... Official MSDN documentation or Here for WPF.\n\nRemarks:\n\n* Clipboard is desktop UI concept, trying to set it in server side code like ASP.Net will only set value on the server and has no impact on what user can see in they browser. While linked answer lets one to run Clipboard access code server side with ```SetApartmentState``` it is unlikely what you want to achieve.\n* If after following information in this question code still gets an exception see &quot;Current thread must be set to single thread apartment (STA)&quot; error in copy string to clipboard\n* This question/answer covers regular .NET, for .NET Core see - .Net Core - copy to clipboard?\n"], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "How can I copy a string (e.g \"hello\") to the System Clipboard in C#, so next time I press CTRL+V I'll get \"hello\"?"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "I've taken a look at the list of surveys taken on scala-lang.org and noticed a curious question: \"Can you name all the uses of \u201c_\u201d?\". Can you? If yes, please do so here. Explanatory examples are appreciated."}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks: \"I've taken a look at the list of surveys taken on scala-lang.org and noticed a curious question: 'Can you name all the uses of \u201c_\u201d?'. Can you? If yes, please do so here. Explanatory examples are appreciated.\"\n\nWe need to answer the question: list all uses of underscore `_` in Scala. Provide explanatory examples. Provide comprehensive answer. Use <answer> tags. Ensure it's correct. So we need to enumerate all contexts where underscore is used in Scala language. Let's think of uses:\n\n- Placeholder syntax for anonymous functions: `list.map(_ + 1)`, `_ * _`\n- Wildcard import: `import java.util._`\n- Wildcard pattern: case _: Int =>, ignore variable: `_` in pattern matching\n- Ignoring a value: val _ = expr (unused)\n- Ignoring a parameter in lambda: `(x, _) => x`\n- Ignoring import: `import scala.collection.{mutable => _, immutable => _}`\n- As a wildcard in type arguments: existential types: `List[_]` (unknown type)\n- As a type placeholder for partial type application: `def foo[A, B] = ...` Not exactly underscore.\n- In pattern matching: `case _ =>` default case\n- In for comprehensions: `_ <- seq` to discard generator\n- As a placeholder for default arguments? Not exactly.\n- As a wildcard in named arguments: `def foo(x: Int, y: Int) = ...; foo(x = _, y = 5)` not typical.\n- As a placeholder for a variable name when using import rename: `import foo.{bar => _}`\n- As a placeholder for a \"hole\" in partial function literal: `(_: Int) + 1`? Actually underscore can be used to convert a method to a function: `list.map(_.toString)`, also `_` can be used for eta-expansion: `val f = println _`\n- As a placeholder for a partially applied function: `val add = _ + _`\n- As a wildcard for ignoring a parameter: `_` in lambda `(x, _) => x`\n- As a wildcard for ignoring a result: `_ = expr` or `_` as a placeholder for a variable assignment.\n- As a wildcard for naming a variable that is never used: `val _ = someExpression`\n- In pattern matching, underscore as a wildcard pattern.\n- As a placeholder for a type: `_ >: LowerBound <: UpperBound`? Actually underscore used in type bounds: `def foo[T <: Upper]` not underscore.\n- In existential types: `List[_ <: Number]`, `Map[_, _]`\n- In higher-kinded type: `F[_]`\n- As a placeholder for a type parameter in a type lambda: `type Id[A] = A` not underscore.\n- In a method that returns a placeholder for a function: `(_: Int) => ???` Not sure.\n\nSpecifically, uses of `_` in Scala language:\n\n1. As a wildcard import: `import p._`\n2. As a wildcard pattern: `_` in match expression and default case.\n3. As a wildcard in for-comprehensions: `_ <- expr` (ignore value)\n4. As a placeholder for anonymous function parameters: `list.map(_ + 1)`, `list.filter(_ > 0)`, `(_ + _)`\n5. As a placeholder for partially applied methods: `val f = println _` (eta-expansion)\n6. As a placeholder for type arguments: `List[_]` existential, `F[_]` type constructor.\n7. As a placeholder in existential types: `List[_ <: Number]`, `Map[_, _]`.\n8. As a placeholder in higher-kinded type definitions: `trait Functor[F[_]]`.\n9. As a placeholder for ignored variable in pattern: `val (_, y) = tuple`\n10. As a placeholder for ignored parameter: `(x, _) => x`\n11. As a placeholder for ignored import rename: `import foo.{Bar => _, Baz => _}`\n12. As a placeholder for ignored assignment: `_ = expr` (discard result)\n13. As a placeholder for wildcard in type ascription: `val x: _ = ???` Not allowed.\n14. As a placeholder for a \"hole\" in partial functions: `case _ =>` already covered.\n\nAlso underscore used in method definitions: `def foo = _`? Not.\n\nOther uses:\n\n- In string interpolation: `s\"${_}\"`? Actually underscore not used there.\n\n- In pattern matching with `case (a, _) =>` etc.\n\n- As a wildcard for the \"default argument\" in partial functions? Not.\n\n- As a placeholder for a parameter type in a method reference: `val f: Int => String = _.toString` same as placeholder.\n\n- As a placeholder for a parameter in a constructor reference: `new MyClass(_, _)` not typical.\n\n- As a placeholder for a type argument when calling generic methods with inferred type: `list.map[String](_ => \"x\")` not needed.\n\n- As a \"wildcard\" in type ascription: `val x: _ = ???` Not allowed.\n\n- As a \"named underscore\" pattern: `case (_, _, _) =>` ignore all.\n\n- In pattern matching with extractors: `case MyClass(_, b)`.\n\n- In Scala 3, underscore also used for placeholder syntax in given/using? Not sure.\n\n- In Scala 3, underscore is used for a wildcard import, placeholder, etc. Additionally, underscore can be used as a \"partial function\" placeholder: `case _ =>`. Already.\n\n- As a placeholder for \"ignored implicit parameter\": `implicit val _ = something` Not.\n\n- As a wildcard for default imports: `import scala._` includes underscore but not.\n\n- As a wildcard for import renaming: `import scala.collection.{mutable => _, immutable => _}`.\n\n- As a placeholder for a method parameter in a partial function: `(_: Int) + 1` also uses underscore to name a parameter.\n\n- As a placeholder for a function type: `(_: String) => Int` is a function literal using underscore to name parameters.\n\n- As a placeholder for a function literal with type ascription: `(x: Int) => x + 1` not underscore.\n\n- As a placeholder for a partially applied method: `val f = List.apply _` using underscore after method name.\n\n- As a placeholder for a value being ignored in a pattern: `_` pattern.\n\n- As a placeholder for a \"synthetic parameter\" in varargs pattern: `case Seq(_*)` uses underscore star.\n\n- Actually underscore star `_*` is used to expand a sequence into varargs: `method(args: _*)`.\n\n- `_` is used in pattern matching to ignore sequence parts: `case Seq(head, _*) =>`.\n\n- `_` used as a \"wildcard type\" in existential types: `def foo[T] : List[T] forSome { type T }` not underscore.\n\n- underscore in path-dependent types: `obj.type` not.\n\n- underscore in implicit conversion: not.\n\n- underscore as a placeholder for \"placeholder syntax for multiple parameters\": `list.foldLeft(0)(_ + _)`.\n\n- underscore used as a \"named placeholder\" for a type argument in a function literal: `(x: Int) => x + 1` not.\n\n- underscore used for \"type lambda\" like `({type L[A] = List[A]})#L` not.\n\n- underscore used in `import scala.collection.mutable.{Map => _, Set => _}` to hide members.\n\n- underscore used in \"wildcard for default import\": not.\n\n- underscore used in \"pattern wildcard in extractor\" we covered.\n\n- underscore used in \"case class pattern with unapply\" we covered.\n\n- underscore used for \"ignored result of expression\": `expr; _` no.\n\n- underscore used in \"named parameter placeholder\" `foo(_: Int, _: String)` to create a function value with given types.\n\n- underscore used as a placeholder in \"partial application of constructors\": `new MyClass(_, _)`.\n\n- underscore used in \"placeholder syntax for function literals\" can be used with type ascription: `( _: Int ) + 1`.\n\n- underscore used for \"wildcard in type parameter lists\" like `def foo[T[_]]` where underscore is a type constructor placeholder.\n\n- underscore used in \"type projection\" not.\n\n- underscore used in \"implicit parameters\" not.\n\n- underscore used in \"pattern for extracting wildcard sequence\" `_ *` we mentioned.\n\n- underscore used in \"type parameter bound wildcard\"? Not.\n\n- underscore used as \"anonymous function syntax for multiple parameters\" we covered.\n\n- underscore used as \"ignore a binding\" `val (a, _) = tuple`.\n\n- underscore used in \"assignment ignoring\": `_ = expr`.\n\n- underscore used in \"