 as a \"partial function\" placeholder: `case _ =>`. Already.\n\n- As a placeholder for \"ignored implicit parameter\": `implicit val _ = something` Not.\n\n- As a wildcard for default imports: `import scala._` includes underscore but not.\n\n- As a wildcard for import renaming: `import scala.collection.{mutable => _, immutable => _}`.\n\n- As a placeholder for a method parameter in a partial function: `(_: Int) + 1` also uses underscore to name a parameter.\n\n- As a placeholder for a function type: `(_: String) => Int` is a function literal using underscore to name parameters.\n\n- As a placeholder for a function literal with type ascription: `(x: Int) => x + 1` not underscore.\n\n- As a placeholder for a partially applied method: `val f = List.apply _` using underscore after method name.\n\n- As a placeholder for a value being ignored in a pattern: `_` pattern.\n\n- As a placeholder for a \"synthetic parameter\" in varargs pattern: `case Seq(_*)` uses underscore star.\n\n- Actually underscore star `_*` is used to expand a sequence into varargs: `method(args: _*)`.\n\n- `_` is used in pattern matching to ignore sequence parts: `case Seq(head, _*) =>`.\n\n- `_` used as a \"wildcard type\" in existential types: `def foo[T] : List[T] forSome { type T }` not underscore.\n\n- underscore in path-dependent types: `obj.type` not.\n\n- underscore in implicit conversion: not.\n\n- underscore as a placeholder for \"placeholder syntax for multiple parameters\": `list.foldLeft(0)(_ + _)`.\n\n- underscore used as a \"named placeholder\" for a type argument in a function literal: `(x: Int) => x + 1` not.\n\n- underscore used for \"type lambda\" like `({type L[A] = List[A]})#L` not.\n\n- underscore used in `import scala.collection.mutable.{Map => _, Set => _}` to hide members.\n\n- underscore used in \"wildcard for default import\": not.\n\n- underscore used in \"pattern wildcard in extractor\" we covered.\n\n- underscore used in \"case class pattern with unapply\" we covered.\n\