urning point so that parties can survive typical encounters without requiring many pauses. The \"survivability jump\" is due to the presence of a bonus action that can be used for Healing Word or Misty Step, etc., plus more hit points.\n\nFurthermore, from a game design perspective, the transition from 0 to 1 to 2 is where you get your first subclass features, although subclass is later. But the biggest conceptual shift is that 1st level characters are more \"lifestyle\" than \"adventurer.\" At level 2 they become proper adventurers.\n\nThus answer: mechanical reasons: extra hit die, additional HP and spell slots, class features that give extra actions or healing (Second Wind, Rage, Ki, Cunning Action, Action Surge, Channel Divinity). The math: HP increase about 50-60%; total spell slots increase also; PC can regain some HP each short rest; average damage output increase due to more attacks, etc. The later level differences are incremental and balanced.\n\nI'll craft final answer with bullet points.</think>\n**Short answer:**  \nLevel\u202f1 in 5e is deliberately \u201cbare\u2011bones\u201d.  Characters have only one hit die, a handful of spell slots, virtually no bonus\u2011action options and almost no special resources.  \nWhen they reach level\u202f2 they"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I find that the survivability and general performance of my party increases massively from levels 1 to 2. At times, level 1 feels like a completely different game from level 2. However, I can't fathom how or why. I think that the availability of healing has something to do with it. From a mechanical perspective, is there any deep reason why level 1 and level 2 seem so radically different? Furthermore, why I do find no similar differences between later levels, such as 6 and 7?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer: Why is there such a jump from level 1 to 2 in D&D 5e (presumably). The question mentions survivability and performance of party increase massively from levels 1 to 2. Level 1 feels like a completely different game from level 2. Wants mechanical perspective: deep reasons? Also why similar differences aren't seen between later levels, such as 6 and 7.\n\nWe need to discuss the mechanics: at level 1 characters have very few hit points, low AC (no proficiency with medium armor unless Fighter/Paladin), limited resources: low proficiency bonus (+2), limited spells (1st level spells at lvl 1). At level 2 they gain more HP (hit die, 1d8/d10 etc), second hit die (extra HP). More importantly they gain class features that dramatically improve survivability: for many classes, at level 2 they get a feature like \"action surge\" (Fighter), \"Eldritch Invocations\"? Actually Invocations at 2? No. Many classes gain something at level 2: barbarian gets reckless attack and danger sense; fighter gets Action Surge; monk gets Ki; rogue gets \u201cCunning Action\u201d; wizard gets Arcane Recovery; cleric gets Channel Divinity, etc. Also the big thing is they get their first spell slots at level 2 for most spellcasting classes (except full casters get at 1? Actually in 5e, full casters start with 1st-level spells at level 1? Wait: In 5e, spellcasting classes get spells at 1st level, but they have spell slots of level 1 starting at 1. So they already have spells. But they get 2nd-level spells at 3.\n\nHealing: At level 1 there are very limited ways to heal: only basic healing spells like Cure Wounds (1st level) and Healing Word, plus Lay on Hands (paladin) which starts at level 1? Paladin's Lay on Hands is at level 1, with 5 HP per long rest (so 5). but not much.\n\nAt level 2, they get short rest healing: hit dice can be used after short rest, but that also is present at level 1 (they have hit dice). However, the number of hit dice increases with level. But you can't heal HP from a short rest at level 1? Actually you can spend hit dice after a short rest even at level 1, but you have only 1 hit die.\n\nMain differences: more HP (HP increase from level 1 to 2 is roughly +1dX+Con). That's about +50% increase in HP (average). Also proficiency bonus same until level 5. So damage output also increases maybe small.\n\nBut more importantly, level 2 gives all classes a second level in their class. Many features dramatically increase effectiveness: Barbarian gets Reckless Attack (adds +2 damage and advantage on attack rolls but disadvantage on AC). Rogue gets Sneak Attack (starting level 1? Actually rogue gets Sneak Attack at level 1). Some classes get extra attack? No, that arrives later. The major differences: the progression per level is not linear; early levels give big jumps.\n\nBut the OP notes survivability and performance increase massively. So answer: the core mechanic of 5e has a \"jump\" from level 1 to 2 because at level 1 characters are fragile: they have minimal HP, no extra actions, no bonus actions except maybe Use of certain class features, limited spell slots, can't regain hit points except by potions/long rests. At level 2 they get more HP, the ability to use hit dice after short rests (but same), they get class features like Action Surge, Cunning Action, patient etc. They also get more spell slots (for half casters etc). Also they can cast 1st-level spells? Actually at level 1 they can cast 1st-level spells, but they have only a limited number of spell slots (2). At level 2 they get 3 slots of 1st level (for full casters). This extra slot means more healing spells per day (Cure Wounds) and more damage spells. That adds survivability.\n\nAnother factor: proficiency bonus remains same, but monsters get more HP and damage at higher challenge ratings, but not relevant.\n\nThe bigger shift is at level 1, you have only 1 action, 1 movement, no bonus action (unless class features). At level 2 many classes get bonus actions (e.g., Barbarian's Rage is a bonus action, Fighter's Second Wind? Actually Second Wind is also bonus action). Many classes get a resource that can be used in combat (e.g., Second Wind, Ki, Cunning Action). Healing: you get second wind at level 2 for fighters and other classes like soldier background? Actually second wind is fighter class feature at 1? No, fighter gets Second Wind at level 1 as part of fighting style? Wait, second wind is a fighter feature at Level 1? Actually it appears at 1: Second Wind is a fighter feature at 1st level. So fighters get it at 1. But others get at 2: Barbarian's Rage is bonus action (level 1). So not all.\n\nBut the main difference might be that at level 2 characters get *extra hit die* (more HP) and *extra ability scores*? Actually ability score improvements come at 4, 8,...\n\nBut the core reason: D&D is built around important class features that significantly improve abilities at low levels to get players moving, to avoid level 1 being too fragile. Level 2 essentially gives characters a \"plateau\" where they are less likely to die because they have actions and more options. The disparity lessens later because each level's added power is incremental: +HP, +1 spell slot, perhaps class feature at occasional levels. But the early jump is the biggest because the baseline at level 0 (no level) would be very weak.\n\nExplain also the \"brittle\" nature of level 1: because of low HP, any damage can kill. Also monsters (CR 0, 1/2) still can kill due to damage dice relative to HP. In 5e, monsters are built assuming party at level 1 with average HP ~10. So a monster with 2d6 damage average 7 could kill half. At level 2 HP ~14, the same damage less lethal.\n\nGive calculations: average HP at 1st level for d8+Con = 5.5 + Con (say 12 => +1) ~6.5 HP. At level 2: add another 5.5 + Con => average 12 HP. That's 80% increase. Also those characters now get a bonus action to use for healing (e.g., spell Cure Wounds is action, but healing word is bonus action at level 1? Actually Healing Word is a bonus action spell available to clerics, bards, druids... they have at level 1. So they can heal quickly.\n\nAdditionally, level 2 marks the start of \"subclasses\" at level 2? Wait, subclasses begin at 3 for most classes, some at 2 (e.g., Barbarian's Path, Fighter's Martial Archetype at 3? Actually Fighter gets at 3). Some classes get at 2: Rangers choose a Favored Enemy? That's 1. Warlock gets Pact? That's 3? Not relevant.\n\nSo answer: there are many mechanical changes at level 2: a second hit die, more HP, more spell slots, bonus actions/class features (Second Wind, Rage, Ki, Cunning Action, Action Surge). Also ability to regain resources on short rests.\n\nWhy not similar later: later levels have diminishing returns; each level adds only one extra HP die which is relatively small compared to total HP, adds at most one spell slot of a given level, some occasional class feature but not all. The gap between 1 and 2 is unique because level 1 is a \"minimum viable hero\" while level 2 crosses a threshold, providing enough resources to be viable.\n\nAdditionally, many monsters are designed to be lethal at level 1 so that the game is dangerous. The game is balanced such that 1-2 is a high variance zone. At higher levels, the variance smoothes out.\n\nWe can mention design philosophy: level 1 is \"zilch versatility.\" The designers purposely make level 2 a turning point so that parties can survive typical encounters without requiring many pauses. The \"survivability jump\" is due to the presence of a bonus action that can be used for Healing Word or Misty Step, etc., plus more hit points.\n\nFurthermore, from a game design perspective, the transition from 0 to 1 to 2 is where you get your first subclass features, although subclass is later. But the biggest conceptual shift is that 1st level characters are more \"lifestyle\" than \"adventurer.\" At level 2 they become proper adventurers.\n\nThus answer: mechanical reasons: extra hit die, additional HP and spell slots, class features that give extra actions or healing (Second Wind, Rage, Ki, Cunning Action, Action Surge, Channel Divinity). The math: HP increase about 50-60%; total spell slots increase also; PC can regain some HP each short rest; average damage output increase due to more attacks, etc. The later level differences are incremental and balanced.\n\nI'll craft final answer with bullet points.</think>\n**Short answer:**  \nLevel\u202f1 in 5e is deliberately \u201cbare\u2011bones\u201d.  Characters have only one hit die, a handful of spell slots, virtually no bonus\u2011action options and almost no special resources.  \nWhen they reach level\u202f2 they"}], "q_num": 141, "originalconversations": ["I find that the survivability and general performance of my party increases massively from levels 1 to 2. At times, level 1 feels like a completely different game from level 2. However, I can't fathom how or why. I think that the availability of healing has something to do with it. From a mechanical perspective, is there any deep reason why level 1 and level 2 seem so radically different? Furthermore, why I do find no similar differences between later levels, such as 6 and 7?", "Welcome to Rusty Dagger Shanktown!\nWhat you have noticed is very widely commented upon\u20141st level is often called \u201crusty dagger shanktown.\u201d It\u2019s hyper-lethal, rather than the kind of heroic fantasy Dungeons & Dragons pitches itself as. It is very, very difficult for 1st-level PCs to actually really adventure at all. Many, many groups avoid 1st for this reason (many skip 2nd too, even though it is already dramatically different). Personally, I\u2019ll include 1st level often, but the PCs basically always level-up to 2nd almost entirely on roleplaying XP.\nNote that this isn\u2019t unique to 3.5e; it goes back to the earliest days of D&D. Pathfinder, of course, inherited it from 3.5e (I don\u2019t know about PF 2e). It\u2019s also very much still true in 5e\u2014after 4e actually managed to avoid it, which may mean that this is considered a \u201cfeature\u201d by some, enough that Wizards of the Coast felt the need to restore it as part of 5e\u2019s general \u201creturn to form\u201d after the divisive fourth edition.\nAnyway, the primary causes of rusty dagger shanktown:\nHit points\nAlmost anyone can one-shot almost any 1st-level character. It\u2019s very, very easy to have 6 hp in a world where a decent, but basic, weapon\u2019s base damage averages 7. Even smaller weapons can easily deal that much damage, either through luck or skill. We expect 1st-level characters to often go down in one hit.\nA 2nd-level character can have nearly twice as much hp. Actually, could easily be twice as much, if they multiclass from a small-HD class to a large-HD class. That drastically changes the game, because suddenly you can feel reasonably confident that you aren\u2019t going to just suddenly die before anyone can do anything. Healing actually becomes relevant, where at 1st it\u2019s often too little, too late.\nNotably, this only has this effect because damage doesn\u2019t grow at the same rate. The base damage of weapons is fixed, and ability scores start at double digits but grow very slowly from there. That\u2019s the biggest chunk of early damage for most characters. Even beyond that, a lot of damage boosts\u2014inspire courage, rage, sneak attack\u2014come at 1st anyway. And monsters tend to kind of mirror these trends.\nSo what ends up happening is that a 1st-level character can\u2019t really take a chance in order to get something accomplished. A 2nd-level character can feel reasonably comfortable taking a chance at least once. That dramatically affects the style of play. At 2nd-level D&D literally feels like a different game. Really, it is a different game.\nWealth\nCharacters start with less than 200 gp worth of gear. That\u2019s not enough for a masterwork weapon, not enough even for non-masterwork medium or heavy armors, definitely not enough for any kind of magic item beyond maybe an extremely basic consumable. No 1st-level character has even their complete basic kit yet.\nThe expected wealth of a 2nd-level character is 900 gp. That can afford masterwork armor of anything short of full-plate, and for many armors leave enough left over for a masterwork weapon. Most importantly, that is enough money for a party of 4 to trivially afford a wand of cure light wounds. A wand of cure light wounds is a game-changer. It means you can easily start every fight at full hp. And with 2nd-level hp, that means you can actually take a few risks.\n2nd-level features\nThis varies considerably from one class to the next, but several classes get major class features at 2nd level. The paladin\u2019s 2nd-level feature, divine grace, might be the single greatest class feature in the game (assuming we don\u2019t count \u201cspells\u201d and the like as a singular feature). Rangers don\u2019t get their combat style until 2nd. Bards get 1st-level spells at 2nd (though inspire courage from 1st level is probably more important). Outside of core, meldshapers don\u2019t get any chakra binds until 2nd, which can leave them with zero offense at 1st. Swordsages don\u2019t get Wis-to-AC until 2nd.\nBut plenty of characters are fine from 1st, so this can vary a lot."], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "I find that the survivability and general performance of my party increases massively from levels 1 to 2. At times, level 1 feels like a completely different game from level 2. However, I can't fathom how or why. I think that the availability of healing has something to do with it. From a mechanical perspective, is there any deep reason why level 1 and level 2 seem so radically different? Furthermore, why I do find no similar differences between later levels, such as 6 and 7?"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "How to send HTML-formatted email in C#?"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks: \"How to send HTML-formatted email in C#?\" We need to provide a answer: likely show using SmtpClient, MailMessage, set IsBodyHtml = true, set Body to HTML, etc. Also discuss .NET Core vs .NET Framework, using MailKit etc. Provide code sample.\n\nWe need to respond in a helpful manner, explain steps, mention SMTP configuration, optional attachments, alternate view, using using statements, async sending, etc.\n\nWe must output final answer within <answer></answer> tags.\n\nLet's craft.\n\n</think>\n<answer>\n**Sending an HTML\u2011formatted e\u2011mail from C# is straightforward** \u2013 you create a `MailMessage`, place the HTML in its `Body`, set `IsBodyHtml` to `true`, and send it with an `SmtpClient` (or with a modern library such as **MailKit**).  \nBelow are two complete examples:\n\n---\n\n## 1\ufe0f\u20e3 Using the built\u2011in `System.Net.Mail` (works in .NET Framework and .NET\u202fCore\u202f2.0+)\n\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing System.Net;\nusing System.Net.Mail;\nusing System.Threading.Tasks;\n\npublic class EmailHelper\n{\n    /// <summary>\n    /// Sends an HTML e\u2011mail asynchronously.\n    /// </summary>\n    /// <param name=\"smtpHost\">SMTP server host (e.g. smtp.gmail.com)</param>\n    /// <param name=\"smtpPort\">SMTP server port (usually 587 for TLS)</param>\n    /// <param name=\"smtpUser\">SMTP username (often the e\u2011mail address)</param>\n    /// <param name=\"smtpPwd\">SMTP password or app\u2011specific password</param>\n    /// <param name=\"from\">Sender address (display name optional)</param>\n    /// <param name=\"to\">Recipient address (or a collection)</param>\n    /// <param name=\"subject\">Mail subject</param>\n    /// <param name=\"htmlBody\">HTML content of the mail</param>\n    /// <param name=\"isBodyHtml\">Set to true (default) \u2013 kept for flexibility</param>\n    /// <returns>A Task representing the async operation</returns>\n    public static async Task SendHtmlEmailAsync(\n        string smtpHost,\n        int smtpPort,\n        string smtpUser,\n        string smtpPwd,\n        MailAddress from,\n        MailAddress to,\n        string subject,\n        string htmlBody,\n        bool isBodyHtml = true)\n    {\n        // Construct the message\n        using var message = new MailMessage();\n        message.From = from;\n        message.To.Add(to);\n        message.Subject = subject;\n        message.Body = htmlBody;\n        message.IsBodyHtml = isBodyHtml;          // <-- **critical**\n        message.BodyEncoding = System.Text.Encoding.UTF8;\n        message.SubjectEncoding = System.Text.Encoding.UTF8;\n\n        // Optional: add a plain\u2011text fallback (good practice for accessibility)\n        // var plainView = AlternateView.CreateAlternateViewFromString(\n        //     HtmlToPlainText(htmlBody), null, \"text/plain\");\n        // var htmlView = AlternateView.CreateAlternateViewFromString(htmlBody, null, \"text/html\");\n        // message.AlternateViews.Add(plainView);\n        // message.AlternateViews.Add(htmlView);\n\n        // Configure the SMTP client\n        using var smtp = new SmtpClient(smtpHost, smtpPort)\n        {\n            EnableSsl = true,                     // TLS is required by most providers\n            Credentials = new NetworkCredential(smtpUser, smtpPwd),\n            // Timeout = 10000, // optional timeout in ms\n        };\n\n        // Send the mail\n        await smtp.SendMailAsync(message);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### How to call it\n\n```csharp\nawait EmailHelper.SendHtmlEmailAsync(\n    smtpHost: \"smtp.gmail.com\",\n    smtpPort: 587,\n    smtpUser: \"myaddress@gmail.com\",\n    smtpPwd: \"my-app-password\",\n    from: new MailAddress(\"myaddress@gmail.com\", \"My Name\"),\n    to: new MailAddress(\"receiver@example.com\", \"Receiver\"),\n    subject: \"Welcome to our service!\",\n    htmlBody: @\"\n        <h1>Hello, World!</h1>\n        <p>Thanks for joining us. Please click <a href='https://example.com/confirm'>here</a> to confirm your account.</p>\n        <img src='https://example.com/logo.png' alt='Logo' />\n    \"\n);\n```\n\n**Key points**\n\n| Setting | Why it matters |\n|---------|----------------|\n| `IsBodyHtml = true` | Tells the e\u2011mail client that the `Body` contains HTML. |\n| `BodyEncoding = UTF8` | Guarantees proper handling of special characters (e.g., emojis). |\n| `EnableSsl = true` | Modern SMTP servers require TLS encryption. |\n| `AlternateViews` (optional) | Provides a plain\u2011text version for clients that block HTML or for better accessibility. |\n\n---\n\n## 2\ufe0f\u20e3 Using **MailKit** (recommended for new projects, especially .NET\u202f5/6/7+)\n\n`MailKit` is a fully\u2011featured, actively\u2011maintained library that supersedes `System.Net.Mail`. It gives you better control over authentication mechanisms (OAuth2, STARTTLS, etc.) and richer MIME handling.\n\n### Install the package\n\n```bash\ndotnet add package MailKit\n```\n\n### Sample code\n\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing System.Threading.Tasks;\nusing MimeKit;\nusing MailKit.Net.Smtp;\nusing MailKit.Security;\n\npublic static class MailKitHelper\n{\n    public static async Task SendHtmlAsync(\n        string smtpHost,\n        int smtpPort,\n        string smtpUser,\n        string smtpPwd,\n        string fromAddress,\n        string fromName,\n        string toAddress,\n        string subject,\n        string htmlBody)\n    {\n        // 1\ufe0f\u20e3 Build the message\n        var message = new MimeMessage();\n        message.From.Add(new MailboxAddress(fromName, fromAddress));\n        message.To.Add(MailboxAddress.Parse(toAddress));\n        message.Subject = subject;\n\n        // 2\ufe0f\u20e3 Create the body (HTML + plain\u2011text fallback)\n        var body = new BodyBuilder\n        {\n            HtmlBody = htmlBody,\n            TextBody = HtmlToPlainText(htmlBody)   // simple conversion, optional\n        };\n        message.Body = body.ToMessageBody();\n\n        // 3\ufe0f\u20e3 Send via SMTP\n        using var client = new SmtpClient();\n\n        // Accept all SSL certificates (for demo only!)\n        // client.ServerCertificateValidationCallback = (s, c, h, e) => true;\n\n        await client.ConnectAsync(smtpHost, smtpPort, SecureSocketOptions.StartTls);\n        await client.AuthenticateAsync(smtpUser, smtpPwd);\n        await client.SendAsync(message);\n        await client.DisconnectAsync(true);\n    }\n\n    // Very basic HTML \u2192 plain\u2011text conversion; replace with a proper library if needed\n    private static string HtmlToPlainText(string html)\n    {\n        return System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex\n               .Replace(html, \"<.*?>\", string.Empty)\n               .Replace(\"&nbsp;\", \" \")\n               .Trim();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Using the helper\n\n```csharp\nawait MailKitHelper.SendHtmlAsync(\n    smtpHost: \"smtp.gmail.com\",\n    smtpPort: 587,\n    smtpUser: \"myaddress@gmail.com\",\n    smtpPwd: \"my-app-password\",\n    fromAddress: \"myaddress@gmail.com\",\n    fromName: \"My Company\",\n    toAddress: \"client@example.com\",\n    subject: \"Your Invoice\",\n    htmlBody: @\"\n        <h2>Invoice #12345</h2>\n        <p>Dear Customer,</p>\n        <p>Please find your invoice attached.</p>\n        <table style='border-collapse:collapse; width:100%;'>\n            <tr><th style='border:1px solid #ddd;'>Item</th><th style='border:1px solid #ddd;'>Qty</th><th style='border:1px solid #ddd;'>Price</th></tr>\n            <tr><td style='border:1px solid #ddd;'>Widget A</td><td style='border:1px solid #ddd;'>2</td><td style='border:1px solid #ddd;'>$10</td></tr>\n        </table>\n        <p>Thank you!</p>\n    \"\n);\n```\n\n**Advantages of MailKit**\n\n| Feature | Benefit |\n|---------|---------|\n| `SecureSocketOptions.StartTls` or `SslOnConnect` | Full TLS control. |\n| OAuth2, NTLM, custom SASL mechanisms | Works with modern providers (e.g., Gmail, Office\u202f365). |\n| Rich `MimeMessage` API | Easy to add attachments, inline images, DKIM signatures, etc. |\n| Async\u2011first design | Naturally fits `await`/`async`. |\n| Actively maintained | Receives security updates. |\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udccc Common Gotchas & Tips\n\n1. **SMTP Provider Limits**  \n   - Gmail, Outlook, etc., often require an *app\u2011specific password* or OAuth2 token.  \n   - Free accounts may cap the number of e\u2011mails per day.\n\n2. **HTML Rendering Variability**  \n   - E\u2011mail clients (Outlook, Gmail, Apple Mail) support only a subset of CSS.  \n   - Stick to inline styles, avoid JavaScript, and test with tools like *Litmus* or *Email on Acid*.\n\n3. **Images**  \n   - Use absolute URLs (`https://\u2026`) for external images.  \n   - For embedded images, attach them as `LinkedResource` (System.Net.Mail) or as `MimePart` with `Content-Id` ("}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How to send HTML-formatted email in C#?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks: \"How to send HTML-formatted email in C#?\" We need to provide a answer: likely show using SmtpClient, MailMessage, set IsBodyHtml = true, set Body to HTML, etc. Also discuss .NET Core vs .NET Framework, using MailKit etc. Provide code sample.\n\nWe need to respond in a helpful manner, explain steps, mention SMTP configuration, optional attachments, alternate view, using using statements, async sending, etc.\n\nWe must output final answer within <answer></answer> tags.\n\nLet's craft.\n\n</think>\n<answer>\n**Sending an HTML\u2011formatted e\u2011mail from C# is straightforward** \u2013 you create a `MailMessage`, place the HTML in its `Body`, set `IsBodyHtml` to `true`, and send it with an `SmtpClient` (or with a modern library such as **MailKit**).  \nBelow are two complete examples:\n\n---\n\n## 1\ufe0f\u20e3 Using the built\u2011in `System.Net.Mail` (works in .NET Framework and .NET\u202fCore\u202f2.0+)\n\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing System.Net;\nusing System.Net.Mail;\nusing System.Threading.Tasks;\n\npublic class EmailHelper\n{\n    /// <summary>\n    /// Sends an HTML e\u2011mail asynchronously.\n    /// </summary>\n    /// <param name=\"smtpHost\">SMTP server host (e.g. smtp.gmail.com)</param>\n    /// <param name=\"smtpPort\">SMTP server port (usually 587 for TLS)</param>\n    /// <param name=\"smtpUser\">SMTP username (often the e\u2011mail address)</param>\n    /// <param name=\"smtpPwd\">SMTP password or app\u2011specific password</param>\n    /// <param name=\"from\">Sender address (display name optional)</param>\n    /// <param name=\"to\">Recipient address (or a collection)</param>\n    /// <param name=\"subject\">Mail subject</param>\n    /// <param name=\"htmlBody\">HTML content of the mail</param>\n    /// <param name=\"isBodyHtml\">Set to true (default) \u2013 kept for flexibility</param>\n    /// <returns>A Task representing the async operation</returns>\n    public static async Task SendHtmlEmailAsync(\n        string smtpHost,\n        int smtpPort,\n        string smtpUser,\n        string smtpPwd,\n        MailAddress from,\n        MailAddress to,\n        string subject,\n        string htmlBody,\n        bool isBodyHtml = true)\n    {\n        // Construct the message\n        using var message = new MailMessage();\n        message.From = from;\n        message.To.Add(to);\n        message.Subject = subject;\n        message.Body = htmlBody;\n        message.IsBodyHtml = isBodyHtml;          // <-- **critical**\n        message.BodyEncoding = System.Text.Encoding.UTF8;\n        message.SubjectEncoding = System.Text.Encoding.UTF8;\n\n        // Optional: add a plain\u2011text fallback (good practice for accessibility)\n        // var plainView = AlternateView.CreateAlternateViewFromString(\n        //     HtmlToPlainText(htmlBody), null, \"text/plain\");\n        // var htmlView = AlternateView.CreateAlternateViewFromString(htmlBody, null, \"text/html\");\n        // message.AlternateViews.Add(plainView);\n        // message.AlternateViews.Add(htmlView);\n\n        // Configure the SMTP client\n        using var smtp = new SmtpClient(smtpHost, smtpPort)\n        {\n            EnableSsl = true,                     // TLS is required by most providers\n            Credentials = new NetworkCredential(smtpUser, smtpPwd),\n            // Timeout = 10000, // optional timeout in ms\n        };\n\n        // Send the mail\n        await smtp.SendMailAsync(message);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### How to call it\n\n```csharp\nawait EmailHelper.SendHtmlEmailAsync(\n    smtpHost: \"smtp.gmail.com\",\n    smtpPort: 587,\n    smtpUser: \"myaddress@gmail.com\",\n    smtpPwd: \"my-app-password\",\n    from: new MailAddress(\"myaddress@gmail.com\", \"My Name\"),\n    to: new MailAddress(\"receiver@example.com\", \"Receiver\"),\n    subject: \"Welcome to our service!\",\n    htmlBody: @\"\n        <h1>Hello, World!</h1>\n        <p>Thanks for joining us. Please click <a href='https://example.com/confirm'>here</a> to confirm your account.</p>\n        <img src='https://example.com/logo.png' alt='Logo' />\n    \"\n);\n```\n\n**Key points**\n\n| Setting | Why it matters |\n|---------|----------------|\n| `IsBodyHtml = true` | Tells the e\u2011mail client that the `Body` contains HTML. |\n| `BodyEncoding = UTF8` | Guarantees proper handling of special characters (e.g., emojis). |\n| `EnableSsl = true` | Modern SMTP servers require TLS encryption. |\n| `AlternateViews` (optional) | Provides a plain\u2011text version for clients that block HTML or for better accessibility. |\n\n---\n\n## 2\ufe0f\u20e3 Using **MailKit** (recommended for new projects, especially .NET\u202f5/6/7+)\n\n`MailKit` is a fully\u2011featured, actively\u2011maintained library that supersedes `System.Net.Mail`. It gives you better control over authentication mechanisms (OAuth2, STARTTLS, etc.) and richer MIME handling.\n\n### Install the package\n\n```bash\ndotnet add package MailKit\n```\n\n### Sample code\n\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing System.Threading.Tasks;\nusing MimeKit;\nusing MailKit.Net.Smtp;\nusing MailKit.Security;\n\npublic static class MailKitHelper\n{\n    public static async Task SendHtmlAsync(\n        string smtpHost,\n        int smtpPort,\n        string smtpUser,\n        string smtpPwd,\n        string fromAddress,\n        string fromName,\n        string toAddress,\n        string subject,\n        string htmlBody)\n    {\n        // 1\ufe0f\u20e3 Build the message\n        var message = new MimeMessage();\n        message.From.Add(new MailboxAddress(fromName, fromAddress));\n        message.To.Add(MailboxAddress.Parse(toAddress));\n        message.Subject = subject;\n\n        // 2\ufe0f\u20e3 Create the body (HTML + plain\u2011text fallback)\n        var body = new BodyBuilder\n        {\n            HtmlBody = htmlBody,\n            TextBody = HtmlToPlainText(htmlBody)   // simple conversion, optional\n        };\n        message.Body = body.ToMessageBody();\n\n        // 3\ufe0f\u20e3 Send via SMTP\n        using var client = new SmtpClient();\n\n        // Accept all SSL certificates (for demo only!)\n        // client.ServerCertificateValidationCallback = (s, c, h, e) => true;\n\n        await client.ConnectAsync(smtpHost, smtpPort, SecureSocketOptions.StartTls);\n        await client.AuthenticateAsync(smtpUser, smtpPwd);\n        await client.SendAsync(message);\n        await client.DisconnectAsync(true);\n    }\n\n    // Very basic HTML \u2192 plain\u2011text conversion; replace with a proper library if needed\n    private static string HtmlToPlainText(string html)\n    {\n        return System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex\n               .Replace(html, \"<.*?>\", string.Empty)\n               .Replace(\"&nbsp;\", \" \")\n               .Trim();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Using the helper\n\n```csharp\nawait MailKitHelper.SendHtmlAsync(\n    smtpHost: \"smtp.gmail.com\",\n    smtpPort: 587,\n    smtpUser: \"myaddress@gmail.com\",\n    smtpPwd: \"my-app-password\",\n    fromAddress: \"myaddress@gmail.com\",\n    fromName: \"My Company\",\n    toAddress: \"client@example.com\",\n    subject: \"Your Invoice\",\n    htmlBody: @\"\n        <h2>Invoice #12345</h2>\n        <p>Dear Customer,</p>\n        <p>Please find your invoice attached.</p>\n        <table style='border-collapse:collapse; width:100%;'>\n            <tr><th style='border:1px solid #ddd;'>Item</th><th style='border:1px solid #ddd;'>Qty</th><th style='border:1px solid #ddd;'>Price</th></tr>\n            <tr><td style='border:1px solid #ddd;'>Widget A</td><td style='border:1px solid #ddd;'>2</td><td style='border:1px solid #ddd;'>$10</td></tr>\n        </table>\n        <p>Thank you!</p>\n    \"\n);\n```\n\n**Advantages of MailKit**\n\n| Feature | Benefit |\n|---------|---------|\n| `SecureSocketOptions.StartTls` or `SslOnConnect` | Full TLS control. |\n| OAuth2, NTLM, custom SASL mechanisms | Works with modern providers (e.g., Gmail, Office\u202f365). |\n| Rich `MimeMessage` API | Easy to add attachments, inline images, DKIM signatures, etc. |\n| Async\u2011first design | Naturally fits `await`/`async`. |\n| Actively maintained | Receives security updates. |\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udccc Common Gotchas & Tips\n\n1. **SMTP Provider Limits**  \n   - Gmail, Outlook, etc., often require an *app\u2011specific password* or OAuth2 token.  \n   - Free accounts may cap the number of e\u2011mails per day.\n\n2. **HTML Rendering Variability**  \n   - E\u2011mail clients (Outlook, Gmail, Apple Mail) support only a subset of CSS.  \n   - Stick to inline styles, avoid JavaScript, and test with tools like *Litmus* or *Email on Acid*.\n\n3. **Images**  \n   - Use absolute URLs (`https://\u2026`) for external images.  \n   - For embedded images, attach them as `LinkedResource` (System.Net.Mail) or as `MimePart` with `Content-Id` ("}], "q_num": 212, "originalconversations": ["How to send HTML-formatted email in C#?", "Setting isBodyHtml to true allows you to use HTML tags in the message body:\n```\nmsg = new MailMessage(\"xxxx@gmail.com\",\n                \"yyyy@gmail.com\", \"Message from PSSP System\",\n                \"This email sent by the PSSP system<br />\" +\n                \"<b>this is bold text!</b>\");\n\nmsg.IsBodyHtml = true;\n```"], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "How to send HTML-formatted email in C#?"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "I've been writing C and C++ code for almost twenty years, but there's one aspect of these languages that I've never really understood. I've obviously used regular casts i.e.\n```MyClass *m = (MyClass *)ptr;\n```\nall over the place, but there seem to be two other types of casts, and I don't know the difference. What's the difference between the following lines of code?\n```MyClass *m = (MyClass *)ptr;\nMyClass *m = static_cast(ptr);\nMyClass *m = dynamic_cast(ptr);\n```"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks about difference between C-style cast, static_cast, dynamic_cast. They ask for C and C++, but the examples use C++ syntax. Need to explain C-style cast in C++ context, static_cast, dynamic_cast semantics, compile-time vs run-time checks, allowed conversions, polymorphic types, etc. Also note that static_cast requires explicit type, syntax static_cast<type>(expr). dynamic_cast works for polymorphic types (has virtual functions), requires RTTI, can cast down/up/down the inheritance hierarchy, performs run-time check; returns nullptr or throws std::bad_cast for references. Provide examples. Also explain that C-style cast can do many things: const_cast, reinterpret_cast, static_cast, combination. It picks first that works, can be dangerous. Also talk about C casts in C (just reinterpretation). Provide guidance: prefer C++-style casts. Also note need to include template syntax for static_cast<...>. Provide note about reinterpret_cast. Provide sample code. Use <answer> tags.\n\nLet's craft answer.</think>\n<answer>\n**Short answer**\n\n| Cast form | What it does (C++) | When it succeeds | Run\u2011time checking? |\n|-----------|-------------------|------------------|--------------------|\n| **C\u2011style cast** `(T)expr` | Tries, in order, a `static_cast`, a `const_cast`, a `reinterpret_cast`, or a combination of them. It can do *any* conversion the compiler knows about, even ones that are unsafe. | The first of the above that compiles. | No (except if the chosen conversion is a `dynamic_cast` performed implicitly, which is rare). |\n| **`static_cast<T>(expr)`** | Performs only conversions that are well\u2011defined at compile time: <br> \u2022 standard numeric conversions<br> \u2022 pointer up\u2011/down\u2011casts within an inheritance hierarchy (no run\u2011time check)<br> \u2022 `void*` \u2194 object\u2011pointer conversions<br> \u2022 explicit non\u2011polymorphic `reinterpret_cast`\u2011like conversions that are allowed by the language rules (e.g. between unrelated pointer types) | The conversion must be permitted by the language rules; if it isn\u2019t, compilation fails. | No run\u2011time check. |\n| **`dynamic_cast<T>(expr)`** | Safe run\u2011time cast for *polymorphic* types (the source type must have at least one `virtual` function). It uses RTTI to verify that the object actually is of the target type (or a base/derived thereof). | \u2022 Up\u2011cast (base \u2192 derived) is allowed if the object is really of that derived type. <br> \u2022 Down\u2011cast (derived \u2192 base) always succeeds. <br> \u2022 Cross\u2011cast (sibling classes) works if the object is a shared most\u2011derived object. | Yes: returns `nullptr` for pointer casts or throws `std::bad_cast` for reference casts when the check fails. |\n\n---\n\n### 1. C\u2011style cast `(MyClass *)ptr`\n\nIn C++ this form is a *catch\u2011all* that tries several different cast operators until one works:\n\n1. **`static_cast`** (including an implicit `const_cast` to drop qualifiers)\n2. **`reinterpret_cast`** (bit\u2011wise reinterpretation)\n3. **`const_cast`** (remove/restore `const` or `volatile`)\n4. **A combination**, e.g. `static_cast`\u202f+\u202f`const_cast`.\n\nBecause the compiler stops at the first one that compiles, you can end up with a cast that silently discards `const`, or that reinterprets a pointer to an unrelated type \u2013 both of which can be sources of bugs. In pure C ( C++) the same syntax only does a *reinterpretation* of bits; it does **no** type checking.\n\n```cpp\nvoid *ptr = \u2026;\nMyClass *m = (MyClass *)ptr;          // C\u2011style\n// Might be static_cast, reinterpret_cast, const_cast, or a mix.\n```\n\n**Why it\u2019s dangerous**\n\n* No explicit indication of *what* kind of conversion you intend.\n* Easy to accidentally cast away `const` or to reinterpret unrelated pointers.\n* If a better, safer cast exists (e.g., `static_cast`), the C\u2011style version hide that intention.\n\n---\n\n### 2. `static_cast<MyClass*>(ptr)`\n\n`static_cast` is the **preferred** cast when you know the conversion is safe **and** it does **not** require run\u2011time type information.\n\nTypical uses:\n\n```cpp\nint i = 42;\ndouble d = static_cast<double>(i);          // numeric conversion\n\nBase *b = derivedPtr;                       // up\u2011cast, implicit; can also be explicit:\nDerived *d = static_cast<Derived*>(b);      // down\u2011cast \u2013 compile\u2011time only check\n\nvoid *v = somePtr;                          // void* \u2194 object pointer\nMyClass *m = static_cast<MyClass*>(v);\n```\n\n*It never removes `const` or `volatile` qualifiers.* If you need to do that you must add a separate `const_cast`.\n\nIf the conversion is illegal (e.g., casting between unrelated class hierarchies without a `reinterpret_cast`), the code **does not compile** \u2013 giving you a safety net.\n\n---\n\n### 3. `dynamic_cast<MyClass*>(ptr)`\n\n`dynamic_cast` is the **run\u2011time checked** cast. It works **only** when the source type is **polymorphic** (has at least one `virtual` function). The compiler generates RTTI (run\u2011time type information) for those classes.\n\n```cpp\nstruct Base { virtual ~Base() {} };\nstruct Derived : Base {};\n\nBase *b = new Derived;\nDerived *d = dynamic_cast<Derived*>(b);   // succeeds, d != nullptr\n\nBase *b2 = new Base;\nDerived *d2 = dynamic_cast<Derived*>(b2); // fails, d2 == nullptr\n```\n\n* For **pointer** casts: if the check fails, the result is `nullptr`.\n* For **reference** casts: if the check fails, a `std::bad_cast` exception is thrown.\n\n`dynamic_cast` can also be used for **cross\u2011casts** (casting from one base class to another base class the same most\u2011derived object) and for **down\u2011casting** safely.\n\nBecause it incurs a run\u2011time cost (RTTI lookup) and requires the class hierarchy to be polymorphic, you should use it only when you genuinely need that safety.\n\n---\n\n### When to use which cast?\n\n| Situation | Recommended cast |\n|-----------|-----------------|\n| Simple numeric conversion, or safe pointer up\u2011/down\u2011cast where the relationship is known at compile time | `static_cast` |\n| You must *remove* `const`/`volatile` (rare, and you should ask why) | `const_cast` |\n| You need to reinterpret the underlying bits (e.g., converting between unrelated pointer types, or to/from integral types) | `reinterpret_cast` |\n| You have a polymorphic base pointer/reference and need to verify at run time that it actually points to a particular derived type | `dynamic_cast` |\n| You are maintaining old C code or quick\u2011and\u2011dirty prototypes and accept the risk | C\u2011style cast (but **avoid** in modern C++ code) |\n\n---\n\n### TL;DR\n\n```cpp\nMyClass *m1 = (MyClass *)ptr;          // C\u2011style: tries static, const, reinterpret, in that order.\nMyClass *m = static_cast<MyClass*>(ptr); // Compile\u2011time only, safe if the conversion is allowed.\nMyClass *m3 =_cast<MyClass*>(ptr); // Run\u2011time checked, works only for polymorphic bases.\n```\n\nPrefer the C++\u2011style casts (`static_cast`, `dynamic_cast`, `const_cast`, `reinterpret_cast`) because they make **exactly** what of conversion you intend explicit and give the compiler (and later readers of your code) a clear guarantee about safety. The C\u2011style cast should be considered legacy or a last resort."}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I've been writing C and C++ code for almost twenty years, but there's one aspect of these languages that I've never really understood. I've obviously used regular casts i.e.\n```MyClass *m = (MyClass *)ptr;\n```\nall over the place, but there seem to be two other types of casts, and I don't know the difference. What's the difference between the following lines of code?\n```MyClass *m = (MyClass *)ptr;\nMyClass *m = static_cast(ptr);\nMyClass *m = dynamic_cast(ptr);\n```"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks about difference between C-style cast, static_cast, dynamic_cast. They ask for C and C++, but the examples use C++ syntax. Need to explain C-style cast in C++ context, static_cast, dynamic_cast semantics, compile-time vs run-time checks, allowed conversions, polymorphic types, etc. Also note that static_cast requires explicit type, syntax static_cast<type>(expr). dynamic_cast works for polymorphic types (has virtual functions), requires RTTI, can cast down/up/down the inheritance hierarchy, performs run-time check; returns nullptr or throws std::bad_cast for references. Provide examples. Also explain that C-style cast can do many things: const_cast, reinterpret_cast, static_cast, combination. It picks first that works, can be dangerous. Also talk about C casts in C (just reinterpretation). Provide guidance: prefer C++-style casts. Also note need to include template syntax for static_cast<...>. Provide note about reinterpret_cast. Provide sample code. Use <answer> tags.\n\nLet's craft answer.</think>\n<answer>\n**Short answer**\n\n| Cast form | What it does (C++) | When it succeeds | Run\u2011time checking? |\n|-----------|-------------------|------------------|--------------------|\n| **C\u2011style cast** `(T)expr` | Tries, in order, a `static_cast`, a `const_cast`, a `reinterpret_cast`, or a combination of them. It can do