 fly), rated \u201330\u202f\u00b0C, double\u2011wall, pole\u2011grid for wind resistance | Keeps wind out, prevents collapse under snow/ice. |\n| **Sleeping bag** | Down or synthetic, EN\u2011type \u201330\u202f\u00b0C (or lower). Prefer a **hood** with a hood\u2011drawstring. | Guarantees you stay above your comfort temperature even if you \u201cover\u2011bundle\u201d. |\n| **Sleeping pad** | Closed\u2011cell foam (e.g., EVA) + inflatable \u201cself\u2011inflating\u201d R\u2011value\u202f\u2265\u202f3\u202f\u00b0C, total thickness \u2265\u202f2\u202fin. | Insulates you from conductive heat loss to the frozen ground. |\n| **Base layers** | Moisture\u2011wicking (merino wool or synthetics) \u2013 no cotton. | Keeps skin dry, avoids conductive cooling. |\n| **Insulation layers** | Fleece or down jacket, insulated pants, insulated parka for outer. | Traps body heat. |\n| **Outer shell** | Waterproof, breathable, wind\u2011proof (e.g., GORE\u2011TEX or similar). | Shields against wind, snow, and dampness. |\n| **Head & hands** | Insulated hat, balaclava, insulated gloves/mitts (prefer mitts). | Prevents heat loss from extremities; a lot of heat is lost via the head. |\n| **Nutrition & hydration** | 300\u202fg+ of food per night (fatty, high\u2011protein), insulated thermos of hot water, dehydrated meals, extra snack for \u201cwarm\u2011up\u201d. | Eating raises metabolic rate; hot liquids keep you warm from inside. |\n| **Portable heat source** | Battery\u2011operated hand\u2011warmers, chemical heating pads, or a **certified portable stove** for a small \u201cwarm drink\u201d before bed (if permitted). | Quick spot\u2011heat for extremities. |\n| **Lighting & signalling** | Headlamp with extra batteries, whistle, reflective gear. | Allows you to see and call for help if needed. |\n| **Safety gear** | Mobile phone with power bank, **GPS tracker/PLB** (personal locator beacon), fire\u2011starter, first\u2011aid kit. | For emergency rescue. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Legal & practical considerations in Sweden\n\n1. **Allemansr\u00e4tten (Right of public access)** \u2013 You may camp on open land for up to 2\u202fnights (or 48\u202fhours) without landowner permission, provided you stay at least 20\u202fm from houses/buildings and you leave no traces. A *tent\u2011camp** can be set up under these rules, but a month\u2011long stay is **not covered** and could be seen as trespassing.\n2. **Local regulations** \u2013 Municipalities may have stricter rules for fire, waste, and permanent structures. Check with the **local kommun** (e.g., Nacka, Solna) about any camping restrictions.\n3. **Fire bans** \u2013 During especially cold, dry periods, Sweden may institute a *fire ban** prohibiting open flames. A portable stove is usually still allowed if it is *fuel\u2011based** (e.g., isobutane) and used in a controlled manner.\n4. **Insurance & health coverage** \u2013 The Swedish health system covers emergencies, but you need a **EU\u2011Health\u2011Card** or private insurance; a PLB can trigger faster assistance.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Risks beyond simple cold\n\n| Risk | Details | Mitigation |\n|------|---------|------------|\n| **Hypothermia** | Core body temperature <\u202f35\u202f\u00b0C. Can set in after a few hours of exposure if you\u2019re damp. | Keep all clothing dry, change into dry base layers before bed, use a high\u2011R\u2011value sleeping pad and sub\u2011\u20110\u202f\u00b0C sleeping bag. |\n| **Frostbite** | Fingers, toes, ears can freeze in \u201320\u202f\u00b0C winds. | Keep extremities insulated (mitts, insulated booties, hat). |\n| **Wildlife** | Large mammals (elves) are rare, but moose or fox may investigate. | Store food in sealed bags, keep a clean campsite, do not feed animals. |\n| **Isolation / mental health** | Sleeping alone in a small space can be stressful, especially in perpetual darkness (early mornings). | Arrange a weekly \u201ccheck\u2011in\u201d with friends, bring a small radio or portable music device, maintain a regular routine. |\n| **Injury** | Slipping on ice, falling from snow banks, or a tent collapse. | Choose a flat, stable campsite; clear snow around the tent; use pole\u2011grid to brace against wind. |\n| **Lack of medical help** | Nighttime emergencies can be hard to reach quickly. | Carry a **PLB** or **sat\u2011phone**; let someone know your exact GPS coordinates. |\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to make a single night as safe as possible\n\n1. **Site selection**  \n   - Look for a **south\u2011facing slope** sheltered by a hill or dense tree line that blocks wind.  \n   - Avoid low\u2011lying depressions where cold air pools (cold\u2011air \u201cbasins\u201d).  \n   - Ensure the ground is not prone to deep snow drift; a thin layer of pine needles can act as natural insulation.\n\n2. **Tent set\u2011up**  \n   - Pitch the tent with the **fly downwind** to minimise wind penetration.  \n   - If possible, add a **snow skirt** (roll a tarp around the base, bury under a few inches of snow) to seal gaps.  \n\n3. **Sleeping system**  \n   - Lay the insulated pad **directly on the ground**, then place a **cirque\u2011shaped \u201cspoon\u201d** of extra foam under the pad\u2019s sleeping area to create an air gap, further reducing conduction.  \n   - Put the sleeping bag **inside a bivy sack** (optional) for an extra barrier against drafts.  \n\n4. **Pre\u2011bed routine**  \n   - Do a **light cardio exercise** (e.g., jog in place) for 3\u20115 minutes to raise core temperature.  \n   - **Eat** a high\u2011fat snack (e.g., cheese, nuts) immediately before sleeping.  \n   - **Drink** a warm beverage (tea/coffee) to warm hands, but avoid large amounts of fluid that may cause nocturnal bathroom trips (keep a bottle of water handy).  \n   - **Dry off** any sweat and change into completely dry layers before entering the bag.\n\n5. **During the night**  \n   - Keep a **hand\u2011warmer** in each glove at the start of sleep.  \n   - If you wake and feel cold, add an extra **layer of blankets** inside the bag (e.g., a fleece or a spare"}], "q_num": 228, "originalconversations": ["Can I spend the night alone in a tent in a forest outside Stockholm in -20\u00b0C without risking my life?\n\nThe backstory\nFrom the end of January, I'm starting my studies in a suburb of Stockholm. I've decided to, if it turns out plausible, not rent an apartment, but live in a tent. (This is not out of frugality, but out of a will to try something new.)\nI do have friends who I could visit once a week or so to prepare food and wash my clothes, so I think I can solve the practical problems, or at least those that I've come to think of. I'd camp in one of the forests, maybe 1 km from \"civilisation\". I'd have access to showers etc at university every day.\nHowever: I don't want to freeze to death in my sleep! That's very important to me. I've read that the nights can get as cold as -20\u00b0C (-4\u00b0F). With the proper preparations, would this be a plausible way of living, at least for a month or so?\nI do have camping experience, and have been hiking for three weeks, but only in summer.", "Yes, it is definitely doable. -20&deg;C is only -4&deg;F. The real question is whether it is doable by you at the level of discomfort and hassle you are willing to put up with. Only you can answer that. At best we can point out what the hassles and discomforts will be.\n\nFirst, your fear of dying of cold in your sleep is silly. You'd have to do something pretty stupid to die of hypothermia, and even that's not going to happen when you're inside the sleeping bag. The main danger will be from frostbite, but that again would largely need stupidity to help it along, although that's easier to do than outright death from hypothermia.\n\nYour tent will be a long term fixed installation you set up once at a time and conditions of your choosing. You can therefore afford a larger and heavier tent with more stuff you bring in once. Definitely get a tent you can stand upright in. That will make changing clothes much quicker and more comfortable. Since you should be able to keep water out of the tent, get a nice down sleeping bag and a few light blankets. The down bag should be rated for most nights, then put the blankets on top for the few unusually cold nights. Since again weight is not really a issue, get a full sleeping bag, not a mummy bag. They are simply more comfortable. Get a good insulating pad, and another two as backup. Get a tent large enough to fit your sleeping bag and something to sit on next to it, like a folding chair. Put something under the legs to spread out the weight to that they don't hurt the tent floor. Get one of those rubber-backed mats people sometimes put just inside their doors and put it just inside your tent. That allows a place to step with boots still on, then you can sit down on the chair with boots still on the mat to take them off. The crud stays on the mat, which you can shake clean by reaching outside after having put on your down hut booties.\n\nSome things are going to be a hassle. At -4&deg;F you want to keep your gloves on whenever possible, but some tasks will be difficult that way. You end up taking your gloves on and off a lot, trading off efficiency with cold fingers. Get a pair of polypro glove liners. They are thin and still allow many tasks to be done, but provide at least a little insulation. Their main advantage is that any metal you touch won't immediately conduct the heet from your hand away. Touching bare metal at -4&deg;F is a good way to get frostbite.\n\nBe prepared for some discomfort no matter what equipment you have. The toughest part will be getting yourself out of the sleeping bag in the morning. You'll really have to will yourself to leave the warm comfort of the bag and get into the air at probably the coldest part of the day. At some point you'll have to change your clothes and get undressed in the process. That's going to be cold. It won't be cold long enough to be any real danger, so it's really a mindset issue to get over. Whether you can or not and are willing to push yourself in that way only you can say.\n\nYou say you have access to heated buildings during the day, so it would make things a lot simpler for you if you don't have to deal with cooking and eating at your camp. Perparing food outside in the cold takes a lot longer than in a heated kitchen, severly limits what you can do, and may also risk predator encounters depending on what is around your area in the winter."], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "Can I spend the night alone in a tent in a forest outside Stockholm in -20\u00b0C without risking my life?\n\nThe backstory\nFrom the end of January, I'm starting my studies in a suburb of Stockholm. I've decided to, if it turns out plausible, not rent an apartment, but live in a tent. (This is not out of frugality, but out of a will to try something new.)\nI do have friends who I could visit once a week or so to prepare food and wash my clothes, so I think I can solve the practical problems, or at least those that I've come to think of. I'd camp in one of the forests, maybe 1 km from \"civilisation\". I'd have access to showers etc at university every day.\nHowever: I don't want to freeze to death in my sleep! That's very important to me. I've read that the nights can get as cold as -20\u00b0C (-4\u00b0F). With the proper preparations, would this be a plausible way of living, at least for a month or so?\nI do have camping experience, and have been hiking for three weeks, but only in summer."}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "What factors determine the maximum altitude for a plane?\nIs it limited by wing design, engine thrust, and so on?\nIs there a formula by which one can calculate the maximum altitude a plane can reach?"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer user question: factors that determine max altitude for a plane, like wing design, engine, etc., and any formula to calculate max altitude. Provide answer in <answer></answer> XML tags as per instruction. Should be thorough, discuss service ceiling, absolute ceiling, limiting factors: lift, thrust, power, drag, weight, density, atmospheric temperature, structural limits, pressurization, oxygen, engine performance (turbine/jet vs piston), air intake, compressor, aerodynamic stall, etc. Show equations: lift = 0.5 * rho * V^2 * S * Cl; thrust vs drag: T = D at level flight; power required equals power available; max altitude where thrust equals drag at max continuous power, or where lift equals weight at stall speed, etc. Provide formula: service ceiling when rate of climb < some small value (e.g., 100 fpm). So use climb rate: R/C = (T - D) * V / W. Set to zero gives absolute ceiling. Provide simple method: solving for altitude where (T0 * rho/ rho0)^(something) = D, etc. Jet: thrust decreases roughly linearly with air density. Propeller: power decreases as rho^0.5. Wing: required stall speed increases with sqrt(W/(rho * S * Cl_max)). So altitude limit when stall speed exceeds maximum speed due to thrust/drag.\n\nHence answer: No single simple formula; need performance curves and atmospheric model (ISA). Provide steps.\n\nWill output final answer with <answer>.</think>\n<answer>\n**Maximum altitude of an aircraft \u2013 the \u201cceiling\u201d \u2013 is not set by a single number but by the point at which one or more of the aircraft\u2019s performance limits are reached.**  \nThe two most common definitions are:\n\n| Term | How it is defined | Typical use |\n|------|-------------------|-------------|\n| **Service ceiling** | Altitude at which the aircraft can maintain a **rate of climb** of 100\u202fft\u202f/min (\u22480.5\u202fm\u202fs\u207b\u00b9) in clean configuration. | Operational limit used by pilots and operators. |\n| **Absolute ceiling** | Altitude at which the **rate of climb drops to zero** \u2013 the aircraft can no longer climb because thrust (or power) just balances drag while lift just balances weight. | Theoretical limit; \u201cmaximum possible\u201d altitude. |\n\nBelow is a systematic look at the *factors* that determine these ceilings and the *equations* that allow a calculation (in practice you need the aircraft\u2019s performance curves).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Fundamental physical relationships\n\n1. **Lift must equal weight for steady, level flight**  \n\n   \\[\n   L = \\tfrac12 \\,\\rho\\, V^{2}\\,S\\,C_{L}=W\n   \\]\n\n   where  \n\n   * \u03c1 \u2013 air density (decreases with altitude)  \n   * V \u2013 true airspeed  \n   * S \u2013 wing reference area  \n   * \\(C_{L}\\) \u2013 lift coefficient (cannot exceed the wing\u2019s \\(C_{L_{\\max}}\\) without stalling)\n\n2. **Thrust (or power) must equal drag for zero\u2011acceleration flight**  \n\n   \\[\n   T(V,\\rho) = D(V,\\rho)=\\tfrac12 \\,\\rho\\,V^{2}\\,S\\,C_{D}\n   \\]\n\n   \\(C_{D}=C_{D_{0}}+k\\,C_{L}^{2}\\) (parabolic drag polar).\n\n3. **Rate of climb** (derived from the excess thrust/power)  \n\n   \\[\n   \\text{RC} = \\frac{(T-D)\\,V}{W}\n   \\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n   \\text{RC} = \\frac{P_{\\text{avail}}-P_{\\text{req}}}{W}\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(P = T\\,V\\) for jets and \\(P = \\eta_{\\text{prop}}\\,\\rho^{1/2} \\,V^{3}\\,S_{\\text{prop}}\\) for prop\u2011driven aircraft.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. How each factor limits altitude\n\n| Limiting factor | Why it matters | How it appears in the equations |\n|-----------------|----------------|--------------------------------|\n| **Wing lift capability** | As \u03c1 falls, the same weight needs a higher \\(C_{L}\\) or a higher speed. \\(C_{L}\\) is bounded by \\(C_{L_{\\max}}\\). If the required stall speed exceeds the maximum speed the aircraft can generate, the aircraft cannot stay aloft. | From lift\u202f=\u202fweight: \\(\\displaystyle V_{\\text{stall}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2W}{\\rho S C_{L_{\\max}}}}\\). When \\(V_{\\text{stall}}\\) > available V (limited by thrust) \u2192 stall limit reached. |\n| **Engine thrust / power** | Jet thrust roughly follows \\(T \\propto \\rho\\) (or a slightly steeper curve for low\u2011speed compressors). Propeller power falls roughly as \\(\\rho^{1/2}\\). When thrust can no longer balance drag, the aircraft cannot maintain level flight. | Jet: \\(T(h)=T_{0}\\,\\frac{\\rho(h)}{\\rho_{0}}\\). Prop: \\(P_{\\text{avail}}(h)=P_{0}\\,\\big(\\frac{\\rho(h)}{\\rho_{0}}\\big)^{1/2}\\). |\n| **Drag growth with speed** | To keep lift at lower density you must fly faster, which increases parasite drag (\u221d\u202f\u03c1\u202fV\u00b2) and induced drag (\u221d\u202f1/V\u00b2). The higher drag consumes more thrust/power, reducing excess for climb. | Drag polar gives \\(D=\\tfrac12\\rho V^{2}S(C_{D0}+kC_{L}^{2})\\). |\n| **Weight** | Heavier aircraft need more lift, thus higher speed or higher \\(C_{L}\\). More speed \u2192 more drag \u2192 more thrust required \u2192 lower ceiling. | Appears directly in lift equation and in RC expression as denominator. |\n| **Aerodynamic design (wing loading, aspect ratio, airfoil)** | High wing loading (W/S) raises stall speed. Low aspect ratio raises parasite drag. Both lower ceiling. | Influences \\(C_{L_{\\max}}\\) and drag parameters \\(C_{D0}, k\\). |\n| **Environmental limits** | Ambient temperature, pressure, and humidity affect \u03c1 (ISA model). Colder air \u2192 higher density \u2192 higher ceiling; hot \u201chigh\u2011density\u2011altitude\u201d reduces it. | Use ISA or a real\u2011time atmospheric model to get \u03c1(h). |\n| **Structural & systems limits** | Maximum allowable dynamic pressure \\(q_{\\max}=\u00bd\u03c1V^{2}\\) (e.g., \u201cVNE\u201d for light aircraft, \u201climit load factor\u201d for jets) and cabin pressurization limits. At very high altitudes a pressurised aircraft may be limited to a lower altitude to keep cabin differential pressure within design limits. | Imposed as an upper bound on V for a given \u03c1, or as a hard altitude (e.g., 41\u202f000\u202fft for most commercial jets). |\n| **Regulatory / operational constraints** | Minimum required engine-out climb performance, required reserve fuel, etc., can effectively lower the usable ceiling. | Usually expressed as a required minimum RC at a given altitude. |\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Calculating the **service ceiling** (practical approach)\n\n1. **Obtain performance curves** for the aircraft at several standard altitudes:  \n   * Thrust (or power) available vs. altitude.  \n   * Drag or fuel\u2011flow vs. airspeed at each altitude.  \n   * Weight (including fuel burned to that point).\n\n2. **For each altitude** compute the **maximum rate of climb** (RC) using the excess thrust/power equation:\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{RC}(h) = \\frac{[T(h)-D(V_{\\text{opt}},h)]\\,V_{\\text{opt}}}{W(h)}\n   \\]\n\n   *\\(V_{\\text{opt}}\\) is the speed that gives the greatest RC (often near the \u201cbest rate of climb\u201d speed).*\n\n3. **Interpolate** the altitude where RC falls to the chosen limit (commonly 100\u202fft\u202f/min).  \n   That altitude is the **service ceiling**.\n\n*Example (simplified jet)*  \n\nAssume: \\(T_{0}= 100\\,\\text{kN}\\) at sea level, \\(W= 600\\,\\text{kN}\\), \\(S= 120\\,\\text{m}^2\\), \\(C_{D0}=0.02\\), \\(k=0.045\\), \\(C_{L_{\\max}}=1.6\\).  \n\nUsing the ISA density table, compute \u03c1 at successive heights, evaluate:\n\n* Required stall speed \\(V_{s}= \\sqrt{2W/(\u03c1 S C_{L_{\\max}})}\\).  \n* Choose a climb speed a little above \\(V_{s}\\) (\u22481.2\u202f\\(V_{s}\\)).  \n* Compute thrust \\(T= T_{0}\u00b7\u03c1/\u03c1_{0}\\).  \n* Compute drag \\(D = \u00bd\u03c1V^{2}S(C_{D0}+k(C_{L})^{2})\\) with \\(C_{L}=W/(\u00bd\u03c1V^{2}S)\\).  \n* Compute RC = (T\u2011D)\u00b7V/W.\n\nWhen RC \u2248 0.5\u202fm\u202fs\u207b\u00b9 (\u2248100\u202fft\u202f/min"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What factors determine the maximum altitude for a plane?\nIs it limited by wing design, engine thrust, and so on?\nIs there a formula by which one can calculate the maximum altitude a plane can reach?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer user question: factors that determine max altitude for a plane, like wing design, engine, etc., and any formula to calculate max altitude. Provide answer in <answer></answer> XML tags as per instruction. Should be thorough, discuss service ceiling, absolute ceiling, limiting factors: lift, thrust, power, drag, weight, density, atmospheric temperature, structural limits, pressurization, oxygen, engine performance (turbine/jet vs piston), air intake, compressor, aerodynamic stall, etc. Show equations: lift = 0.5 * rho * V^2 * S * Cl; thrust vs drag: T = D at level flight; power required equals power available; max altitude where thrust equals drag at max continuous power, or where lift equals weight at stall speed, etc. Provide formula: service ceiling when rate of climb < some small value (e.g., 100 fpm). So use climb rate: R/C = (T - D) * V / W. Set to zero gives absolute ceiling. Provide simple method: solving for altitude where (T0 * rho/ rho0)^(something) = D, etc. Jet: thrust decreases roughly linearly with air density. Propeller: power decreases as rho^0.5. Wing: required stall speed increases with sqrt(W/(rho * S * Cl_max)). So altitude limit when stall speed exceeds maximum speed due to thrust/drag.\n\nHence answer: No single simple formula; need performance curves and atmospheric model (ISA). Provide steps.\n\nWill output final answer with <answer>.</think>\n<answer>\n**Maximum altitude of an aircraft \u2013 the \u201cceiling\u201d \u2013 is not set by a single number but by the point at which one or more of the aircraft\u2019s performance limits are reached.**  \nThe two most common definitions are:\n\n| Term | How it is defined | Typical use |\n|------|-------------------|-------------|\n| **Service ceiling** | Altitude at which the aircraft can maintain a **rate of climb** of 100\u202fft\u202f/min (\u22480.5\u202fm\u202fs\u207b\u00b9) in clean configuration. | Operational limit used by pilots and operators. |\n| **Absolute ceiling** | Altitude at which the **rate of climb drops to zero** \u2013 the aircraft can no longer climb because thrust (or power) just balances drag while lift just balances weight. | Theoretical limit; \u201cmaximum possible\u201d altitude. |\n\nBelow is a systematic look at the *factors* that determine these ceilings and the *equations* that allow a calculation (in practice you need the aircraft\u2019s performance curves).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Fundamental physical relationships\n\n1. **Lift must equal weight for steady, level flight**  \n\n   \\[\n   L = \\tfrac12 \\,\\rho\\, V^{2}\\,S\\,C_{L}=W\n   \\]\n\n   where  \n\n   * \u03c1 \u2013 air density (decreases with altitude)  \n   * V \u2013 true airspeed  \n   * S \u2013 wing reference area  \n   * \\(C_{L}\\) \u2013 lift coefficient (cannot exceed the wing\u2019s \\(C_{L_{\\max}}\\) without stalling)\n\n2. **Thrust (or power) must equal drag for zero\u2011acceleration flight**  \n\n   \\[\n   T(V,\\rho) = D(V,\\rho)=\\tfrac12 \\,\\rho\\,V^{2}\\,S\\,C_{D}\n   \\]\n\n   \\(C_{D}=C_{D_{0}}+k\\,C_{L}^{2}\\) (parabolic drag polar).\n\n3. **Rate of climb** (derived from the excess thrust/power)  \n\n   \\[\n   \\text{RC} = \\frac{(T-D)\\,V}{W}\n   \\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n   \\text{RC} = \\frac{P_{\\text{avail}}-P_{\\text{req}}}{W}\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(P = T\\,V\\) for jets and \\(P = \\eta_{\\text{prop}}\\,\\rho^{1/2} \\,V^{3}\\,S_{\\text{prop}}\\) for prop\u2011driven aircraft.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. How each factor limits altitude\n\n| Limiting factor | Why it matters | How it appears in the equations |\n|-----------------|----------------|--------------------------------|\n| **Wing lift capability** | As \u03c1 falls, the same weight needs a higher \\(C_{L}\\) or a higher speed. \\(C_{L}\\) is bounded by \\(C_{L_{\\max}}\\). If the required stall speed exceeds the maximum speed the aircraft can generate, the aircraft cannot stay aloft. | From lift\u202f=\u202fweight: \\(\\displaystyle V_{\\text{stall}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2W}{\\rho S C_{L_{\\max}}}}\\). When \\(V_{\\text{stall}}\\) > available V (limited by thrust) \u2192 stall limit reached. |\n| **Engine thrust / power** | Jet thrust roughly follows \\(T \\propto \\rho\\) (or a slightly steeper curve for low\u2011speed compressors). Propeller power falls roughly as \\(\\rho^{1/2}\\). When thrust can no longer balance drag, the aircraft cannot maintain level flight. | Jet: \\(T(h)=T_{0}\\,\\frac{\\rho(h)}{\\rho_{0}}\\). Prop: \\(P_{\\text{avail}}(h)=P_{0}\\,\\big(\\frac{\\rho(h)}{\\rho_{0}}\\big)^{1/2}\\). |\n| **Drag growth with speed** | To keep lift at lower density you must fly faster, which increases parasite drag (\u221d\u202f\u03c1\u202fV\u00b2) and induced drag (\u221d\u202f1/V\u00b2). The higher drag consumes more thrust/power, reducing excess for climb. | Drag polar gives \\(D=\\tfrac12\\rho V^{2}S(C_{D0}+kC_{L}^{2})\\). |\n| **Weight** | Heavier aircraft need more lift, thus higher speed or higher \\(C_{L}\\). More speed \u2192 more drag \u2192 more thrust required \u2192 lower ceiling. | Appears directly in lift equation and in RC expression as denominator. |\n| **Aerodynamic design (wing loading, aspect ratio, airfoil)** | High wing loading (W/S) raises stall speed. Low aspect ratio raises parasite drag. Both lower ceiling. | Influences \\(C_{L_{\\max}}\\) and drag parameters \\(C_{D0}, k\\). |\n| **Environmental limits** | Ambient temperature, pressure, and humidity affect \u03c1 (ISA model). Colder air \u2192 higher density \u2192 higher ceiling; hot \u201chigh\u2011density\u2011altitude\u201d reduces it. | Use ISA or a real\u2011time atmospheric model to get \u03c1(h). |\n| **Structural & systems limits** | Maximum allowable dynamic pressure \\(q_{\\max}=\u00bd\u03c1V^{2}\\) (e.g., \u201cVNE\u201d for light aircraft, \u201climit load factor\u201d for jets) and cabin pressurization limits. At very high altitudes a pressurised aircraft may be limited to a lower altitude to keep cabin differential pressure within design limits. | Imposed as an upper bound on V for a given \u03c1, or as a hard altitude (e.g., 41\u202f000\u202fft for most commercial jets). |\n| **Regulatory / operational constraints** | Minimum required engine-out climb performance, required reserve fuel, etc., can effectively lower the usable ceiling. | Usually expressed as a required minimum RC at a given altitude. |\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Calculating the **service ceiling** (practical approach)\n\n1. **Obtain performance curves** for the aircraft at several standard altitudes:  \n   * Thrust (or power) available vs. altitude.  \n   * Drag or fuel\u2011flow vs. airspeed at each altitude.  \n   * Weight (including fuel burned to that point).\n\n2. **For each altitude** compute the **maximum rate of climb** (RC) using the excess thrust/power equation:\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{RC}(h) = \\frac{[T(h)-D(V_{\\text{opt}},h)]\\,V_{\\text{opt}}}{W(h)}\n   \\]\n\n   *\\(V_{\\text{opt}}\\) is the speed that gives the greatest RC (often near the \u201cbest rate of climb\u201d speed).*\n\n3. **Interpolate** the altitude where RC falls to the chosen limit (commonly 100\u202fft\u202f/min).  \n   That altitude is the **service ceiling**.\n\n*Example (simplified jet)*  \n\nAssume: \\(T_{0}= 100\\,\\text{kN}\\) at sea level, \\(W= 600\\,\\text{kN}\\), \\(S= 120\\,\\text{m}^2\\), \\(C_{D0}=0.02\\), \\(k=0.045\\), \\(C_{L_{\\max}}=1.6\\).  \n\nUsing the ISA density table, compute \u03c1 at successive heights, evaluate:\n\n* Required stall speed \\(V_{s}= \\sqrt{2W/(\u03c1 S C_{L_{\\max}})}\\).  \n* Choose a climb speed a little above \\(V_{s}\\) (\u22481.2\u202f\\(V_{s}\\)).  \n* Compute thrust \\(T= T_{0}\u00b7\u03c1/\u03c1_{0}\\).  \n* Compute drag \\(D = \u00bd\u03c1V^{2}S(C_{D0}+k(C_{L})^{2})\\) with \\(C_{L}=W/(\u00bd\u03c1V^{2}S)\\).  \n* Compute RC = (T\u2011D)\u00b7V/W.\n\nWhen RC \u2248 0.5\u202fm\u202fs\u207b\u00b9 (\u2248100\u202fft\u202f/min"}], "q_num": 196, "originalconversations": ["What factors determine the maximum altitude for a plane?\nIs it limited by wing design, engine thrust, and so on?\nIs there a formula by which one can calculate the maximum altitude a plane can reach?", "The higher you get, the lower the density of the air becomes. This lower density results in a lower lift being generated for the same airspeed and angle of attack. Effectively, the higher you fly the higher your minimum speed becomes. So while climbing, your speed needs to increase to compensate for the lower air density. As long a you can fly faster, the lower density at altitude can be compensated for. \nBasically there are two things that limit your maximum speed: thrust and speed of sound and with that your maximum altitude.\nFirst is thrust; the higher you get, the lower the thrust your engines deliver. You might note that drag goes down with the air density as well but since you are flying faster and faster during the climb the drag doesn't decrease at all. If your maximum altitude is limited by thrust then at some point during the climb the thrust and drag are getting close to equal and that is where the climb stops. When you can no longer climb with more than 100ft per minute (for propeller aircraft) or 500ft per minute (for jet / turbofan aircraft) you have reached your service ceiling. If the aircraft maximum altitude is determined by thrust, the absolute ceiling will take very long to reach. \nAt high altitudes air breathing engines will get difficulties eventually. Due to the lower air density the mass flow through the engine is reduced up to a point where it causes a flame out. \nThe other limitation is the speed of sound, at least for subsonic aircraft. In the process of generating lift, air flowing over the top of the wing is accelerated. At one point, when the aircraft is still flying below the speed of sound, shock waves will start to form over the wing. This results in increase of drag and reduces the lift. So provided you have enough engine power at your disposal you can climb to an altitude where your minimum speed is also your maximum speed. This is called the coffin corner. In the coffin corner:\n\n* if you fly any faster, you will exceed the maximum Mach number ($M_{mo}$) of your aircraft, resulting in high speed buffet, vibrations and possible loss of control. \n* if you fly any slower, the maximum lift that the wing can provide will be insufficient to maintain altitude. Descent or the aircraft will stall. \n* if you fly any higher and you will be too fast and too slow at the same time. \n* if you turn, you increase the wing loading, thereby increasing the minimum speed needed to create the required lift. Also the outer wing will easily exceed the maximum speed while at the same time the inner wing is below stall speed. This can quickly develop into a spin. \n\nSince accurate knowledge of engine performance, drag and wing characteristics of the aircraft is needed, there is not a simple formula to derive the maximum altitude for an aircraft.  \n\nApart from the aircraft performance related limitations above there is a certified maximum operating altitude for the pressurized cabin. This takes into account the structural properties of the hull (pressure difference between inside and outside) and the achievable emergency descent rate in case of a depressurization event."], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "What factors determine the maximum altitude for a plane?\nIs it limited by wing design, engine thrust, and so on?\nIs there a formula by which one can calculate the maximum altitude a plane can reach?"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "In my table view I have to scroll to the top. But I cannot guarantee that the first object is going to be section 0, row 0. May be that my table view will start from section number 5.\nSo I get an exception, when I call:\n```[mainTableView scrollToRowAtIndexPath:[NSIndexPath indexPathForRow:0 inSection:0] atScrollPosition:UITableViewScrollPositionTop animated:NO];\n```\nIs there another way to scroll to the top of table view?"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer: How to scroll to top of table view regardless of first visible section. Use scrollToRowAtIndexPath with first row of first section (0,0) but might not exist. Could use setContentOffset to (0,0) or scrollRectToVisible. Use tableView.setContentOffset(CGPointZero, animated:). But need to consider contentInset etc. Or use scrollToRowAtIndexPath using tableView numberOfSections and rowsInSection. Compute first existing row: find first section with rows >0. Or call [tableView setContentOffset:CGPointZero animated:NO]; or scrollToRowAtIndexPath with indexPathForRow:0 inSection:0 only if section exists, else scroll to top using scrollView's content offset. Provide code in Objective-C and Swift.\n\nAlso mention that table view inherits from UIScrollView, so you can call scrollToTop using \"[tableView setContentOffset:CGPointZero animated:YES];\". Also property scrollsToTop for navigation bar? Actually scrollToTop is for tapping status bar. But setContentOffset works.\n\nEdge cases: if you have contentInset, use CGPointMake(0, -tableView.contentInset.top). Use -tableView.adjustedContentInset.top for iOS 11+. Also method scrollRectToVisible: animated:.\n\nThus answer: Use scrollToRowAtIndexPath after getting the first index: \n\n```\nNSInteger sections = [tableView numberOfSections];\nfor (NSInteger s = 0; s < sections; s++) {\n    NSInteger rows = [tableView numberOfRowsInSection:s];\n    if (rows > 0) {\n        NSIndexPath *indexPath = [NSIndexPath indexPathForRow:0 inSection:s];\n        [tableView scrollToRowAtIndexPath:indexPath atScrollPosition:UITableViewScrollPositionTop animated:NO];\n        break;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBut easier: set content offset.\n\nProvide answer accordingly. Use final tags.</think>\n<answer>\nYou can scroll a `UITableView` to its very beginning without knowing which section contains the first row.  \nBecause a `UITableView` is a subclass of `UIScrollView`, you have two simple, safe options:\n\n---\n\n## 1.\u202fSet the scroll\u2011view\u2019s content offset directly  \n\n```objective\u2011c\n// iOS\u00a011+ \u2013 respects safe\u2011area and adjusted insets\n[mainTableView setContentOffset:CGPointMake(0, -mainTableView.adjustedContentInset.top)\n                       animated:NO];\n\n// pre\u2011iOS\u00a011 \u2013 use the plain contentInset\n//[mainTableView setContentOffset:CGPointMake(0, -mainTableView.contentInset.top) animated:NO];\n```\n\n*Pros:*  \n* Works even when the table has no rows at all.  \n* No need to query sections/rows.  \n\n*Cons:*  \n* If you have a custom `contentOffset` (e.g., a pinned header) you must add that offset manually.\n\n---\n\n## 2.\u202fFind the first real row and use `scrollToRowAtIndexPath:`  \n\nIf you prefer to keep the \u201cscroll\u2011to\u2011row\u201d semantics (e.g., you want the row to snap to the top exactly), locate the first section that actually contains rows and scroll to its row\u00a00:\n\n```objective\u2011c\nNSInteger sections = [mainTableView numberOfSections];\n\nfor (NSInteger s = 0; s < sections; ++s) {\n    NSInteger rows = [mainTableView numberOfRowsInSection:s];\n    if (rows > 0) {\n        NSIndexPath *first = [NSIndexPath indexPathForRow:0 inSection:s];\n        [mainTableView scrollToRowAtIndexPath:first\n                               atScrollPosition:UITableViewScrollPositionTop\n                                       animated:NO];\n        break;          // we\u2019ve scrolled \u2013 stop the loop\n    }\n}\n\n/* If the table is completely empty, the loop does nothing, which is safe. */\n```\n\n*Pros:*  \n* Guarantees that a row is positioned exactly at the top of the visible area.  \n\n*Cons:*  \n* Slightly more code, and you have to guard against an empty table (the loop already does that).\n\n---\n\n### Which one should you use?\n\n| Situation | Recommended approach |\n|-----------|----------------------|\n| You just want \u201cshow the very beginning\u201d (no need for a specific row to be flush with the top) | **Option\u00a01** \u2013 set `contentOffset`. |\n| You need the first row to align perfectly with the table\u2011view\u2019s top (e.g., for a \u201cjump\u2011to\u2011first\u2011item\u201d button) | **Option\u00a02** \u2013 find the first row and call `scrollToRowAtIndexPath:`. |\n| Your table may be completely empty | Both options are safe; Option\u00a01 is the simplest. |\n\n---\n\n### Quick Swift equivalents (if you code in Swift)\n\n```swift\n// 1\ufe0f\u20e3 Content\u2011offset method (iOS\u00a011+)\ntableView.setContentOffset(\n    CGPoint(x: 0, y: -tableView.adjustedContentInset.top),\n    animated: false\n)\n\n// 2\ufe0f\u20e3 Find first row and scroll\nlet sections = tableView.numberOfSections\nfor s in 0..<sections {\n    let rows = tableView.numberOfRows(inSection: s)\n    if rows > 0 {\n        let first = IndexPath(row: 0, section: s)\n        tableView.scrollToRow(at: first,\n                              at: .top,\n                              animated: false)\n        break\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBoth solutions avoid the crash you see when you blindly request section\u202f0, row\u202f0 while that index path does not exist."}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "In my table view I have to scroll to the top. But I cannot guarantee that the first object is going to be section 0, row 0. May be that my table view will start from section number 5.\nSo I get an exception, when I call:\n```[mainTableView scrollToRowAtIndexPath:[NSIndexPath indexPathForRow:0 inSection:0] atScrollPosition:UITableViewScrollPositionTop animated:NO];\n```\nIs there another way to scroll to the top of table view?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer: How to scroll to top of table view regardless of first visible section. Use scrollToRowAtIndexPath with first row of first section (0,0) but might not exist. Could use setContentOffset to (0,0) or scrollRectToVisible. Use tableView.setContentOffset(CGPointZero, animated:). But need to consider contentInset etc. Or use scrollToRowAtIndexPath using tableView numberOfSections and rowsInSection. Compute first existing row: find first section with rows >0. Or call [tableView setContentOffset:CGPointZero animated:NO]; or scrollToRowAtIndexPath with indexPathForRow:0 inSection:0 only if section exists, else scroll to top using scrollView's content offset. Provide code in Objective-C and Swift.\n\nAlso mention that table view inherits from UIScrollView, so you can call scrollToTop using \"[tableView setContentOffset:CGPointZero animated:YES];\". Also property scrollsToTop for navigation bar? Actually scrollToTop is for tapping status bar. But setContentOffset works.\n\nEdge cases: if you have contentInset, use CGPointMake(0, -tableView.contentInset.top). Use -tableView.adjustedContentInset.top for iOS 11+. Also method scrollRectToVisible: animated:.\n\nThus answer: Use scrollToRowAtIndexPath after getting the first index: \n\n```\nNSInteger sections = [tableView numberOfSections];\nfor (NSInteger s = 0; s < sections; s++) {\n    NSInteger rows = [tableView numberOfRowsInSection:s];\n    if (rows > 0) {\n        NSIndexPath *indexPath = [NSIndexPath indexPathForRow:0 inSection:s];\n        [tableView scrollToRowAtIndexPath:indexPath atScrollPosition:UITableViewScrollPositionTop animated:NO];\n        break;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBut easier: set content offset.\n\nProvide answer accordingly. Use final tags.</think>\n<answer>\nYou can scroll a `UITableView` to its very beginning without knowing which section contains the first row.  \nBecause a `UITableView` is a subclass of `UIScrollView`, you have two simple, safe options:\n\n---\n\n## 1.\u202fSet the scroll\u2011view\u2019s content offset directly  \n\n```objective\u2011c\n// iOS\u00a011+ \u2013 respects safe\u2011area and adjusted insets\n[mainTableView setContentOffset:CGPointMake(0, -mainTableView.adjustedContentInset.top)\n                       animated:NO];\n\n// pre\u2011iOS\u00a011 \u2013 use the plain contentInset\n//[mainTableView setContentOffset:CGPointMake(0, -mainTableView.contentInset.top) animated:NO];\n```\n\n*Pros:*  \n* Works even when the table has no rows at all.  \n* No need to query sections/rows.  \n\n*Cons:*  \n* If you have a custom `contentOffset` (e.g., a pinned header) you must add that offset manually.\n\n---\n\n## 2.\u202fFind the first real row and use `scrollToRowAtIndexPath:`  \n\nIf you prefer to keep the \u201cscroll\u2011to\u2011row\u201d semantics (e.g., you want the row to snap to the top exactly), locate the first section that actually contains rows and scroll to its row\u00a00:\n\n```objective\u2011c\nNSInteger sections = [mainTableView numberOfSections];\n\nfor (NSInteger s = 0; s < sections; ++s) {\n    NSInteger rows = [mainTableView numberOfRowsInSection:s];\n    if (rows > 0) {\n        NSIndexPath *first = [NSIndexPath indexPathForRow:0 inSection:s];\n        [mainTableView scrollToRowAtIndexPath:first\n                               atScrollPosition:UITableViewScrollPositionTop\n                                       animated:NO];\n        break;          // we\u2019ve scrolled \u2013 stop the loop\n    }\n}\n\n/* If the table is completely empty, the loop does nothing, which is safe. */\n```\n\n*Pros:*  \n* Guarantees that a row is positioned exactly at the top of the visible area.  \n\n*Cons:*  \n* Slightly more code, and you have to guard against an empty table (the loop already does that).\n\n---\n\n### Which one should you use?\n\n| Situation | Recommended approach |\n|-----------|----------------------|\n| You just want \u201cshow the very beginning\u201d (no need for a specific row to be flush with the top) | **Option\u00a01** \u2013 set `contentOffset`. |\n| You need the first row to align perfectly with the table\u2011view\u2019s top (e.g., for a \u201cjump\u2011to\u2011first\u2011item\u201d button) | **Option\u00a02** \u2013 find the first row and call `scrollToRowAtIndexPath:`. |\n| Your table may be completely empty | Both options are safe; Option\u00a01 is the simplest. |\n\n---\n\n### Quick Swift equivalents (if you code in Swift)\n\n```swift\n// 1\ufe0f\u20e3 Content\u2011offset method (iOS\u00a011+)\ntableView.setContentOffset(\n    CGPoint(x: 0, y: -tableView.adjustedContentInset.top),\n    animated: false\n)\n\n// 2\ufe0f\u20e3 Find first row and scroll\nlet sections = tableView.numberOfSections\nfor s in 0..<sections {\n    let rows = tableView.numberOfRows(inSection: s)\n    if rows > 0 {\n        let first = IndexPath(row: 0, section: s)\n        tableView.scrollToRow(at: first,\n                              at: .top,\n                              animated: false)\n        break\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBoth solutions avoid the crash you see when you blindly request section\u202f0, row\u202f0 while that index path does not exist."}], "q_num": 142, "originalconversations": ["In my table view I have to scroll to the top. But I cannot guarantee that the first object is going to be section 0, row 0. May be that my table view will start from section number 5.\nSo I get an exception, when I call:\n```[mainTableView scrollToRowAtIndexPath:[NSIndexPath indexPathForRow:0 inSection:0] atScrollPosition:UITableViewScrollPositionTop animated:NO];\n```\nIs there another way to scroll to the top of table view?", "Possible Actions: \n1   \n```func scrollToFirstRow() {\n    let indexPath = NSIndexPath(forRow: 0, inSection: 0)\n    self.tableView.scrollToRowAtIndexPath(indexPath, atScrollPosition: .Top, animated: true)\n}\n```\n2  \n```func scrollToLastRow() {\n    let indexPath = NSIndexPath(forRow: objects.count - 1, inSection: 0)\n    self.tableView.scrollToRowAtIndexPath(indexPath, atScrollPosition: .Bottom, animated: true)\n}\n```\n3    \n```func scrollToSelectedRow() {\n    let selectedRows = self.tableView.indexPathsForSelectedRows\n    if let selectedRow = selectedRows?[0] as? NSIndexPath {\n        self.tableView.scrollToRowAtIndexPath(selectedRow, atScrollPosition: .Middle, animated: true)\n    }\n}\n```\n4   \n```func scrollToHeader() {\n    self.tableView.scrollRectToVisible(CGRect(x: 0, y: 0, width: 1, height: 1), animated: true)\n}\n```\n5 \n```func scrollToTop(){\n    self.tableView.setContentOffset(CGPointMake(0,  UIApplication.sharedApplication().statusBarFrame.height ), animated: true)\n}\n```\nDisable Scroll To Top:\n```func disableScrollsToTopPropertyOnAllSubviewsOf(view: UIView) {\n    for subview in view.subviews {\n        if let scrollView = subview as? UIScrollView {\n            (scrollView as UIScrollView).scrollsToTop = false\n        }\n        self.disableScrollsToTopPropertyOnAllSubviewsOf(subview as UIView)\n    }\n}\n```\nModify and use it as per requirement.\nSwift 4\n```  func scrollToFirstRow() {\n    let indexPath = IndexPath(row: 0, section: 0)\n    self.tableView.scrollToRow(at: indexPath, at: .top, animated: true)\n  }\n```"], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "In my table view I have to scroll to the top. But I cannot guarantee that the first object is going to be section 0, row 0. May be that my table view will start from section number 5.\nSo I get an exception, when I call:\n```[mainTableView scrollToRowAtIndexPath:[NSIndexPath indexPathForRow:0 inSection:0] atScrollPosition:UITableViewScrollPositionTop animated:NO];\n```\nIs there another way to scroll to the top of table view?"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "Why does this throw ```NullPointerException```\n```public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n    Boolean b = true ? returnsNull() : false; // NPE on this line.\n    System.out.println(b);\n}\npublic static Boolean returnsNull() {\n    return null;\n}\n```\nwhile this doesn't\n```public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n    Boolean b = true ? null : false;\n    System.out.println(b); // null\n}\n```\n?\nThe solution is by the way to replace ```false``` by ```Boolean.FALSE``` to avoid ```null``` being unboxed to ```boolean``` --which isn't possible. But that isn't the question. The question is why? Are there any references in JLS which confirms this behaviour, especially of the 2nd case?"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to explain why the first case throws NPE: because ternary conditional operator with primitive boolean vs Boolean wrapper leads to unboxing. In first case, the second and third operands are boolean and Boolean? Actually expression: true ? returnsNull() : false. returnsNull() returns Boolean (reference type). The other operand is boolean primitive false, which is boxed/unboxed as needed. The conditional operator's type determination: if one operand is of primitive type and the other is of reference type, then boxing/unboxing conversion applied. JLS 15.25.2. There are rules: if one operand is type T where T is boolean primitive and the other is Boolean, then unboxing of Boolean to boolean occurs, then result type is boolean. So the second operand (returnsNull()) is unboxed, causing NPE. In the second example, both second and third operands are null and false? Actually expression: true ? null : false. Here null is a null literal (type is null type). False is boolean primitive. The conditional operator uses null as reference, boolean primitive as other operand; the result type is Boolean? Need to see: null literal is compatible with any reference type; but there is also rule that if one operand is null and the other is primitive, then boxing conversion is applied to the primitive, resulting type is the boxed type. So false is boxed to Boolean, and null stays null, no unboxing. So result type is Boolean, and value is null.\n\nNeed to cite JLS sections: 15.25 (Conditional Operator), especially 15.25.2 Type Determination, 15.24? Also 5.2 Boxing Conversion, 5.1 Unboxing Conversion.\n\nExplain that in first case, the null literal is not present; return null is a nullable reference that will be unboxed. The compiler decides that result type is boolean (primitive) because both operands can be converted to boolean: the null reference operand can be unboxed (null -> NPE). The null literal case is special: null can be converted to any reference type, not unboxed. Thus the conditional operator picks reference type Boolean because one operand is null literal. Actually 2nd case: operands are null literal and boolean primitive, rule: if one operand is null type and the other is primitive, boxing conversion is applied to the primitive, yielding reference type, hence result is reference Boolean, and null stays null. That's why no NPE.\n\nNeed to provide JLS references: \u00a715.25, \u00a715.25.2, also \u00a75.2.\n\nWill produce answer in <answer></answer>.</think>\n<answer>\n**Short answer**\n\n* In `true ? returnsNull() : false` the conditional operator chooses the **primitive\n  `boolean`** as the result type.  \n  To do that it must *unbox* the value returned by `returnsNull()`.  \n  Since `returnsNull()` actually returns `null`, the unboxing step throws a\n  `NullPointerException`.\n\n* In `true ? null : false` the two operands are a *null\u2011type literal* and a\n  primitive `boolean`.  \n  The JLS rule for the conditional operator says that when one operand is the\n  null type and the other is a primitive, the primitive is **boxed** to its\n  wrapper type and the result type becomes the corresponding reference type\n  (`Boolean`). No unboxing is required, so the value of the expression is simply\n  `null`.\n\nThe difference is caused by the distinct treatment of a **null literal**\n(`null`) and a **null reference value** (`(Boolean) null`) in the type\u2011selection\nrules for the conditional (`?:`) operator.\n\n\n\n**Detailed explanation with JLS references**\n\n### 1. The conditional operator (\u00a715.25)\n\nFor an expression  \n\n```java\ncond ? expr1 : expr2\n```  \n\nthe compile\u2011time type of the whole expression is determined by the\n*type\u2011determination* rules in **\u00a715.25.2**.  \nThe relevant parts are reproduced below (Java\u202f17, but unchanged in later\nversions).\n\n```\nIf one operand is of primitive type and the other is of reference type,\nunboxing conversion (\u00a75.1.8) is applied to the reference operand, and the\nresult type is the primitive type.\n\nOtherwise, if one operand is of the null type and the other is of a\nprimitive type, boxing conversion (\u00a75.1.7) is applied to the primitive\noperand, and the result type is the corresponding wrapper reference type.\n```\n\n### 2. First program\n\n```java\nBoolean b = true ? returnsNull() : false;\n```\n\n* `returnsNull()` has type **`Boolean`** (reference type).  \n* `false` has type **`boolean`** (primitive).\n\nBoth operands are therefore *one primitive, one reference*.  \nAccording to the rule above the compiler **unboxes** the reference operand to\n`boolean`. The resulting type of the conditional expression is `boolean`.  \nAt run time the unboxing conversion is performed:\n\n```\nBoolean -> boolean   // unboxing conversion\n```\n\nUnboxing a `null` reference triggers a `NullPointerException`\n(\u00a75.1.8 \u201cUnboxing Conversion\u201d). Hence the NPE.\n\n### 3. Second program\n\n```java\nBoolean b = true ? null : false;\n```\n\n* `null` is a **null\u2011type literal**; its type is the special *null type*.\n* `false` is again `boolean`.\n\nNow we have *one operand of the null type and one operand of a primitive type*.\nThe second rule applies: the primitive operand is **boxed** to its wrapper\ntype (`Boolean`) and the whole conditional expression has **reference type\n`Boolean`**. No unboxing occurs, so the value contributed by the `null` operand\nremains the `null` reference, and the expression evaluates to `null` without\nan exception.\n\n### 4. Why `Boolean.FALSE` works\n\nIf we write\n\n```java\nBoolean b = true ? returnsNull() : Boolean.FALSE;\n```\n\nboth operands are of reference type `Boolean`. No unboxing is required, so the\nconditional expression\u2019s type is `Boolean` and the result can be `null`\nwithout throwing.\n\n### 5. Summary of the JLS references\n\n| Situation                              | JLS rule used                           | Effect                |\n|----------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------|-----------------------|\n| One operand primitive, other reference | \u00a715.25.2 (first bullet) \u2013 unboxing      | Primitive result, unboxing of `null` \u2192 NPE |\n| One operand null literal, other primitive | \u00a715.25.2 (second bullet) \u2013 boxing       | Reference result, boxing of primitive \u2192 no NPE |\n\nThus the observed behaviour is fully defined by the Java Language Specification,\nand the two programs differ because the first involves unboxing a `null`\nreference, while the second involves boxing a primitive to `Boolean` and\nleaving the `null` literal untouched."}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Why does this throw ```NullPointerException```\n```public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n    Boolean b = true ? returnsNull() : false; // NPE on this line.\n    System.out.println(b);\n}\npublic static Boolean returnsNull() {\n    return null;\n}\n```\nwhile this doesn't\n```public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n    Boolean b = true ? null : false;\n    System.out.println(b); // null\n}\n```\n?\nThe solution is by the way to replace ```false``` by ```Boolean.FALSE``` to avoid ```null``` being unboxed to ```boolean``` --which isn't possible. But that isn't the question. The question is why? Are there any references in JLS which confirms this behaviour, especially of the 2nd case?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to explain why the first case throws NPE: because ternary conditional operator with primitive boolean vs Boolean wrapper leads to unboxing. In first case, the second and third operands are boolean and Boolean? Actually expression: true ? returnsNull() : false. returnsNull() returns Boolean (reference type). The other operand is boolean primitive false, which is boxed/unboxed as needed. The conditional operator's type determination: if one operand is of primitive type and the other is of reference type, then boxing/unboxing conversion applied. JLS 15.25.2. There are rules: if one operand is type T where T is boolean primitive and the other is Boolean, then unboxing of Boolean to boolean occurs, then result type is boolean. So the second operand (returnsNull()) is unboxed, causing NPE. In the second example, both second and third operands are null and false? Actually expression: true ? null : false. Here null is a null literal (type is null type). False is boolean primitive. The conditional operator uses null as reference, boolean primitive as other operand; the result type is Boolean? Need to see: null literal is compatible with any reference type; but there is also rule that if one operand is null and the other is primitive, then boxing conversion is applied to the primitive, resulting type is the boxed type. So false is boxed to Boolean, and null stays null, no unboxing. So result type is Boolean, and value is null.\n\nNeed to cite JLS sections: 15.25 (Conditional Operator), especially 15.25.2 Type Determination, 15.24? Also 5.2 Boxing