``E1: `true ? returnsNull() : false` - boolean (auto-unboxing 2nd operand to boolean)\nE2: `true ? null : false` - Boolean (autoboxing of 3rd operand to Boolean)\n```\nSee Java Language Specification, section 15.25 Conditional Operator ? :\n\n* For E1, the types of the 2nd and 3rd operands are ```Boolean``` and ```boolean``` respectively, so this clause applies:\n\n  If one of the second and third operands is of type boolean and the type of the other is of type Boolean, then the type of the conditional expression is boolean.\n\nSince the type of the expression is ```boolean```, the 2nd operand must be coerced to ```boolean```.  The compiler inserts auto-unboxing code to the 2nd operand (return value of ```returnsNull()```) to make it type ```boolean```.  This of course causes the NPE from the ```null``` returned at run-time.\n* For E2, types of the 2nd and 3rd operands are `````` (not ```Boolean``` as in E1!) and ```boolean``` respectively, so no specific typing clause applies (go read 'em!), so the final \"otherwise\" clause applies:\n\n  Otherwise, the second and third operands are of types S1 and S2 respectively. Let T1 be the type that results from applying boxing conversion to S1, and let T2 be the type that results from applying boxing conversion to S2. The type of the conditional expression is the result of applying capture conversion (\u00a75.1.10) to lub(T1, T2) (\u00a715.12.2.7).\n\n\n* S1 == `````` (see \u00a74.1)\n* S2 == ```boolean```\n* T1 == box(S1) == `````` (see last item in list of boxing conversions in  \u00a75.1.7)\n* T2 == box(S2) == `Boolean\n* lub(T1, T2) == ```Boolean```\n\nSo the type of the conditional expression is ```Boolean``` and the 3rd operand must be coerced to ```Boolean```.  The compiler inserts auto-boxing code for the 3rd operand (```false```).  The 2nd operand doesn't need the auto-unboxing as in ```E1```, so no auto-unboxing NPE when ```null``` is returned.\n\n\nThis question needs a similar type analysis:\nJava conditional operator ?: result type"], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "Why does this throw ```NullPointerException```\n```public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n    Boolean b = true ? returnsNull() : false; // NPE on this line.\n    System.out.println(b);\n}\npublic static Boolean returnsNull() {\n    return null;\n}\n```\nwhile this doesn't\n```public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n    Boolean b = true ? null : false;\n    System.out.println(b); // null\n}\n```\n?\nThe solution is by the way to replace ```false``` by ```Boolean.FALSE``` to avoid ```null``` being unboxed to ```boolean``` --which isn't possible. But that isn't the question. The question is why? Are there any references in JLS which confirms this behaviour, especially of the 2nd case?"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "I want to sum a list of Integers. It works as follows, but the syntax does not feel right. Could the code be optimized?\n```Map integers;\nintegers.values().stream().mapToInt(i -> i).sum();\n```"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to respond: question about Java streams. They have a Map of integers? The code: `Map integers; integers.values().stream().mapToInt(i -> i).sum();` They likely want to sum values of a Map. Suggest using `Map<Integer, Integer>`? Or using `mapToInt(Integer::intValue).sum();` Also could use `integers.values().stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).sum();` Or just use `ints.stream().mapToInt(i -> i).sum();` But they can also use `integers.values().stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).sum();` Or `integers.replaceAll...` ??? The optimization may be that `mapToInt(i -> i)` is an identity mapping; can be replaced with `mapToInt(Integer::intValue)`. Also if using primitive int values in a collection, can use `IntStream` directly via `ints.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).sum();`. If the map is a `Map<K, Integer>` you can sum values directly via `integers.values().stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).sum();`\n\nAdditionally, could use `int total = integers.values().stream().reduce(0, Integer::sum);` but `mapToInt(...).sum()` is fine.\n\nPotential improvements: Use `Collection<Integer> values = integers.values();` If map may contain null values, you could filter out null.\n\nIf Java 8+, you can also use `integers.values().stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).sum();` but you could also use `ints.reduce(0, Integer::sum)` etc.\n\nMaybe also note that you should parameterize the Map: `Map<?, Integer> integers` and use `Map<Integer, Integer>`?\n\nThe code as given is missing generics. Should be `Map<K, Integer> integers = ...; int sum = integers.values().stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).sum();`\n\nIf you want to sum keys vs values? Provide both.\n\nExplain that `mapToInt(i -> i)` is redundant, use method reference.\n\nIf you want to avoid autoboxing/unboxing overhead: you could use a primitive map (e.g., `Int2IntOpenHashMap` from fastutil) or collect into `int[]`.\n\nBut typical Java solution: `int sum = integers.values().stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).sum();`\n\nIf you want to use parallel stream: `.parallelStream()` maybe.\n\nThus answer: Provide code and explanation.\n\nWe'll answer with optimized code, best practices, null safety.\n\nLet's produce final answer.</think>\n**Short answer**\n\n```java\n// assuming the map holds Integer values\nint sum = integers.values()\n                 .stream()\n                 .mapToInt(Integer::intValue)   // identity conversion, no lambda needed\n                 .sum();\n```\n\nThat one\u2011liner does the same work as your original snippet, but it is more readable, type\u2011safe, and avoids the unnecessary `i -> i` lambda.\n\n---\n\n## Why the original code feels \u201coff\u201d\n\n```java\nMap integers;                      // raw type \u2013 loses compile\u2011time checking\nintegers.values().stream()\n        .mapToInt(i -> i)          // identity mapping, does nothing useful\n        .sum();\n```\n\n* **Raw `Map`** \u2013 Without generic type arguments the compiler cannot verify that the map actually contains `Integer` values. It also forces you to do unchecked casts internally.\n* **`i -> i`** \u2013 This lambda just returns its argument unchanged. `mapToInt` already expects a function that turns an element into an `int`; feeding it an identity lambda just adds a layer of indirection and a (tiny) boxing/unboxing cost.\n* **Readability** \u2013 A method reference (`Integer::intValue`) conveys the intent (\u201cconvert each `Integer` to its primitive `int`\u201d) much clearer than an anonymous lambda.\n\n---\n\n## A clean, type\u2011safe version\n\n```java\n// 1\ufe0f\u20e3 Declare the map with proper generics.\n//    The key type can be anything; we only care that the value type is Integer.\nMap<?, Integer> integers = ...;   // or Map<String,Integer>, Map<Long,Integer>, etc.\n\n// 2\ufe0f\u20e3 Compute the sum.\nint sum = integers.values()\n                 .stream()\n                 .mapToInt(Integer::intValue)\n                 .sum();\n```\n\n* `Map<?, Integer>` (or a concrete key type) tells the compiler that the *values* are `Integer`s.\n* `Integer::intValue` is a method reference that unboxes each `Integer` to an `int` \u2013 exactly what `mapToInt` needs.\n* The result of `sum()` is a primitive `int`, so no extra boxing occurs.\n\n---\n\n## Optional enhancements\n\n### 1. Guard against possible `null` values  \nIf the map might contain `null` entries, filter them out before the conversion:\n\n```java\nint sum = integers.values()\n                 .stream()\n                 .filter(Objects::nonNull)          // drop nulls\n                 .mapToInt(Integer::intValue)\n                 .sum();\n```\n\n### 2. Parallel execution (use only when the map is large)  \n\n```java\nint sum = integers.values()\n                 .parallelStream()                  // split work across cores\n                 .mapToInt(Integer::intValue)\n                 .sum();\n```\n\n> **Caution:** Parallel streams add overhead. They are beneficial only for **large** collections (typically >\u202f10\u202f000 elements) and when the work per element is non\u2011trivial.\n\n### 3. Using a primitive\u2011specialised map (zero boxing)  \n\nIf you control the data structure and need to sum millions of numbers repeatedly, consider a library that provides a primitive map, e.g. **fastutil** or **HPPC**:\n\n```java\nInt2IntOpenHashMap intMap = new Int2IntOpenHashMap();\n// \u2026 fill the map \u2026\nint sum = intMap.values().intStream().sum();   // fastutil provides an IntStream directly\n```\n\nThese libraries store the ints as primitives internally, eliminating the autoboxing that a `Map<K,Integer>` inevitably incurs.\n\n### 4. Alternative (no streams) \u2013 classic loop  \n\nFor the absolute fastest path in hot code, a hand\u2011written loop can beat streams because it eliminates the stream pipeline overhead:\n\n```java\nint sum = 0;\nfor (Integer value : integers.values()) {\n    if (value != null) {          // optional null\u2011check\n        sum += value;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBenchmarking on modern JVMs usually shows:\n\n| Approach                     | Typical overhead (per element) |\n|------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n| `stream().mapToInt(...).sum()` | ~10\u201320\u202fns (pipeline + boxing) |\n| `parallelStream()`           | higher unless data set is huge |\n| Primitive map (`fastutil`)   | ~0\u202fns (no boxing)              |\n| Plain `for` loop             | ~0\u20135\u202fns (minimal)              |\n\nIf you\u2019re summing a *small* or *moderate* number of elements, the readability of streams usually outweighs the few\u2011nanosecond difference.\n\n---\n\n## TL;DR \u2013 Recommended one\u2011liner\n\n```java\nint sum = integers.values()\n                 .stream()\n                 .mapToInt(Integer::intValue)\n                 .sum();\n```\n\nAdd a `filter(Objects::nonNull)` if `null`s are possible, and switch to a primitive\u2011specialised map or a hand\u2011rolled loop only when profiling shows the need for extra performance."}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I want to sum a list of Integers. It works as follows, but the syntax does not feel right. Could the code be optimized?\n```Map integers;\nintegers.values().stream().mapToInt(i -> i).sum();\n```"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to respond: question about Java streams. They have a Map of integers? The code: `Map integers; integers.values().stream().mapToInt(i -> i).sum();` They likely want to sum values of a Map. Suggest using `Map<Integer, Integer>`? Or using `mapToInt(Integ