e map holds Integer values\nint sum = integers.values()\n                 .stream()\n                 .mapToInt(Integer::intValue)   // identity conversion, no lambda needed\n                 .sum();\n```\n\nThat one\u2011liner does the same work as your original snippet, but it is more readable, type\u2011safe, and avoids the unnecessary `i -> i` lambda.\n\n---\n\n## Why the original code feels \u201coff\u201d\n\n```java\nMap integers;                      // raw type \u2013 loses compile\u2011time checking\nintegers.values().stream()\n        .mapToInt(i -> i)          // identity mapping, does nothing useful\n        .sum();\n```\n\n* **Raw `Map`** \u2013 Without generic type arguments the compiler cannot verify that the map actually contains `Integer` values. It also forces you to do unchecked casts internally.\n* **`i -> i`** \u2013 This lambda just returns its argument unchanged. `mapToInt` already expects a function that turns an element into an `int`; feeding it an identity lambda just adds a layer of indirection and a (tiny) boxing/unboxing cost.\n* **Readability** \u2013 A method reference (`Integer::intValue`) conveys the intent (\u201cconvert each `Integer` to its primitive `int`\u201d) much clearer than an anonymous lambda.\n\n---\n\n## A clean, type\u2011safe version\n\n```java\n// 1\ufe0f\u20e3 Declare the map with proper generics.\n//    The key type can be anything; we only care that the value type is Integer.\nMap<?, Integer> integers = ...;   // or Map<String,Integer>, Map<Long,Integer>, etc.\n\n// 2\ufe0f\u20e3 Compute the sum.\nint sum = integers.values()\n                 .stream()\n                 .mapToInt(Integer::intValue)\n                 .sum();\n```\n\n* `Map<?, Integer>` (or a concrete key type) tells the compiler that the *values* are `Integer`s.\n* `Integer::intValue` is a method reference that unboxes each `Integer` to an `int` \u2013 exactly what `mapToInt` needs.\n* The result of `sum()` is a primitive `int`, so no extra boxing occurs.\n\n---\n\n## Optional enhancements\n\n### 1. Guard against possible `null` values  \nIf the map might contain `null` entries, filter them out before the conversion:\n\n```java\nint sum = integers.values()\n                 .stream()\n                 .filter(Objects::nonNull)          // drop nulls\n                 .mapToInt(Integer::intValue)\n                 .sum();\n```\n\n### 2. Parallel execution (use only when the map is large)  \n\n```java\nint sum = integers.values()\n                 .parallelStream()                  // split work across cores\n                 .mapToInt(Integer::intValue)\n                 .sum();\n```\n\n> **Caution:** Parallel streams add overhead. They are beneficial only for **large** collections (typically >\u202f10\u202f000 elements) and when the work per element is non\u2011trivial.\n\n### 3. Using a primitive\u2011specialised map (zero boxing)  \n\nIf you control the data structure and need to sum millions of numbers repeatedly, consider a library that provides a primitive map, e.g. **fastutil** or **HPPC**:\n\n```java\nInt2IntOpenHashMap intMap = new Int2IntOpenHashMap();\n// \u2026 fill the map \u2026\nint sum = intMap.values().intStream().sum();   // fastutil provides an IntStream directly\n```\n\nThese libraries store the ints as primitives internally, eliminating the autoboxing that a `Map<K,Integer>` inevitably incurs.\n\n### 4. Alternative (no streams) \u2013 classic loop  \n\nFor the absolute fastest path in hot code, a hand\u2011written loop can beat streams because it eliminates the stream pipeline overhead:\n\n```java\nint sum = 0;\nfor (Integer value : integers.values()) {\n    if (value != null) {          // optional null\u2011check\n        sum += value;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBenchmarking on modern JVMs usually shows:\n\n| Approach                     | Typical overhead (per element) |\n|------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n| `stream().mapToInt(...).sum()` | ~10\u201320\u202fns (pipeline + boxing) |\n| `parallelStream()`           | higher unless data set is huge |\n| Primitive map (`fastutil`)   | ~0\u202fns (no boxing)              |\n| Plain `for` loop             | ~0\u20135\u202fns (minimal)              |\n\nIf you\u2019re summing a *small* or *moderate* number of elements, the readability of streams usually outweighs the few\u2011nanosecond difference.\n\n---\n\n## TL;DR \u2013 Recommended one\u2011liner\n\n```java\nint sum = integers.values()\n                 .stream()\n                 .mapToInt(Integer::intValue)\n                 .sum();\n```\n\nAdd a `filter(Objects::nonNull)` if `null`s are possible, and switch to a primitive\u2011specialised map or a hand\u2011rolled loop only when profiling shows the need for extra performance."}], "q_num": 213, "originalconversations": ["I want to sum a list of Integers. It works as follows, but the syntax does not feel right. Could the code be optimized?\n```Map integers;\nintegers.values().stream().mapToInt(i -> i).sum();\n```", "This will work, but the `i -> i` is doing some automatic unboxing which is why it \"feels\" strange. `mapToInt` converts the stream to an `IntStream` \"of primitive int-valued elements\". Either of the following will work and better explain what the compiler is doing under the hood with your original syntax:\n```\nintegers.values().stream().mapToInt(i -> i.intValue()).sum();\nintegers.values().stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).sum();\n```\nThe other simple option is `BigDecimal sum = numbers.stream().reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);`."], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "I want to sum a list of Integers. It works as follows, but the syntax does not feel right. Could the code be optimized?\n```Map integers;\nintegers.values().stream().mapToInt(i -> i).sum();\n```"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "A lot of ShaderToy demos share the Ray Marching algorithm to render the scene, but they are often written with a very compact style and i can't find any straightforward examples or explanation.\nSo what is Ray Marching? Some comments suggests that it is a variation of Sphere Tracing. What are the computational advantages of a such approach?"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks: explanation of ray marching, relation to sphere tracing, computational advantages. Need to provide answer in <answer></answer> XML tags. Must be helpful, detailed, maybe code examples, explanation of algorithm, differences, advantages, limitations. Use clear sections.\n\nProvide definition: ray marching as stepping along ray using distance estimation or fixed step. Sphere tracing is a specific type of ray marching using signed distance fields (SDFs) to take larger steps.\n\nExplain computational advantages: fewer steps due to larger steps, exploit distance field's lower bound, enables rendering implicit surfaces/volumetric effects, easy to code, less geometry overhead, GPU friendly, etc. Also disadvantages: requires SDFs, may miss thin features, may cause artifacts.\n\nProvide simple example in GLSL.\n\nProvide reference to resources.\n\nWrap answer in <answer> tags.\n\nProceed.</think>\n<answer>\n<h1>Ray Marching \u2013 What Is It?</h1>\n\n**Ray marching** is a family of rendering techniques in which a view\u2011ray is advanced step\u2011by\u2011step (\u201cmarched\u201d) through the scene until it either:\n* hits something (the surface is found),\n* reaches a far\u2011plane (the ray missed everything),\n* or exceeds a maximum number of steps (to avoid infinite loops).\n\nAt each step a point **p** = **origin** + *t*\u00b7**direction** is evaluated by a *scene function* that tells the algorithm how far it can safely travel without intersecting geometry.  \nThe classic version that appears in many ShaderToy demos is **sphere tracing**, a special case of ray marching that uses **signed distance fields (SDFs)** as the scene function.\n\n---\n\n<h2>Signed Distance Fields (SDFs)</h2An SDF *d(p)* returns the **signed distance** from point **p** to the nearest surface:\n* *d(p) > 0*  \u2013 point is outside, distance to the nearest surface.\n* *d(p) = 0*  \u2013 point lies exactly on the surface.\n* *d(p) < 0*  \u2013 point is inside (some authors only use the absolute value).\n\nBecause the distance is a lower bound on how far you can move without crossing any surface, it can be used as a safe step size.\n\n---\n\n<h2>Sphere Tracing Algorithm (Ray Marching with SDFs)</h2>\n\n```glsl\n// Returns signed distance to the whole scene (union of objects)\nfloat map(vec3 p);\n\n// ------------------------------------------------------\n// Main tracing routine (run for every pixel)\nfloat trace(vec3 ro, vec3 rd)               // ro = ray origin, rd = normalized ray direction\n{\n    const int   MAX_STEPS   = 128;\n    const float HIT_EPS     = 0.001;        // tolerance for \"hit\"\n    const float MAX_DIST    = 100.0;        // far clipping plane\n\n    float t = 0.0;                          // current traveled distance\n\n    for (int i = 0 i < MAX_STEPS; ++i)\n    {\n        vec3  pos = ro + rd * t;            // current point the ray\n        float d   = map(pos);               // signed distance to nearest surface\n\n        if (d < HIT_EPS)                    // we are close enough to the surface\n            return t;                       // hit distance\n\n        t += d; // safe step forward\n        if (t > MAX_DIST) break;            // missed everything\n    }\n    return -1.0; // no hit\n}\n```\n\n* `map(p)` is a **scene SDF** \u2013 usually a combination (union, intersection, subtraction) of primitive SDFs like spheres, boxes, planes, etc.\n* `t += d` is the core of sphere tracing: the step size equals the distance field value, guaranteeing we never \u201cstep over\u201d a surface.\n* The loop stops when the distance falls below a small epsilon (`HIT_EPS`), which we treat as a hit, or when we exceed `MAX_DIST`.\n\nThe algorithm is *iterative* and completely data\u2011parallel, which makes it a perfect fit for GPU fragment shaders (e.g. ShaderToy).\n\n---\n\n<h2>How Does This Relate to General Ray Marching?</h2>\n\n| Feature                     | General Ray Marching                     | Sphere Tracing (Ray Marching with SDFs) |\n|-----------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|\n| **Step size**               | Fixed step (e.g., \u0394t = 0.01) **or** custom function | Distance from SDF (often much larger)  | **Scene description**      | Arbitrary volumetric fields, procedural density, or distance estimators | Signed distance fields (lower\u2011bound distance) |\n| **Accuracy**                | Depends on step size; smaller steps \u2192 more accurate but slower | Guarantees no overshoot; accuracy limited by epsilon |\n| **Typical use\u2011cases**       | Participating media (fog, smoke), volume, procedural textures | Implicit surfaces, smooth objects, solid geometry |\n| **Primary advantage**      | Simpler to implement for any scalar field | Fewer steps, fast convergence, easy surface normal extraction |\n| **Drawbacks**               | May require many tiny steps \u2192 slower; can miss thin features | Needs a good SDF; may fail on non\u2011Lipschitz (sharp) geometry, \u201cfalse negatives\u201d for very thin structures |\n\nIn practice, you will see both terms used interchangeably when the underlying scene function is an SDF, because the *marching* part of the algorithm is identical; the *sphere* part simply refers to the fact that the distance field gives the radius of a maximal sphere that can be placed around the current point without intersecting geometry.\n\n---\n\n<h2>Computational Advantages of Sphere Tracing / Ray Marching</h2>\n\n1. **Adaptive step size \u2192 fewer iterations**  \n   The distance field tells you exactly how far you can safely move. Near empty space you take huge steps; only when you approach geometry do the steps shrink. This often reduces the number of iterations by an order of magnitude compared to a naive fixed\u2011step ray march.\n\n2. **No explicit geometry storage**  \n   The whole scene lives in a few analytical functions. No vertex buffers, no BVH construction, no triangle rasterization. This makes ideal for procedurally generated worlds, fractals, or scenes that would be expensive to mesh.\n\n3. **GPU\u2011friendly, fully**  \n   Each pixel\u2019s march is independent, requiring only scalar arithmetic and a few texture fetches. No recursion, no pointer chasing \u2192 perfect for fragment shaders on modern GPUs.\n\n4. **Easy normal and curvature extraction**  \n   The surface normal at a hit point is simply the gradient of the distance field:\n   ```glsl\n   vec3 normal(vec3 p) {\n       const vec2 e = vec2(0.001, 0);\n       return normalize(vec3(\n           map(p + e.xyy) - map(p - e.xyy),\n           map(p + e.yxy) - map(p - e.yxy),\n           map(p + e.yyx) - map(p - e.yyx)\n       ));\n   }\n   ```\n   No need for per\u2011triangle normal data.\n\n5. **Procedural materials and effects are cheap**  \n   Since you already evaluate a scalar field per step, you can reuse it for shading (e.g., density fog, displacement for bump maps) without extra passes.\n\n6. **Uniform handling of solid objects and volumes**  \n   The same loop can render a solid SDF surface and a volumetric field (by accumulating color/opacity along the march), making hybrid scenes trivial.\n\n---\n\n<h2>Limitations & Pitfalls</h2>\n\n* **Requires a good signed distance function** \u2013 constructing tight SDFs for complex meshes is non\u2011trivial. Poor SDFs (over\u2011estimates) lead to unnecessary steps; under\u2011estimates cause *misses* (the ray \u201cjumps through\u201d geometry).\n* **Thin features may be missed** \u2013 if the distance to a thin wall is smaller than the step size before the ray reaches it, the algorithm can skip it. Solutions: add a global minimum step, use *cone step* heuristics, or combine SDF with fixed\u2011step marching near potential hits.\n* **Performance varies with scene complexity** \u2013 highly detailed SDFs (e.g., fractals) may require many arithmetic operations per step, offsetting the benefit of fewer steps.\n* **No built\u2011in occlusion handling** \u2013 you still need to test for shadows, ambient occlusion, reflections, etc., typically by launching secondary rays (the same marching code is reused).\n\n---\n\n<h2>Simple Example \u2013 Rendering a Sphere and a Box</h2>\n\n```glsl\n// --- primitive SDFs -------------------------------------------------\nfloat sdSphere(vec3 p, float r) {\n    return length(p) - r;\n}\nfloat sdBox(vec3 p, vec3 b) {               // b = half\u2011size\n    vec3 d = abs(p) - b;\n    return length(max(d, 0.0)) + min(max(d.x, max(d.y, d.z)), 0.0);\n}\n\n// --- combine primitives (union) ------------------------------------\nfloat map(vec3 p) {\n    float d1 = sdSphere(p - vec3(0.0, 0.5, 0.0), 0.5);\n    float d2 = sdBox(p - vec3(0.8, 0.0, 0.0), vec3(0.3));\n return min(d1, d2);                     // union = min distance\n}\n\n// --- main fragment shader -------------------------------------------\nvoid mainImage( out vec4 fragColor, in vec2 fragCoord"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "A lot of ShaderToy demos share the Ray Marching algorithm to render the scene, but they are often written with a very compact style and i can't find any straightforward examples or explanation.\nSo what is Ray Marching? Some comments suggests that it is a variation of Sphere Tracing. What are the computational advantages of a such approach?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks: explanation of ray marching, relation to sphere tracing, computational advantages. Need to provide answer in <answer></answer> XML tags. Must be helpful, detailed, maybe code examples, explanation of algorithm, differences, advantages, limitations. Use clear sections.\n\nProvide definition: ray marching as stepping along ray using distance estimation or fixed step. Sphere tracing is a specific type of ray marching using signed distance fields (SDFs) to take larger steps.\n\nExplain computational advantages: fewer steps due to larger steps, exploit distance field's lower bound, enables rendering implicit surfaces/volumetric effects, easy to code, less geometry overhead, GPU friendly, etc. Also disadvantages: requires SDFs, may miss thin features, may cause artifacts.\n\nProvide simple example in GLSL.\n\nProvide reference to resources.\n\nWrap answer in <answer> tags.\n\nProceed.</think>\n<answer>\n<h1>Ray Marching \u2013 What Is It?</h1>\n\n**Ray marching** is a family of rendering techniques in which a view\u2011ray is advanced step\u2011by\u2011step (\u201cmarched\u201d) through the scene until it either:\n* hits something (the surface is found),\n* reaches a far\u2011plane (the ray missed everything),\n* or exceeds a maximum number of steps (to avoid infinite loops).\n\nAt each step a point **p** = **origin** + *t*\u00b7**direction** is evaluated by a *scene function* that tells the algorithm how far it can safely travel without intersecting geometry.  \nThe classic version that appears in many ShaderToy demos is **sphere tracing**, a special case of ray marching that uses **signed distance fields (SDFs)** as the scene function.\n\n---\n\n<h2>Signed Distance Fields (SDFs)</h2An SDF *d(p)* returns the **signed distance** from point **p** to the nearest surface:\n* *d(p) > 0*  \u2013 point is outside, distance to the nearest surface.\n* *d(p) = 0*  \u2013 point lies exactly on the surface.\n* *d(p) < 0*  \u2013 point is inside (some authors only use the absolute value).\n\nBecause the distance is a lower bound on how far you can move without crossing any surface, it can be used as a safe step size.\n\n---\n\n<h2>Sphere Tracing Algorithm (Ray Marching with SDFs)</h2>\n\n```glsl\n// Returns signed distance to the whole scene (union of objects)\nfloat map(vec3 p);\n\n// ------------------------------------------------------\n// Main tracing routine (run for every pixel)\nfloat trace(vec3 ro, vec3 rd)               // ro = ray origin, rd = normalized ray direction\n{\n    const int   MAX_STEPS   = 128;\n    const float HIT_EPS     = 0.001;        // tolerance for \"hit\"\n    const float MAX_DIST    = 100.0;        // far clipping plane\n\n    float t = 0.0;                          // current traveled distance\n\n    for (int i = 0 i < MAX_STEPS; ++i)\n    {\n        vec3  pos = ro + rd * t;            // current point the ray\n        float d   = map(pos);               // signed distance to nearest surface\n\n        if (d < HIT_EPS)                    // we are close enough to the surface\n            return t;                       // hit distance\n\n        t += d; // safe step forward\n        if (t > MAX_DIST) break;            // missed everything\n    }\n    return -1.0; // no hit\n}\n```\n\n* `map(p)` is a **scene SDF** \u2013 usually a combination (union, intersection, subtraction) of primitive SDFs like spheres, boxes, planes, etc.\n* `t += d` is the core of sphere tracing: the step size equals the distance field value, guaranteeing we never \u201cstep over\u201d a surface.\n* The loop stops when the distance falls below a small epsilon (`HIT_EPS`), which we treat as a hit, or when we exceed `MAX_DIST`.\n\nThe algorithm is *iterative* and completely data\u2011parallel, which makes it a perfect fit for GPU fragment shaders (e.g. ShaderToy).\n\n---\n\n<h2>How Does This Relate to General Ray Marching?</h2>\n\n| Feature                     | General Ray Marching                     | Sphere Tracing (Ray Marching with SDFs) |\n|-----------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|\n| **Step size**               | Fixed step (e.g., \u0394t = 0.01) **or** custom function | Distance from SDF (often much larger)  | **Scene description**      | Arbitrary volumetric fields, procedural density, or distance estimators | Signed distance fields (lower\u2011bound distance) |\n| **Accuracy**                | Depends on step size; smaller steps \u2192 more accurate but slower | Guarantees no overshoot; accuracy limited by epsilon |\n| **Typical use\u2011cases**       | Participating media (fog, smoke), volume, procedural textures | Implicit surfaces, smooth objects, solid geometry |\n| **Primary advantage**      | Simpler to implement for any scalar field | Fewer steps, fast convergence, easy surface normal extraction |\n| **Drawbacks**               | May require many tiny steps \u2192 slower; can miss thin features | Needs a good SDF; may fail on non\u2011Lipschitz (sharp) geometry, \u201cfalse negatives\u201d for very thin structures |\n\nIn practice, you will see both terms used interchangeably when the underlying scene function is an SDF, because the *marching* part of the algorithm is identical; the *sphere* part simply refers to the fact that the distance field gives the radius of a maximal sphere that can be placed around the current point without intersecting geometry.\n\n---\n\n<h2>Computational Advantages of Sphere Tracing / Ray Marching</h2>\n\n1. **Adaptive step size \u2192 fewer iterations**  \n   The distance field tells you exactly how far you can safely move. Near empty space you take huge steps; only when you approach geometry do the steps shrink. This often reduces the number of iterations by an order of magnitude compared to a naive fixed\u2011step ray march.\n\n2. **No explicit geometry storage**  \n   The whole scene lives in a few analytical functions. No vertex buffers, no BVH construction, no triangle rasterization. This makes ideal for procedurally generated worlds, fractals, or scenes that would be expensive to mesh.\n\n3. **GPU\u2011friendly, fully**  \n   Each pixel\u2019s march is independent, requiring only scalar arithmetic and a few texture fetches. No recursion, no pointer chasing \u2192 perfect for fragment shaders on modern GPUs.\n\n4. **Easy normal and curvature extraction**  \n   The surface normal at a hit point is simply the gradient of the distance field:\n   ```glsl\n   vec3 normal(vec3 p) {\n       const vec2 e = vec2(0.001, 0);\n       return normalize(vec3(\n           map(p + e.xyy) - map(p - e.xyy),\n           map(p + e.yxy) - map(p - e.yxy),\n           map(p + e.yyx) - map(p - e.yyx)\n       ));\n   }\n   ```\n   No need for per\u2011triangle normal data.\n\n5. **Procedural materials and effects are cheap**  \n   Since you already evaluate a scalar field per step, you can reuse it for shading (e.g., density fog, displacement for bump maps) without extra passes.\n\n6. **Uniform handling of solid objects and volumes**  \n   The same loop can render a solid SDF surface and a volumetric field (by accumulating color/opacity along the march), making hybrid scenes trivial.\n\n---\n\n<h2>Limitations & Pitfalls</h2>\n\n* **Requires a good signed distance function** \u2013 constructing tight SDFs for complex meshes is non\u2011trivial. Poor SDFs (over\u2011estimates) lead to unnecessary steps; under\u2011estimates cause *misses* (the ray \u201cjumps through\u201d geometry).\n* **Thin features may be missed** \u2013 if the distance to a thin wall is smaller than the step size before the ray reaches it, the algorithm can skip it. Solutions: add a global minimum step, use *cone step* heuristics, or combine SDF with fixed\u2011step marching near potential hits.\n* **Performance varies with scene complexity** \u2013 highly detailed SDFs (e.g., fractals) may require many arithmetic operations per step, offsetting the benefit of fewer steps.\n* **No built\u2011in occlusion handling** \u2013 you still need to test for shadows, ambient occlusion, reflections, etc., typically by launching secondary rays (the same marching code is reused).\n\n---\n\n<h2>Simple Example \u2013 Rendering a Sphere and a Box</h2>\n\n```glsl\n// --- primitive SDFs -------------------------------------------------\nfloat sdSphere(vec3 p, float r) {\n    return length(p) - r;\n}\nfloat sdBox(vec3 p, vec3 b) {               // b = half\u2011size\n    vec3 d = abs(p) - b;\n    return length(max(d, 0.0)) + min(max(d.x, max(d.y, d.z)), 0.0);\n}\n\n// --- combine primitives (union) ------------------------------------\nfloat map(vec3 p) {\n    float d1 = sdSphere(p - vec3(0.0, 0.5, 0.0), 0.5);\n    float d2 = sdBox(p - vec3(0.8, 0.0, 0.0), vec3(0.3));\n return min(d1, d2);                     // union = min distance\n}\n\n// --- main fragment shader -------------------------------------------\nvoid mainImage( out vec4 fragColor, in vec2 fragCoord"}], "q_num": 147, "originalconversations": ["A lot of ShaderToy demos share the Ray Marching algorithm to render the scene, but they are often written with a very compact style and i can't find any straightforward examples or explanation.\nSo what is Ray Marching? Some comments suggests that it is a variation of Sphere Tracing. What are the computational advantages of a such approach?", "## TL;DR\nThey belong to the same family of solvers, where sphere tracing is one method of ray marching, which is the family name.\n\n## Raymarching a definition\nRaymarching is a technique a bit like traditional raytracing where the surface function is not easy to solve (or impossible without numeric iterative methods). In raytracing you just look up the ray intersection, whereas in ray marching you march forward (or back and forth) until you find the intersection, have enough samples or whatever it is your trying to solve. Try to think of it like a newton-raphson method for surface finding, or summing for integrating a varying function.\n\nThis can be useful if you:\n\n* Need to render volumetrics that arenot uniform\n* Rendering implicit functions, fractals\n* Rendering other kinds of parametric surfaces where intersection is not known ahead of time, like paralax mapping\netc.\n\n\n## Sphere tracing\nSphere tracing is one possible Ray marching algorithm. Not all raymarching uses benefit form this method, as they can not be converted into this kind of scheme.\n\nSphere tracing is used for rendering implicit surfaces. Implicit surfaces are formed at some level of a continuous function. In essence solving the equation\n```F(X,Y,Z) = 0\n```\n\nBecause of how this function can be solved at each point, one can go ahead and estimate the biggest possible sphere that can fit the current march step (or if not exactly reasonably safely). You then know that next march distance is at least this big. This way you can have adaptive ray marching steps speeding up the process."], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "A lot of ShaderToy demos share the Ray Marching algorithm to render the scene, but they are often written with a very compact style and i can't find any straightforward examples or explanation.\nSo what is Ray Marching? Some comments suggests that it is a variation of Sphere Tracing. What are the computational advantages of a such approach?"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "So, I'm on vacation in Utah, and so I attended an LDS service. In the morning, I heard a reading from one of the Presidents of the church during the \"Quorum of the Elders,\" then went to Sunday School, and finally witnessed the Sacrement of the bread and water. (I guess it makes sense there was no wine, but it did make me go \"Huh!\") After that, there were testimonies from missionaries and some music - but nothing that struck me as a sermon. \nWas I missing something, or was this an atypical service? I guess I was trying to understand what the \"pastor\"s role in the service was supposed to be - or again, is it just that Mormons are even more Baptist than baptists?\nIf someone could explain how instruction and exhortation are primarily conferred in the LDS church Id appreciate it. "}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks about LDS services, their structure, role of pastor, instruction/exhortation. Need to explain typical Sunday meeting schedule, role of leaders, the sacrament, lack of sermon perhaps they observed, content of talks, etc. Provide thorough answer, respectful, factual. Provide details about Sunday: sacrament meeting, fast Sunday, ward council, etc. Explain the priesthood, role of bishop as pastor, etc. Address their observations: reading from a President during \"Quorum of the Elders\" (maybe a talk from the General Conference or local quorum meeting hearing a talk). Explain Sunday School, Sacrament meeting, the lack of sermon may be because official talk is after sacrament. Provide info about missionary testimonies, hymns, etc. Conclude about instruction and exhortation happening through talks, the priesthood, continuity etc. Use <answer> tags.</think>\n<answer>\n**Overview of a Typical LDS Sunday Meeting**\n\nThe Church of Jesus Christ of Latter\u2011day Saints (often called \u201cMormons\u201d) has a fairly standardized schedule for its weekly worship, but the exact order can vary a little from ward (local congregation) to ward.  Most regular \u201cSunday services\u201d consist of three separate meetings that are usually held back\u2011to\u2011back in the same building:\n\n| Meeting | Approx. Length | Main Purpose | Who Leads It |\n|---------|----------------|--------------|--------------|\n| **Sacrament Meeting** | 70\u202fminutes (\u2248\u202f1\u202fhr\u202f10\u202fmin) | Worship, take the sacrament (bread and water), hear a primary sermon/talk, sing hymns, and receive personal testimonies. | The **bishop** (or a companion in his absence) presides; talks are given by various members of the congregation, not a single \u201cpastor.\u201d |\n| **Sunday School** | 45\u202fminutes | Instruction in **scripture study** \u2013 usually a lesson from the current\u202f\u201cManual\u201d (e.g., *Come, Follow Me*).  Classes meet by age group (children, youth, adults). | A **Sunday School teacher** (volunteer member) prepares the lesson; a **principal** (often the bishop or a counselor) oversees the program. |\n| **Priesthood/Relief Society/Primary** (often called **\u201cauxiliary meetings\u201d)** | 45\u202fminutes | Separate meetings for the **Priesthood quorum of elders** (young men 12\u201118), the **Relief Society** (women 18+), **Young Women**, **Young Men**, **Primary** (children). | Each is led by a volunteer **president** (e.g., Elders\u2019 Quorum President, Relief Society President) and his/her counselors. |\n\nYour description\u2014reading from a President of the Church during a \u201cQuorum of the Elders,\u201d followed by Sunday School and the Sacrament\u2014fits this structure, though the order you experienced may have been slightly rearranged or combined (some wards hold the Elders\u2019 Quorum meeting after the sacrament, others before).\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Where Is the \u201cPreacher\u201d (Pastor) in an LDS Service?\n\nInatter\u2011day Saint worship there is **no single full\u2011time pastor** like a Baptist or Presbyterian minister.  The **bishop** is the spiritual leader of the ward, but:\n\n* **He does not deliver a weekly sermon** unless he is assigned to give one of the scheduled talks.  \n* **All members are encouraged to speak** and many members are invited to give short talks (usually 5\u201110\u202fminutes).  For the sacrament meeting, a **list of speakers** is prepared each week (often a mix of youth, adults, and sometimes a missionary).  \n* The bishop\u2019s role is more akin to a **soul\u2011care overseer**: he conducts priesthood blessings, approves worthiness for temple entry, offers counsel, and administers church discipline when needed.  He also presides over the meeting, giving a short welcome and often a brief **opening prayer** and **closing prayer**.\n\nSo, the \u201cpastor\u201d role is **distributed among the whole congregation**, with the bishop acting as the primary administrative and pastoral overseer.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  What About Sermons / Instruction?\n\n#### a. **Sacrament Meeting Talk (the \u201cSermon\u201d)**\n\n* **Length**: Typically 5\u201110\u202fminutes, sometimes a bit longer for a special topic.  \n* **Content**: A **talk** (not a sermon in the sense of a prepared homily delivered by a professional preacher) that focuses on applying gospel principles, sharing personal experiences, or offering doctrinal clarification.  The speaker is usually **a member of the congregation** who volunteered or was asked to speak.  \n* **Preparation**: Speakers often use the current **General Conference talks** (April and October) or the **Come, Follow Me** manual as a study guide.  \n* **Frequency**: Once each week, so the \u201csermon\u201d is very brief compared with many Protestant services.\n\n#### b. **Sunday School Lessons**\n\n* **Curriculum**: The Church a **weekly lesson plan** (now called *Come, Follow Me*).  The lesson based on a passage from the **Bible, Book of Mormon, Doctrine & Covenants,** or **Pearl of Great Price**, and each lesson includes **discussion questions**.  \n* **Teacher\u2019s Role**: The teacher leads the class in studying the material, asking the discussion questions, and encouraging members to share insights.  The teacher may also give a short \u201ctalk\u201d that expands on a point, but the **focus is on group study rather than a single monologue**.\n\n#### c. **Auxiliary Meetings (Elders\u2019 Quorum, Relief Society, etc.)**\n\n* **Purpose**: These meetings are **instructional and fellowship\u2011oriented** for specific groups (e.g., men, women, youth).  \n* **Format**: A **president or counselor** may give a short talk, a video may be shown, or a guest speaker may be invited.  The content often includes doctrinal instruction, service projects, or personal development topics.  \n\n#### d. **Missionary Testimonies & Musical Numbers**\n\n* **Missionary Testimonies**: Sometimes after the sacrament meeting, **missionary couples** are invited to share a brief personal testimony of the gospel\u2014a tradition that can appear at the end of a service.  \n* **Music**: Hymns (congregational singing) and **musical numbers** performed by the **ward choir** or a piano accompanist are interspersed throughout sacrament meeting. Music is considered a form of worship and spiritual instruction.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Why Did It Feel Like \u201cNo Sermon\u201d?\n\n* **Length & Style**: The **talk** you heard was likely a **5\u2011minute \u201ctalk\u201d**, which can feel like a brief anecdote rather than a full sermon.  \n* **Multiple Speakers**: In many wards the **talks are given by several members** in succession, each focusing on a small point, so the overall effect is a series of personal reflections rather than a single, continuous preaching.  \n* **Emphasis on Personal Revelation**: Latter\u2011day Saint worship places great value on **individual spiritual experience**.  Rather than a lengthy doctrinal sermon, members are encouraged to **listen to the Spirit, ponder the talk, and apply it personally**.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  How Is Instruction and Exhortation Primarily Conferred?\n\n| Source | What It Provides | Typical Frequency |\n|--------|------------------|-------------------|\n| **General Conference** (April & October) | **Global, authoritative teachings** from the President of the Church and other senior leaders.  These talks are broadcast worldwide and later printed in *Ensign* (now *Liahona*). | Twice a year (live broadcast) |\n| **Sacrament Meeting Talks** | **Short, member\u2011led exhortations** that apply a gospel principle to everyday life. | Weekly |\n| **Sunday School Lessons** | **Systematic study** of scriptures and doctrine, encouraging discussion and personal insight. | Weekly |\n| **Auxiliary Meetings (Elders\u2019 Quorum, Relief Society, etc.)** | **Targeted instruction (e.g., priesthood duties, family life, youth responsibilities). | Weekly (often simultaneous with or after sacrament meeting) |\n| **Personal Study & Home Teaching/Ministering** | **One\u2011to\u2011one or family\u2011to\u2011family** teaching and support, including visiting, sharing messages, caring for needs. | Ongoing, usually weekly focus |\n| **Church Publications** (*Ensign*, *Liahona*, *Come, Follow Me* manuals) | **Extended doctrinal and material** that members study individually or in groups. | Continuous |\n| **Missionary Work & Temple Ordinances** | **Additional contexts for teaching** about salvation, covenants, and the plan of happiness. Ongoing for missionaries; temple work is periodic for members |\n\nAll of these channels together form the **educational and exhortative system** of the LDS Church.  No single person delivers all the instruction; it is a **distributed, participatory model** that emphasizes personal revelation and community involvement.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Quick Summary for a Visitor\n\n* **The bishop** is the closest equivalent to a pastor, but he does **not give a weekly sermon**.  \n* **Sacrament Meeting** includes a short **member talk** (the \u201csermon\u201d you may have missed or thought was brief).  \n* **Instruction** is largely **lesson\u2011based** (Sunday School) and **discussion\u2011driven**, with supplemental talks from various leaders.  \n* **"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "So, I'm on vacation in Utah, and so I attended an LDS service. In the morning, I heard a reading from one of the Presidents of the church during the \"Quorum of the Elders,\" then went to Sunday School, and finally witnessed the Sacrement of the bread and water. (I guess it makes sense there was no wine, but it did make me go \"Huh!\") After that, there were testimonies from missionaries and some music - but nothing that struck me as a sermon. \nWas I missing something, or was this an atypical service? I guess I was trying to understand what the \"pastor\"s role in the service was supposed to be - or again, is it just that Mormons are even more Baptist than baptists?\nIf someone could explain how instruction and exhortation are primarily conferred in the LDS church Id appreciate it. "}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks about LDS services, their structure, role of pastor, instruction/exhortation. Need to explain typical Sunday meeting schedule, role of leaders, the sacrament, lack of sermon perhaps they observed, content of talks, etc. Provide thorough answer, respectful, factual. Provide details about Sunday: sacrament meeting, fast Sunday, ward council, etc. Explain the priesthood, role of bishop as pastor, etc. Address their observations: reading from a President during \"Quorum of the Elders\" (maybe a talk from the General Conference or local quorum meeting hearing a talk). Explain Sunday School, Sacrament meeting, the lack of sermon may be because official talk is after sacrament. Provide info about missionary testimonies, hymns, etc. Conclude about instruction and exhortation 