t me search memory: In standard play (classical), two illegal moves result in loss.\n\nActually: The law states: 7.5a (or 7.5.1?) ... In rapid and blitz, more specific: If the opponent makes a claim after an illegal move has been completed and before he has made his own move, then the claimant gets their opponent's clock etc.\n\nLet's read: As per FIDE (from memory) - Article 7.5.1: \"If a player makes an illegal move, the position immediately before the irregularity shall be reinstated.\" Then the player must make a legal move with the same piece if possible; otherwise any legal move. 7.5.2: \"If the player ... makes an illegal move, the arbiter shall give two minutes extra time to the opponent; for the first illegal move in rapid or blitz, the opponent is given two minutes\". Actually there are separate 7.5.3 for Rapidplay and Blitz: \"If the opponent has made a move, the illegal move cannot be corrected\" \u2013 I'm not sure.\n\nBetter to review actual FIDE law text. I know in standard chess, the first illegal move results in a time penalty (2 minutes) and the opponent can claim. In rapid and blitz, a single illegal move loses the game (unless the illegal move was not noticed before the opponent made a move - then the position stands). Let's verify: In FIDE 2023, Articles 7.5a and 7.5b maybe.\n\nLet's open recollection: Under 7.5.1: Illegal move \u2013 rectify as above. 7.5.2: \u201cIf a player has moved a pawn to the last rank and promoted it to a queen, but fails to replace the pawn with a queen\u2026 etc.\u201d Not that.\n\nBetter to search memory: Actually there is a rule: In blitz, once the opponent has made a move, the illegal move cannot be corrected (the game continues). The opponent may \u201cclaim a win\u201d only if he has not made his move. In FIDE rule, after each move, the clock is started for the opponent; if the opponent makes a move, the illegal move is considered accepted. So some tournaments have a rule: In blitz, illegal move results in loss unless the opponent made a move before noticing.\n\nAnyway the answer should parse the user questions:\n\n1. If the opponent does notice illegal move: must be corrected. If no legal moves with the same piece exist (i.e., piece cannot move legally), player must make any other legal move. If there is no legal move at all (i.e., player is in checkmate or stalemate?), let's think. Illegal move occurs after a move that left player in check maybe; then we revert to the position before illegal move; then the player must make a legal move (if possible). An illegal move cannot be a checkmate; but suppose the illegal move was something like moving a pinned piece, leaving king in check. If no legal move is possible with that piece (because all squares are illegal), then the player may move another piece according to rules, but must ensure the king is not left in check. Actually the rule: \"If a player has made an illegal move, the position shall be reinstated ... the player shall make a legal move with the same piece if possible; if it is not possible, any legal move shall be made\". So we answer accordingly.\n\nIf there's no legal move at all (the player is checkmated), but illegal move was made (e.g., they moved a piece illegally but were actually in checkmate). But if they were in checkmate, game would be over. However illegal move can only happen when you have a legal move; if you were checkmated before illegal move you'd be out of turn. So they'd have at least one legal move (the illegal one). So there will be some legal move.\n\n2. If opponent does not notice and makes a move, then according to FIDE: The illegal move stands; the opponent's move is now part of the game. If the illegal move is discovered later, the game continues; the irregularity is not corrected (in blitz). Actually rule: \"If a player has made an illegal move and press his clock, the opponent may claim a win, provided the opponent has not made his move.\" If the opponent has made the move, the illegal move cannot be corrected. So the opponent's move ensures the illegal move is accepted. The game continues. At the end of the game, if the illegal move is discovered, the result stands (unless the arbiter decides otherwise). In standard or rapid, the opponent may still claim, I think.\n\nThus we need to give rule citations. Provide final answer in <answer> tags.\n\nAlso discuss bullet chess (often online). But we can focus on FIDE regulated play. Online bullet may have automatic detection, but in face-to-face blitz, same.\n\nLet's craft final answer: Use FIDE Laws of Chess, Articles 7.5, 7.5.1, 7.5.2, 7.5.3 (explain for Classical, Rapid, Blitz). Provide steps.\n\nAlso mention that in many tournaments there is an arbiter; they can intervene. So answer: first illegal move in classical/rapid: add two minutes to opponent's clock; second illegal move loses the game. In blitz, first illegal move loses the game (unless opponent has already moved). Or maybe more precisely: In blitz, if you make an illegal move you lose the game, unless your opponent has already made a move; then the move stands and the game continues.\n\nLet's verify: Actually FIDE 2023: 7.5.7 (blitz): \"If the player makes an illegal move and his opponent has not yet made his move, the opponent may claim a win. If the opponent does not claim and makes his move, the illegal move is considered accepted and the game shall continue.\" Also for rapid: \"If the opponent makes a claim, the claimant is awarded the win if the opponent has not made a move; otherwise the game shall continue.\" But there is also a time penalty: \"For the first illegal move, the opponent receives two extra minutes; for the second illegal move, the player loses the game.\" That's for standard and rapid with a certain time control? Actually 7.5.5: \"If a player makes an illegal move, the arbiter shall give two minutes extra time to the opponent; for the first illegal move in Rapid or Blitz the opponent receives two minutes\". Wait, that seems contradictory. Let's summarise: In classical: first illegal move - 2 minutes added to opponent; second illegal move - loss. In rapid & blitz: first illegal move - loss (unless opponent already moved). Maybe both Penalties and claims exist.\n\nLet\u2019s quote: FIDE Laws 2023 (approx):\n\nArticle 7.5 \u2013 Illegal move\n7.5.1 If a player makes an illegal move, the position immediately before the irregularity shall be reinstated. ... (same piece if possible else any piece)\n7.5.2 The arbiter shall give two minutes extra time to the opponent; for the first illegal move the player shall also receive a penalty of ... Actually after checking sources: (From 2020 edition) \u2013 \"If the player makes an illegal move, the arbiter shall give two minutes extra time to the opponent; for the first illegal move (in Rapid or Blitz), the opponent shall receive two extra minutes; for the second illegal move the player shall lose the game.\"\n\nLet's provide final answer summarising: \n\n- If noticed before opponent's reply: revert to position before illegal move, give opponent extra time (2 min), and player must make a legal move; if no legal move with that piece, any legal move.\n\n- If opponent has already replied, the illegal move cannot be undone; the game continues. In classical/rapid, the opponent may still claim the win before making a move, but after making a move the irregularity is accepted.\n\n- If no legal move at all (i.e., player is in check), then the illegal move couldn't have happened; the position before illegal move must have been illegal; but the rules ensure there is at least one legal move; else the game is over (checkmate or stalemate). So practically there will always be a legal move.\n\n- Regarding bullet: same as blitz.\n\nWill craft a</think>\n"}], "q_num": 98, "originalconversations": ["Especially in blitz or bullet games, it is possible that a player makes an illegal move, for example castles through check. \n\n* If the opponent does notice the illegal move, then as far as I know the first player is obliged to make a legal move with the same piece, if one exists. What if there are no legal moves? \n* What if the opponent, being in time pressure, doesn't notice the illegal move and makes a move. What happens after they realize that? Does the person who made the illegal move forfeit? Or is the whole game cancelled?\n\nAre there any standard rules for these kinds of situations?", "This only really applies to over the board games (since online chess servers prevent illegal moves).\nIn a standard time control game, under USCF rules, when the illegal move is noticed by one of the players, a few things happen.  First, the illegal move must have been made within the last 10 moves.  If not, then the current position stands, and play continues.  Assuming that the move is recent, the position is restored to the position immediately before the illegal move.\nThen, the player that made the illegal move now must make a legal move with the same piece (if one exists).  If there are no legal moves, then the player is free to make any move.  This is due to the touch move rule.  Finally, if the offending player has pressed the clock, a two minute &quot;bonus&quot; is added to the opponent's clock to compensate them for any time lost and to penalize the offending player.\nHere is the relevant passage from the USCF rulebook:\n\n11A. Illegal move during last ten moves. If, during a game, it is found that one of either player's last ten moves was illegal, the position shall be reinstated to what it was before the illegal move. The game shall then continue by applying Rule 10, The Touched Piece, to the move replacing the illegal move. If the position cannot be reinstated, then the illegal move shall stand. Move counters on clocks that have them may by readjusted.\n11D. Illegal move. If, a player completes an illegal move by pressing the clock, in addition to the usual obligation to make a legal move with the touched piece if possible, the standard penalty specified in rule 1C2a applies (i.e. two minutes added to the opponent's clock).\n\nIn blitz chess, the rules are different.  According to the WBCA (World Blitz Chess Association) rules, a player who makes an illegal move loses the game instantly.  This rule is frequently enforced in casual games too.  The most common way that this occurs is that one player doesn't notice that they are in check and makes a move that doesn't get out of check.  Then the checking player simply captures the king and claims a victory.\nBecause blitz is a much faster pace game, the illegal move must be noticed within two moves.  If it is not, then the current position stands and play continues.\nAt no point is the game cancelled due to an illegal move.  Pretty much once a game starts and both players make a move, a result of win, loss, or draw will be recorded."], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "Especially in blitz or bullet games, it is possible that a player makes an illegal move, for example castles through check. \n\n* If the opponent does notice the illegal move, then as far as I know the first player is obliged to make a legal move with the same piece, if one exists. What if there are no legal moves? \n* What if the opponent, being in time pressure, doesn't notice the illegal move and makes a move. What happens after they realize that? Does the person who made the illegal move forfeit? Or is the whole game cancelled?\n\nAre there any standard rules for these kinds of situations?"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "Assume an environment with a puppet-managed cluster of different servers - various hardware, software, operating systems, virtual/dedicated, etc.\nWould you choose meaningful hostnames (mysqlmaster01..99, mysqlslave001..999, vpnprimary, vpnbackup, etc.) or would you prefer meaningless hostnames such as characters from a book or movie?\nThe problem I see with meaningful hostnames is that names usually represent a single service and if a server has more than one purpose it gets really messy (especially if server roles change often).\nIsn't mapping a service name to an IP address and maintaining that mapping what DNS is supposed to do?\nWhat are the advantages and drawbacks of both approaches and what actual problems have you had to tackle with the approach you chose?"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer: discuss meaningful vs meaningless hostnames in puppet-managed environments, address service naming, DNS, etc. Provide advantages/drawbacks, real-world issues, recommendations. Provide final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags.\n\nUser wants advice. Provide thorough analysis.\n\nWe should discuss:\n\n- Hostnames purpose: unique identifier; human-friendly; integration with monitoring, automation, config mgmt.\n- Meaningful: describe role, environment, location.\n- Pros: easier for ops, troubleshooting, documentation, correlation with service; easier to locate server's purpose; helpful in alerts, logs.\n- Cons: role changes cause rename, causing config changes, confusion; may run out of naming scheme; if multiple services on same host, naming becomes ambiguous; DNS updates may break; scaling issues.\n- Meaningless: random words, movie characters; pros: decouples name from function, encourages abstraction, easier to rename or repurpose without renaming; can use inventory IDs; reduces assumptions; useful for dynamic or cloud environments; supports immutability.\n- Cons: harder for humans to identify purpose, increasing reliance on CMDB; more effort to track; need additional mapping (e.g., via DNS, etcd, CMDB, puppet facts).\n- Use of DNS: concept of service discovery. DNS can have records like mysql01.example.com pointing to IP; CNAME to service-specific name; SRV records for load balancer; but hostnames still needed for OS-level identity.\n\n- Recommendations: Use a naming convention that encodes environment, role, location, and possibly unique index (e.g., prod-db-01, dev-web-02). Keep names stable (don't rename on role change). If role changes, treat as new host (decommission old, provision new). Use virtualization/containers; immutable infrastructure: naming decoupled from service.\n\n- Use FQDN with generic host part (e.g., host01, host02) and separate DNS entries for services (e.g., db-prod.example.com CNAME host01). Use Puppet to manage DNS, separate service names from host names.\n\n- Use Puppet classification (facts, roles, profiles) \u2013 hostnames may be generic but facts provide role, environment. Use Hiera and hiera-eyaml.\n\n- Real issues: confusion when hostnames indicate outdated role; difficulty in automation for 'db' vs 'app' roles; duplicate IPs after rename; DNS TTL causing stale caches; monitoring alerts showing wrong hostnames.\n\n- Combine: meaningful hostnames for static services (e.g., database, LDAP) where role rarely changes; for dynamic servers (e.g., build agents, test nodes) use abstract names.\n\n- Provide best practice: store mapping in CMDB and/or Puppet external nodes; use service discovery via DNS SRV, Consul, etc.\n\nLet's craft answer in a structured way.\n\nNow produce final answer in <answer></answer>.</think>\n<answer>\n**Short answer:**  \nUse *stable, predictable hostnames* that are **not tied to a specific service** but still give you enough information to locate the machine (environment, location, type). Treat the hostname as an immutable identifier and let DNS (or other service\u2011discovery mechanisms) provide the *service\u2011to\u2011host* mapping.  \n\nBelow is a detailed discussion of the two extremes, the trade\u2011offs, and a practical hybrid approach that works well in a Puppet\u2011managed, heterogeneous cluster.\n\n---\n\n## 1.  What a hostname really is\n\n| Purpose | What it gives you |\n|---------|-------------------|\n| **Unique identifier** for the OS, SSH, syslog, etc. | Must be stable OS services assume the name never changes. |\n| **Human\u2011readable hint** about the machine | Makes troubleshooting, documentation and on\u2011call rotation easier. |\n| **Part of DNS** \u2013 the \u201cA\u201d record that resolves the name to an IP. | DNS can also hold *additional* names (CNAME, SRV) that describe services. |\n\nBecause the OS and many tools treat the hostname as an immutable primary key, changing it is expensive (re\u2011boot, key distribution, log\u2011correlation, monitoring, etc.). Therefore the hostname should **not be used as a mutable \u201crole tag\u201d.**  \n\nInstead, keep the role information elsewhere (Puppet facts, CMDB, service\u2011specific DNS names, service\u2011discovery tools).\n\n---\n\n## 2.  Meaningful hostnames (role\u2011centric)\n\n**Examples**: `mysql\u2011master\u201101`, `vpn\u2011primary`, `web\u2011eu\u2011prod\u201103`\n\n### Advantages\n| Benefit | Why it helps |\n|---------|--------------|\n| **Immediate context** for an operator looking at a console or a log line. | No need to look up a database \u2013 the name tells you the purpose. |\n| **Simple static DNS** \u2013 one A\u2011record per host, no extra CNAMEs. | Easy to write a quick `dig` or `ping`. |\n| **Straight\u2011forward monitoring templates** \u2013 you can map `host -> service` by regex. | Reduces chart/alert configuration. |\n\n### Drawbacks\n| Problem | Impact |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Couples identity & role** \u2013 when the machine\u2019s role changes you have to rename it or provision a new one. | Renaming breaks SSH known\u2011hosts, Kerberos logs, monitoring caches, etc. |\n| **Multi\u2011purpose servers become ambiguous** \u2013 e.g. a host that runs both MySQL and Redis. | Either you pick one \u201cprimary\u201d name (confusing) or you end up with a long hybrid (`db\u2011redis\u201101`). |\n| **Naming\u2011scheme exhaustion** \u2013 you may run out of numbers or need ad\u2011hoc exceptions. | Inconsistent naming hurts automation. |\n| **Harder to reuse hardware** \u2013 after a de\u2011commission you cannot take the machine and give it a completely different job without a rename. | Forces you to keep the old host in the inventory (asted asset). |\n\n**When it works best:**  \n- Infrastructure that is *static* for years (e.g., a dedicated LDAP server, a primary database node).  \n- Small\u2011to\u2011medium environments where a human can keep the naming spreadsheet up to date.  \n\n---\n\n## 3.  \u201cMeaningless\u201d hostnames (abstract / random)\n\n**Examples**: `frodo`, `zaphod`, `e11a2b`, `node-00123`\n\n### Advantages\n| Benefit | Why it helps |\n|---------|--------------|\n| **Decouples identity from purpose** \u2013 you can repurpose a machine without any rename. | Immutable host = less operational churn. |\n| **Encourages proper service discovery** \u2013 you *must* create DNS CNAME/SRV records like `db-prod.example.com \u2192 node-00123`. | Makes you use the right abstraction layer. |\n| **Simplifies scaling** \u2013 just increment a counter or use UUIDs, no need to think about \u201cmaster\u201101 vs slave\u201105\u201d. | Works well with auto\u2011scaling groups, cloud VMs, containers. |\n| **Uniform naming across environments** \u2013 the same pattern works for dev, test, prod. | Less chance of \u201cnaming drift\u201d. |\n\n### Drawbacks\n| Problem | Impact |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Harder for humans to locate a machine** \u2013 you need an external inventory (CMDB, PuppetDB, spreadsheet). | On\u2011call rotates spend extra time looking up \u201cwhich node is the MySQL master?\u201d. |\n| **More DNS records to maintain** \u2013 you now manage additional CNAME/SRV entries for each service. | Requires reliable automation (Puppet, BIND, Route53, etc.). |\n| **Log and metric correlation** \u2013 logs show `frodo` while the alert says \u201cdb\u2011prod\u201d. | Must have a reliable mapping function in the monitoring stack. |\n| **Potential for accidental reuse** \u2013 if you forget to retire the old host, a new one could get the same abstract name. | Leads to duplicate IPs / split\u2011brain. |\n\n**When it works best:**  \n- Highly dynamic environments (auto\u2011scaled cloud instances, CI workers, test labs).  \n- \u201cImmutable\u2011infrastructure\u201d philosophy where a server is never re\u2011purposed; you just kill the old one and spin a new one.  \n- Organizations that already have a strong CMDB or service\u2011discovery layer (Consul, etcd, DNS SRV).  \n\n---\n\n## 4.  A pragmatic hybrid approach (recommended)\n\n1. **Give every machine a *stable, generic* hostname** that is *only* an identifier.  \n   ```\n   prod-db\u201101.example.com\n   prod-web\u2011a3b5c6.example.com\n   dev\u2011ci\u2011014.example.com\n   ```\n   *Pattern*: `<environment>-<type>-<unique\u2011suffix>`  \n   - `environment` = prod / dev / test / qa  \n   - `type` = db / web / ci / cache (just a broad category, not a specific service)  \n   - `unique\u2011suffix` = sequential number, short hash, or short\u2011word list to keep it readable.\n\n2. **Never change a hostname** after it is provisioned. If the role changes, de\u2011commission the node and provision a *new* host with a new identifier.\n\n3. **Use DNS (or a service\u2011discovery system) to publish *service names*** that point to the host.  \n   - `db-prod.example.com   CNAME  prod-db-01.example.com`  \n   - `vpn-primary.example.com CNAME prod-vpn-a1b2c3.example.com`  \n   - For load\u2011balanced services: use **SRV** records or a managed load\u2011balancer that resolves to a pool of hosts.\n\n4. **Let Puppet ("}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Assume an environment with a puppet-managed cluster of different servers - various hardware, software, operating systems, virtual/dedicated, etc.\nWould you choose meaningful hostnames (mysqlmaster01..99, mysqlslave001..999, vpnprimary, vpnbackup, etc.) or would you prefer meaningless hostnames such as characters from a book or movie?\nThe problem I see with meaningful hostnames is that names usually represent a single service and if a server has more than one purpose it gets really messy (especially if server roles change often).\nIsn't mapping a service name to an IP address and maintaining that mapping what DNS is supposed to do?\nWhat are the advantages and drawbacks of both approaches and what actual problems have you had to tackle with the approach you chose?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer: discuss meaningful vs meaningless hostnames in puppet-managed environments, address service naming, DNS, etc. Provide advantages/drawbacks, real-world issues, recommendations. Provide final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags.\n\nUser wants advice. Provide thorough analysis.\n\nWe should discuss:\n\n- Hostnames purpose: unique identifier; human-friendly; integration with monitoring, automation, config mgmt.\n- Meaningful: describe role, environment, location.\n- Pros: easier for ops, troubleshooting, documentation, correlation with service; easier to locate server's purpose; helpful in alerts, logs.\n- Cons: role changes cause rename, causing config changes, confusion; may run out of naming scheme; if multiple services on same host, naming becomes ambiguous; DNS updates may break; scaling issues.\n- Meaningless: random words, movie characters; pros: decouples name from function, encourages abstraction, easier to rename or repurpose without renaming; can use inventory IDs; reduces assumptions; useful for dynamic or cloud environments; supports immutability.\n- Cons: harder for humans to identify purpose, increasing reliance on CMDB; more effort to track; need additional mapping (e.g., via DNS, etcd, CMDB, puppet facts).\n- Use of DNS: concept of service discovery. DNS can have records like mysql01.example.com pointing to IP; CNAME to service-specific name; SRV records for load balancer; but hostnames still needed for OS-level identity.\n\n- Recommendations: Use a naming convention that encodes environment, role, location, and possibly unique index (e.g., prod-db-01, dev-web-02). Keep names stable (don't rename on role change). If role changes, treat as new host (decommission old, provision new). Use virtualization/containers; immutable infrastructure: naming decoupled from service.\n\n- Use FQDN with generic host part (e.g., host01, host02) and separate DNS entries for services (e.g., db-prod.example.com CNAME host01). Use Puppet to manage DNS, separate service names from host names.\n\n- Use Puppet classification (facts, roles, profiles) \u2013 hostnames may be generic but facts provide role, environment. Use Hiera and hiera-eyaml.\n\n- Real issues: confusion when hostnames indicate outdated role; difficulty in automation for 'db' vs 'app' roles; duplicate IPs after rename; DNS TTL causing stale caches; monitoring alerts showing wrong hostnames.\n\n- Combine: meaningful hostnames for static services (e.g., database, LDAP) where role rarely changes; for dynamic servers (e.g., build agents, test nodes) use abstract names.\n\n- Provide best practice: store mapping in CMDB and/or Puppet external nodes; use service discovery via DNS SRV, Consul, etc.\n\nLet's craft answer in a structured way.\n\nNow produce final answer in <answer></answer>.</think>\n<answer>\n**Short answer:**  \nUse *stable, predictable hostnames* that are **not tied to a specific service** but still give you enough information to locate the machine (environment, location, type). Treat the hostname as an immutable identifier and let DNS (or other service\u2011discovery mechanisms) provide the *service\u2011to\u2011host* mapping.  \n\nBelow is a detailed discussion of the two extremes, the trade\u2011offs, and a practical hybrid approach that works well in a Puppet\u2011managed, heterogeneous cluster.\n\n---\n\n## 1.  What a hostname really is\n\n| Purpose | What it gives you |\n|---------|-------------------|\n| **Unique identifier** for the OS, SSH, syslog, etc. | Must be stable OS services assume the name never changes. |\n| **Human\u2011readable hint** about the machine | Makes troubleshooting, documentation and on\u2011call rotation easier. |\n| **Part of DNS** \u2013 the \u201cA\u201d record that resolves the name to an IP. | DNS can also hold *additional* names (CNAME, SRV) that describe services. |\n\nBecause the OS and many tools treat the hostname as an immutable primary key, changing it is expensive (re\u2011boot, key distribution, log\u2011correlation, monitoring, etc.). Therefore the hostname should **not be used as a mutable \u201crole tag\u201d.**  \n\nInstead, keep the role information elsewhere (Puppet facts, CMDB, service\u2011specific DNS names, service\u2011discovery tools).\n\n---\n\n## 2.  Meaningful hostnames (role\u2011centric)\n\n**Examples**: `mysql\u2011master\u201101`, `vpn\u2011primary`, `web\u2011eu\u2011prod\u201103`\n\n### Advantages\n| Benefit | Why it helps |\n|---------|--------------|\n| **Immediate context** for an operator looking at a console or a log line. | No need to look up a database \u2013 the name tells you the purpose. |\n| **Simple static DNS** \u2013 one A\u2011record per host, no extra CNAMEs. | Easy to write a quick `dig` or `ping`. |\n| **Straight\u2011forward monitoring templates** \u2013 you can map `host -> service` by regex. | Reduces chart/alert configuration. |\n\n### Drawbacks\n| Problem | Impact |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Couples identity & role** \u2013 when the machine\u2019s role changes you have to rename it or provision a new one. | Renaming breaks SSH known\u2011hosts, Kerberos logs, monitoring caches, etc. |\n| **Multi\u2011purpose servers become ambiguous** \u2013 e.g. a host that runs both MySQL and Redis. | Either you pick one \u201cprimary\u201d name (confusing) or you end up with a long hybrid (`db\u2011redis\u201101`). |\n| **Naming\u2011scheme exhaustion** \u2013 you may run out of numbers or need ad\u2011hoc exceptions. | Inconsistent naming hurts automation. |\n| **Harder to reuse hardware** \u2013 after a de\u2011commission you cannot take the machine and give it a completely different job without a rename. | Forces you to keep the old host in the inventory (asted asset). |\n\n**When it works best:**  \n- Infrastructure that is *static* for years (e.g., a dedicated LDAP server, a primary database node).  \n- Small\u2011to\u2011medium environments where a human can keep the naming spreadsheet up to date.  \n\n---\n\n## 3.  \u201cMeaningless\u201d hostnames (abstract / random)\n\n**Examples**: `frodo`, `zaphod`, `e11a2b`, `node-00123`\n\n### Advantages\n| Benefit | Why it helps |\n|---------|--------------|\n| **Decouples identity from purpose** \u2013 you can repurpose a machine without any rename. | Immutable host = less operational churn. |\n| **Encourages proper service discovery** \u2013 you *must* create DNS CNAME/SRV records like `db-prod.example.com \u2192 node-00123`. | Makes you use the right abstraction layer. |\n| **Simplifies scaling** \u2013 just increment a counter or use UUIDs, no need to think about \u201cmaster\u201101 vs slave\u201105\u201d. | Works well with auto\u2011scaling groups, cloud VMs, containers. |\n| **Uniform naming across environments** \u2013 the same pattern works for dev, test, prod. | Less chance of \u201cnaming drift\u201d. |\n\n### Drawbacks\n| Problem | Impact |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Harder for humans to locate a machine** \u2013 you need an external inventory (CMDB, PuppetDB, spreadsheet). | On\u2011call rotates spend extra time looking up \u201cwhich node is the MySQL master?\u201d. |\n| **More DNS records to maintain** \u2013 you now manage additional CNAME/SRV entries for each service. | Requires reliable automation (Puppet, BIND, Route53, etc.). |\n| **Log and metric correlation** \u2013 logs show `frodo` while the alert says \u201cdb\u2011prod\u201d. | Must have a reliable mapping function in the monitoring stack. |\n| **Potential for accidental reuse** \u2013 if you forget to retire the old host, a new one could get the same abstract name. | Leads to duplicate IPs / split\u2011brain. |\n\n**When it works best:**  \n- Highly dynamic environments (auto\u2011scaled cloud instances, CI workers, test labs).  \n- \u201cImmutable\u2011infrastructure\u201d philosophy where a server is never re\u2011purposed; you just kill the old one and spin a new one.  \n- Organizations that already have a strong CMDB or service\u2011discovery layer (Consul, etcd, DNS SRV).  \n\n---\n\n## 4.  A pragmatic hybrid approach (recommended)\n\n1. **Give every machine a *stable, generic* hostname** that is *only* an identifier.  \n   ```\n   prod-db\u201101.example.com\n   prod-web\u2011a3b5c6.example.com\n   dev\u2011ci\u2011014.example.com\n   ```\n   *Pattern*: `<environment>-<type>-<unique\u2011suffix>`  \n   - `environment` = prod / dev / test / qa  \n   - `type` = db / web / ci / cache (just a broad category, not a specific service)  \n   - `unique\u2011suffix` = sequential number, short hash, or short\u2011word list to keep it readable.\n\n2. **Never change a hostname** after it is provisioned. If the role changes, de\u2011commission the node and provision a *new* host with a new identifier.\n\n3. **Use DNS (or a service\u2011discovery system) to publish *service names*** that point to the host.  \n   - `db-prod.example.com   CNAME  prod-db-01.example.com`  \n   - `vpn-primary.example.com CNAME prod-vpn-a1b2c3.example.com`  \n   - For load\u2011balanced services: use **SRV** records or a managed load\u2011balancer that resolves to a pool of hosts.\n\n4. **Let Puppet ("}], "q_num": 275, "originalconversations": ["Assume an environment with a puppet-managed cluster of different servers - various hardware, software, operating systems, virtual/dedicated, etc.\nWould you choose meaningful hostnames (mysqlmaster01..99, mysqlslave001..999, vpnprimary, vpnbackup, etc.) or would you prefer meaningless hostnames such as characters from a book or movie?\nThe problem I see with meaningful hostnames is that names usually represent a single service and if a server has more than one purpose it gets really messy (especially if server roles change often).\nIsn't mapping a service name to an IP address and maintaining that mapping what DNS is supposed to do?\nWhat are the advantages and drawbacks of both approaches and what actual problems have you had to tackle with the approach you chose?", "This largely comes down to whether your servers are ```pets``` or ```livestock```.\n\nPets get individual names. They're distinct from each other, and we care about those differences. When one gets sick, we usually try to nurse it back to health. Traditionally, servers have been pets.\n\nLivestock get numbers. They're mostly identical, and what differences there are, we don't care about and usually try to minimize. When one gets sick, we put it down and get another one. Fully virtualized servers, especially IaaS servers such as AWS, are livestock.\n\nIn most complex environments, you have a mix. Your web backends, for instance, are almost certainly livestock. If you need more, you spin up a few more with the standard config; if you don't need as many you turn some off. Your database servers, in some configurations, are pets. There may be a lot of special setup on each; you may even be running them on bare metal instead of virtualization.\n\nOf course, in either environment, you can name SERVICES and address those directly. This is a best practice in any case; your developers shouldn't need to know or care what the actual hostname of a service is. The hostname should be a purely operational detail. Think, then, of encoding information that is useful to your ops staff in the hostnames - for instance, it's often helpful to denote which datacenter a server is in."], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "Assume an environment with a puppet-managed cluster of different servers - various hardware, software, operating systems, virtual/dedicated, etc.\nWould you choose meaningful hostnames (mysqlmaster01..99, mysqlslave001..999, vpnprimary, vpnbackup, etc.) or would you prefer meaningless hostnames such as characters from a book or movie?\nThe problem I see with meaningful hostnames is that names usually represent a single service and if a server has more than one purpose it gets really messy (especially if server roles change often).\nIsn't mapping a service name to an IP address and maintaining that mapping what DNS is supposed to do?\nWhat are the advantages and drawbacks of both approaches and what actual problems have you had to tackle with the approach you chose?"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "I'm working on 2 different branches: release and development. \nI noticed I still need to integrate some changes that were committed to the release branch back into the development branch.  \nThe problem is I don't need all of the commit, only some hunks in certain files, so a simple  \n```git cherry-pick bc66559\n```\ndoes not do the trick.  \nWhen I do a\n```git show bc66559\n```\nI can see the diff but don't really know a good way of applying that partially to my current working tree.  "}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer: how to apply partial changes from a commit (hunks) onto another branch, selectively cherry pick parts. Options: git cherry-pick -n and then edit the changes, or use git format-patch and git apply with -p, or use git checkout -p, or use git show | git apply -R etc. Possibly use 'git cherry-pick -n' to apply full commit to index, then use 'git reset -p' to unstage unwanted hunks, then amend commit. Or using 'git cherry-pick -n' then 'git add -p' to select hunks. Or use 'git show <commit> | git apply -R' (?) Actually to apply only selected hunks, you can use 'git checkout <commit> -- <path>' but that includes all changes to that file. Better: use 'git cherry-pick -n <commit>', which applies changes to working tree and index, but doesn't commit. Then use 'git reset -p' or 'git reset HEAD <file>' and 'git add -p' to stage only needed hunks, then commit. Or use interactive rebase with edit and then 'git reset' plus 'git add -p'.\n\nAnswer should give steps: checkout dev branch, then git cherry-pick -n <commit>, then edit/unstage unwanted hunks with 'git reset -p' or 'git restore -p' and commit. Or use 'git show <commit> | git apply --cached -R' etc.\n\nProvide alternative: 'git checkout -p' to pick patches from commit.\n\nAlso 'git format-patch -1 <commit> && git apply -R' but interactive.\n\nSo answer: use 'git cherry-pick -n' (no commit), then use 'git add -p' to stage selected hunks, then commit. Or use 'git checkout -p' with commit reference.\n\nLet's craft final answer with guidelines and examples.\n\n</think>\n<answer>\nYou can \u201ccherry\u2011pick\u201d a commit **without** committing it, then interactively choose which\nhunks you want to keep.  \nThe usual workflow is:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Switch to the branch that must receive the changes\ngit checkout development\n\n# 2. Apply the whole commit to your working tree, but do NOT create a commit yet\ngit cherry-pick -n bc66559      # -n = --no-commit\n```\n\nNow the changes from `bc66559` are in both the index (staged) **and** the working\ntree.  Use the interactive staging commands to drop the parts you don\u2019t want.\n\n### Option A \u2013 Unstage everything and stage only the wanted hunks\n\n```bash\n# Unstage everything that came from the cherry\u2011pick\ngit reset HEAD .\n\n# Now interactively stage the pieces you need\ngit add -p                     # press 'y' to stage a hunk, 'n' to skip,\n                              # 's' to split a hunk further, etc.\n```\n\nWhen you are satisfied:\n\n```bash\ngit commit -m \"Cherry\u2011picked selected parts of bc66559\"\n```\n\n### Option B \u2013 Keep the commit as\u2011is, then remove unwanted hunks\n\nIf you prefer to keep the original staged changes and just remove a few\nhunks, you can do the opposite:\n\n```bash\n# Unstage only the parts you don\u2019t want\ngit reset -p                # works like `git add -p` but *unstages* hunk\n\n# Or, if you have already committed, use:\ngit restore -p <file>       # interactively revert hunk(s) from the working tree\n```\n\n### Option C \u2013 Using `git checkout -p` (works on any commit)\n\nYou can also pull selected hunks directly from a commit without a full cherry\u2011pick:\n\n```bash\ngit checkout -p bc66559 -- <path/to/file>\n```\n\n`git checkout -p` will present each hunk from that file and let you decide\nwhether to apply it to the current working tree.  Run it for each file you\nneed, then commit the resulting changes.\n\n---\n\n## Full step\u2011by\u2011step example\n\n```bash\n# 1. Move to the target branch\ngit switch development\n\n# 2. Apply the whole commit, but keep it uncommitted\ngit cherry-pick -n bc66559\n\n# 3. Unstage everything (so we start with a clean slate)\ngit reset HEAD .\n\n# 4. Interactively stage only the hunks we want\ngit add -p               # choose y/n/s/... for each hunk\n\n# 5. Verify the staged changes (optional)\ngit diff --cached\n\n# 6. Commit the selected pieces\ngit commit -m \"Partial back\u2011port of bc66559: only the needed changes\"\n```\n\n### Tips & gotchas\n\n* **Splitting hunks** \u2013 Inside `git add -p` press `s` to split a hunk into smaller\n  pieces, making it easier to pick exactly what you need.\n* **Undoing a mistake** \u2013 If you make a mistake while staging, `git restore --staged <file>`\n  will unstage the file, and you can run `git add -p` again.\n* **Multiple commits** \u2013 Repeat the same process for each commit you want to pull\n  partially. You can also use a range with `git cherry-pick -n A..B` and then\n  prune the unwanted parts with `git reset -p`/`git add -p`.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\n1. `git cherry-pick -n <sha>` \u2013 bring the commit into your work tree without committing.\n2. Use **interactive staging** (`git add -p` or `git reset -p`) to keep only the\n   hunks you need.\n3. Commit the result.\n\nThis gives you the same effect as a \u201cpartial cherry\u2011pick\u201d without any extra tools."}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I'm working on 2 different branches: release and development. \nI noticed I still need to integrate some changes that were committed to the release branch back into the development branch.  \nThe problem is I don't need all of the commit, only some hunks in certain files, so a simple  \n```git cherry-pick bc66559\n```\ndoes not do the trick.  \nWhen I do a\n```git show bc66559\n```\nI can see the diff but don't really know a good way of applying that partially to my current working tree.  "}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer: how to apply partial changes from a commit (hunks) onto another branch, selectively cherry pick parts. Options: git cherry-pick -n and then edit the changes, or use git format-patch and git apply with -p, or use git checkout -p, or use git show | git apply -R etc. Possibly use 'git cherry-pick -n' to apply full commit to index, then use 'git reset -p' to unstage unwanted hunks, then amend commit. Or using 'git cherry-pick -n' then 'git add -p' to select hunks. Or use 'git show <commit> | git apply -R' (?) Actually to apply only selected hunks, you can use 'git checkout <commit> -- <path>' but that includes all changes to that file. Better: use 'git cherry-pick -n <commit>', which applies changes to working tree and index, but doesn't commit. Then use 'git reset -p' or 'git reset HEAD <file>' and 'git add -p' to stage only needed hunks, then commit. Or use interactive rebase with edit and then 'git reset' plus 'git add -p'.\n\nAnswer should give steps: checkout dev branch, then git cherry-pick -n <commit>, then edit/unstage unwanted hunks with 'git reset -p' or 'git restore -p' and commit. Or use 'git show <commit> | git apply --cached -R' etc.\n\nProvide alternative: 'git checkout -p' to pick patches from commit.\n\nAlso 'git format-patch -1 <commit> && git apply -R' but interactive.\n\nSo answer: use 'git cherry-pick -n' (no commit), then use 'git add -p' to stage selected hunks, then commit. Or use 'git checkout -p' with commit reference.\n\nLet's craft final answer with guidelines and examples.\n\n</think>\n<answer>\nYou can \u201ccherry\u2011pick\u201d a commit **without** committing it, then interactively choose which\nhunks you want to keep.  \nThe usual workflow is:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Switch to the branch that must receive the changes\ngit checkout development\n\n# 2. Apply the whole commit to your working tree, but do NOT create a commit yet\ngit cherry-pick -n bc66559      # -n = --no-commit\n```\n\nNow the changes from `bc66559` are in both the index (staged) **and** the working\ntree.  Use the interactive staging commands to drop the parts you don\u2019t want.\n\n### Option A \u2013 Unstage everything and stage only the wanted hunks\n\n```bash\n# Unstage everything that came from the cherry\u2011pick\ngit reset HEAD .\n\n# Now interactively stage the pieces you need\ngit add -p                     # press 'y' to stage a hunk, 'n' to skip,\n                              # 's' to split a hunk further, etc.\n```\n\nWhen you are satisfied:\n\n```bash\ngit commit -m \"Cherry\u2011picked selected parts of bc66559\"\n```\n\n### Option B \u2013 Keep the commit as\u2011is, then remove unwanted hunks\n\nIf you prefer to keep the original staged changes and just remove a few\nhunks, you can do the opposite:\n\n```bash\n# Unstage only the parts you don\u2019t want\ngit reset -p                # works like `git add -p` but *unstages* hunk\n\n# Or, if you have already committed, use:\ngit restore -p <file>       # interactively revert hunk(s) from the working tree\n```\n\n### Option C \u2013 Using `git checkout -p` (works on any commit)\n\nYou can also pull selected hunks directly from a commit without a full cherry\u2011pick:\n\n```bash\ngit checkout -p bc66559 -- <path/to/file>\n```\n\n`git checkout -p` will present each hunk from that file and let you decide\nwhether to apply it to the current working tree.  Run it for each file you\nneed, then commit the resulting changes.\n\n---\n\n## Full step\u2011by\u2011step example\n\n```bash\n# 1. Move to the target branch\ngit switch development\n\n# 2. Apply the whole commit, but keep it uncommitted\ngit cherry-pick -n bc66559\n\n# 3. Unstage everything (so we start with a clean slate)\ngit reset HEAD .\n\n# 4. Interactively stage only the hunks we want\ngit add -p               # choose y/n/s/... for each hunk\n\n# 5. Verify the staged changes (optional)\ngit diff --cached\n\n# 6. Commit the selected pieces\ngit commit -m \"Partial back\u2011port of bc66559: only the needed changes\"\n```\n\n### Tips & gotchas\n\n* **Splitting hunks** \u2013 Inside `git add -p` press `s` to split a hunk into smaller\n  pieces, making it easier to pick exactly what you need.\n* **Undoing a mistake** \u2013 If you make a mistake while staging, `git restore --staged <file>`\n  will unstage the file, and you can run `git add -p` again.\n* **Multiple commits** \u2013 Repeat the same process for each commit you want to pull\n  partially. You can also use a range with `git cherry-pick -n A..B` and then\n  prune the unwanted parts with `git reset -p`/`git add -p`.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\n1. `git cherry-pick -n <sha>` \u2013 bring the commit into your work tree without committing.\n2. Use **interactive staging** (`git add -p` or `git reset -p`) to keep only the\n   hunks you need.\n3. Commit the result.\n\nThis gives you the same effect as a \u201cpartial cherry\u2011pick\u201d without any extra tools."}], "q_num": 158, "originalconversations": ["I'm working on 2 different branches: release and development. \nI noticed I still need to integrate some changes that were committed to the release branch back into the development branch.  \nThe problem is I don't need all of the commit, only some hunks in certain files, so a simple  \n```git cherry-pick bc66559\n```\ndoes not do the trick.  \nWhen I do a\n```git show bc66559\n```\nI can see the diff but don't really know a good way of applying that partially to my current working tree.  ", "The core thing you're going to want here is ```git add -p``` (```-p``` is a synonym for ```--patch```). This provides an interactive way to add in content, letting you decide whether each hunk should go in or not, and even letting you manually edit the patch if necessary.\nTo use it in combination with cherry-pick:\n```git cherry-pick -n  # get your patch, but don't commit (-n = --no-commit)\ngit reset                   # unstage the changes from the cherry-picked commit\ngit add -p                  # make all your choices (add the changes you do want)\ngit commit                  # make the commit!\n```\n``git-cherry-pick``` has a ```--no-commit``` option, and you need to do ```git reset```. If you only want to leave a few things out of the commit, you could use ```git reset ...``` to unstage just those files.)\nYou can provide specific paths to ```add -p``` if necessary. If you're starting with a patch you could replace the ```cherry-pick``` with ```apply```.\n\nIf you really want to ```git cherry-pick -p ``` (that option does not exist), you can use\n```git checkout -p \n```\nThat will diff the current commit against the commit you specify, and allow you to apply hunks from that diff individually. This option may be more useful if the commit you're pulling in has merge conflicts in part of the commit you're not interested in. (Note, however, that ```checkout``` differs from ```cherry-pick```: ```checkout``` tries to apply ``````'s contents entirely, while ```cherry-pick``` applies the diff of the specified commit from it's parent. This means that ```checkout``` can apply more than just that commit, which might be more than you want.)"], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "I'm working on 2 different branches: release and development. \nI noticed I still need to integrate some changes that were committed to the release branch back into the development branch.  \nThe problem is I don't need all of the commit, only some hunks in certain files, so a simple  \n```git cherry-pick bc66559\n```\ndoes not do the trick.  \nWhen I do a\n```git show bc66559\n```\nI can see the diff but don't really know a good way of applying that partially to my current working tree.  "}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "What is the difference between an object and a companion object in a class in kotlin?\nExample:\n```class MyClass {\n    object Holder {\n        //something\n    }\n    companion object {\n        //something\n    }\n}\n```\nI already read that companion object shall be used, if the containing parameters/methods are closely related to its class.\nBut why is there also the possibility of declaring a normal object in the class? Because it behaves exactly like the companion, but it must have a name.\nIs there maybe a difference in its \"static\" (I'm from the java side) lifecycle? "}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe question: difference between an object and a companion object in a class in Kotlin. Provide explanation: object declaration inside class is a nested object, which is a singleton instance associated with the outer class. It's similar to a static nested class in Java? Actually object is like a class with a single instance; when declared inside a class, it's like a static nested object (i.e., like a static nested class with a single instance). Companion object is a special object that can be referenced via the class name without needing to specify its name, and its members can be accessed as if they were static members of the class (including Java interop, static fields/methods). Moreover, companion object can implement interfaces, create factory methods, have extensions. It also allows you to define extension functions on the containing class that are resolved in the companion scope.\n\nKey points:\n\n- object declared inside class: named singleton; needs explicit name to access its members: MyClass.Holder.someMethod().\n  It has its own independent lifecycle \u2014 it's created the first time it's accessed (lazy initialization). It's essentially a static final field containing instance (like static final MyClass$Holder$INSTANCE, as generated). It cannot be referenced via class name without qualifier; cannot be used where static members expected.\n\n- companion object: a singleton associated with its containing class; its members can be accessed directly via class name: MyClass.someMethod(). In Kotlin, these are members of the companion object; they are not static but they appear as static in Java code (via @JvmStatic). If you want Java interop to have static members, you can use companion object with @JvmStatic annotation (or without? Actually Kotlin generates static methods for companion object members only if annotated with @JvmStatic; else they become MyClass$Companion methods). But Kotlin sees the class name as a reference to the companion object: e.g., MyClass.foo() resolves to companion object's foo; we can also refer to companion instance via MyClass.Companion. Also you can have only one companion per class.\n\n- The companion can be omitted name; default name Companion. It enables import of members without class qualifier (via import MyClass.Companion.*). Also used for extension functions on the class (declaring extensions on companion works as static-like). Also companion used for factory methods (like static methods in Java). It's also used for implementing interfaces and being used as a type.\n\n- Difference in lifecycle? both are lazily initialized when first accessed. Not much difference there.\n\n- Another difference: companion object can be declared `private` (or internal etc.) and also can have `@JvmStatic` etc.\n\n- Example: if you need a singleton that has a name to be accessed via qualified name from Kotlin or Java, you use object; use companion when you want to expose members as static-like, and you want to give the class the ability to have extensions based on companion, e.g., operator invoke for factory pattern.\n\nConcepts: nested object = a static final field of type MyClass$Holder. Companion object = also static final field (MyClass$Companion) plus compiler generated static members for members annotated w/@JvmStatic; also the presence of a default 'Companion' name; also provides a way to define static-like members without static keyword.\n\nExplain also: from Java, a nested object appears as a static final field holding the instance; you need to reference it via MyClass.Holder.INSTANCE; members are accessed via MyClass.Holder.INSTANCE.method() unless @JvmStatic. Companion object: accessible via MyClass.Companion (static field), and static members can be called as MyClass.foo() if annotated @JvmStatic. Kotlin treats companion object members as members of the containing class, allowing synthetic static calls. Also companion lost ability to contain a name; you can give a name but not required.\n\nHence answer: object inside class is just a regular static nested singleton, cannot be referred via class name directly. Companion object is syntactic sugar for static members & a place to put factory methods, it can be accessed without naming, there can be only one per class, and Kotlin treats its members as members of the class. Lifecycle same: lazy init.\n\nNow craft final answer: define differences, examples, usage. Provide also note about visibility, multiple objects per class vs only one companion.\n\nLet's craft final answer:\n\n- `object Holder` is a named singleton nested in MyClass. It is a static final field `Holder.INSTANCE` used to hold the only instance. To call something: `MyClass.Holder.someMethod()` in Kotlin; from Java `MyClass.Holder.INSTANCE.someMethod()`. You can have many such objects inside a class.\n\n- `companion object` is a special singleton that the compiler treats as the \"static\" part of the class. Its members can be accessed as if they were members of MyClass: `MyClass.someMethod()`. The instance can be referenced as `MyClass.Companion` (or the given name). Only one companion per class.\n\n- The companion object's members are not static in the JVM bytecode unless annotated with @JvmStatic; however Kotlin resolves calls through the companion automatically, providing static-like usage. When compiled, the companion generates a static field `Companion` and optionally static methods for those annotated @JvmStatic.\n\n- Differences: naming requirement, number allowed, visibility to Java, static interop, ability to define extension functions on the class via companion, ability to implement interfaces used as a type (object can also implement interface though). But both are lazily initialized.\n\n- Use companion when you want a place to put functions/properties that logically belong to the class (factory methods, constants) and you want to call them without qualifier, especially for Java interop. Use a regular object when you want an independent singleton nested in the class, maybe multiple, or you want to refer to it by name from Java or distinguish it.\n\nLet's craft final answer accordingly.</think>\n**`object` declared inside a class**\n\n```kotlin\nclass MyClass {\n    object Holder { \u2026 }      // a regular object\n}\n```\n\n* `Holder` is a *named* singleton.  \n* It is compiled as a **static final field** that holds the only instance of a\ngenerated class `MyClass$Holder`.  \n* It is accessed with its name:\n\n```kotlin\nMyClass.Holder.doSomething()      // Kotlin\nMyClass.Holder.INSTANCE.doSomething()   // Java\n```\n\n* You may have any number of such objects in a class (`Holder`,\n`Another`, \u2026).  \n* The members are not considered members of `MyClass`; they have to be\nqualified with `Holder`.\n\nThe instance is created lazily the first time it is accessed \u2013 the same as a\ncompanion object.\n\n---\n\n**`companion object`**\n\n```kotlin\nclass MyClass {\n    companion object { \u2026 }   // the companion (named Companion by default)\n}\n```\n\n* There can be **only one** companion per class.\n* Its instance is also a static final field (`Companion`), but Kotlin treats it\nas the *static part* of the class:\n  * Members can be called **as if they belong to the class itself**:\n\n    ```kotlin\n    MyClass.create()          // actually calls Companion.create()\n    ```\n\n  * The instance can be referred to explicitly as `MyClass.Companion`\n    (or with the name you give it).\n\n* For Java inter\u2011op the companion generates a static field `Companion`.  \n  Its members are **not static** on the JVM unless they are marked\n  `@JvmStatic`. With `@JvmStatic` the compiler also creates a static method\n  `MyClass.create()` that Java can call directly.\n\n* Because the compiler treats the companion\u2019s members as members of the\ncontaining class, they can be used for factory methods, constants, extension\nfunctions on the class, etc.\u2014the typical \u201cstatic\u2011like\u201d usage in Java.\n\nThe companion is also lazily initialized when first accessed.\n\n---\n\n### When to use which?\n\n|                     | `object` inside a class | `companion object` |\n|---------------------|------------------------|--------------------|\n| Number per class    | many possible          | at most one        |\n| Need a name to refer to it from Kotlin/Java? | yes (`Holder`) | not required (`MyClass.member`) |\n| Want to call members without qualifying with a name? | no | **yes** (`MyClass.foo()`) |\n| Want Java\u2011style static members (with `@JvmStatic`)? | no (needs qualifier) | **yes** |\n| Typical use case    | an independent singleton that just lives inside the outer class | \u201cstatic\u201d members, factory methods, constants, extensions that logically belong to the class |\n\nSo the main difference is *semantic*: a normal `object` is just a named singleton\nnested inside the class, whereas a `companion object` is the special singleton\nthat Kotlin treats as the class\u2019s static part, allowing its members to be used\nas if they were static members of the class. Their lifecycle (lazy\ninitialisation) is the same."}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "conte