    ```\n\n  * The instance can be referred to explicitly as `MyClass.Companion`\n    (or with the name you give it).\n\n* For Java inter\u2011op the companion generates a static field `Companion`.  \n  Its members are **not static** on the JVM unless they are marked\n  `@JvmStatic`. With `@JvmStatic` the compiler also creates a static method\n  `MyClass.create()` that Java can call directly.\n\n* Because the compiler treats the companion\u2019s members as members of the\ncontaining class, they can be used for factory methods, constants, extension\nfunctions on the class, etc.\u2014the typical \u201cstatic\u2011like\u201d usage in Java.\n\nThe companion is also lazily initialized when first accessed.\n\n---\n\n### When to use which?\n\n|                     | `object` inside a class | `companion object` |\n|---------------------|------------------------|--------------------|\n| Number per class    | many possible          | at most one        |\n| Need a name to refer to it from Kotlin/Java? | yes (`Holder`) | not required (`MyClass.member`) |\n| Want to call members without qualifying with a name? | no | **yes** (`MyClass.foo()`) |\n| Want Java\u2011style static members (with `@JvmStatic`)? | no (needs qualifier) | **yes** |\n| Typical use case    | an independent singleton that just lives inside the outer class | \u201cstatic\u201d members, factory methods, constants, extensions that logically belong to the class |\n\nSo the main difference is *semantic*: a normal `object` is just a named singleton\nnested inside the class, whereas a `companion object` is the special singleton\nthat Kotlin treats as the class\u2019s static part, allowing its members to be used\nas if they were static members of the class. Their lifecycle (lazy\ninitialisation) is the same."}], "q_num": 103, "originalconversations": ["What is the difference between an object and a companion object in a class in kotlin?\nExample:\n```class MyClass {\n    object Holder {\n        //something\n    }\n    companion object {\n        //something\n    }\n}\n```\nI already read that companion object shall be used, if the containing parameters/methods are closely related to its class.\nBut why is there also the possibility of declaring a normal object in the class? Because it behaves exactly like the companion, but it must have a name.\nIs there maybe a difference in its \"static\" (I'm from the java side) lifecycle? ", "There are two different types of ```object``` uses, expression and declaration.\nObject Expression\nAn object expression can be used when a class needs slight modification, but it's not necessary to create an entirely new subclass for it.  Anonymous inner classes are a good example of this.\n```button.setOnClickListener(object: View.OnClickListener() {\n    override fun onClick(view: View) {\n        // click event\n    }\n})\n```\nOne thing to watch out for is that anonymous inner classes can access variables from the enclosing scope, and these variables do not have to be ```final```.  This means that a variable used inside an anonymous inner class that is not considered ```final``` can change value unexpectedly before it is accessed.\nObject Declaration\nAn object declaration is similar to a variable declaration and therefore cannot be used on the right side of an assignment statement.  Object declarations are very useful for implementing the Singleton pattern.\n```object MySingletonObject {\n    fun getInstance(): MySingletonObject {\n        // return single instance of object\n    }\n}\n```\nAnd the ```getInstance``` method can then be invoked like this.\n```MySingletonObject.getInstance()\n```\nCompanion Object\nA companion object is a specific type of object declaration that allows an object to act similar to static objects in other languages (such as Java).  Adding ```companion``` to the object declaration allows for adding the &quot;static&quot; functionality to an object even though the actual static concept does not exist in Kotlin.  Here's an example of a class with instance methods and companion methods.\n```class MyClass {\n  companion object MyCompanionObject {\n    fun actsAsStatic() {\n      // do stuff\n    }\n  }\n  fun instanceMethod() {\n    // do stuff\n  }\n}\n```\nInvoking the instance method would look like this.\n```var myClass = MyClass()\nmyClass.instanceMethod()\n```\nInvoking the companion object method would look like this.\n```MyClass.actsAsStatic()\n```\nSee the Kotlin docs for more info."], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "What is the difference between an object and a companion object in a class in kotlin?\nExample:\n```class MyClass {\n    object Holder {\n        //something\n    }\n    companion object {\n        //something\n    }\n}\n```\nI already read that companion object shall be used, if the containing parameters/methods are closely related to its class.\nBut why is there also the possibility of declaring a normal object in the class? Because it behaves exactly like the companion, but it must have a name.\nIs there maybe a difference in its \"static\" (I'm from the java side) lifecycle? "}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "How to set button click effect in Android?"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks: \"How to set button click effect in Android?\" They need instructions. As per system, answer should be within <answer></answer> tags. Provide explanation of setting click effect (e.g., using selector XML, ripple, using style). Provide code snippets. Possibly include XML for selector, usage in layout, programmatic. Provide steps. Use proper formatting.</think>\n<answer>\n**How to Add a Click Effect to a Button in Android**\n\nAndroid provides several ways to give visual feedback when a user taps a `Button`. The most common approaches are:\n\n1. **Using a Ripple drawable (Material Design default)**\n2. **Using a custom selector drawable (state\u2011list)**\n3. **Applying an animation (scale, alpha, etc.) on click**\n\nBelow you\u2019ll find step\u2011by\u2011step instructions for each method, plus tips for using them with both XML layouts and programmatically in code.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Ripple Effect (Recommended for API\u202f21+)\n\nThe Ripple drawable is the standard \u201ctouch feedback\u201d introduced in Android\u202f5.0 (API\u202f21). It works automatically with the Material Components theme.\n\n### a) Add the Material Components theme (if not already)\n\n```xml\n<!-- res/values/themes.xml -->\n<resources>\n    <style name=\"AppTheme\" parent=\"Theme.MaterialComponents.DayNight.NoActionBar\">\n        <!-- Customize your theme here. -->\n    </style>\n</resources>\n```\n\n### b) Use a MaterialButton (or normal Button) in your layout\n\n```xml\n<com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton\n    android:id=\"@+id/btnRipple\"\n    android:layout_width=\"wrap_content\"\n    android:layout_height=\"wrap_content\"\n    android:text=\"Ripple Button\"\n    style=\"@style/Widget.MaterialComponents.Button\"\n    android:layout_margin=\"16dp\"/>\n```\n\n> **No extra XML is required** \u2013 the ripple is automatically drawn over the button\u2019s background.\n\nIf you need a custom background **and** a ripple, wrap the button in a `MaterialCardView` or use `android:foreground=\"?attr/selectableItemBackgroundBorderless\"` (for borderless) / `?attr/selectableItemBackground` (for default) on a `ViewGroup`.\n\n```xml\n<Button\n    android:id=\"@+id/btnCustomRipple\"\n    android:layout_width=\"wrap_content\"\n    android:layout_height=\"wrap_content\"\n    android:background=\"@drawable/custom_bg\"\n    android:foreground=\"?attr/selectableItemBackground\"\n    android:text=\"Custom Ripple\"/>\n```\n\n c) Optional: Change ripple color\n\nCreate a color state list (optional, otherwise theme\u2019s `colorControlHighlight` is used).\n\n```xml\n<!-- res/color/ripple_color.xml -->\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"utf-8\"?>\n<selector xmlns:android=\"http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android\">\n    <item android:color=\"@color/teal_200\"/>   <!-- pressed -->\n    <item android:color=\"@android:color/transparent\"/> <!-- default -->\n</selector>\n```\n\nApply it:\n\n```xml\n<Button\n    android:id=\"@+id/btnCustomRipple\"\n    \u2026\n    android:foreground=\"?attr/selectableItemBackground\"\n    android:foregroundTint=\"@color/ripple_color\"/>\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Custom Selector Drawable (Works on All API Levels)\n\nA selector is an XML file that defines different drawables for different view states (`pressed`, `focused`, `disabled`, etc.). This works on **any** Android version.\n\n### a) Create the selector file\n\n```xml\n<!-- res/drawable/btn_selector.xml -->\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"utf-8\"?>\n<selector xmlns:android=\"http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android\">\n\n    <!-- Pressed state -->\n    <item android:state_pressed=\"true\">\n        <shape android:shape=\"rectangle\">\n            <solid android:color=\"#FF4081\"/> <!-- pink when pressed -->\n            <corners android:radius=\"4dp\"/>\n        </shape>\n    </item>\n\n    <!-- Focused state (e.g., with D\u2011pad) -->\n    <item android:state_focused=\"true\">\n        <shape android:shape=\"rectangle\">\n            <solid android:color=\"#E91E63\"/>\n            <corners android:radius=\"4dp\"/>\n        </shape>\n    </item>\n\n    <!-- Default (normal) state -->\n    <item>\n        <shape android:shape=\"rectangle\">\n            <solid android:color=\"#6200EE\"/>\n            <corners android:radius=\"4dp\"/>\n        </shape>\n    </item>\n</selector>\n```\n\nYou can also reference image resources (`@drawable/pressed_img`) instead of shapes.\n\n### b) Apply the selector to the button\n\n```xml\n<Button\n    android:id=\"@+id/btnSelector\"\n    android:layout_width=\"wrap_content\"\n    android:layout_height=\"wrap_content\"\n    android:background=\"@drawable/btn_selector\"\n    android:text=\"Selector Button\"\n    android:textColor=\"@android:color/white\"\n    android:paddingStart=\"16dp\"\n    android:paddingEnd=\"16dp\"\n    android:layout_margin=\"16dp\"/>\n```\n\n### c) Optional: Add a ripple on top of the selector (API\u202f21+)\n\nIf you want the selector *and* a ripple:\n\n```xml\n<!-- res/drawable/btn_selector_with_ripple.xml -->\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"utf-8\"?>\n<ripple xmlns:android=\"http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android\"\n    android:color=\"?attr/colorControlHighlight\">\n    <item android:id=\"@android:id/mask\">\n        <shape android:shape=\"rectangle\">\n            <solid android:color=\"@android:color/white\"/>\n        </shape>\n    </item>\n\n    <item>\n        <selector android:constantSize=\"true\">\n            <!-- Same items as btn_selector.xml -->\n            <item android:state_pressed=\"true\">\n                <shape android:shape=\"rectangle\">\n                    <solid android:color=\"#FF4081\"/>\n                    <corners android:radius=\"4dp\"/>\n                </shape>\n            </item>\n            <item>\n                <shape android:shape=\"rectangle\">\n                    <solid android:color=\"#6200EE\"/>\n                    <corners android:radius=\"4dp\"/>\n                </shape>\n            </item>\n        </selector>\n    </item>\n</ripple>\n```\n\nUse `android:background=\"@drawable/btn_selector_withipple\"`.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Adding a Click Animation (Scale, Fade, etc.)\n\nSometimes you want a more dynamic effect than just a color change. You can animate the view when it receives a press event.\n\n### a) Define a scale animation resource\n\n```xml\n<!-- res/anim/button_scale_down.xml -->\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"utf-8\"?>\n<scale xmlns:android=\"http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android\"\n    android:duration=\"100\"\n    android:fromXScale=\"1.0\"\n    android:toXScale=\"0.95\"\n    android:fromYScale=\"1.0\"\n    android:toYScale=\"0.95\"\n    android:fillAfter=\"true\"/>\n```\n\n```xml\n<!-- res/anim/button_scale_up.xml -->\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"utf-8\"?>\n<scale xmlns:android=\"http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android\"\n    android:duration=\"100\"\n    android:fromXScale=\"0.95\"\n    android:toXScale=\"1.0\"\n    android:fromYScale=\"0.95\"\n    android:toYScale=\"1.0\"\n    android:fillAfter=\"true\"/>\n```\n\n### b) Apply the animation from code\n\n```kotlin\n// Kotlin\nval button = findViewById<Button>(R.id.btnAnimated)\n\nbutton.setOnTouchListener { v, event ->\n    when (event.action) {\n        MotionEvent.ACTION_DOWN -> v.startAnimation(\n            AnimationUtils.loadAnimation(this, R.anim.button_scale_down)\n        )\n        MotionEvent.ACTION_UP,\n        MotionEvent.ACTION_CANCEL -> v.startAnimation(\n            AnimationUtils.loadAnimation(this, R.anim.button_scale_up)\n        )\n    }\n    false // return false to let the click event propagate\n}\n```\n\nYou can also combine this with a selector or ripple for a richer experience.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Quick Summary Table\n\n| Requirement | Recommended Approach | Minimum API |\n|-------------|----------------------|--------------|\n| Material\u2011Design default ripple | `MaterialButton` + Material theme | 21 (ripple)\u202f\u2013\u202fworks on older versions via compatibility library |\n| Custom colors/shapes for all states | Selector drawable (`res/drawable/*.xml`) | 1 |\n| Ripple + custom background | `android:foreground=\"?attr/selectableItemBackground\"` (orripple` wrapper) | 21 |\n| Animated press feedback (scale, fade) | XML animation + `OnTouchListener` | 1 |\n| Backward\u2011compatible themed ripple | Use `AppCompatButton` with `android:background=\"?attr/selectableItemBackground\"` | 1 (via AppCompat) |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Full Example (Kotlin + XML)\n\n**layout.xml**  \n\n```xml\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"utf-8\"?>\n<LinearLayout xmlns:android=\"http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android\"\n    xmlns:app=\"http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto\"\n    android:orientation=\"vertical\"\n    android:gravity=\"center\"\n    android:layout_width=\"match_parent\"\n    android:layout_height=\"match_parent\">\n\n    <!-- 1\ufe0f\u20e3 Ripple (MaterialButton) -->\n    <com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton\n        android:id=\"@+id/btnRipple"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How to set button click effect in Android?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks: \"How to set button click effect in Android?\" They need instructions. As per system, answer should be within <answer></answer> tags. Provide explanation of setting click effect (e.g., using selector XML, ripple, using style). Provide code snippets. Possibly include XML for selector, usage in layout, programmatic. Provide steps. Use proper formatting.</think>\n<answer>\n**How to Add a Click Effect to a Button in Android**\n\nAndroid provides several ways to give visual feedback when a user taps a `Button`. The most common approaches are:\n\n1. **Using a Ripple drawable (Material Desi