lower bound.  \n* It tells the reader that the given function describes the *exact* asymptotic growth, up to constant factors.\n\n**Example**  \n\nMergesort performs at most \\(c_{2} n\\log n\\) operations and at least \\(c_{1} n\\log n\\) operations, so  \n\n\\[\nT_{\\text{mergesort}}(n)=\\Theta(n\\log n).\n\\]\n\nBecause we have a matching upper and lower bound, we know no comparison\u2011based sorting algorithm can be asymptotically faster.\n\n---\n\n## 4.  \\(o\\) \u2013 *strict* upper bound  \n\n**Definition**  \n\\(T(n)=o(f(n))\\) iff for **every** constant \\(c>0\\) there exists \\(n_{0}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n0\\le T(n) < c\\,f(n) \\qquad\\forall n\\ge n_{0}.\n\\]\n\nEquivalently, \\(\\displaystyle \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac{T(n)}{f(n)}=0\\).\n\n**When to use it**\n\n* To say that an algorithm is *asymptotically better* than a given bound, not merely \u201cno worse\u201d.  \n* Useful when comparing two algorithms where both satisfy an \\(O\\) bound but one is strictly faster.  \n\n**Example**  \n\nStrassen\u2019s matrix\u2011multiplication algorithm runs in \\(O(n^{\\log_{2}7})\\). Since \\(\\log_{2}7\\approx2.81<3\\),\n\n"}], "q_num": 69, "originalconversations": ["In most introductory algorithm classes, notations like $O$ (Big O) and $\\Theta$ are introduced, and a student would typically learn to use one of these to find the time complexity.\nHowever, there are other notations, such as $o$, $\\Omega$ and $\\omega$. Are there any specific scenarios where one notation would be preferable to another?", "You are referring to the Landau notation. They are not different symbols for the same thing but have entirely different meanings. Which one is \"preferable\" depends entirely on the desired statement.\n$f \\in \\cal{O}(g)$ means that $f$ grows at most as fast as $g$, asymptotically and up to a constant factor; think of it as a $\\leq$. $f \\in o(g)$ is the stricter form, i.e. $\n$f \\in \\Omega(g)$ has the symmetric meaning: $f$ grows at least as fast as $g$. $\\omega$ is its stricter cousin. You can see that $f \\in \\Omega(g)$ is equivalent to $g \\in \\cal{O}(f)$.\n$f \\in \\Theta(g)$ means that $f$ grows about as fast as $g$; formally $f \\in \\cal{O}(g) \\cap \\Omega(g)$. $f \\sim g$ (asymptotic equality) is its stronger form. We often mean $\\Theta$ when we use $\\cal{O}$.\nNote how $\\cal{O}(g)$ and its siblings are function classes. It is important to be very aware of this and their precise definitions -- which can differ depending on who is talking -- when doing \"arithmetics\" with them. \nWhen proving things, take care to work with your precise definition. There are many definitions for Landau symbols around (all with the same basic intuition), some of which are equivalent on some sets on functions but not on others.\n\nIf you are interested in using Landau notation in a rigorous and sound manner, you may be interested in a work by Rutanen et al.. They formulate necessary and sufficient criteria for asymptotic notation as we use them in algorithmics, show that the common definition fails to meet them and provide a (the, in fact) workable definition.\n\n* A general definition of the O-notation for algorithm analysis by K. Rutanen et al. (2015)\n"], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "In most introductory algorithm classes, notations like $O$ (Big O) and $\\Theta$ are introduced, and a student would typically learn to use one of these to find the time complexity.\nHowever, there are other notations, such as $o$, $\\Omega$ and $\\omega$. Are there any specific scenarios where one notation would be preferable to another?"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "In monopoly, can an opponent put a property up for auction at a higher price than I have in cash?"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer the question: In Monopoly, can an opponent put a property up for auction at a higher price than the player has in cash? So answering about the rules: When a property is not purchased from the bank after landing on it, the banker must auction it to the highest bidder. The auction has no set minimum price; it can start at any amount, often $1. Players can bid any amount they can pay, but you can bid beyond your cash? In official rules: \"The bank may decide to force a player to pay with a mortgage or have to borrow,\" but generally you cannot bid more than you have cash; if you win, you must pay immediately. So you cannot bid an amount that exceeds your cash on hand (including any cash from selling houses, etc.) However you could theoretically raise funds by mortgaging properties before the auction or selling houses, but you can only bid up to what you can cover at the moment of the auction. Some house rules allow \"bid up to 2x your cash\" etc., but official rules do not. In official rules, the auction is just an auction: the player who wins must pay the amount immediately, using cash or assets (mortgaging, selling houses). So you can owe money after the auction? Actually standard rules: \"The player who wins the auction pays the bank the price and takes title.\" The bank does not allow debt; you must have enough cash (or liquidate assets). So answer: No, you cannot bid more than available cash unless you liquidate assets before finalizing the bid, but you cannot exceed total assets. So answer: No\u2014bidding is limited by the amount you can actually pay, but you can mortgage or sell to raise cash. Clarify that auctioneer can set a starting bid; normally at $1.\n\nThus answer: According to official Monopoly rules (Hasbro), an opponent cannot set a price higher than a player's cash? Actually the question: \"can an opponent put a property up for auction at a higher price than I have in cash?\" Perhaps means: Can they set a starting bid above your cash? Eg. you have $200 cash, opponent proposes auction starting at $300. In official rules, auction starts at any price, but players can only bid what they can pay; if you cannot meet the starting price, you are effectively out. So you cannot be forced to buy at price you can't cover. So answer: The auctioneer cannot force a player to buy at a price above his cash, but they can start the bidding at a high amount that may be unattainable for you, which would effectively exclude you. However rulebook states start price is $1. But house rules vary. Official: \"If the property is not purchased, it is auctioned by the bank.\" It does not specify a minimum. The bank can start at any price, typically $1. But you cannot bid above your cash.\n\nThus answer: No, you cannot be forced to pay more than you can cover; however the opponent can set a high starting bid that you cannot meet, which may block you from winning. Further nuance: If you have assets, you may mortgage to raise cash before paying. So answer accordingly.\n\nWill produce final answer with explanation and reference to official rulebook.</think>\n**Short answer:**\u202fNo. In an official Monopoly auction you may only win the property if you can actually pay the winning bid \u2013 you cannot be forced to pay more than you have in cash (or can raise by mortgaging or selling houses) at the moment the auction ends.\n\n---\n\n### How the official rules work\n\n1. **When an auction occurs**  \n   *If a player lands on an unowned property and decides not to buy it from the bank, the rules state that \u201cthe Bank shall immediately auction the property to the highest bidder.\u201d*  \n   The auction is conducted by the banker (or the player who just landed on the space) and proceeds like any other auction: bids are placed in turn, each higher than the last, until no one wishes to raise the price.\n\n2. **There is no set \u201cminimum price\u201d**  \n   The rules do **not** prescribe a minimum bid. In practice most groups start the bidding at $1, but the banker may choose any opening amount. House\u2011rules often impose a minimum (e.g., $1 or $10) but the official Hasbro rules leave it to the banker\u2019s discretion.\n\n3. **Payment must be immediate and complete**  \n   The winning bidder **must pay the bank the amount of the winning bid right then**. The rulebook says:  \n\n   > \u201cThe player who wins the auction pays the Bank the price of the bid and takes title to the property.\u201d  \n\n   The game does not allow a player to go into debt to the bank. If the player does not have enough cash on hand, he must first raise the necessary cash by:\n\n   * mortgaging (or selling) his own properties,\n   * selling houses/hotels, or\n   * any other legal transaction that generates cash **before** the payment is made.\n\n   Only after he has the required cash can he complete the purchase.\n\n4. **Consequences of an unaffordable opening bid**  \n   Because the banker can set the opening bid at any amount, it is possible for an opponent (acting as the banker) to start the auction at a level that you cannot meet. If you cannot meet the opening bid, you are simply out of the auction \u2013 you do **not** have to buy at a price you cannot afford. This is the only way an opponent can \u201cprice you out\u201d of a property.\n\n---\n\n### What this means for the specific question\n\n* **Can an opponent dictate a price that is higher than your cash and force you to pay it?**  \n  **No.** The auction closes only when the highest bidder is able to pay the amount he has offered. If you cannot meet the current bid (including the opening bid), you simply do not win the auction.\n\n* **Can an opponent start the bidding at a price you cannot meet?**  \n  Yes. The opening bid can be any amount the banker chooses. If it exceeds the cash you currently have (and you cannot raise more by mortgaging or selling houses), you are effectively excluded from the auction, but you are not compelled to pay that amount.\n\n* **What can you do if you really want the property?**  \n  Before the auction ends you may mortgage a property, sell houses, or otherwise liquidate assets to raise the needed cash. As long as you can produce the cash **before** the banker collects payment, the bid is valid.\n\n---\n\n### Quick checklist for a player during an auction\n\n| Situation | Action required |\n|-----------|-----------------|\n| You are the highest bidder | Verify you have enough cash (or can generate it by mortgaging/selling) before the banker demands payment. |\n| You cannot meet the opening bid | You are out of the auction; you do not have to buy the property. |\n| The banker announces a very high opening bid you can\u2019t afford | You may still stay in the auction if you can raise cash *before* the bid is closed; otherwise you simply wait for a lower bid from another player. |\n| You win with a bid higher than your cash on hand | You must first liquidate assets to cover the payment; otherwise you must relinquish the win (the auction then continues with the next highest bid). |\n\n---\n\n### Bottom line\n\n- **You can only be forced to pay a price you can actually cover.**  \n- **Opponents can set a high opening bid, which may shut you out of the auction, but they cannot make you purchase at a price you cannot meet.**  \n\nIf you ever find a group playing a rule that allows a player to \u201cowe\u201d the bank for an auction win, that is a *house rule* and not part of the official Monopoly rules."}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "In monopoly, can an opponent put a property up for auction at a higher price than I have in cash?"}, {"role": "assistant"