ting bid above your cash? Eg. you have $200 cash, opponent proposes auction starting at $300. In official rules, auction starts at any price, but players can only bid what they can pay; if you cannot meet the starting price, you are effectively out. So you cannot be forced to buy at price you can't cover. So answer: The auctioneer cannot force a player to buy at a price above his cash, but they can start the bidding at a high amount that may be unattainable for you, which would effectively exclude you. However rulebook states start price is $1. But house rules vary. Official: \"If the property is not purchased, it is auctioned by the bank.\" It does not specify a minimum. The bank can start at any price, typically $1. But you cannot bid above your cash.\n\nThus answer: No, you cannot be forced to pay more than you can cover; however the opponent can set a high starting bid that you cannot meet, which may block you from winning. Further nuance: If you have assets, you may mortgage to raise cash before paying. So answer accordingly.\n\nWill produce final answer with explanation and reference to official rulebook.</think>\n**Short answer:**\u202fNo. In an official Monopoly auction you may only win the property if you can actually pay the winning bid \u2013 you cannot be forced to pay more than you have in cash (or can raise by mortgaging or selling houses) at the moment the auction ends.\n\n---\n\n### How the official rules work\n\n1. **When an auction occurs**  \n   *If a player lands on an unowned property and decides not to buy it from the bank, the rules state that \u201cthe Bank shall immediately auction the property to the highest bidder.\u201d*  \n   The auction is conducted by the banker (or the player who just landed on the space) and proceeds like any other auction: bids are placed in turn, each higher than the last, until no one wishes to raise the price.\n\n2. **There is no set \u201cminimum price\u201d**  \n   The rules do **not** prescribe a minimum bid. In practice most groups start the bidding at $1, but the banker may choose any opening amount. House\u2011rules often impose a minimum (e.g., $1 or $10) but the official Hasbro rules leave it to the banker\u2019s discretion.\n\n3. **Payment must be immediate and complete**  \n   The winning bidder **must pay the bank the amount of the winning bid right then**. The rulebook says:  \n\n   > \u201cThe player who wins the auction pays the Bank the price of the bid and takes title to the property.\u201d  \n\n   The game does not allow a player to go into debt to the bank. If the player does not have enough cash on hand, he must first raise the necessary cash by:\n\n   * mortgaging (or selling) his own properties,\n   * selling houses/hotels, or\n   * any other legal transaction that generates cash **before** the payment is made.\n\n   Only after he has the required cash can he complete the purchase.\n\n4. **Consequences of an unaffordable opening bid**  \n   Because the banker can set the opening bid at any amount, it is possible for an opponent (acting as the banker) to start the auction at a level that you cannot meet. If you cannot meet the opening bid, you are simply out of the auction \u2013 you do **not** have to buy at a price you cannot afford. This is the only way an opponent can \u201cprice you out\u201d of a property.\n\n---\n\n### What this means for the specific question\n\n* **Can an opponent dictate a price that is higher than your cash and force you to pay it?**  \n  **No.** The auction closes only when the highest bidder is able to pay the amount he has offered. If you cannot meet the current bid (including the opening bid), you simply do not win the auction.\n\n* **Can an opponent start the bidding at a price you cannot meet?**  \n  Yes. The opening bid can be any amount the banker chooses. If it exceeds the cash you currently have (and you cannot raise more by mortgaging or selling houses), you are effectively excluded from the auction, but you are not compelled to pay that amount.\n\n* **What can you do if you really want the property?**  \n  Before the auction ends you may mortgage a property, sell houses, or otherwise liquidate assets to raise the needed cash. As long as you can produce the cash **before** the banker collects payment, the bid is valid.\n\n---\n\n### Quick checklist for a player during an auction\n\n| Situation | Action required |\n|-----------|-----------------|\n| You are the highest bidder | Verify you have enough cash (or can generate it by mortgaging/selling) before the banker demands payment. |\n| You cannot meet the opening bid | You are out of the auction; you do not have to buy the property. |\n| The banker announces a very high opening bid you can\u2019t afford | You may still stay in the auction if you can raise cash *before* the bid is closed; otherwise you simply wait for a lower bid from another player. |\n| You win with a bid higher than your cash on hand | You must first liquidate assets to cover the payment; otherwise you must relinquish the win (the auction then continues with the next highest bid). |\n\n---\n\n### Bottom line\n\n- **You can only be forced to pay a price you can actually cover.**  \n- **Opponents can set a high opening bid, which may shut you out of the auction, but they cannot make you purchase at a price you cannot meet.**  \n\nIf you ever find a group playing a rule that allows a player to \u201cowe\u201d the bank for an auction win, that is a *house rule* and not part of the official Monopoly rules."}], "q_num": 379, "originalconversations": ["In monopoly, can an opponent put a property up for auction at a higher price than I have in cash?", "This is definitely allowed, and a good tactic to leverage a cash advantage.\nThere is no minimum or maximum price for an auction.  The rules state:\n\nBidding may start at any price.\n\nMonopoly auctions do not have &quot;the bidding will start at $10&quot;.  They just have &quot;bidding is open&quot;.  Once the auction begins, a bid by any player sets the minimum price for the auction at that bid.  Your opponent could easily bid $10 on the property, forcing you to either let them have it for $10 or bid more than you have in cash.  You could bid first, say starting the bid at $5, but that doesn't meaningfully change the situation, as they can just respond by out-bidding you at $10.\n\nYou may bid more money than you have in cash, but you are still obligated to pay it if you win the auction.  This may be a reasonable play if you can afford it by mortgaging property and/or selling houses, especially to drive up the price for your opponent.  But be careful not to bid  more than you can pay after mortgages and sales, as if you win the auction and can't pay your bid, you go bankrupt.  In particular, if you bid more than you can afford, you opponents can all just let you win the bid, at which point you go bankrupt and the property you just bought would go up for auction again (in addition to all your other properties)."], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "In monopoly, can an opponent put a property up for auction at a higher price than I have in cash?"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "Quantum state teleportation is the quantum information protocol where a qubit is transferred between two parties using an initial shared entangled state, Bell measurement, classical communication and local rotation. Apparently, there is also something called quantum gate teleportation.\nWhat is quantum gate teleportation and what is it used for?\nI am particularly interested in possible applications in simulating quantum circuits."}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer: What is quantum gate teleportation and what is it used for? Particularly applications in simulating quantum circuits. Provide explanation, maybe include description of teleportation of gates, using teleportation to implement non-Clifford gates, fault-tolerant quantum computing, measurement-based quantum computation, gate teleportation in circuit simulation (e.g., using gate teleportation for magic state injection, resource saving, remote implementation). Should discuss the protocol: teleport a gate onto a state by preparing entangled resource state that encodes the gate, performing Bell measurement, applying corrections possibly depending on measurement outcomes; used for fault-tolerant universal quantum computing, especially for gates that are hard to implement transversally. Also used in distributed quantum computing, quantum networks.\n\nFocus on simulation: gate teleportation can be used to simulate arbitrary circuits with fewer entangling gates, or to classically simulate circuits with Clifford+T using Gottesman\u2013Knill extended with magic state injection. Also gate teleportation is central to measurement-based quantum computing (MBQC) which can be viewed as teleporting gates through a cluster state. Applications to simulate circuits by preparing specific resource states (cluster, graph states) and then measuring, effectively teleporting gates.\n\nWill produce answer inside <answer></answer> tags. Provide references maybe.\n\nLet's craft a comprehensive answer.\n\n</think>\n<answer>\n**Quantum gate teleportation \u2013 what it is**\n\nQuantum gate teleportation is a protocol that \u201cmoves\u2019\u2019 a quantum gate (a unitary operation) from one location to another without applying the gate directly to the target qubits.  \nThe idea is the same building\u2011block as ordinary state teleportation:\n\n1. **Prepare a special entangled resource state** that already contains the gate you want to apply.  \n2. **Perform a joint Bell\u2011type measurement** on the input qubit(s) together with part of the resource state.  \n3. **Communicate the classical measurement outcomes** to the receiver.  \n4. The receiver **applies a simple (usually Pauli) correction** that depends on the measurement results.  \n   After the correction the remote qubit has undergone the desired unitary, even though the gate itself was never applied locally.\n\nMathematically, if the resource state is \\(|\\Phi_U\\rangle = (I\\otimes U)|\\Phi^+\\rangle\\) (where \\(|\\Phi^+\\rangle = (|00\\rangle+|11\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\(U\\) is the gate to be teleported), the standard teleportation circuit transfers the unknown state \\(|\\psi\\rangle\\) from Alice to Bob while inserting the gate \\(U\\) on Bob\u2019s side:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c}\n\\Qcircuit @C=1em @R=1em {\n\\lstick{| \\psi \\rangle} & \\qw & \\gate{U} & \\qw \\\\\n\\lstick{| \\Phi^+ \\rangle} & \\qw & \\qw & \\qw\n}\n\\end{array}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\begin{array}{c}\n\\Qcircuit @C=1em @R=1em {\n\\lstick{| \\psi \\rangle} & \\qw & \\targ & \\qw & \\measureD{Bell} & \\cwx[1] \\\\\n\\lstick{| \\Phi_U \\rangle} & \\qw & \\ctrl{-1} & \\qw &  & \\\\\n\\lstick{\\text{Bob}} & \\qw & \\gate{X^a Z^b}& \\qw\n}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((a,b)\\) are the two classical bits obtained from the Bell measurement and the final correction \\(\\,X^a Z^b\\) is a Pauli operator.\n\n---\n\n### Why is it useful?\n\n| **Motivation** | **How gate teleportation helps** |\n|----------------|-----------------------------------|\n| **Fault\u2011tolerant quantum computing** | Many error\u2011correcting codes (e.g. the surface code) allow a *restricted* set of transversal (hence easily protected) gates, typically the Clifford group. Non\u2011Clifford gates such as the \\(T\\) (\u03c0/8) gate are not transversal. By preparing a special **magic state** \\(|\\mathrm{magic}_U\\rangle = (I\\otimes U)|\\Phi^+\\rangle\\) and teleporting the gate, the non\u2011Clifford operation can be implemented using only Clifford operations, a Bell measurement, and a Pauli correction. This is the core of *magic\u2011state injection* and the *Bravyi\u2013Kitaev* fault\u2011tolerant scheme. |\n| **Distributed quantum computing / quantum networks** | If two distant nodes each hold a logical qubit, a two\u2011qubit gate can be realized without moving the qubits physically: each node locally creates a resource state, they perform Bell measurements, exchange classical bits, and apply corrections. This avoids long\u2011range coherent interactions. |\n| **Measurement\u2011Based Quantum Computing (MBQC)** | In the cluster\u2011state model, a computation proceeds by **teleporting** logical qubits through a highly entangled resource (graph) state. Each measurement \u201cinjects\u2019\u2019 a single\u2011qubit gate; the pattern of measurements effectively teleports the gate onto the remaining part of the graph. Hence MBQC is essentially **gate teleportation on steroids**. |\n| **Circuit simplification & depth reduction** | By pre\u2011computing complex multi\u2011qubit unitaries as entangled resource states, one can replace a deep sequence of gates by a *single* teleportation step plus cheap Pauli corrections. This can be advantageous when the underlying hardware has a limited native gate set or suffers from large two\u2011qubit gate errors. |\n| **Quantum simulation of circuits** | Many classical simulation algorithms (e.g. stabilizer\u202f+\u202fmagic\u2011state methods) treat a circuit as a sequence of Clifford operations punctuated by magic\u2011state injections. Gate teleportation provides the exact mathematical framework for representing those injections, enabling efficient *hybrid* simulation strategies. |\n\n---\n\n### Gate teleportation in **simulating quantum circuits**\n\n1. **Stabilizer\u202f+\u202fmagic\u2011state simulation**  \n   - The Gottesman\u2013Knill theorem tells us that pure stabilizer circuits (Clifford\u202f+\u202fstate preparation\u202f+\u202fPauli measurement) can be simulated in polynomial time.  \n   - To simulate a universal circuit we supplement the stabilizer simulator with *magic states* (e.g. \\(|T\\rangle = (|0\\rangle + e^{i\\pi/4}|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\)).  \n   - Each time a non\u2011Clifford gate \\(U\\) appears, we reinterpret it as a teleportation of \\(U\\) using a pre\u2011prepared magic state \\(|\\Phi_U\\rangle\\). The simulator then proceeds by:  \n     a. Consuming the magic state (adding it to the stabilizer description),  \n     b. Performing a simulated Bell measurement (which introduces a random Pauli correction),  \n     c. Updating the stabilizer tableau accordingly.  \n   - This approach underpins the **stabilizer\u202f+\u202fMonte\u2011Carlo (or \u201cstabilizer rank\u201d)** simulation techniques used to benchmark near\u2011term quantum advantage experiments.\n\n2. **Tensor\u2011network / PEPS methods**  \n   - In tensor\u2011network simulations of quantum circuits, a two\u2011qubit gate is a tensor of rank\u20114 that can increase bond dimension dramatically.  \n   - By *teleporting* the gate, one replaces the tensor with a *pre\u2011contracted* resource tensor (the entangled state \\(|\\Phi_U\\rangle\\)) and a pair of rank\u20112 tensors (Bell measurement and Pauli correction).  \n   - The bond dimension grows only by the dimension of the Bell measurement (typically 2), which can be advantageous for deep circuits with many non\u2011Clifford gates.\n\n3. **Circuit compilation for NISQ devices**  \n   - Suppose a hardware platform only natively implements a limited gate set \\(\\{R_Z(\\theta),\\text{CNOT}\\}\\). A non\u2011native gate \\(U\\) can be compiled as:  \n     a. Prepare the ancilla resource \\(|\\Phi_U\\rangle\\) using the native gates (often via a short \u201cgate synthesis\u201d subcircuit).  \n     b. Perform a Bell measurement between the ancilla and the data qubit.  \n     c. Apply a cheap Pauli correction (which can be merged into later gates).  \n   - In a *simulation* of a NISQ algorithm, this decomposition lets the simulator count separately the cost of resource\u2011state preparation (which may be expensive) and the cheap measurement\u2011correction step, giving a more realistic resource estimate.\n\n4. **Remote circuit simulation**  \n   - In distributed simulation (e.g., when parts of the circuit are simulated on different classical nodes), gate teleportation provides a clean protocol to **move** a gate from one node to another using only classical communication and a shared entangled buffer.  \n   - This mirrors the *Schr\u00f6dinger\u2013Feynman* hybrid approach where each node simulates a local stabilizer subcircuit and swaps non\u2011local gates via teleportation.\n\n---\n\n### A concrete example: teleporting a \\(T\\) gate\n\nThe most common non\u2011Clifford gate used in fault\u2011tolerant schemes is the \\(T = \\operatorname{diag}(1, e^{i\\pi/4})\\) gate.\n\n1. **Resource state**  \n   \\[\n   |\\Phi_T\\rangle = (I\\otimes T)\\,|\\Phi^+\\rangle\n                = \\"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Quantum state teleportation is the quantum information protocol where a qubit is transferred between two parties using an initial shared entangled state, Bell measurement, classical communication and local rotation. Apparently, there is also something called quantum gate teleportation.\nWhat is quantum gate teleportation and what is it used for?\nI am particularly interested in possible applications in simulating quantum circuits."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer: What is quantum gate teleportation and what is it used for? Particularly applications in simulating quantum circuits. Provide explanation, maybe include description of teleportation of gates, using teleportation to implement non-Clifford gates, fault-tolerant quantum computing, measurement-based quantum computation, gate teleportation in circuit simulation (e.g., using gate teleportation for magic state injection, resource saving, remote implementation). Should discuss the protocol: teleport a gate onto a state by preparing entangled resource state that encodes the gate, performing Bell measurement, applying corrections possibly depending on measurement outcomes; used for fault-tolerant universal quantum computing, especially for gates that are hard to implement transversally. Also used in distributed quantum computing, quantum networks.\n\nFocus on simulation: gate teleportation can be used to simulate arbitrary circuits with fewer entangling gates, or to classically simulate circuits with Clifford+T using Gottesman\u2013Knill extended with magic state injection. Also gate teleportation is central to measurement-based quantum computing (MBQC) which can be viewed as teleporting gates through a cluster state. Applications to simulate circuits by preparing specific resource states (cluster, graph states) and then measuring, effectively teleporting gates.\n\nWill produce answer inside <answer></answer> tags. Provide references maybe.\n\nLet's craft a comprehensive answer.\n\n</think>\n<answer>\n**Quantum gate teleportation \u2013 what it is**\n\nQuantum gate teleportation is a protocol that \u201cmoves\u2019\u2019 a quantum gate (a unitary operation) from one location to another without applying the gate directly to the target qubits.  \nThe idea is the same building\u2011block as ordinary state teleportation:\n\n1. **Prepare a special entangled resource state** that already contains the gate you want to apply.  \n2. **Perform a joint Bell\u2011type measurement** on the input qubit(s) together with part of the resource state.  \n3. **Communicate the classical measurement outcomes** to the receiver.  \n4. The receiver **applies a simple (usually Pauli) correction** that depends on the measurement results.  \n   After the correction the remote qubit has undergone the desired unitary, even though the gate itself was never applied locally.\n\nMathematically, if the resource state is \\(|\\Phi_U\\rangle = (I\\otimes U)|\\Phi^+\\rangle\\) (where \\(|\\Phi^+\\rangle = (|00\\rangle+|11\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\(U\\) is the gate to be teleported), the standard teleportation circuit transfers the unknown state \\(|\\psi\\rangle\\) from Alice to Bob while inserting the gate \\(U\\) on Bob\u2019s side:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c}\n\\Qcircuit @C=1em @R=1em {\n\\lstick{| \\psi \\rangle} & \\qw & \\gate{U} & \\qw \\\\\n\\lstick{| \\Phi^+ \\rangle} & \\qw & \\qw & \\qw\n}\n\\end{array}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\begin{array}{c}\n\\Qcircuit @C=1em @R=1em {\n\\lstick{| \\psi \\rangle} & \\qw & \\targ & \\qw & \\measureD{Bell} & \\cwx[1] \\\\\n\\lstick{| \\Phi_U \\rangle} & \\qw & \\ctrl{-1} & \\qw &  & \\\\\n\\lstick{\\text{Bob}} & \\qw & \\gate{X^a Z^b}& \\qw\n}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((a,b)\\) are the two classical bits obtained from the Bell measurement and the final correction \\(\\,X^a Z^b\\) is a Pauli operator.\n\n---\n\n### Why is it useful?\n\n| **Motivation** | **How gate teleportation helps** |\n|----------------|-----------------------------------|\n| **Fault\u2011tolerant quantum computing** | Many error\u2011correcting codes (e.g. the surface code) allow a *restricted* set of transversal (hence easily protected) gates, typically the Clifford group. Non\u2011Clifford gates such as the \\(T\\) (\u03c0/8) gate are not transversal. By preparing a special **magic state** \\(|\\mathrm{magic}_U\\rangle = (I\\otimes U)|\\Phi^+\\rangle\\) and teleporting the gate, the non\u2011Clifford operation can be implemented using only Clifford operations, a Bell measurement, and a Pauli correction. This is the core of *magic\u2011state injection* and the *Bravyi\u2013Kitaev* fault\u2011tolerant scheme. |\n| **Distributed quantum computing / quantum networks** | If two distant nodes each hold a logical qubit, a two\u2011qubit gate can be realized without moving the qubits physically: each node locally creates a resource state, they perform Bell measurements, exchange classical bits, and apply corrections. This avoids long\u2011range coherent interactions. |\n| **Measurement\u2011Based Quantum Computing (MBQC)** | In the cluster\u2011state model, a computation proceeds by **teleporting** logical qubits through a highly entangled resource (graph) state. Each measurement \u201cinjects\u2019\u2019 a single\u2011qubit gate; the pattern of measurements effectively teleports the gate onto the remaining part of the graph. Hence MBQC is essentially **gate teleportation on steroids**. |\n| **Circuit simplification & depth reduction** | By pre\u2011computing complex multi\u2011qubit unitaries as entangled resource states, one can replace a deep sequence of gates by a *single* teleportation step plus cheap Pauli corrections. This can be advantageous when the underlying hardware has a limited native gate set or suffers from large two\u2011qubit gate errors. |\n| **Quantum simulation of circuits** | Many classical simulation algorithms (e.g. stabilizer\u202f+\u202fmagic\u2011state methods) treat a circuit as a sequence of Clifford operations punctuated by magic\u2011state injections. Gate teleportation provides the exact mathematical framework for representing those injections, enabling efficient *hybrid* simulation strategies. |\n\n---\n\n### Gate teleportation in **simulating quantum circuits**\n\n1. **Stabilizer\u202f+\u202fmagic\u2011state simulation**  \n   - The Gottesman\u2013Knill theorem tells us that pure stabilizer circuits (Clifford\u202f+\u202fstate preparation\u202f+\u202fPauli measurement) can be simulated in polynomial time.  \n   - To simulate a universal circuit we supplement the stabilizer simulator with *magic states* (e.g. \\(|T\\rangle = (|0\\rangle + e^{i\\pi/4}|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\)).  \n   - Each time a non\u2011Clifford gate \\(U\\) appears, we reinterpret it as a teleportation of \\(U\\) using a pre\u2011prepared magic state \\(|\\Phi_U\\rangle\\). The simulator then proceeds by:  \n     a. Consuming the magic state (adding it to the stabilizer description),  \n     b. Performing a simulated Bell measurement (which introduces a random Pauli correction),  \n     c. Updating the stabilizer tableau accordingly.  \n   - This approach underpins the **stabilizer\u202f+\u202fMonte\u2011Carlo (or \u201cstabilizer rank\u201d)** simulation techniques used to benchmark near\u2011term quantum advantage experiments.\n\n2. **Tensor\u2011network / PEPS methods**  \n   - In tensor\u2011network simulations of quantum circuits, a two\u2011qubit gate is a tensor of rank\u20114 that can increase bond dimension dramatically.  \n   - By *teleporting* the gate, one replaces the tensor with a *pre\u2011contracted* resource tensor (the entangled state \\(|\\Phi_U\\rangle\\)) and a pair of rank\u20112 tensors (Bell measurement and Pauli correction).  \n   - The bond dimension grows only by the dimension of the Bell measurement (typically 2), which can be advantageous for deep circuits with many non\u2011Clifford gates.\n\n3. **Circuit compilation for NISQ devices**  \n   - Suppose a hardware platform only natively implements a limited gate set \\(\\{R_Z(\\theta),\\text{CNOT}\\}\\). A non\u2011native gate \\(U\\) can be compiled as:  \n     a. Prepare the ancilla resource \\(|\\Phi_U\\rangle\\) using the native gates (often via a short \u201cgate synthesis\u201d subcircuit).  \n     b. Perform a Bell measurement between the ancilla and the data qubit.  \n     c. Apply a cheap Pauli correction (which can be merged into later gates).  \n   - In a *simulation* of a NISQ algorithm, this decomposition lets the simulator count separately the cost of resource\u2011state preparation (which may be expensive) and the cheap measurement\u2011correction step, giving a more realistic resource estimate.\n\n4. **Remote circuit simulation**  \n   - In distributed simulation (e.g., when parts of the circuit are simulated on different classical nodes), gate teleportation provides a clean protocol to **move** a gate from one node to another using only classical communication and a shared entangled buffer.  \n   - This mirrors the *Schr\u00f6dinger\u2013Feynman* hybrid approach where each node simulates a local stabilizer subcircuit and swaps non\u2011local gates via teleportation.\n\n---\n\n### A concrete example: teleporting a \\(T\\) gate\n\nThe most common non\u2011Clifford gate used in fault\u2011tolerant schemes is the \\(T = \\operatorname{diag}(1, e^{i\\pi/4})\\) gate.\n\n1. **Resource state**  \n   \\[\n   |\\Phi_T\\rangle = (I\\otimes T)\\,|\\Phi^+\\rangle\n                = \\"}], "q_num": 277, "originalconversations": ["Quantum state teleportation is the quantum information protocol where a qubit is transferred between two parties using an initial shared entangled state, Bell measurement, classical communication and local rotation. Apparently, there is also something called quantum gate teleportation.\nWhat is quantum gate teleportation and what is it used for?\nI am particularly interested in possible applications in simulating quantum circuits.", "Quantum gate teleportation is the act of being able to apply a quantum gate on the unknown state while it is being teleported. This is one of the ways in which measurement-based computation can be described using graph states.\n\nUsually, teleportation works by having an unknown quantum state $|\\psi\\rangle$ held by Alice, and two qubits in the Bell state $|\\Psi\\rangle=(|00\\rangle+|11\\rangle)/\\sqrt{2}$ shared between Alice and Bob. Alice performs a Bell state measurement, getting one of 4 possible answers and Bob holds on his qubit, depending on the measurement result of Alice, one of the 4 states $|\\psi\\rangle,X|\\psi\\rangle,Z|\\psi\\rangle,ZX|\\psi\\rangle.$ So, once Bob learns what result Alice got, he can compensate by applying the appropriate Paulis.\n\nLet $U$ be a 1-qubit unitary. Assume Alice and Bob share $(\\mathbb{I}\\otimes U)|\\Psi\\rangle$ instead of $|\\Psi\\rangle$. If they repeat the teleportation protocol, Bob now has one of $U|\\psi\\rangle,UX|\\psi\\rangle,UZ|\\psi\\rangle,UZX|\\psi\\rangle$, which we can rewrite as $U|\\psi\\rangle,(UXU^\\dagger)U|\\psi\\rangle,(UZU^\\dagger)U|\\psi\\rangle,(UZXU^\\dagger)U|\\psi\\rangle.$ The compensations that Bob has to make for a given measurement result are given by the bracketed terms. Often, these are no worse than the compensations you would have to make for normal teleportation (i.e. just the Pauli rotations). For example, if $U$ is the Hadamard rotation, then the corrections are just $(\\mathbb{I},Z,X,XZ)$ respectively. So, you can apply the Hadamard during teleportation just be changing the state that you teleport through (There is a strong connection here to the Choi-Jamio\u0142kowski isomorphism). You can do the same for Pauli gates, and the phase gate $\\sqrt{Z}=S$. Moreover, if you repeat this protocol to build up a more complicated computation, it is often sufficient to keep a record of what these corrections are, and to apply them later.\n\nEven if you don't only need the Pauli gates (as is the case for $T=\\sqrt{S}$), the compensations may be easier than implementing the gate directly. This is the basis of the construction of the fault-tolerant T gate.\n\nIn fact, you can do something similar to apply a controlled-NOT between a pair of qubits as well. This time, the state you need is $|\\Psi\\rangle_{A_1B_1}|\\Psi\\rangle_{A_1B_1}$, and a controlled-NOT applied between $B_1$ and $B_2$. This time, there are 16 possible compensating rotations, but all of them are just about how Pauli operations propagate through the action of a controlled-NOT and, again, that just gives Pauli operations out."], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "Quantum state teleportation is the quantum information protocol where a qubit is transferred between two parties using an initial shared entangled state, Bell measurement, classical communication and local rotation. Apparently, there is also something called quantum gate teleportation.\nWhat is quantum gate teleportation and what is it used for?\nI am particularly interested in possible applications in simulating quantum circuits."}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "What are the general tactics of Krav Maga as opposed to Systema? \nFor instance, the tactics of Silat are to hurt the other person so badly they can't hurt back. Another example would be that the tactics of boxing would be to knock out the other person first using only punches. So, as far as I know, the goal of Systema and Krav Maga are both to do anything you can to defeat your attacker because they are serious about self defense. Does that mean that Krav Maga and Systema are strategical identical? Does Krav use strategies that Systema doesn't? Does Systema use any strategies that Krav doesn't? Is there a difference or do they generally work the same way?"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer: general tactics of Krav Maga vs Systema. Explain the underlying philosophies, tactics, principles, differences. Provide overview: Krav Maga = Israeli, combat-oriented, emphasis on aggression, simultaneous defense/offense, target vital points, \"neutralize threat quickly\", use of weapons, realistic training, stress inoculation. Systema = Russian martial art, holistic, breathing, movement, body mechanics, fluid adaptation, using opponent's energy, de-escalation, psychological components, less emphasis on high-impact striking, more on joint manipulation, strikes, breathing, relaxed movement, principle of \"principles over techniques\". Also differences in training methodology: scenario based vs forms? Also tactical differences: Krav uses aggressive counterattacks, hard striking, use of environment, weapons, \"no rules\", aim to end fight quickly; Systema uses softer, relaxation, unpredictable attacks, use of structure, pulses, internal soft/hard, aiming to control, incapacitate with less force sometimes, focus on survivability, psychological calm.\n\nAnswer should compare tactics, not just philosophy. Mention difference in approaches: Krav: \"strike first, strike hard, target eyes, throat, groin, etc.\" Simultaneous defense/attack, use of weapons as soon as possible, training with pads and realistic scenarios, principle of \"the attacker is a threat, you must neutralize them\". Systema: \"the flow and adaptation\", \"use of breathing, lateral movement, turning the attacker's force, joint locks, pressure points, weapons defense, awareness, improvisation\".\n\nAlso mention that both are modern self-defense systems but not identical. Provide specific tactics that Krav includes that Systema may not (e.g., \"preemptive aggression\", \"multiple attackers\", \"use of improvised weapons as primary\". Systema includes \"non-resistance, using the attacker's momentum, hitting vulnerable zones but also using internal pressure points, disarming through redirection\").\n\nMake answer comprehensive.\n\nNow create final.</think>\n<answer>\n**Short answer:**  \nNo \u2013 Krav\u202fMaga and Systema are not \u201cstrategically identical.\u201d  \nBoth are modern self\u2011defence systems that train you to survive a violent encounter, but they are built on very different tactical philosophies and therefore emphasize different actions, priorities and methods in a fight.\n\nBelow is a side\u2011by\u2011side overview of the *general tactics* of each art, followed by a list of tactics that one typically uses and the other rarely does.  The goal is to show where the overlap ends and the divergence begins.\n\n---\n\n## 1.  Core Tactical Philosophy\n\n|                     | **Krav\u202fMaga** (Israeli) | **Systema** (Russian) |\n|---------------------|--------------------------|-----------------------|\n| **Primary aim**     | **Neutralise the threat as quickly as possible** \u2013 usually by causing maximal pain or rapid incapacitation. | **Survive and stay functional** \u2013 neutralise the threat while preserving the defender\u2019s balance, breathing and mental composure. |\n| **Approach to violence** | \u201cIf you are attacked, **attack back** \u2013 hard, fast, and with no hesitation.\u201d | \u201cUse the attacker\u2019s own energy against him; stay relaxed and adaptable.\u201d |\n| **View of the opponent** | A *danger* that must be *destroyed or driven away*. | A *force* that can be *redirected or rendered harmless* \u2013 often without needing to inflict severe injury. |\n| **Training emphasis** | Stress\u2011inoculation drills, realistic scenario sparring, weapons & multiple\u2011attacker drills, \u201cfight\u2011or\u2011flight\u201d decision\u2011making. | Breathing, posture, body\u2011mechanics, fluid movement, internal body awareness, \u201chard\u2011soft\u201d spectrum, moral/psychological preparation. |\n| **Rule set** | \u201cNo rules\u201d \u2013 any technique that stops the attacker is allowed (eye gouge, groin strike, head\u2011butt, etc.). | \u201cStay in the *principle* of non\u2011excessive force*; if you can stop the attack without breaking bones, you do so.  Excess force is a last resort.*\u201d |\n\n---\n\n## 2.  Tactical Toolbox \u2013 What Each System Actually Does in a Fight\n\n### 2.1 Krav\u202fMaga \u2013 Typical Tactics\n\n| Tactical Category | Representative Techniques / Concepts |\n|-------------------|---------------------------------------|\n| **1. Simultaneous defence\u2011offence** | Cover\u2011and\u2011strike (e.g. forearm block + cross to the jaw), \u201cspear\u2011hand\u201d block + palm strike, elbows while defending. |\n| **2. \u201cFirst\u2011strike\u201d aggression** | Immediate, powerful strikes to *vital targets*: eyes, throat, solar plexus, groin, knee, maxilla. |\n| **3. Weapon use as soon as possible** | Grab\u2011and\u2011strike with a pen, use a broken bottle, employ any found object as a club; \u201cweapon\u2011first\u201d philosophy. |\n| **4. Use of the environment** | Kick\u2011out a chair leg, slam into a wall, throw the attacker into a solid object, use cars, stairs, etc. |\n| **5. Multiple\u2011attacker protocol** | 360\u00b0 awareness, moving to a position where you can face all threats, \u201crun\u2011and\u2011strike\u201d to keep distance, use one attacker as a shield against another. |\n| **6. Disarming** | Aggressive grabs, cross\u2011body arm\u2011breaks, \u201cfront\u2011hand\u2011pull\u2011while\u2011kicking\u201d the weapon\u2011hand, then immediate striking. |\n| **7. High\u2011impact striking** | Straight punches, hammer fists, knee\u2011to\u2011body, low kicks (to knee\u2011joint, shin), \u201cup\u2011kicks\u201d to the face when pressed. |\n| **8. \u201cFinish\u201d mindset** | Continue striking until the attacker collapses, loses consciousness, or is clearly unable to continue. |\n| **9. Training under stress** | \u201cAdrenaline\u2011driven\u201d drills with loud music, timed decision\u2011making, \u201cfight\u2011or\u2011flight\u201d scenarios, often with protective gear. |\n\n### 2.2 Systema \u2013 Typical Tactics\n\n| Tactical Category | Representative Techniques / Concepts |\n|-------------------|---------------------------------------|\n| **1. Relaxed, fluid movement** | \u201cSoft\u201d stance, natural walking gait, minimal tension; strike while keeping the body loose. |\n| **2. Breathing\u2011centered action** | \u201cBreath\u2011flow\u201d \u2013 exhale on strike, inhale on release; breath controls aggression and timing. |\n| **3. Using the attacker's momentum** | Redirection, \u201cpush\u2011pull\u201d to off\u2011balance, circular parries that guide the attacker\u2019s force away. |\n| **4. Minimal force, maximum effect** | Light strikes to nerve clusters, pressure\u2011point attacks, \u201cpain compliance\u201d rather than bone\u2011breaking blows. |\n| **5. Whole\u2011body strikes** | \u201cKicks from the hips,\u201d \u201clow\u2011to\u2011high strikes,\u201d use of torso rotation to generate power without obvious muscular effort. |\n| **6. Joint\u2011locks & manipulations** | \u201cSoft\u2011joint breaks,\u201d \u201cwrist\u2011twist,\u201d \u201cunder\u2011hook\u201d \u2013 often combined with a strike to keep the opponent compliant. |\n| **7. Disarming through flow** | \u201cRedirect the weapon,\u201d \u201cstep inside the line of attack,\u201d then neutralise the weapon hand while staying relaxed. |\n| **8. Psychological control** | Maintaining calm, reading the attacker\u2019s intent, de\u2011escalation / verbal engagement when possible. |\n| **9. Training under \u201cnatural\u201d conditions** | Slow\u2011speed drills that focus on movement patterns, \u201cfree\u2011flow\u201d sparring without pads, emphasis on body awareness rather than high\u2011intensity stress. |\n| **10. \u201cSpar with the environment\u201d** | Use of natural obstacles (walls, chairs) not as weapons but as support for balance, leverage, or to limit the attacker\u2019s space. |\n\n---\n\n## 3.  Where the Tactical Overlap Ends\n\n| Tactic | Krav\u202fMaga **does** (rare in Systema) | Systema **does** (rare in Krav\u202fMaga) |\n|--------|--------------------------------------|--------------------------------------|\n| **Pre\u2011emptive, aggressive striking** | *Yes* \u2013 \u201cstrike first, strike hard\u201d is the hallmark. | *No* \u2013 Systema usually waits for the attack to materialise, then redirects. |\n| **Eye\u2011gouge, throat strike, groin kick** | *Standard* and taught as \u201cfirst\u2011level\u201d attacks. | *Sometimes* taught as \u201cpain compliance\u201d but not emphasized as a primary \u201cfinish.\u201d |\n| **Weapon\u2011first mindset** | *Grab any object and use it immediately* (even before a defence). | *Weapon is another tool to be integrated into flow*; rarely the first action unless the situation demands it. |\n| **Multiple\u2011attacker \u201ccover\u2011and\u2011strike\u201d drills** | *High\u2011frequency* \u2013 Krav has specific drills for 2\u20113 attackers. | *Rare* \u2013 Systema focuses on movement & awareness rather than simultaneous strikes on many foes. |\n| **Hard\u2011impact, bone\u2011breaking blows** | *Encouraged* (e.g., hammer\u2011fist to the skull). | *Discouraged* \u2013 Systema prefers \u201csoft\u2011hard\u201d continuum; breaking bone is a last resort. |\n| **Relentless \u201cfinish\u2011the\u2011job\u201d** | *Yes* \u2013 fight till the opponent is incapacitated. | *No* \u2013 Goal is to neutralise while preserving your own health and mental state; you stop once the threat is no longer able to act. |\n| **Bre"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the general tactics of Krav Maga as opposed to Systema? \nFor instance, the tactics of Silat are to hurt the other person so badly they can't hurt back. Another example would be that the tactics of boxing would be to knock out the other person first using only punches. So, as far as I know, the goal of Systema and Krav Maga are both to do anything you can to defeat your attacker because they are serious about self defense. Does that mean that Krav Maga and Systema are strategical identical? Does Krav use strategies that Systema doesn't? Does Systema use any strategies that Krav doesn't? Is there a difference or do they generally work the same way?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer: general tactics of Krav Maga vs Systema. Explain the underlying philosophies, tactics, principles, differences. Provide overview: Krav Maga = Israeli, combat-oriented, emphasis on aggression, simultaneous defense/offense, target vital points, \"neutralize threat quickly\", use of weapons, realistic training, stress inoculation. Systema = Russian martial art, holistic, breathing, movement, body mechanics, fluid adaptation, using opponent's energy, de-escalation, psychological components, less emphasis on high-impact striking, more on joint manipulation, strikes, breathing, relaxed movement, principle of \"principles over techniques\". Also differences in training methodology: scenario based vs forms? Also tactical differences: Krav