\n\nprint('Result with lock:', counter['value'])\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```\nResult with lock: 400000\n```\n\nThe lock guarantees that only one thread can execute the three byte\u2011code\nsteps at a time, eliminating the race condition.\n\n---\n\n## 3. When to use `threading.local` (thread\u2011local storage)\n\nSometimes you *do* want each thread to have its own instance of an object, but you don\u2019t want to pass it around as a function argument all the time.  \n`threading.local()` provides a per\u2011thread namespace:\n\n```python\nimport threading\n\nthread_data = threading.local()    # creates a thread\u2011local container\n\ndef init_thread():\n    # each thread gets its own attribute 'id'\n    thread_data.id = threading.get_ident()   # store thread\u2011specific value\n    print('Thread', thread_data.id, 'started')\n\ndef show_id():\n    # any function can read the per\u2011thread attribute without arguments\n    print('Running in thread', thread_data.id)\n\nthreads = [threading.Thread(target=lambda: (init_thread(), show_id()))\n           for _ in range(3)]\n\nfor t in threads:\n    t.start()\nfor t in threads:\n    t.join()\n```\n\n**Typical output (order may vary)**\n\n```\nThread 140292657885824 started\nRunning in thread 140292657885824\nThread 140292649493120 started\nRunning in thread 140292649493120\nThread 140292641100416 started\nRunning in thread 140292641100416\n```\n\n### Real\u2011world use cases\n\n| Situation | Why thread\u2011local? |\n|-----------|-------------------|\n| **Database connections** (e.g., a `sqlite3.Connection`) | Most DB drivers are not thread\u2011safe; each thread needs its own connection object. |\n| **Per\u2011thread cache** (e.g., `functools.lru_cache` with thread\u2011specific state) | Avoid sharing mutable cache that could be corrupted by concurrent writes. |\n| **Random number generators** (`random.Random`) | The global PRNG has a global state; using a separate instance per thread removes contention and makes results reproducible per thread. |\n| **Logging contexts** (e.g., request\u2011ID in a web server) | Attach a request identifier to the current thread so every log line automatically includes it. |\n\nIf you *do* need to share data (e.g., a work queue, a result list, or a cache), you must protect that shared mutable object with a lock *or* use a thread\u2011safe container such as `queue.Queue`, `collections.deque` with a lock, or `concurrent.futures.Future`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Quick checklist for thread\u2011shared data in Python\n\n| What you\u2019re doing | Is data shared? | What you should do |\n|-------------------|----------------|--------------------|\n| **Read\u2011only access** to a global constant (e.g., a tuple of configuration values) | Yes, but read\u2011only is safe | No lock needed |\n| **Mutating a global container** (list, dict, set, custom mutable object) | Yes, all threads see the same object | Use `threading.Lock` (or `RLock`), or a thread\u2011safe structure (`queue.Queue`) |\n| **Appending to a list** from many threads | Shared mutable state | Protect with a lock (`with lock: mylist.append(x)`) |\n| **Storing per\u2011thread context** (cookies, DB cursors, thread\u2011specific counters) | You *don\u2019t* want sharing | Use `threading.local()` or pass the object explicitly |\n| **Calling a function that only uses its own local variables** | No sharing (locals live on the call stack) | No lock needed |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Minimal complete example that mixes everything\n\n```python\nimport threading, queue, time, random\n\n# ---------- shared objects ----------\nwork_q   = queue.Queue()            # thread\u2011safe queue \u2192 no extra lock needed\nresults  = []                       # a plain list, will need a lock\nresults_lock = threading.Lock()\n\n# ---------- thread\u2011local storage ----------\nthread_state = threading.local()    # each thread will get its own rng\n\ndef worker():\n    # give each thread its own random generator\n    thread_state.rng = random.Random"}], "q_num": 215, "originalconversations": ["In Python specifically, how do variables get shared between threads?\nAlthough I have used ```threading.Thread``` before I never really understood or saw examples of how variables got shared.  Are they shared between the main thread and the children or only among the children? When would I need to use thread local storage to avoid this sharing?\nI have seen many warnings about synchronizing access to shared data among threads by using locks but I have yet to see a really good example of the problem. \nThanks in advance!", "In Python, everything is shared, except for function-local variables (because each function call gets its own set of locals, and threads are always separate function calls.) And even then, only the variables themselves (the names that refer to objects) are local to the function; objects themselves are always global, and anything can refer to them. The `Thread` object for a particular thread is not a special object in this regard. If you store the `Thread` object somewhere all threads can access (like a global variable) then all threads can access that one Thread object. If you want to atomically modify anything that another thread has access to, you have to protect it with a lock. And all threads must of course share this very same lock, or it wouldn't be very effective.\n\nIf you want actual thread-local storage, that's where `threading.local` comes in. Attributes of `threading.local` are not shared between threads; each thread sees only the attributes it itself placed in there. If you're curious about its implementation, the source is in _threading_local.py in the standard library."], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "In Python specifically, how do variables get shared between threads?\nAlthough I have used ```threading.Thread``` before I never really understood or saw examples of how variables got shared.  Are they shared between the main thread and the children or only among the children? When would I need to use thread local storage to avoid this sharing?\nI have seen many warnings about synchronizing access to shared data among threads by using locks but I have yet to see a really good example of the problem. \nThanks in advance!"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "How to make a lesson plan to accommodate all of the learning difficulties in the classroom?"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks: \"How to make a lesson plan to accommodate all of the learning difficulties in the classroom?\" They want guidance on lesson planning for inclusive education. Provide a comprehensive answer: steps, universal design for learning (UDL), differentiation, assessment, scaffolding, etc. Also mention specific learning difficulties: dyslexia, dyscalculia, ADHD, autism, sensory processing, etc. Provide strategies and examples.\n\nNeed to ensure compliance with policy: no disallowed content. It's a valid request for educational advice.\n\nProvide structured answer: overview, steps, principles, practical strategies, resources, sample lesson plan outline, reflection.\n\nProceed.</think>\n<answer>\n**Creating an Inclusive Lesson Plan that Supports All Learning Difficulties**\n\nDesigning a lesson that works for every learner is challenging, but with a systematic approach you can make your teaching \u201cuniversal\u201d \u2013\u202fso that it meets the needs of students with dyslexia, dyscalculia, ADHD, autism spectrum disorder, language\u2011processing issues, sensory sensitivities, and other learning differences. Below is a step\u2011by\u2011step framework, key principles, and concrete strategies you can embed directly into a lesson plan.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Adopt a Universal Design for Learning (UDL) Mind\u2011set\nUDL is a research\u2011based framework that asks you to plan **multiple ways of**:\n\n| **Goal** | **What to Provide** |\n|----------|---------------------|\n| **Representation** \u2013 how information is presented | Text, audio, video, graphics, manipulatives, gestures |\n| **Action & Expression** \u2013 how students demonstrate learning | Writing, speaking, drawing, digital tools, oral presentations, models |\n| **Engagement** \u2013 how students are motivated & stay on task | Choices, relevance, collaborative work, sensory breaks, gamified elements |\n\nStart each lesson by checking the three UDL \u201ccheckpoints\u201d to ensure you have at least **two options** for each column.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Gather Quick Diagnostic Data (Pre\u2011Lesson)\n\n| **Tool** | **What It Gives You** | **Time Required** |\n|----------|----------------------|-------------------|\n| **Learning profiles / IEP summaries** | Specific accommodations (e.g., extra time, text\u2011to\u2011speech) | 5\u202fmin |\n| **One\u2011minute \u201cwarm\u2011up\u201d poll** (e.g., digital exit ticket) | Current confidence level, preferred learning mode | 2\u202fmin |\n| **Classroom observation notes** | Behavioral triggers, sensory needs, peer dynamics | Ongoing |\n\nUse this data to **prioritize** which supports are essential for the day\u2019s lesson.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Lesson\u2011Plan Template (Inclusive)\n\nBelow is a flexible template you can paste into any word\u2011processor or digital planner. Fill in each section with *at least two* options for representation, expression, and engagement.\n\n| **Section** | **What to Include** | **Inclusive Options** |\n|-------------|--------------------|-----------------------|\n| **1. Learning Objectives** | Clear, measurable (Bloom\u2019s taxonomy) | Write objectives in **plain language** + **visual icons** (e.g., a brain for \u201canalyze\u201d) |\n| **2. Materials & Resources** | Texts, manipulatives, tech | \u2022 Printable handouts with **high\u2011contrast fonts** (Arial 14, 1.5\u202fline spacing) <br>\u2022 Audio version of the text (recorded or text\u2011to\u2011speech) <br>\u2022 Graphic organizer templates (digital and paper) |\n| **3. Hook / Entry Event** (5\u201110\u202fmin) | Capture interest | \u2022 Short video clip (subtitles) <br>\u2022 Real\u2011world problem scenario (pictures) <br>\u2022 \u201cThink\u2011pair\u2011share\u201d with a **choice board** for response mode (draw, speak, write) |\n| **4. Direct Instruction** (10\u201115\u202fmin) | Core content delivery | \u2022 **Chunked** presentation (3\u20115\u202fmin segments) <br>\u2022 Use **multi\u2011sensory cues** (color\u2011coded slides, spoken keyword, tactile object) <br>\u2022 Provide **guided notes** with blanks for students to fill (supports dysgraphia) |\n| **5. Guided Practice** (15\u201120\u202fmin) | Scaffolded application | \u2022 **Station rotation** with three tasks: (a) visual matching, (b) manipulative problem, (c) digital quiz with audio feedback <br>\u2022 Pair students strategically (peer\u2011tutor, complementary strengths) <br>\u2022 Offer **timer** or **visual countdown** for students with ADHD |\n| **6. Independent Work** (10\u201115\u202fmin) | Demonstrate mastery | \u2022 Choice of **output**: written paragraph, recorded audio, infographic, or physical model <br>\u2022 Provide **assistive tech** (speech\u2011to\u2011text, graphic\u2011organizer apps) <br>\u2022 Offer **alternative length** (short answer vs. extended) |\n| **7. Check for Understanding** (5\u202fmin) | Formative assessment | \u2022 Exit ticket with **multiple modalities** (draw a symbol, type a sentence, select a meme) <br>\u2022 Quick \u201cthumbs up/down/sideways\u201d poll for confidence level |\n| **8. Reflection & Closure** (5\u202fmin) | Meta\u2011cognition | \u2022 Prompt: \u201cWhat helped you learn today? What could be different?\u201d <br>\u2022 Record answers in a shared digital board (e.g., Padlet) so students can type, draw, or record audio |\n| **9. Accommodations Checklist** | Quick reference for you | \u2022 Extra time (+30\u202f%) <br>\u2022 Text\u2011to\u2011speech activated <br>\u2022 Seating near teacher/low\u2011distraction area <br>\u2022 Sensory break card (use anytime) <br>\u2022 Preferential grouping (peer\u2011mentor) |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Specific Strategies for Common Learning Difficulties\n\n| **Difficulty** | **Targeted Strategy** | **How to Embed in Lesson** |\n|----------------|-----------------------|----------------------------|\n| **Dyslexia** | \u2022 Sans\u2011serif fonts, 1.5\u202fline spacing, colored overlay <br>\u2022 Audio narration of written material <br>\u2022 Use of **speech\u2011to\u2011text** for written responses | Provide a **read\u2011aloud** version of the handout and allow students to dictate their responses using a tablet or laptop. |\n| **Dyscalculia** | \u2022 Visual number lines, manipulatives (base\u201110 blocks) <br>\u2022 Real\u2011world context problems, step\u2011by\u2011step scaffolding <br>\u2022 Explicit vocabulary (e.g., \u201csum,\u201d \u201cdifference\u201d) | During guided practice, include a **hands\u2011on math station** with counters and a \u201csolve\u2011step\u201d checklist. |\n| **ADHD** | \u2022 Short, varied activity blocks <br>\u2022 Physical movement breaks (30\u2011second stretch) <br>\u2022 Clear, written agenda displayed all day | Begin each segment with a **visual timer** and give a \u201cmovement cue\u201d card for a quick stretch at the 10\u2011minute mark. |\n| **Autism Spectrum** | \u2022 Predictable routine (agenda on board) <br>\u2022 Literal language, avoid idioms <br>\u2022 Options for low\u2011sensory seating (noise\u2011cancelling headphones) | Post the **lesson agenda** at the start; provide a **visual schedule** with icons for each activity. |\n| **Language Processing / ESL** | \u2022 Pre\u2011teach key vocabulary with images <br>\u2022 Sentence frames (e.g., \u201cThe main idea is ___ because ___.\u201d) <br>\u2022 Allow oral explanations before written | Offer **vocab cards** and sentence starters during independent work; allow a spoken recording instead of a written paragraph. |\n| **Sensory Processing** | \u2022 Flexible seating, fidget tools <br>\u2022 Reduced background noise (soft lighting) <br>\u2022 Provide \u201csensory break\u201d cards | Keep a **basket of fidget items** accessible; let students use a **quiet corner** when needed. |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Build in Assessment & Data Loops\n\n1. **Formative Checkpoints** \u2013 Use quick low\u2011stakes tools (Kahoot!, Google Forms, exit tickets) that can be completed via keyboard, mouse, or voice.\n2. **Differentiated Rubrics** \u2013 Define mastery criteria that account for **process** (e.g., \u201cused graphic organizer\u201d) *and* product (e.g., \u201canswer is accurate\u201d). Provide separate rubrics for written, oral, and visual outputs.\n3. **Reflection Log** \u2013 Have each student fill a short digital note: *What strategy helped me? What would I try next time?* This data guides future lesson tweaks.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Sample Mini\u2011Lesson (Illustrated)\n\n**Topic:** *Photosynthesis* (Grade 5 Science) \u2013 45\u202fmin\n\n| Time | Activity | UDL Options |\n|------|----------|------------|\n| 0\u20115\u202fmin | **Hook:** 30\u2011second video with subtitles showing a leaf \u201cbreathing.\u201d | Visual + captioned audio |\n| 5\u201112\u202fmin | **Direct Instruction:** 3 slide \u201cchunks\u201d: (1) Light energy, (2) Chlorophyll, (3) Sugar creation. | Slides with bullet points **+** spoken keywords; printed handout in large font |\n| 12\u201120\u202fmin | **Guided Practice \u2013 Station Rotation**:<br>1\ufe0f\u20e3 **Model**: Build a leaf using colored paper pieces (tactile).<br>2\ufe0f\u20e3 **Digital**: Interactive simulation (audio narration).<br>3\ufe0f\u20e3 **Graphic**: Fill\u2011in a diagram with word bank. | Hands\u2011on, tech, written |\n"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How to make a lesson plan to accommodate all of the learning difficulties in the classroom?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks: \"How to make a lesson plan to accommodate all of the learning difficulties in the classroom?\" They want guidance on lesson planning for inclusive education. Provide a comprehensive answer: steps, universal design for learni