voir* expressed possession, but in French it has been grammaticalised and no longer carries a \u201cpossessive\u201d meaning.  The same construction is used for most French verbs; a few verbs of motion or change of state take *\u00eatre* (\u201cto be\u201d) instead of *avoir* (e.g. *je suis parti*).\n\nThus *J\u2019ai trouv\u00e9 le chat* corresponds roughly to English *I have found the cat* **or** to the simple past *I found the cat*, because French normally uses the pass\u00e9 compos\u00e9 where English would use a simple past.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Languages that use a \u201chave\u2011auxiliary\u201d for the perfect\n\n### 2.1. Closely related Indo\u2011European languages  \n\n| Language | Auxiliary for perfect | Example (\u2018to find the cat\u2019) |\n|----------|----------------------|-----------------------------|\n| **Spanish** | *haber* (\u201cto have\u201d) | *He encontrado al gato* |\n| **Italian** | *avere* (\u201cto have\u201d) | *Ho trovato il gatto* |\n| **Portuguese** | *ter* (\u201cto have\u201d) | *Tenho encontrado o gato* |\n| **Romanian** | *a avea* (\u201cto have\u201d) | *Am g\u0103sit pisica* |\n| **German** | *haben* (\u201cto have\u201d) | *Ich habe die Katze gefunden* |\n| **Dutch** | *hebben* (\u201cto have\u201d) | *Ik heb de kat gevonden* |\n| **Swedish** | *ha* (\u201cto have\u201d) | *Jag har hittat katten* |\n| **Icelandic** | *hafa* (\u201cto have\u201d) | *\u00c9g hef fundi\u00f0 kettuna* |\n| **Modern Greek** | *\u03ad\u03c7\u03c9* (\u00e9cho, \u201cto have\u201d) | *\u0388\u03c7\u03c9 \u03b2\u03c1\u03b5\u03b9 \u03c4\u03b7 \u03b3\u03ac\u03c4\u03b1* |\n\nAll of these are Germanic or Romance languages that inherited the analytic perfect from Proto\u2011Indo\u2011European.\n\n### 2.2. Languages that use a \u201cbe\u2011auxiliary\u201d\n\nSome Indo\u2011European languages use *to be* instead of *to have* for many verbs, especially verbs of motion or change of state.\n\n| Language | Auxiliary for perfect | Example |\n|----------|----------------------|---------|\n| **French** (for motion verbs) | *\u00eatre* | *Je suis parti* |\n| **Spanish** (for some intransitives) | *estar* in colloquial perfects | *Estoy ido* (regional) |\n| **German** (archaic) | *sein* for a few verbs | *Ich bin gegangen* |\n| **Italian** (regional) | *essere* for some verbs | *Sono andato* |\n| **Irish** | *b\u00ed* (\u201cto be\u201d) | *T\u00e1 m\u00e9 tar \u00e9is dul* (\u201cI have gone\u201d) |\n| **Serbo\u2011Croatian** | *b* (\u201cto be\u201d) | *Ja sam na\u0161ao* |\n| **Finnish** | *on* (3rd\u2011person) from *olla* (\u201cto be\u201d) | *On l\u00f6yt\u00e4nyt kissa* |\n| **Hungarian** | *van* (\u201cto be\u201d) | *Tal\u00e1lt a macska* (literally \u201chas found the cat\u201d) |\n\n### 2.3. Non\u2011Indo\u2011European languages with a \u201chave\u201d perfect\n\n| Language | Auxiliary | Example |\n|----------|-----------|---------|\n| **Mandarin Chinese** \u2013 periphrastic perfect with *\u6709* (y\u01d2u, \u201cto have\u201d) in some dialects (e.g., *\u6211\u6709\u627e\u5230* \u201cI have found\u201d) \u2013 not standard, but the structure exists in some Chinese varieties. |\n| **Arabic** \u2013 uses *\u0643\u0627\u0646* (\u201cto be\u201d) for past continuous, but Classical Arabic forms a perfect without an auxiliary; Modern Standard Arabic occasionally uses *\u0642\u062f* + perfect for a recent perfect, not a \u201chave\u201d. |\n| **Mongolian** \u2013 has a perfective auxiliary *bex* (\u201cto have\u201d) in some dialects. |\n| **Korean** \u2013 analytic perfects often use *\uc5c8\ub2e4* (past) plus a stative verb *\uc788\ub2e4* (\u201cto exist/have\u201d) in colloquial constructions, e.g., *\ub193\uc544\ub450\uc5c8\ub2e4* \u2192 \u201chave left\u201d. |\n| **Japanese** \u2013 the *te\u2011iru* construction (*\u898b\u3064\u3051\u3066\u3044\u308b* \u201chave found\u201d) is not a true auxiliary *have*, but functions similarly for a resultant state. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Why do many languages end up with a \u201chave\u2011perfect\u201d?\n\n1. **Historical grammaticalisation** \u2013 In Proto\u2011Indo\u2011European the perfect was formed with the auxiliary **h\u2081\u00e9s\u2011* (\u201cto be\u201d) and *\\*\u01f5\u02b0ab\u2011* (\u201cto have\u201d).  Over time the *have* auxiliary became dominant for most verbs.\n2. **Semantic plausibility** \u2013 The notion \u201cthe subject *has* a completed event\u201d maps naturally onto a possessive verb.\n3. **Analytic tendency** \u2013 Languages that lost (or never had) a rich synthetic past often create a periphrastic perfect with an auxiliary to distinguish a *recent* or *relevant* past from a simple past narrative.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Languages that **don\u2019t** use a \u201chave\u201d auxiliary\n\n| Language | Past\u2011tense strategy |\n|----------|--------------------|\n| **Russian** | Simple past inflection on the verb, no auxiliary. |\n| **Chinese (Mandarin)** | Aspect particles (*\u4e86* le) indicate perfective aspect, no auxiliary verb. |\n| **Turkish** | Uses a suffix *\u2011mi\u015f* for the reported past; no auxiliary. |\n| **Vietnamese** | Aspect markers (*\u0111\u00e3*, *\u0111ang*) but no auxiliary verb. |\n| **Arabic (Classical)** | Single\u2011form perfect; auxiliaries only for modal or aspectual nuances. |\n| **Zulu** | Past tense marked by prefixes on the verb; no auxiliary. |\n\nIn these languages the perfect or past meaning is expressed **morphologically** (suffixes, inflections) or with **aspect particles**, not with a separate verb"}], "q_num": 210, "originalconversations": ["When learning some basic French, I was somewhat surprised to learn that phrases of the form \"I have found the cat\" generally translate almost word-for-word from English (J'ai trouv\u00e9 le chat). To me, it's not immediately obvious that possession (\"I have\"/\"J'ai\") has a correspondence with past tense, although if I think about it a little more I suppose I can kind of see how it makes sense.\nThis makes me wonder: Is this a common pattern in other languages? Especially ones not closely related to English.", "This is what's called a \"Sprachbund\" feature: it's a trait shared by a bunch of languages in an area, even ones that aren't genetically related. In particular, this one is a feature of the \"Standard Average European\" Sprachbund, a group of languages centered in Western Europe, and it's one of the features that was originally used to define that Sprachbund in the first place!\n\nThe construction seems to have arisen back in Latin. In Classical Latin, past aoristic verbs (\"I loved\") and present perfective verbs (\"I have loved\") look exactly the same: both of those would be written am\u0101v\u012b. But it's a pretty useful distinction to be able to make! So in Vulgar Latin, a new construction arose, using the verb hab\u0113re (\"to have\"). It's thought that a phrase like habe\u014d litter\u0101s script\u0101s \"I have (written letters)\" got reanalyzed into \"I (have written) letters\", with hab\u0113re no longer indicating that you're actually holding anything in your hands, just that an action's been completed in the past.\n\nVulgar Latin eventually evolved into French, Italian, Spanish, and all the other Romance languages, and brought this construction with it; hab\u0113re is the direct ancestor of French avoir, via a series of sound changes. And once various Romance languages and Germanic languages and others were all being spoken in the same area, this feature spread through the Sprachbund: people speaking Germanic languages started to use the same construction. English \"have\"/German haben/etc isn't actually at all related to Latin hab\u0113re, but they looked similar, so it was the obvious choice when adopting the construction into Germanic.\n\nNowadays, this feature is called the \"have-perfective\", and it shows up in all sorts of languages within the Standard Average European (SAE) Sprachbund. It's not at all universal, but can be a good way to determine if a language has been influenced by SAE or not!"], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "When learning some basic French, I was somewhat surprised to learn that phrases of the form \"I have found the cat\" generally translate almost word-for-word from English (J'ai trouv\u00e9 le chat). To me, it's not immediately obvious that possession (\"I have\"/\"J'ai\") has a correspondence with past tense, although if I think about it a little more I suppose I can kind of see how it makes sense.\nThis makes me wonder: Is this a common pattern in other languages? Especially ones not closely related to English."}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "I had an interview with an employer working on a software-based vehicle solution.\nBefore going further in the interview process, he gave me a task to find out if a potential customer (automotive OEMs) is interested.\nMy question is, how can I approach a potential customer and arrange some time together to present to them the solution?. I'm intending to use Linkedin, but I'm not sure how to proceed.\n\n* Who to contact (I mean the person position in the company)\n* How to formulate the request?\n"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer: ways to approach potential automotive OEM customers for a software-based vehicle solution, using LinkedIn. Should describe who to contact (titles: Head of R&D, CTO, Chief Digital Officer, VP of Engineering, Product Manager, Innovation Manager, etc.) How to formulate request: personalized connection request, brief intro, value proposition, ask for short call. Also mention research, building relationship, using mutual connections, content, webinars. Provide steps: identify decision makers, use LinkedIn Sales Navigator, look for titles, also consider procurement, VP of Supplier Management, etc. Provide sample messages for connection request and follow-up. Also alternative channels: email, conferences, industry events. Provide tip on respecting corporate hierarchy, focusing on main problem they have. Provide note on compliance, confidentiality.\n\nWe need to present as helpful guidance. Use bullet points. Provide a template.\n\nLet's craft final answer.</think>\nBelow is a practical \u201cplay\u2011by\u2011play\u201d you can use the next time you need to reach an automotive OEM (or any large tier\u20111 supplier) on LinkedIn.  \nIt covers **who to target**, **what research to do before you write**, and **exact wording** for the first contact and the follow\u2011up.  \nFeel free to copy\u2011paste the templates and then customise them for each company.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Do a quick \u201cwho\u2011does\u2011what\u201d audit first  \n\n| Typical OEM org\u2011chart (software side) | Why they are relevant | What you\u2019ll need to know about them |\n|--------------------------------------|----------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| **Chief Digital Officer / Head of Digital Transformation** | Owns the OEM\u2019s overall strategy for connected, over\u2011the\u2011air, and \u201csoftware\u2011first\u201d vehicles. | Their public statements on digital roadmaps, any recent hires or partnerships. |\n| **CTO / VP of Engineering (Vehicle Software)** | Directly responsible for architecture, platform choices, and integration of third\u2011party solutions. | Which vehicle platforms they are building (e.g., EV, autonomous, infotainment). |\n| **Head of R&D \u2013 Vehicle Systems / Software Architecture** | Decides which modules (ADAS, OTA, diagnostics) get outsourced or co\u2011developed. | Recent patents, research papers, or conference talks that reveal pain points. |\n| **Product Manager \u2013 Connected Services / OTA Updates** | The day\u2011to\u2011day champion of any new software service. | Specific product backlog items they have publicly hinted at (e.g., \u201cremote diagnostics\u201d). |\n| **Supplier / Ecosystem Manager / Procurement Lead** | Gatekeeper for vendor onboarding, contracts, and pilots. | Preferred supplier list, vendor evaluation cycles. |\n| **Innovation / Future Mobility Lead** (sometimes a \u201cStrategic Projects\u201d role) | Looks for disruptive ideas and pilots; often the first to say \u201cyes\u201d to a proof\u2011of\u2011concept. | Their involvement in start\u2011up programmes, accelerators, or hack\u2011athons. |\n\n**How to locate them on LinkedIn**\n\n1. **Advanced Search** \u2013 type the OEM name + \u201cdigital\u201d / \u201csoftware\u201d / \u201cCTO\u201d.  \n2. **Sales Navigator (if you have it)** \u2013 filter by \u201cTitle contains: digital, software, CTO, head of R&D, product manager, innovation\u201d.  \n3. **Look at the \u201cPeople also viewed\u201d column** on any senior profile \u2013 it often surfaces the next\u2011most\u2011relevant colleague.  \n4. **Check the OEM\u2019s \u201cAbout\u201d and \u201cPeople\u201d tabs** \u2013 many companies list their leadership team directly on LinkedIn.  \n\n**Tip:** If the OEM has a \u201cConnected Car\u201d or \u201cMobility\u201d hub on its website, note the name of the person heading that hub \u2013 that\u2019s usually the perfect first contact.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Craft a **two\u2011step** outreach plan  \n\n### Step\u202f1 \u2013 Connection request (the \u201cdoor\u2011opener\u201d)\n\n> **Subject:**\u202fWould love to connect \u2013 quick idea for [OEM]\u2019s next OTA platform  \n> **Message (\u2264300\u202fchars, no attachment):**  \n> ```\n> Hi [First\u2011Name],\n> \n> I\u2019m [Your Name] \u2013 product lead at [Your Company], a software platform that cuts OTA integration time by 40\u202f% while keeping ISO\u201126262 compliance. I noticed you\u2019re steering [OEM]\u2019s digital vehicle roadmap and thought a brief 10\u2011min chat could be valuable.\n> \n> Thanks,  \n> [Your First\u2011Name]  \n> ```\n\n**Why it works**\n\n* **Personalisation** \u2013 you mention their specific responsibility (digital roadmap, OTA).  \n* **Value hook** \u2013 a concrete metric (\u201ccuts integration time by 40\u202f%\u201d) and a compliance keyword (ISO\u201126262) that resonates with OEM engineers.  \n* **Low commitment** \u2013 a 10\u2011minute chat, not a full demo.\n\n**Do NOT** attach a deck or link at this stage \u2013 LinkedIn treats that as spam.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f2 \u2013 Follow\u2011up after they accept (or after a few days if no response)\n\n#### A. If they **accept** the request\n\n```\nHi [First\u2011Name],\n\nThanks for connecting!  \nA quick note: we\u2019ve helped [a comparable OEM or Tier\u20111, e.g., \u201cHyundai\u201d] streamline its OTA update pipeline, saving roughly 3\u202fmonths of development per model year. I\u2019d love to show you a 5\u2011slide snapshot of the results and explore whether a pilot could fit into [OEM]\u2019s 2025 roadmap.\n\nWould a 15\u2011minute call next week work for you? I\u2019m flexible on Tuesday\u202f10\u202fam\u202fCET or Thursday\u202f3\u202fpm\u202fCET \u2013 happy to adjust to your calendar.\n\nBest,\n[Your Name]  \n[Title] | [Company]  \n[Phone] | [LinkedIn profile URL]  \n```\n\n#### B. If **no reply** after 5\u20137\u202fdays\n\n```\nHi [First\u2011Name],\n\nJust wanted to make sure my previous note didn\u2019t get lost in the feed.  \nWe\u2019re currently running a short \u201cOEM\u2011Ready OTA\u201d pilot with a leading Tier\u20111 supplier and can share the findings in under 10\u202fminutes. Might be worth a quick look as you plan next year\u2019s software rollout.\n\nDo you have 10\u202fminutes for a call later this week?\n\nThanks,\n[Your Name]\n```\n\n**Key ingredients in every follow\u2011up**\n\n| Element | Reason |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Reference to a known peer/comparable OEM** | Shows you\u2019re already trusted in the space. |\n| **Specific, quantifiable benefit** | \u201c40\u202f% faster integration\u201d, \u201c3\u202fmonths saved\u201d. |\n| **Clear next step & limited time** | 10\u201315\u202fmin call, two suggested slots \u2192 reduces friction. |\n| **Optional \u201cpilot\u201d hook** | OEMs love low\u2011risk proofs-of\u2011concept. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Parallel channels \u2013 don\u2019t rely *only* on LinkedIn  \n\n| Channel | How to use it | What to include |\n|---------|---------------|-----------------|\n| **Email (if you find a corporate address)** | Use the same 2\u2011step cadence (short intro \u2192 value \u2192 request). | Add a one\u2011page \u201cExecutive Summary\u201d PDF (keep < 1\u202fMB). |\n| **Industry events / webinars** | Attend a connected\u2011car summit, then reference the session when you reach out (\u201cI saw you spoke on \u2018OTA security\u2019 at CES 2024\u201d). | Mention the event in the first line \u2013 it creates instant relevance. |\n| **Company\u2019s \u201cSupplier Portal\u201d** | Some OEMs have a formal supplier registration. Fill it, then send a short note to the contact you found on LinkedIn, referencing the portal ID. | Show you respect their procurement process. |\n| **Mutual introductions** | Look for any 2nd\u2011degree connection (e.g., a former colleague) and ask for a warm intro. | \u201cWould you feel comfortable introducing me to\u202f[Name]\u202fto discuss \u2026?\u201d |\n\n---\n\n## 4. What to research **before** you write ANY message\n\n1. **Strategy docs / press releases** \u2013 Identify a pain point they\u2019ve publicly admitted (e.g., \u201creducing OTA downtime\u201d, \u201cimproving over\u2011the\u2011air security\u201d). Echo that language in your hook.\n2. **Recent vehicle launches** \u2013 If they just launched a new EV, they will be thinking about software updates, telematics, etc. Mention \u201cnew model year X\u201d.\n3. **Competitive landscape** \u2013 Know which other suppliers are already in their ecosystem (e.g., Bosch, Continental, Harman). Position yourself as complementary, not a duplicate.\n4. **Regulatory focus** \u2013 In Europe, ISO\u201126262, UNECE R155, R156 are hot; in the US, FMVSS 150 for OTA. Drop the relevant standard to prove you speak their language.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Quick template library (copy\u2011paste & tweak)\n\n### Connection request (\u2264300\u202fchars)\n\n```\nHi [First\u2011Name],\n\nI\u2019m [Your Name] at [Your Company]. We enable OEMs to roll out OTA updates 40\u202f% faster while staying ISO\u201126262 compliant. Noticed you\u2019re leading [OEM]\u2019s digital vehicle roadmap \u2013 would love to connect.\n\nBest,\n[Your First\u2011Name]\n```\n\n### Follow\u2011up after acceptance\n\n```\nHi [First\u2011Name],\n\nThanks for connecting! We recently helped [Comparable OEM/Tier\u20111] cut OTA integration time by 40\u202f% and meet R155 requirements. I think a 10\u2011minute call could surface quick wins for [OEM]\u2019s 2025 plan.\n\nAre you free Tuesday\u202f10\u202fam\u202fCET or Thursday"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I had an interview with an employer working on a software-based vehicle solution.\nBefore going further in the interview process, he gave me a task to find out if a potential customer (automotive OEMs) is interested.\nMy question is, how can I approach a potential customer and arrange some time together to present to them the solution?. I'm intending to use Linkedin, but I'm not sure how to proceed.\n\n* Who to contact (I mean the person position in the company)\n* How to formulate the request?\n"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer: ways to approach potential automotive OEM customers for a software-based vehicle solution, using LinkedIn. Should describe who to contact (titles: Head of R&D, CTO, Chief Digital Officer, VP of Engineering, Product Manager, Innovation Manager, etc.) How to formulate request: personalized connection request, brief intro, value proposition, ask for short call. Also mention research, building relationship, using mutual connections, content, webinars. Provide steps: identify decision makers, use LinkedIn Sales Navigator, look for titles, also consider procurement, VP of Supplier Management, etc. Provide sample messages for connection request and follow-up. Also alternative channels: email, conferences, industry events. Provide tip on respecting corporate hierarchy, focusing on main problem they have. Provide note on compliance, confidentiality.\n\nWe need to present as helpful guidance. Use bullet points. Provide a template.\n\nLet's craft final answer.</think>\nBelow is a practical \u201cplay\u2011by\u2011play\u201d you can use the next time you need to reach an automotive OEM (or any lar