ion size, etc.) and get \\(O(f(k)\\cdot n^{O(1)})\\) running time. | \\(f(k)\\) may be exponential in the parameter but independent of \\(n\\). | When a natural small parameter exists (e.g., \u201csolution size \u2264 k\u201d). |\n\n**Key surveys / references**\n\n- R.\u202fR.\u202fDowney & M.\u202fR.\u202fFellows, *Parameterized Complexity* (1999). \u2013 Chapter\u202f2 gives many FPT exact algorithms for classic NPC problems.\n- F.\u202fV.\u202fFomin & D.\u202fKratsch, *Exact Exponential Algorithms* (2019). \u2013 A modern, extensive survey with tables of best known \\(c^n\\) bounds.\n- A.\u202fP.\u202fBuchanan & J.\u202fK.\u202fUllman, \u201cA Survey of Exact Algorithms for NP\u2011Hard Problems\u201d, *ACM Computing Surveys*, 2018.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Approximation algorithms  \n\nWhen optimality can be relaxed, polynomial\u2011time algorithms with provable approximation ratios are often the method of choice.\n\n| Problem family | Typical guarantee | Representative algorithm |\n|----------------|-------------------|---------------------------|\n| **Metric TSP** | 1.5\u2011approx (Christofides) | Christofides (1976) |\n| **Vertex Cover** | 2\u2011approx (simple greedy) | Pick an uncovered edge, add both endpoints |\n| **Set Cover** | \\(\\ln n\\)\u2011approx (greedy) | Classical greedy set\u2011cover |\n| **MAX\u2011SAT / MAX\u2011CUT** | 0.878\u2011approx (Goemans\u2011Williamson SDP) | SDP rounding |\n| **Facility Location** | Constant\u2011factor (1.488) | LP\u2011based rounding |\n| **Scheduling, Load Balancing** | PTAS for special cases (e.g., identical machines) | PTAS via \u201cguessing\u201d large jobs |\n\n**Surveys**\n\n- V.\u202fVazirani, *Approximation Algorithms* (2001) \u2013 Chapters 1\u201110 cover most classic NPC problems.\n- J.\u202fA.\u00a0Caragiannis, \u201cA Survey of Approximation Algorithms for NP\u2011Hard Problems\u201d, *Foundations and Trends in Theoretical Computer Science*, 2022.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Heuristics & Meta\u2011heuristics  \n\nWhen neither exactness nor provable guarantees are required, practitioners turn to fast, often simple heuristics that work well on typical instances.\n\n| Approach | Typical use cases | Example |\n|----------|-------------------|---------|\n| **Greedy constructive heuristics** | Quick baseline for TSP, knapsack, vertex cover. | Nearest\u2011neighbor, cheapest\u2011insertion. |\n| **Local search / 2\u2011opt, 3\u2011opt** | Improves a feasible solution by swapping parts. | TSP tour improvement. |\n| **Simulated annealing, tabu search** | Escape local minima; widely used for scheduling, routing. | Job\u2011shop scheduling. |\n| **Genetic / evolutionary algorithms** | Population\u2011based search; good for heterogeneous search spaces. | Graph coloring, design problems. |\n| **Ant colony optimization, particle swarm** | Inspired by natural processes; often applied to routing, assignment. | Vehicle routing. |\n| **Lagrangian relaxation & column generation** | Decompose large ILPs; yields strong lower bounds + feasible solutions. | Cutting stock, crew scheduling. |\n\n**References**\n\n- P.\u202fH.\u202fAarts & E.\u202fH.\u202fL.\u202fK.\u202fKoehler, *Local Search in Combinatorial Optimization* (1997).\n- M.\u202fGendreau & J.\u202fM.\u202fPotvin (eds.), *Handbook of Metaheuristics* (2010) \u2013 Chapter\u2011by\u2011chapter surveys of each meta\u2011heuristic.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Parameterized Complexity (FPT)  \n\nIf a natural parameter \\(k\\ (solution size, tree\u2011width, feedback vertex set size, etc.) is small, many NPC problems become tractable:\n\n- **Vertex Cover**: \\(O(1.2738^k + kn)\\) algorithm (branching) \u2192 Fixed\u2011Parameter Tractable.\n- **Feedback Vertex Set**, **Odd Cycle Transversal**, **Treewidth\u2011bounded problems**: FPT algorithms with runtime \\(2^{O(k)}\\cdot n^{O(1)}\\).\n- **Kernelization**: Reduce the instance to a size polynomial in \\(k\\) (e.g., Vertex Cover kernel of size \\(2k\\)).\n\nA recent, comprehensive survey:  \n\n- J.\u202fCygan, M.\u202fLokshtanov, D.\u202fMarx, et\u202fal., *Parameterized Algorithms* (2020).  \n\n---\n\n## 5. Probabilistic / Randomized Algorithms  \n\n- **Monte\u2011Carlo & Las\u2011Vegas**: For problems like SAT, randomized algorithms (e.g., Sch\u00f6ning\u2019s algorithm for k\u2011SAT, runtime \\((2-2/k)^n\\)) can be faster in practice.\n- **Probabilistic Checkable Proofs (PCP)**: Yield hardness of approximation results, guiding whether to seek approximations.\n- **Quantum algorithms**: Grover\u2019s search gives a quadratic speed\u2011up for unstructured search, so a brute\u2011force \\(O(2^{n})\\) algorithm becomes \\(O(2^{n/2})\\). For certain structured problems (e.g., Element Distinctness) quantum algorithms give polynomial improvements.\n\n:\n\n- A.\u202fAmbainis & R.\u202fde\u00a0Wolf, \u201cQuantum Algorithms for NP\u2011Complete Problems\u201d, *Reviews in Mathematical Physics* (2021).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Practical \u201chybrid\u201d frameworks  \n\n1. **Branch\u2011and\u2011cut** \u2013 combines branch\u2011and\u2011bound with cutting\u2011plane generation (used in modern MILP solvers).\n2. **Constraint Programming + SAT** \u2013 encode constraints in a CP model, use SAT\u2011based propagation (e.g., MiniZinc with Glucose).\n3. **Iterative deepening + heuristic pruning** \u2013 common in AI planning (e.g., IDA\\* with admissible heuristic).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Choosing the right approach  \n\n| Situation | Recommended primary technique |\n|-----------|--------------------------------|\n| **Instance size \u2264 30\u201340** and exact solution required | Exact exponential DP / branch\u2011and\u2011bound with strong pruning. |\n| **Instances with exploitable structure (sparse graphs, bounded tree\u2011width)** | Parameterized/FPT algorithms; tree\u2011decomposition + DP. |\n| **Large real\u2011world instances, need good\u2011enough solutions quickly** | ILP/SAT solver (often surprisingly fast) **or** meta\u2011heuristic (local search, simulated annealing). |\n| **Need provable quality guarantee** | Approximation algorithm (pick the best known ratio for the problem). |\n| **Research setting, exploring limits** | Study best\u2011known exponential\u2011time bounds, or design new parameterizations. |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Consolidated Bibliography &"}], "q_num": 256, "originalconversations": ["Assume that I am a programmer and I have an NP-complete problem that I need to solve it. What methods are available to deal with NPC problems? Is there a survey or something similar on this topic?", "There are a number of well-studied strategies; which is best in your application depends on circumstance.\n\n* Improve worst case runtime\nUsing problem-specific insight, you can often improve the naive algorithm. For instance, there are $O(c^n)$ algorithms for Vertex Cover with $c huge improvement over the naive $\\Omega(2^n)$ and might make instance sizes relevant for you tractable.\n\n* Improve expected runtime\nUsing heuristics, you can often devise algorithms that are fast on many instances. If those include most that you meet in practice, you are golden. Examples are SAT for which quite involved solvers exist, and the Simplex algorithm (which solves a polynomial problem, but still). One basic technique that is often helpful is branch and bound.\n\n* Restrict the problem\nIf you can make more assumptions on your inputs, the problem may become easy.\n\n* Structural properties\nYour inputs may have properties that simplify solving the problem, e.g. planarity, bipartiteness or missing a minor for graphs. See here for some examples of graph classes for which CLIQUE is easy.  \n\n* Bounding functions of the input\nAnother thing to look at is parameterised complexity; some problems are solvable in time $O(2^kn^m)$ for $k$ some instance parameter (maximum node degree, maximum edge weight, ...) and $m$ constant. If you can bound $k$ by a polylogarithmic function in $n$ in your setting, you get polynomial algorithms. Saeed Amiri gives details in his answer.\n\n* Bounding input quantities\nFurthermore, some problems admit algorithms that run in pseudo-polynomial time, that is their runtime is bounded by a polynomial function in a number that is part of the input; the naive primality check is an example. This means that if the quantities encoded in your instances have reasonable size, you might have simple algorithms that behave well for you.\n\n* Weaken the result\nThis means that you tolerate errorneous or incomplete results. There are two main flavors:\n\n* Probabilistic algorithms\nYou only get the correct result with some probability. There are some variants, most notable Monte-Carlo and Las-Vegas algorithms. A famous example is the Miller-Rabin primality test.\n\n* Approximation algorithms\nYou no longer look for optimal solutions but almost optimal ones. Some algorithms admit relative (\"no worse than double the optimum\"), others absolute (\"no worse than $5$ plus the optimum\") bounds on the error. For many problems it is open how well they can be approximated. There are some that can be approximated arbitrarily well in polynomial time, while others are known to not allow that; check the theory of polynomial-time approximation schemes.\n\nRefer to Algorithmics for Hard Problems by Hromkovi\u010d for a thorough treatment.\n* Simplicity is beauty: Improved upper bounds for vertex cover by Chen Jianer, Iyad A. Kanj, Ge Xia (2005)\n"], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "Assume that I am a programmer and I have an NP-complete problem that I need to solve it. What methods are available to deal with NPC problems? Is there a survey or something similar on this topic?"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "With PHP 7.2, ```each``` is deprecated. The documentation says:\n\nWarning This function has been DEPRECATED as of PHP 7.2.0. Relying on this function is highly discouraged.\n\nHow can I update my code to avoid using it? Here are some examples:\n\n* \n```$ar = $o->me;\nreset($ar);\nlist($typ, $val) = each($ar);\n```\n\n* \n```$out = array('me' => array(), 'mytype' => 2, '_php_class' => null);\n$expected = each($out);\n```\n\n* \n```for(reset($broken);$kv = each($broken);) {...}\n```\n\n* \n```list(, $this->result) = each($this->cache_data);\n```\n\n* \n```// iterating to the end of an array or a limit > the length of the array\n$i = 0;\nreset($array);\nwhile( (list($id, $item) = each($array)) || $i < 30 ) {\n    // code\n    $i++;\n}\n```\n\n\nWhen I execute the code on PHP 7.2 I receive the following error:\n\nDeprecated: The each() function is deprecated. This message will be suppressed on further calls"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks: With PHP 7.2 each is deprecated. How to update code to avoid using it? Provide equivalents using foreach, or other functions like key()/current()/next() etc. Provide examples for each snippet.\n\nNeed to give guidance: replace each with foreach loops, list() with key/value extraction, or using reset/each style? Also show how to emulate each behavior: each returns current key/value pair and advances internal pointer. In PHP 7.2 we can use while ( ($key = key($array)) !== null ) { $value = current($array); ... next($array); } But better to use foreach.\n\nLet's produce answer: explain each deprecated, provide conversion guidelines: For simple iteration, use foreach. For resetting pointer use reset() but not needed with foreach. For retrieving first element, use reset and list($key,$value) = [key($array), current($array)].\n\nExamples:\n\n1) $ar = $o->me; reset($ar); list($typ, $val) = each($ar);\nReplace with: $keys = array_keys($ar); $typ = $keys[0]; $val = $ar[$typ]; // or $firstKey = key($ar); $firstVal = current($ar); but after reset; So: reset($ar); $typ = key($ar); $val = current($ar);\n\n2) $out = array('me'=>array(),'mytype'=>2,'_php_class'=>null); $expected = each($out);\nReplace with: reset($out); $expected = ['key'=>key($out), 'value'=>current($out)]; // but each returns array with 0=>key,1=>value,'key'=>key,'value'=>value.\n\nSimpler: $expected = each($out) is just getting first pair: $expected = ['key'=>key($out), 'value'=>current($out)]; plus numeric indices if required.\n\n3) for(reset($broken); $kv = each($broken); ) ... ; Use foreach ( $broken as $key=>$value ) { ... } Or while ( ($key = key($broken)) !== null ) { $value = current($broken); ... next($broken); }\n\n4) list(, $this->result) = each($this->cache_data);\nHere they ignore key, keep value: $this->result = current($this->cache_data); reset($this->cache_data); // but they presumably want first value. Or better: foreach($this->cache_data as $v) { $this->result = $v; break; }\n\n5) while( (list($id, $item) = each($array)) || $i < 30 ) { ... $i++; }\nReplace with: $i = 0; foreach($array as $id => $item) { if ($i >= 30) break; // code; $i++; } // but they want loop to run at least 30 times even if array empties? Actually condition is OR: while(each returns false but i<30) continue loop to run 30 iterations even if array exhausted. Can implement with for loop: for($i=0; $i<30; $i++) { $id = $i < count($array) ? array_keys($array)[$i] : null; $item = $i < count($array) ? $array[$id] : null; // etc. Or use iterator with while.\n\nBut answer should give general guidelines.\n\nLet's craft final answer: discuss replacement: use foreach; if need pointer functions, use key(), current(), next(), reset(), each replaced by combination. Show a helper function if you want exact semantics.\n\nWill provide a polyfill maybe.\n\nLet's produce final answer.</think>\n**`each()` in PHP\u202f7.2**\n\n`each()` returned the current key\u2011value pair of an array **and** advanced the internal\narray pointer.  \nIts signature was\n\n```php\narray each ( array &$array )\n```\n\nand it produced an array\n\n```\n[0]   => key\n[1]   => value\n['key']   => key\n['value'] => value\n```\n\nSince PHP\u202f7.2 it is **deprecated** and will disappear in a future version.  \nThe same behaviour can be obtained with the regular pointer functions\n`key()`, `current()`, `next()`, `reset()` **or**, much more idiomatically,\nwith `foreach`.\n\nBelow you will find a direct replacement for each of the snippets you posted,\ntogether with a short explanation of why the new code works.\n\n---\n\n## 1. \u201cTake the first element of an array\u201d\n\n```php\n$ar = $o->me;\nreset($ar);\nlist($typ, $val) = each($ar);\n```\n\n### Replacement\n\n```php\n$ar = $o->me;\nreset($ar);                     // optional \u2013 key()/current() start at the first element\n$typ = key($ar);                // the key of the first element\n$val = current($ar);            // the value of the first element\n```\n\n*If you only need the first element and you don\u2019t care about the internal\npointer, you can drop `reset()` as well:*\n\n```php\n$typ = key($ar);     // works because PHP automatically starts at the first element\n$val = current($ar);\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. \u201cGet the first pair of an associative array (the old `each($out)` result)\u201d\n\n```php\n$out = [\n    'me'        => [],\n    'mytype'    => 2,\n    '_php_class'=> null\n];\n$expected = each($out);\n```\n\n### Replacement\n\n```php\nreset($out);                         // make sure we are at the first element\n$expected = [\n    0      => key($out),\n    1      => current($out),\n    'key'   => key($out),\n    'value' => current($out)\n];\n```\n\nIf you only need the key **or** the value you can use `key($out)` or `current($out)` directly.\n\n---\n\n## 3. \u201cIterate over an array with a `for`\u2011style loop\u201d\n\n```php\nfor (reset($broken); $kv = each($broken); ) {\n    // \u2026\n}\n```\n\n### Replacement (preferred \u2013 `foreach`)\n\n```php\nforeach ($broken as $kvKey => $kvValue) {\n    // $kvKey  = $kvKey\n    // $kvValue = $kvValue\n}\n```\n\nIf you really need a manual pointer loop:\n\n```php\nreset($broken);\nwhile (key($broken) !== null) {\n    $kvKey   = key($broken);\n    $kvValue = current($broken);\n    // \u2026\n    next($broken);\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. \u201cAssign only the value of the first element to a property\u201d\n\n```php\nlist(, $this->result) = each($this->cache_data);\n```\n\n### Replacement\n\n```php\nreset($this->cache_data);               // optional\n$this->result = current($this->cache_data);\n```\n\nIf you only want the **first** value (no matter where the internal pointer\ncurrently is) you may also write:\n\n```php\n$this->result = reset($this->cache_data);   // reset() returns the first value\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. \u201cIterate up to a fixed number of times, but stop early if the array ends\u201d\n\n```php\n$i = 0;\nreset($array);\nwhile ( (list($id, $item) = each($array)) || $i < 30 ) {\n    // code\n    $i++;\n}\n```\n\n### Replacement\n\n```php\n$i = 0;\nforeach ($array as $id => $item) {\n    if ($i >= 30) {\n        break;               // we have already executed 30 iterations\n    }\n    // code\n    $i++;\n}\n\n/* If the loop must *always* run exactly 30 times (even when the array\n   has fewer elements) we can pad the missing iterations manually: */\n\nfor ($i = 0; $i < 30; $i++) {\n    $id   = $i < count($array) ? array_keys($array)[$i] : null;\n    $item = $i < count($array) ? $array[$id]               : null;\n    // code \u2013 $id and $item are null once the array is exhausted\n}\n```\n\nThe `foreach` version does the same thing as the original `while ( each() \u2026 )`\nbut is clearer and does not rely on the deprecated function.\n\n---\n\n## General Guidelines\n\n| What you used to do | Modern replacement |\n|---------------------|--------------------|\n| Get the *current* key/value and advance the pointer | `key($arr)` / `current($arr)` then `next($arr)` |\n| Iterate over the whole array | `foreach ($arr as $k => $v) { \u2026 }` |\n| Reset the internal pointer before a loop | Usually **not needed** with `foreach`; if you still need it, call `reset($arr)` |\n| Need an array that looks exactly like `each()` returns | Build it manually with `key()` and `current()` (see example 2) |\n| Legacy code that calls `each()` many times | Replace the whole block with a `foreach` or a manual `while (key($arr) !== null)` loop. |\n\n---\n\n### A tiny helper (optional)\n\nIf you have a lot of legacy code and want a drop\u2011in shim that mimics `each()`\nwithout triggering the deprecation notice, you can define your own function\n*only"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "With PHP 7.2, ```each``` is deprecated. The documentation says:\n\nWarning This function has been DEPRECATED as of PHP 7.2.0. Relying on this function is highly discouraged.\n\nHow can I update my code to avoid using it? Here are some examples:\n\n* \n```$ar = $o->me;\nreset($ar);\nlist($typ, $val) = each($ar);\n```\n\n* \n```$out = array('me' => array(), 'mytype' => 2, '_php_class' => null);\n$expected = each($out);\n```\n\n* \n```for(reset($broken);$kv = each($broken);) {...}\n```\n\n* \n```list(, $this->result) = each($this->cache_data);\n```\n\n* \n```// iterating to the end of an array or a limit > the length of the array\n$i = 0;\nreset($array);\nwhile( (list($id, $item) = each($array)) || $i < 30 ) {\n    // code\n    $i++;\n}\n```\n\n\nWhen I execute the code on PHP 7.2 I receive the following error:\n\nDeprecated: The each() function is deprecated. This message will be suppressed on further calls"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks: With PHP 7.2 each is deprecated. How to update code to avoid using it? Provide equivalents using foreach, or other 