nt('RMS of non\u2011zero harmonics:', rms)\n```\n\nA low RMS (approaching \\(1/\\sqrt{N}\\)) confirms uniformity.\n\n---\n\n## Summary checklist\n\n| Step | Action | What to look for |\n|------|--------|------------------|\n| **Generate** | Use one of the methods above (Gaussian normalisation is simplest and robust)."}], "q_num": 41, "originalconversations": ["I am wondering how to generate uniformly distributed points on the surface of the 3-d unit sphere? Also after generating those points, what is the best way to visualize and check whether they are truly uniform on the surface $x^2+y^2+z^2=1$?", "A standard method is to generate three standard normals and construct a unit vector from them. That is, when $X_i \\sim N(0,1)$ and $\\lambda^2 = X_1^2 + X_2^2 + X_3^2$, then $(X_1/\\lambda, X_2/\\lambda, X_3/\\lambda)$ is uniformly distributed on the sphere.  This method works well for $d$-dimensional spheres, too.\nIn 3D you can use rejection sampling: draw $X_i$ from a uniform$[-1,1]$ distribution until the length of $(X_1, X_2, X_3)$ is less than or equal to 1, then--just as with the preceding method--normalize the vector to unit length.  The expected number of trials per spherical point equals $2^3/(4 \\pi / 3)$ = 1.91.  In higher dimensions the expected number of trials gets so large this rapidly becomes impracticable.\nThere are many ways to check uniformity.  A neat way, although somewhat computationally intensive, is with Ripley's K function.  The expected number of points within (3D Euclidean) distance $\\rho$ of any location on the sphere is proportional to the area of the sphere within distance $\\rho$, which equals $\\pi\\rho^2$.  By computing all interpoint distances you can compare the data to this ideal.\nGeneral principles of constructing statistical graphics suggest a good way to make the comparison is to plot variance-stabilized residuals $e_i(d_{[i]} - e_i)$ against $i = 1, 2, \\ldots, n(n-1)/2=m$ where $d_{[i]}$ is the $i^\\text{th}$ smallest of the mutual distances and $e_i = 2\\sqrt{i/m}$.  The plot should be close to zero. "], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "I am wondering how to generate uniformly distributed points on the surface of the 3-d unit sphere? Also after generating those points, what is the best way to visualize and check whether they are truly uniform on the surface $x^2+y^2+z^2=1$?"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "I see hugely varied performance depending on how many newlines there are in the file I'm visiting.\nHere's an example. I have two JSON files:\n```$ wget https://github.com/Wilfred/ReVo-utilities/blob/a4bdc40dd2656c496defc461fc19c403c8306d9f/revo-export/dictionary.json?raw=true -O one_line.json\n$ python -m json.tool pretty_printed.json\n```\nThese are two JSON files with the same content. ```one_line.json``` is 18MiB of JSON without any newlines. ```pretty_printed.json``` has newlines and whitespace added, making it 41MiB.\nHowever, the bigger file split over many lines is much faster to open in Emacs, both in Javascript mode and Fundamental mode.\nWhy does Emacs have such poor performance with long lines, since it's actually fewer bytes? Is there anything I can do to improve performance without reformatting the data outside of Emacs?"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer: Emacs performance with long lines. Explanation: Emacs has to deal with line-based operations; long lines cause costly redisplay, col calculations, syntax highlighting, etc. Internally uses buffers with text properties and line start positions; long lines cause scanning each time for line boundaries, also font lock uses regex that can become O(n) per line. Also Emacs uses Emacs Lisp for many functions, which may be slower on long strings. Emacs uses column calculations that traverse characters; each line the whole line may be scanned when moving point, etc. Scrolling and display engine need to consider line breaks. For huge lines, display may need to truncate or wrap; line wrapping is expensive because need to compute display width of each character. Also most operations are O(N) in line length. Solutions: enable `so-long-mode` (since Emacs 27.1), set `so-long-action` to `t` to use fundamental mode, disable font-lock, line-number-mode, etc. Use `global-so-long-mode`. Also `M-x whitespace-mode`? Not. Suggest using `global-so-long-mode`, `so-long` library for handling \"so long lines\". Also consider setting `auto-fill-mode` off, turn off `jit-lock-mode`/font-lock, `global-font-lock-mode` off, use `view-mode`, `read-only`, or `M-x hexl-mode`. Use `set-buffer-multibyte`? Not.\n\nAlso can use `M-x whitespace-mode` not.\n\nEmacs can handle large files via `jka-compr`? No.\n\nOther tips: `M-x fundamental-mode`, `M-x view-mode` with `view-mode`? Use `setq inhibit-local-menu-bar t`. Use `set-buffer-major-mode`?\n\nLarge single line reduces the number of line break positions, but Emacs internally uses line start positions stored in a vector; scanning can be fast. However many functions assume line ends at newline char, and for long lines they repeatedly need to scan entire line for things like column width, syntax highlighting. Also for JSON, font lock may have regex scanning that can be expensive on long lines.\n\nThus answer: Explanation of Emacs's line orientation, display, font-lock, line break searching; Tools: `so-long-mode`, `global-so-long-mode`, increase `so-long-max-lines`, `so-long-max-chars`; use `M-x fundamental-mode` or `M-x text-mode` without font-lock; disable `jit-lock-mode`, `linum-mode`, `display-line-numbers-mode`; use `global-hl-line-mode` off, `column-number-mode` off.\n\nAdvice: Use `M-x json-mode`? That uses font lock.\n\nTo improve: open file with `C-x C-f` and prefix `C-u` to disable mode detection (`find-file-literally`), view with `view-mode`, `hexl-mode`, or `M-x so-long-mode`. Use `M-x recover-file`? Not.\n\nExplain that the underlying reason is that Emacs stores text as a doubly-linked list of characters (or gaps buffer) and many operations are O(line length). So file with long lines slower. Provide advice.\n\nNow produce final answer in `<answer>` tags.</think>\n<answer>\n**Why a single very\u2011long line is slow in Emacs**\n\nEmacs was designed as a *line\u2011oriented* editor.  \nEven though the internal representation (a gap buffer) stores characters\ncontinuously, almost every operation that touches the buffer assumes that\nlines are relatively short:\n\n| Operation | What Emacs has to do | Cost on a normal line | Cost on a 18\u202fMiB line |\n|-----------|----------------------|-----------------------|----------------------|\n| **Moving point** (`C-f`, `C-b`, `M\u2011>`, `M\u2011<`) | Find the next/previous newline to compute line/column numbers | Scan a few dozen characters | Scan *the whole 18\u202fMiB* to know the line limits |\n| **Redisplay** (screen update, scrolling) | Compute the visual width of every character from the line start to the part that is on screen | O(screen\u2011width) | O(line\u2011length) \u2013 the whole line may be scanned to know where to wrap/truncate |\n| **Syntax highlighting / Font\u2011lock** | Run regular\u2011expression searches (via `jit-lock`) over the line to apply faces | Small regex matches, limited to a few characters | The same regex is run over a huge string; many regex engines (including Emacs\u2019s) degenerate to O(N\u00b2) on pathological inputs |\n| **Line\u2011number / column\u2011number display** | Count newlines up to point, then count characters in the current line | Fast | Requires walking the entire giant line |\n| **`linum-mode` / `display-line-numbers-mode`** | Ask \u201cwhat line am I on?\u201d \u2192 same as point\u2011movement cost | Negligible | Very expensive |\n\nBecause Emacs has to *scan the line* many times, a file that is only half the size\n(18\u202fMiB vs. 41\u202fMiB) can be dramatically slower if it consists of a single line.\nThe problem is not the *byte count* but the *algorithmic cost* of operations that\nare linear in the line length.\n\n---\n\n**What you can do without re\u2011formatting the file**\n\n1. **Enable the built\u2011in \u201cso\u2011long\u201d mode**  \n   ```elisp\n   (global-so-long-mode 1)\n   ```  \n   *What it does*: when a buffer contains a line longer than\n   `so-long-max-line-length` (default 1000 characters) it automatically\n   switches to a lightweight major mode (usually `fundamental-mode`) and\n   disables costly features such as `font-lock`, `jit-lock`, `line-number-mode`,\n   `display-line-numbers-mode`, `column-number-mode`, `hl-line-mode`, etc.\n   This is the simplest way to make very long\u2011line files snappy.\n\n2. **Open the file \u201cliterally\u201d** \u2013 bypass all automatic mode activation and\n   font\u2011locking:  \n   ```elisp\n   C-x C-f filename RET C-u M-x find-file-literally RET\n   ```  \n   This gives you a read\u2011only buffer that only displays raw characters.\n   You can later switch to a proper mode if you need it.\n\n3. **Temporarily turn off font\u2011lock and JIT\u2011lock**  \n   ```elisp\n   (font-lock-mode -1)   ;; no syntax highlighting\n   (jit-lock-mode -1)    ;; stop incremental regex scanning\n   ```\n   Even in `json-mode` you can keep the mode active but with highlighting\n   disabled, which removes the most expensive regex work.\n\n4. **Disable line\u2011number and column\u2011number displays**  \n   ```elisp\n   (display-line-numbers-mode -1)\n   (column-number-mode -1)\n   (line-number-mode -1)\n   ```\n   These modes force Emacs to recompute line numbers on every display\n   update.\n\n5. **Turn off wrapping** (or force truncation)  \n   ```elisp\n   (setq truncate-lines t)   ;; do not try to wrap the long line\n   (setq word-wrap nil)      ;; disable soft wrap\n   ```\n   When Emacs knows the line will be truncated it doesn\u2019t have to scan\n   the whole line to calculate wrap points.\n\n6. **Use a read\u2011only \u201cview\u201d mode**  \n   ```elisp\n   M-x view-mode\n   ```\n   `view-mode` disables editing commands that would otherwise modify the\n   buffer and also disables many minor modes.\n\n7. **If you really need editing capabilities**, consider splitting the line\n   *inside Emacs* once you have opened the file:\n\n   ```elisp\n   (replace-string \"\" \"\\n\")   ;; insert line breaks at convenient places\n   ```\n   (Do this on a copy of the file or after saving a backup.)\n\n8. **Upgrade Emacs** \u2013 Emacs\u202f28+ has many performance improvements in the\n   redisplay code and in `jit-lock`. The \u201cso\u2011long\u201d package is built\u2011in from\n   Emacs\u202f27.1 onward.\n\n---\n\n**Quick checklist for a \u201cbig\u2011line\u201d file**\n\n```elisp\n;; 1. Open it literally\nC-x C-f big.json RET C-u M-x find-file-literally RET\n\n;; 2. Make the buffer lightweight\n(when (not (derived-mode-p 'fundamental-mode))\n  (fundamental-mode))\n\n;; 3. Disable everything that scans the line\n(font-lock-mode -1)\n(jit-lock-mode -1)\n(display-line-numbers-mode -1)\n(column-number-mode -1)\n(line-number-mode -1)\n(setq truncate-lines t\n      word-wrap nil)\n```\n\nAfter these steps the same 18\u202fMiB single\u2011line JSON file scrolls and moves\nas fast as a 41\u202fMiB prettified file.\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line**\n\nEmacs\u2019s slowness on huge single\u2011line buffers is a consequence of its\nline\u2011oriented design: many core operations must scan the entire line to\ndetermine its length, column number, wrapping points, and to apply\nfont\u2011lock. By disabling the features that trigger those scans\u2014most\neffectively with `so-long-mode` or by opening the file literally\u2014you can\nrecover interactive speed without having to re\u2011format the JSON outside\nEmacs."}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I see hugely varied performance depending on how many newlines there are in the file I'm visiting.\nHere's an example. I have two JSON files:\n```$ wget https://github.com/Wilfred/ReVo-utilities/blob/a4bdc40dd2656c496defc461fc19c403c8306d9f/revo-export/dictionary.json?raw=true -O one_line.json\n$ python -m json.tool pretty_printed.json\n```\nThese are two JSON files with the same content. ```one_line.json``` is 18MiB of JSON without any newlines. ```pretty_printed.json``` has newlines and whitespace added, making it 41MiB.\nHowever, the bigger file split over many lines is much faster to open in Emacs, both in Javascript mode and Fundamental mode.\nWhy does Emacs have such poor performance with long lines, since it's actually fewer bytes? Is there anything I can do to improve performance without reformatting the data outside of Emacs?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer: Emacs performance with long lines. Explanation: Emacs has to deal with line-based operations; long lines cause costly redisplay, col calculations, syntax highlighting, etc. Internally uses buffers with text properties and line start positions; long lines cause scanning each time for line boundaries, also font lock uses regex that can become O(n) per line. Also Emacs uses Emacs Lisp for many functions, which may be slower on long strings. Emacs uses column calculations that traverse characters; each line the whole line may be scanned when moving point, etc. Scrolling and display engine need to consider line breaks. For huge lines, display may need to truncate or wrap; line wrapping is expensive because need to compute display width of each character. Also most operations are O(N) in line length. Solutions: enable `so-long-mode` (since Emacs 27.1), set `so-long-action` to `t` to use fundamental mode, disable font-lock, line-number-mode, etc. Use `global-so-long-mode`. Also `M-x whitespace-mode`? Not. Suggest using `global-so-long-mode`, `so-long` library for handling \"so long lines\". Also consider setting `auto-fill-mode` off, turn off `jit-lock-mode`/font-lock, `global-font-lock-mode` off, use `view-mode`, `read-only`, or `M-x hexl-mode`. Use `set-buffer-multibyte`? Not.\n\nAlso can use `M-x whitespace-mode` not.\n\nEmacs can handle large files via `jka-compr`? No.\n\nOther tips: `M-x fundamental-mode`, `M-x view-mode` with `view-mode`? Use `setq inhibit-local-menu-bar t`. Use `set-buffer-major-mode`?\n\nLarge single line reduces the number of line break positions, but Emacs internally uses line start positions stored in a vector; scanning can be fast. However many functions assume line ends at newline char, and for long lines they repeatedly need to scan entire line for things like column width, syntax highlighting. Also for JSON, font lock may have regex scanning that can be expensive on long lines.\n\nThus answer: Explanation of Emacs's line orientation, display, font-lock, line break searching; Tools: `so-long-mode`, `global-so-long-mode`, increase `so-long-max-lines`, `so-long-max-chars`; use `M-x fundamental-mode` or `M-x text-mode` without font-lock; disable `jit-lock-mode`, `linum-mode`, `display-line-numbers-mode`; use `global-hl-line-mode` off, `column-number-mode` off.\n\nAdvice: Use `M-x json-mode`? That uses font lock.\n\nTo improve: open file with `C-x C-f` and prefix `C-u` to disable mode detection (`find-file-literally`), view with `view-mode`, `hexl-mode`, or `M-x so-long-mode`. Use `M-x recover-file`? Not.\n\nExplain that the underlying reason is that Emacs stores text as a doubly-linked list of characters (or gaps buffer) and many operations are O(line length). So file with long lines slower. Provide advice.\n\nNow produce final answer in `<answer>` tags.</think>\n<answer>\n**Why a single very\u2011long line is slow in Emacs**\n\nEmacs was designed as a *line\u2011oriented* editor.  \nEven though the internal representation (a gap buffer) stores characters\ncontinuously, almost every operation that touches the buffer assumes that\nlines are relatively short:\n\n| Operation | What Emacs has to do | Cost on a normal line | Cost on a 18\u202fMiB line |\n|-----------|----------------------|-----------------------|----------------------|\n| **Moving point** (`C-f`, `C-b`, `M\u2011>`, `M\u2011<`) | Find the next/previous newline to compute line/column numbers | Scan a few dozen characters | Scan *the whole 18\u202fMiB* to know the line limits |\n| **Redisp