   ```  \n   This gives you a read\u2011only buffer that only displays raw characters.\n   You can later switch to a proper mode if you need it.\n\n3. **Temporarily turn off font\u2011lock and JIT\u2011lock**  \n   ```elisp\n   (font-lock-mode -1)   ;; no syntax highlighting\n   (jit-lock-mode -1)    ;; stop incremental regex scanning\n   ```\n   Even in `json-mode` you can keep the mode active but with highlighting\n   disabled, which removes the most expensive regex work.\n\n4. **Disable line\u2011number and column\u2011number displays**  \n   ```elisp\n   (display-line-numbers-mode -1)\n   (column-number-mode -1)\n   (line-number-mode -1)\n   ```\n   These modes force Emacs to recompute line numbers on every display\n   update.\n\n5. **Turn off wrapping** (or force truncation)  \n   ```elisp\n   (setq truncate-lines t)   ;; do not try to wrap the long line\n   (setq word-wrap nil)      ;; disable soft wrap\n   ```\n   When Emacs knows the line will be truncated it doesn\u2019t have to scan\n   the whole line to calculate wrap points.\n\n6. **Use a read\u2011only \u201cview\u201d mode**  \n   ```elisp\n   M-x view-mode\n   ```\n   `view-mode` disables editing commands that would otherwise modify the\n   buffer and also disables many minor modes.\n\n7. **If you really need editing capabilities**, consider splitting the line\n   *inside Emacs* once you have opened the file:\n\n   ```elisp\n   (replace-string \"\" \"\\n\")   ;; insert line breaks at convenient places\n   ```\n   (Do this on a copy of the file or after saving a backup.)\n\n8. **Upgrade Emacs** \u2013 Emacs\u202f28+ has many performance improvements in the\n   redisplay code and in `jit-lock`. The \u201cso\u2011long\u201d package is built\u2011in from\n   Emacs\u202f27.1 onward.\n\n---\n\n**Quick checklist for a \u201cbig\u2011line\u201d file**\n\n```elisp\n;; 1. Open it literally\nC-x C-f big.json RET C-u M-x find-file-literally RET\n\n;; 2. Make the buffer lightweight\n(when (not (derived-mode-p 'fundamental-mode))\n  (fundamental-mode))\n\n;; 3. Disable everything that scans the line\n(font-lock-mode -1)\n(jit-lock-mode -1)\n(display-line-numbers-mode -1)\n(column-number-mode -1)\n(line-number-mode -1)\n(setq truncate-lines t\n      word-wrap nil)\n```\n\nAfter these steps the same 18\u202fMiB single\u2011line JSON file scrolls and moves\nas fast as a 41\u202fMiB prettified file.\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line**\n\nEmacs\u2019s slowness on huge single\u2011line buffers is a consequence of its\nline\u2011oriented design: many core operations must scan the entire line to\ndetermine its length, column number, wrapping points, and to apply\nfont\u2011lock. By disabling the features that trigger those scans\u2014most\neffectively with `so-long-mode` or by opening the file literally\u2014you can\nrecover interactive speed without having to re\u2011format the JSON outside\nEmacs."}], "q_num": 234, "originalconversations": ["I see hugely varied performance depending on how many newlines there are in the file I'm visiting.\nHere's an example. I have two JSON files:\n```$ wget https://github.com/Wilfred/ReVo-utilities/blob/a4bdc40dd2656c496defc461fc19c403c8306d9f/revo-export/dictionary.json?raw=true -O one_line.json\n$ python -m json.tool pretty_printed.json\n```\nThese are two JSON files with the same content. ```one_line.json``` is 18MiB of JSON without any newlines. ```pretty_printed.json``` has newlines and whitespace added, making it 41MiB.\nHowever, the bigger file split over many lines is much faster to open in Emacs, both in Javascript mode and Fundamental mode.\nWhy does Emacs have such poor performance with long lines, since it's actually fewer bytes? Is there anything I can do to improve performance without reformatting the data outside of Emacs?", "Emacs' handling of long lines is not very well optimized. For a number of operations, Emacs has to scan the whole line repeatedly. For example, to display a line, Emacs has to figure out the height of the line, which requires scanning the whole line to find the tallest glyph. Additionally, scanning for bidirectional display eats up a lot of time. You can get some additional information in, for example, the docstring of ```cache-long-line-scans``` (renamed ```cache-long-scans``` in 24.4).\n\nYou can try and see if setting ```bidi-paragraph-direction``` to ```left-to-right``` and setting ```bidi-inhibit-bpa``` (new in Emacs 27, see footnote 1) to ```t``` improves the speed for you (see footnote 2). This removes some significant contributors to line scans, but sadly not the only ones.\n\nThe best option is to add newlines. You can pipe a JSON file through e.g. ```python -c 'import json, sys ; json.dump(json.load(sys.stdin), sys.stdout, indent=2)'``` to add newlines and improve readability in general.\n\nFootnote 1: ```bidi-inhibit-bpa``` is new in Emacs 27.  Setting it to ```t``` can have a noticeable (sometimes dramatic) effect in cases where (a) a physical line is very long, and (b) the line includes many parentheses and/or braces, especially if they are nested.\nFootnote 2: There is also ```bidi-display-reordering```, but this is only for internal/debugging purposes.  Setting it to ```nil``` may improve performance, however that is an unsupported setting which may cause other problems, so it is not recommended.  In Emacs 27, the combination of setting ```bidi-paragraph-direction``` and ```bidi-inhibit-bpa``` is a supported configuration which provides approximately the same performance boost."], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "I see hugely varied performance depending on how many newlines there are in the file I'm visiting.\nHere's an example. I have two JSON files:\n```$ wget https://github.com/Wilfred/ReVo-utilities/blob/a4bdc40dd2656c496defc461fc19c403c8306d9f/revo-export/dictionary.json?raw=true -O one_line.json\n$ python -m json.tool pretty_printed.json\n```\nThese are two JSON files with the same content. ```one_line.json``` is 18MiB of JSON without any newlines. ```pretty_printed.json``` has newlines and whitespace added, making it 41MiB.\nHowever, the bigger file split over many lines is much faster to open in Emacs, both in Javascript mode and Fundamental mode.\nWhy does Emacs have such poor performance with long lines, since it's actually fewer bytes? Is there anything I can do to improve performance without reformatting the data outside of Emacs?"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "When encoding a logic into a proof assistant such as Coq or Isabelle, a choice needs to be made between using a shallow and a deep embedding. In a shallow embedding logical formulas are written directly in the logic of the theorem prover, whereas in a deep embedding logical formulas are represented as a datatype. \n\n* What are the advantages and\nlimitations of the various\napproaches?\n* Are there any guidelines available for determining which to use? \n* Is it possible to switch between the two representations in any systematic fashion?\n\nAs motivation, I would like to encode various security related logics into Coq and am wondering what the pros and cons of the different approaches are."}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer about shallow vs deep embedding, pros/cons, guidelines, possibility to switch. Provide detailed discussion focusing on Coq but generally. Also mention examples, security logics. Provide references. Output inside <answer> tags.</think>\n<answer>\n<h2>Short answer</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Shallow embedding</strong> \u2013 write the object\u2011logic directly in the host logic (Coq/Isabelle).  It gives you all the host\u2011logic\u2019s inference, automation, type\u2011checking and proof facilities \u201cfor free\u201d.  It is ideal when you only need to reason *about* formulas, not *manipulate* their syntax.</li>\n  <li><strong>Deep embedding</strong> \u2013 define an inductive datatype that represents the syntax of the object\u2011logic and give a separate (usually recursive) \u201cinterpretation\u201d or \u201cderivation\u201d relation on that datatype.  It lets you talk about syntactic properties (substitution, renaming, normalisation, meta\u2011theoretic results) and to define multiple, possibly conflicting, semantics.</li>\n  <li>Both styles can coexist: a deep embedding gives you a syntax that can be *interpreted* in a shallow embedding, and conversely a shallow embedding can be *re\u2011ified* into a deep term for meta\u2011reasoning.</li>\n</ul>\n\nBelow is a detailed comparison, a set of practical guidelines, and a discussion of systematic transformations between the two styles, with concrete Coq sketches and pointers to the literature that are especially relevant for security logics (e.g., access\u2011control logics, epistemic logics, crypto\u2011protocol logics).\n\n---\n\n<h2>1.  Shallow vs. Deep embedding \u2013 definitions</h2>\n\n| Aspect | Shallow embedding | Deep embedding |\n|--------|-------------------|----------------|\n| **Representation** | Object\u2011logic connectives are the host\u2011logic\u2019s connectives (=>, /\\ , \\/ , \u2200, \u2026) and host types are used for object types. | A datatype <code>expr</code> (or similar) that enumerates all syntactic forms, usually via an <code>Inductive</code> definition. |\n| **Inference** | Directly uses host\u2019s inference rules (Coq\u2019s <code>Qed</code>, <code>apply</code>, <code>auto</code>, etc.). | Inference is defined as an inductive relation <code>provable : expr \u2192 Prop</code> (or a judgmental typing relation). |\n| **Semantics** | Usually given by the host\u2019s semantics (Coq\u2019s constructive logic). | Given by a recursive interpreter <code>\u27e6\u00b7\u27e7 : expr \u2192 semantic\u2011domain</code> or by a proof\u2011system relation. |\n| **Meta\u2011theory** | Limited to what can be expressed about host terms (e.g., you cannot talk about the *shape* of a formula). | Full access to syntactic manipulation, substitution lemmas, structural induction, etc. |\n| **Automation** | Leverages all existing tactics, type\u2011class resolution, Ltac, OCaml plugins, etc. | Requires custom tactics or automation for the inductive judgment; often more boilerplate. |\n| **Trust** | Relies on the correctness of the host logic \u2013 no extra trusted code. | Adds a trusted layer: the inductive definition of syntax + the interpreter/derivation relation must be proved sound/complete. |\n| **Extensibility** | Adding a new connective often means adding a new host definition/notation, potentially changing many proofs. | Adding a connective only changes the <code>expr</code> datatype and its interpretation; existing proofs remain (if they are parametric). |\n\n---\n\n<h2>2.  Advantages & limitations</h2>\n\n<h3>2.1  Shallow embedding</h3>\n\n**Advantages**\n1. **Immediate use of the host logic** \u2013 you get quantifiers, equality, higher\u2011order functions, dependent types, and a mature automation stack without extra effort.\n2. **Concise definitions** \u2013 a modality such as <code>\u25a1 \u03c6</code> can be a simple Coq function <code>Box : Prop \u2192 Prop := fun \u03c6 => \u2026</code>.  No need for separate constructors.\n3. **Proof development is faster** \u2013 you can apply Coq\u2019s powerful tactics (e.g., <code>auto</code>, <code>eauto</code>, <code>lia</code>) directly on the object\u2011logic statements.\n4. **Better integration with extracted code** \u2013 if you want to run the logic or extract it to OCaml/Haskell, shallow embeddings are usually easier.\n5. **Proofs are usually more readable** for people already familiar with Coq.\n\n**Limitations**\n1. **No access to syntax** \u2013 you cannot state \u201cthe formula <code>\u03c6</code> contains no occurrences of a given variable\u201d or \u201cthe size of a formula is <code>n</code>\u201d.  Any meta\u2011theoretic property that depends on the concrete syntactic shape cannot be expressed.\n2. **Hard to reason about multiple semantics** \u2013 you cannot simultaneously interpret the same term under two different models without re\u2011defining it.\n3. **Difficult to encode non\u2011standard inference rules** (e.g., structural rules that manipulate *derivation trees* rather than formulas).  The host logic\u2019s typing discipline may block the rule you need.\n4. **Potential for \u201ccheating\u201d** \u2013 some security logics require *intuitionistic* or *linear* reasoning while Coq\u2019s base logic is constructive.  You have to encode the desired logic as a *predicate* on <code>Prop</code>, which can be subtle.\n5. **Limited support for \u201creflection\u201d** \u2013 writing decision procedures that examine the syntactic form of a proposition requires re\u2011ification (see 3.2 below).\n\n<h3>2.2  Deep embedding</h3>\n\n**Advantages**\n1. **Full syntactic control** \u2013 you can define substitution, capture\u2011avoiding renaming, size, free\u2011variables, etc., and prove lemmas about them.\n2. **Multiple or alternative semantics** \u2013 the same <code>expr</code> can be interpreted in several models (e.g., a Kripke semantics, a trace semantics, a probabilistic semantics) simply by providing different interpretation functions.\n3. **Meta\u2011theoretic reasoning** \u2013 you can formalise soundness, completeness, cut\u2011elimination, decidability, or complexity results *inside* Coq.\n4. **Uniform treatment of modalities** \u2013 e.g., for a modal logic you can have a constructor <code>Box : expr \u2192 expr</code> and then prove the modal axioms as inductive rules; you can also encode *binding* (quantifiers) as explicit <code>Lam</code> and <code>App</code> nodes, making it easier to manipulate them.\n5. **Separation of concerns** \u2013 the syntax is independent of the underlying Coq logic; you can change the proof system (e.g., from natural deduction to sequent calculus) without touching the syntax.\n\n**Limitations**\n1. **More boilerplate** \u2013 every connective needs a constructor, a \u201cwell\u2011formedness\u201d predicate (if you use a dependently typed syntax), a semantics, and often an induction principle.  Proofs become longer because you must repeatedly use the inductive judgment.\n2. **Automation is weaker** \u2013 generic tactics do not automatically work on <code>provable</code> judgments; you need to write custom tactics (e.g., <code>solve [ eauto using provable_ind ]</code>) or use reflection.\n3. **Trust overhead** \u2013 the correctness of the whole development now depends on the soundness proof of the interpretation relation; any mistake there may lead to an unsound logic.\n4. **Performance** \u2013 induction over a large syntax tree may be slower, and extracted programs may need a lot of runtime overhead if the interpreter is executed.\n5. **Complexity of binding** \u2013 representing binders (\u2203, \u2200, \u03bb, \u25c7) requires either de\u202fBruijn indices, locally nameless representation, or nominal techniques.  Each adds its own proof burden.\n\n---\n\n<h2>3.  Guidelines for choosing a style</h2>\n\nBelow is a decision tree that many Coq/Isabelle practitioners find useful.  It is deliberately coarse\u2011grained; you can always combine the two styles (see \u00a74).\n\n<ol>\n  <li>\n    <strong>Do you need to reason about the *syntactic* structure of formulas?</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Yes \u2192 <strong>deep embedding</strong> (or a hybrid). Typical reasons: \n        <ul>\n          <li>Proving substitution lemmas, cut\u2011elimination, or decidability.</li>\n          <li>Manipulating derivation trees (e.g., proof\u2011search, automated verification of protocol traces).</li>\n          <li>Defining a language of *policies* or *rules* that will be *interpreted* later (common in access\u2011control logics).</li>\n        </ul>\n      </li>\n      <li>No \u2192 proceed to (2).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n\n  <li>\n    <strong>Do you have more than one semantics or want to change the semantics later?</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Yes \u2192 <strong>deep embedding</strong> (you can keep separate interpretation functions).</li>\n      <li>No \u2192 (3).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n\n  <li>\n    <strong>Is the object logic a *sub\u2011logic"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When encoding a logic into a proof assistant such as Coq or Isabelle, a choice needs to be made between using a shallow and a deep embedding. In a shallow embedding logical formulas are written directly in the logic of the theorem prover, whereas in a deep embedding logical formulas are represented as a datatype. \n\n* What are the advantages and\nlimitations of the various\napproaches?\n* Are there any guidelines available for determining which to use? \n* Is it possible to switch between the two representations in any systematic fashion?\n\nAs motivation, I would like to encode various security related logics into Coq and am wondering what the pros and cons of the different approaches are."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to answer about shallow vs deep embedding, pros/cons, guidelines, possibility to switch. Provide detailed discussion focusing on Coq but generally. Also mention examples, security logics. Provide references. Output inside <answer> tags.</think>\n<answer>\n<h2>Short answer</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Shallow embedding</strong> \u2013 write the object\u2011logic directly in the host logic (Coq/Isabelle).  It gives you all the host\u2011logic\u2019s inference, automation, type\u2011checking and proof facilities \u201cfor free\u201d.  It is ideal when you only need to reason *about* formulas, not *manipulate* their syntax.</li>\n  <li><strong>Deep embedding</strong> \u2013 define an inductive datatype that represents the syntax of the object\u2011logic and give a separate (usually recursive) \u201cinterpretation\u201d or \u201cderivation\u201d relation on that datatype.  It lets you talk about syntactic properties (substitution, renaming, normalisation, meta\u2011theoretic results) and to define multiple, possibly conflicting, semantics.</li>\n  <li>Both styles can coexist: a deep embedding gives you a syntax that can be *interpreted* in a shallow embedding, and conversely a shallow embedding can be *re\u2011ified* into a deep term for meta\u2011reasoning.</li>\n</ul>\n\nBelow is a detailed comparison, a set of practical guidelines, and a discussion of systematic transformations between the two styles, with concrete Coq sketches and pointers to the literature that are especially relevant for security logics (e.g., access\u2011control logics, epistemic logics, crypto\u2011protocol logics).\n\n---\n\n<h2>1.  Shallow vs. Deep embedding \u2013 definitions</h2>\n\n| Aspect | Shallow embedding | Deep embedding |\n|--------|-------------------|----------------|\n| **Representation** | Object\u2011logic connectives are the host\u2011logic\u2019s connectives (=>, /\\ , \\/ , \u2200, \u2026) and host types are used for object types. | A datatype <code>expr</code> (or similar) that enumerates all syntactic forms, usually via an <code>Inductive</code> definition. |\n| **Inference** | Directly uses host\u2019s inference rules (Coq\u2019s <code>Qed</code>, <code>apply</code>, <code>auto</code>, etc.). | Inference is defined as an inductive relation <code>provable : expr \u2192 Prop</code> (or a judgmental typing relation). |\n| **Semantics** | Usually given by the host\u2019s semantics (Coq\u2019s constructive logic). | Given by a recursive interpreter <code>\u27e6\u00b7\u27e7 : expr \u2192 semantic\u2011domain</code> or by a proof\u2011system relation. |\n| **Meta\u2011theory** | Limited to what can be expressed about host terms (e.g., you cannot talk about the *shape* of a formula). | Full access to syntactic manipulation, substitution lemmas, structural induction, etc. |\n| **Automation** | Leverages all existing tactics, type\u2011class resolution, Ltac, OCaml plugins, etc. | Requires custom tactics or automation for the inductive judgment; often more boilerplate. |\n| **Trust** | Relies on the correctness of the host logic \u2013 no extra trusted code. | Adds a trusted layer: the inductive definition of syntax + the interpreter/derivation relation must be proved sound/complete. |\n| **Extensibility** | Adding a new connective often means adding a new host definition/notation, potentially changing many proofs. | Adding a connective only changes the <code>expr</code> datatype and its interpretation; existing proofs remain (if they are parametric). |\n\n---\n\n<h2>2.  Advantages & limitations</h2>\n\n<h3>2.1  Shallow embedding</h3>\n\n**Advantages**\n1. **Immediate use of the host logic** \u2013 you get quantifiers, equality, higher\u2011order functions, dependent types, and a mature automation stack without extra effort.\n2. **Concise definitions** \u2013 a modality such as <code>\u25a1 \u03c6</code> can be a simple Coq function <code>Box : Prop \u2192 Prop := fun \u03c6 => \u2026</code>.  No need for separate constructors.\n3. **Proof development is faster** \u2013 you can apply Coq\u2019s powerful tactics (e.g., <code>auto</code>, <code>eauto</code>, <code>lia</code>) directly on the object\u2011logic statements.\n4. **Better integration with extracted code** \u2013 if you want to run the logic or extract it to OCaml/Haskell, shallow embeddings are usually easier.\n5. **Proofs are usually more readable** for people already familiar with Coq.\n\n**Limitations**\n1. **No access to syntax** \u2013 you cannot state \u201cthe formula <code>\u03c6</code> contains no occurrences of a given variable\u201d or \u201cthe size of a formula is <code>n</code>\u201d.  Any meta\u2011theoretic property that depends on the concrete syntactic shape cannot be expressed.\n2. **Hard to reason about multiple semantics** \u2013 you cannot simultaneously interpret the same term under two different models without re\u2011defining it.\n3. **Difficult to encode non\u2011standard inference rules** (e.g., structural rules that manipulate *derivation trees* rather than formulas).  The host logic\u2019s typing discipline may block the rule you need.\n4. **Potential for \u201ccheating\u201d** \u2013 some security logics require *intuitionistic* or *linear* reasoning while Coq\u2019s base logic is constructive.  You have to encode the desired logic as a *predicate* on <code>Prop</code>, which can be subtle.\n5. **Limited support for \u201creflection\u201d** \u2013 writing decision procedures that examine the syntactic form of a proposition requires re\u2011ification (see 3.2 below).\n\n<h3>2.2  Deep embedding</h3>\n\n**Advantages**\n1. **Full syntactic control** \u2013 you can define substitution, capture\u2011avoiding renaming, size, free\u2011variables, etc., and prove lemmas about them.\n2. **Multiple or alternative semantics** \u2013 the same <code>expr</code> can be interpreted in several models (e.g., a Kripke semantics, a trace semantics, a probabilistic semantics) simply by providing different interpretation functions.\n3. **Meta\u2011theoretic reasoning** \u2013 you can formalise soundness, completeness, cut\u2011elimination, decidability, or complexity results *inside* Coq.\n4. **Uniform treatment of modalities** \u2013 e.g., for a modal logic you can have a constructor <code>Box : expr \u2192 expr</code> and then prove the modal axioms as inductive rules; you can also encode *binding* (quantifiers) as explicit <code>Lam</code> and <code>App</code> nodes, making it easier to manipulate them.\n5. **Separation of concerns** \u2013 the syntax is independent of the underlying Coq logic; you can change the proof system (e.g., from natural deduction to sequent calculus) without touching the syntax.\n\n**Limitations**\n1. **More boilerplate** \u2013 every connective needs a constructor, a \u201cwell\u2011formedness\u201d predicate (if you use a dependently typed syntax), a semantics, and often an induction principle.  Proofs become longer because you must repeatedly use the inductive judgment.\n2. **Automation is weaker** \u2013 generic tactics do not automatically work on <code>provable</code> judgments; you need to write custom tactics (e.g., <code>solve [ eauto using provable_ind ]</code>) or use reflection.\n3. **Trust overhead** \u2013 the correctness of the whole development now depends on the soundness proof of the interpretation relation; any mistake there may lead to an unsound logic.\n4. **Performance** \u2013 induction over a large syntax tree may be slower, and extracted programs may need a lot of runtime overhead if the interpreter is executed.\n5. **Complexity of binding** \u2013 representing binders (\u2203, \u2200, \u03bb, \u25c7) requires either de\u202fBruijn indices, locally nameless representation, or nominal techniques.  Each adds its own proof burden.\n\n---\n\n<h2>3.  Guidelines for choosing a style</h2>\n\nBelow is a decision tree that many Coq/Isabelle practitioners find useful.  It is deliberately coarse\u2011grained; you can always combine the two styles (see \u00a74).\n\n<ol>\n  <li>\n    <strong>Do you need to reason about the *syntactic* structure of formulas?</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Yes \u2192 <strong>deep embedding</strong> (or a hybrid). Typical reasons: \n        <ul>\n          <li>Proving substitution lemmas, cut\u2011elimination, or decidability.</li>\n          <li>Manipulating derivation trees (e.g., proof\u2011search, automated verification of protocol traces).</li>\n          <li>Defining a language of *policies* or *rules* that will be *interpreted* later (common in access\u2011control logics).</li>\n        </ul>\n      </li>\n      <li>No \u2192 proceed to (2).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n\n  <li>\n    <strong>Do you have more than one semantics or want to change the semantics later?</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Yes \u2192 <strong>deep embedding</strong> (you can keep separate interpretation functions).</li>\n      <li>No \u2192 (3).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n\n  <li>\n    <strong>Is the object logic a *sub\u2011logic"}], "q_num": 290, "originalconversations": ["When encoding a logic into a proof assistant such as Coq or Isabelle, a choice needs to be made between using a shallow and a deep embedding. In a shallow embedding logical formulas are written directly in the logic of the theorem prover, whereas in a deep embedding logical formulas are represented as a datatype. \n\n* What are the advantages and\nlimitations of the various\napproaches?\n* Are there any guidelines available for determining which to use? \n* Is it possible to switch between the two representations in any systematic fashion?\n\nAs motivation, I would like to encode various security related logics into Coq and am wondering what the pros and cons of the different approaches are.", "> What are the advantages and limitations of the various approaches?\n\n* Pros of deep embeddings : You can prove and define things by induction on formulas' structure. Examples of interests are the size of a formula. \n* Cons of deep embeddings: You have do deal explicitly with binding of variables. That's usually very laborious. \n\n> Are there any guidelines available for determining which to use ?\n\nShallow embeddings are very useful to import result proved in the object logic. For instance, if you have prove something in a small logic (e.g. separation logic) shallow embeddings can be a tool of choice to import your result in Coq.\nOn the other side, deep embedding are almost mandatory when you want to prove meta-theorems about the object logic (like cut-elimination for instance).    \n\n> Is it possible to switch between the two representations in any systematic fashion?\n\nThe idea behind the shallow embedding is really to work directly in a model of the object formulas. Usually people will maps an object formula P directly (using notations or by doing the translation by hand) to an inhabitant of Prop. Of course, there are inhabitants of Prop which cannot be obtained by embedding a formula of the object logic. Therefore you lose some kind of completeness. \n\nSo it is possible to send every result obtained in a deep embedding setting through an interpretation function. \nHere is a little coq example: \n```\nInductive formula : Set :=\n    Ftrue : formula\n  | Ffalse : formula\n  | Fand : formula -> formula -> formula\n  | For : formula -> formula -> formula.\nFixpoint interpret (F : formula) : Prop := match F with\n    Ftrue => True\n  | Ffalse => False\n  | Fand a b => (interpret a) /\\ (interpret b)\n  | For a b => (interpret a) \\/ (interpret b)\n end.\nInductive derivable : formula -> Prop :=\n    deep_axiom : derivable Ftrue\n  | deep_and : forall a b, derivable a -> derivable b -> derivable (Fand a b)\n  | deep_or1 : forall a b, derivable a -> derivable (For a b)\n  | deep_or2 : forall a b, derivable b -> derivable (For a b).\nInductive sderivable : Prop -> Prop :=\n    shallow_axiom : sderivable True\n  | shallow_and : forall a b, sderivable a -> sderivable b -> sderivable (a /\\ b)\n  | shallow_or1 : forall a b, sderivable a -> sderivable (a \\/ b)\n  | shallow_or2 : forall a b, sderivable b -> sderivable (a \\/ b).\n(* You can prove the following lemma: *)\nLemma shallow_deep :\n   forall F, derivable F -> sderivable (interpret F).\n(* You can NOT prove the following lemma :*)\nLemma t :\n   forall P, sderivable P -> exists F, interpret F = P.\n```"], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "When encoding a logic into a proof assistant such as Coq or Isabelle, a choice needs to be made between using a shallow and a deep embedding. In a shallow embedding logical formulas are written directly in the logic of the theorem prover, whereas in a deep embedding logical formulas are represented as a datatype. \n\n* What are the advantages and\nlimitations of the various\napproaches?\n* Are there any guidelines available for determining which to use? \n* Is it possible to switch between the two representations in any systematic fashion?\n\nAs motivation, I would like to encode various security related logics into Coq and am wondering what the pros and cons of the different approaches are."}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "I have a 9 year old daughter that has expressed some interest in manga, but I'm having trouble locating series that are appropriate for her age. No one at our local bookstore could offer any advice. Is there a kid-friendly imprint or other resource I could use to help her find something appropriate? My preference is for physical books but I'm willing to explore digital options."}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user wants recommendations for kid-friendly manga for a 9-year-old daughter. Provide resources, imprints, suggestions, maybe Kodansha's etc. Also mention Shonen Jump's digital library with free chapters, also mention graphic novels from publishers like Scholastic's \"Manga Mania\" and \"Manga for Kids\". Provide lists of age-appropriate titles, with brief descriptions, rating, content warnings. Also suggest where to find physical copies: local libraries, bookstores, online retailers. Provide digital options: Crunchyroll Manga, ComiXology, VIZ Media's Shonen Jump. Also mention imprint: \"Shogakukan's Children\u2019s Manga\" or \"Kodansha's Comic Blade? Actually \"Kodansha's Manga for Children (Kodansha Comics)\". The \"Boys' Love\"? Not needed. Could mention \"Shonen\" is boys but many are family-friendly. Also \"Naruto\", \"Pokemon Adventures\", \"Yo-Kai Watch\", \"Demashita! Power Rumble\"? Might be better to list titles like \"Pokemon Adventures\", \"Yo-Kai Watch\", \"My Hero Academia\" may be older but maybe 9+; \"One Piece\" is teen but many enjoy. Also \"Ashi no Tsuku Kiyo\"? Not for kids.\n\nBetter to list clearly: \"Mighty Morphin\" no. Actually \"Manga for Kids\" list: \"Yotsuba&!\" (maybe older humor), \"Ellie & the Manga\"? Should be \"Manga Club\", \"Coconut\". Let's research typical kid-friendly manga: \"Avatar: The Last Airbender\" manga, \"Moomin\", \"Sailor Moon\" (maybe 9+), \"Adventure of the Little Prince\"? Hmm.\n\nBetter to use known series: \"Pokemon Adventures\" (pocket monster), \"Pok\u00e9mon manga series (Pokemon Adventures, Pokemon: The Electric Tale of Pikachu)\", \"Yo-Kai Watch\", \"Doraemon\", \"Shin-chan\", \"Chibi Maruko-chan\", \"Dragon Ball (maybe older), probably not.\n\nAlso \"Manga for kids\" imprints: \"Shogakukan's \"CoroCoro Comic\", \"Kodansha's \"Shonen Magazine\" but not. In US, \"Viz Kids\" line? Actually Viz has \"Shonen Jump\" for kids but not specifically. \"Kodansha Comics: Kids\" includes \"The Diary of a Wimpy Kid\"? Not manga.\n\nBetter to reference \"Scribble Club\"? hmm.\n\nI will provide suggestions:\n\n- Kodansha's \"Kodansha Manga Awards\" includes \"Children's\" category. Mention series that have won that award.\n\n- \"Adventure Time\" manga by Pendleton Ward is kid-friendly.\n\n- \"The Baby-Sitters Club: Mama's Little Helpers\" not.\n\nWill answer with helpful resources: \"The Manga Companion\" website, \"MyAnimeList\" with age rating, \"Anime-Planet\" filters.\n\nInclude physical bookstores like \"Barnes & Noble's Manga section\" includes kids' section.\n\nDigital: \"VIZ Media's Shonen Jump for $1.99/month includes many titles, some suitable for kids; also offers free first three chapters.\"\n\nAlso \"ComiXology Unlimited\" includes kids manga.\n\nWill format answer in <answer> tags.</think>\n<answer>\n**Finding Kid\u2011Friendly Manga for a 9\u2011Year\u2011Old**\n\nBelow is a quick\u2011start guide that gives you (1) a handful of titles that are widely accepted as age\u2011appropriate, (2) the publishers/ imprints that specialize in children\u2019s manga, and (3) where you can get physical copies or digital access.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Recommended Series (Ages\u202f8\u201110)\n\n| Title (English) | Publisher (US) | Volumes Available | Why It\u2019s Good for a 9\u2011Year\u2011Old | Content Notes |\n|-----------------|----------------|-------------------|--------------------------------|----------------|\n| **Pok\u00e9mon Adventures** | VIZ Media (Shonen Jump) | 12 (ongoing) | Classic adventure, familiar characters, humor, easy to follow battles. | Light combat; no graphic violence. |\n| **Yo\u2011Kai Watch** | Viz Media (Shonen Jump) | 12 | Funny supernatural \u201cYo\u2011kai\u201d that cause everyday mishaps; bright art. | Some mild spooky jokes (cartoonish). |\n| **Doraemon** | Kodansha (Kodansha Comics) | 35+ (short stories) | Time\u2011travel gadgets, moral lessons, very gentle humor. | None. |\n| **Shin\u2011chan (Crayon Shin\u2011chan)** | VIZ Media | 12 (selected) | Short, slap\u2011stick comedy about a mischievous boy; easy reads. | Occasional toilet\u2011humor; check the edition (some are \u201cKids\u201d\u2011friendly). |\n| **Tiny Titans (Manga\u2011style)** | Scholastic | 2 | Superhero kids in comedic situations; clear, bright panels. | None. |\n| **The\u202fLegend of Zelda: Hyrule Heroes** | Dark Horse | 4 | Adventure with familiar Nintendo characters; simple plot. | Light fantasy combat. |\n| **My\u202fNeighbor\u202fTotoro (Manga Adaptation)** | VIZ Media | 1 | Gentle story from Studio Ghibli\u2019s classic; fantasy world. | None. |\n| **Adventure Time (Manga)** | BOOM! Studios | 6 | Whimsical world, humor, friendship themes; short chapters. | Minimal fantasy combat. |\n| **Moomin (Japanese edition, English translation)** | Yen Press | 12 | Soft\u2011spoken, nature\u2011loving creatures; calm storytelling. | None. |\n| **Yotsuba&!** (first 3 volumes) | Yen Press | 3+ | Slice\u2011of\u2011life comedy about a curious 5\u2011year\u2011old; bright art. | None (very wholesome). |\n\n*Tip:* Start with the first volume or a \u201cstarter\u201d edition and read a few pages together. If she enjoys the art style and pacing, she\u2019ll naturally want the next volume.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Kid\u2011Focused Imprints & Catalogues\n\n| Imprint / Line | Typical Age Range | Notable Kids Titles | Where to Find |\n|----------------|-------------------|---------------------|---------------|\n| **VIZ Media \u2013 \u201cShonen Jump\u201d (Kids\u2011Friendly Section)** | 8\u201112 | Pok\u00e9mon, Yo\u2011Kai Watch, My\u202fHero\u202fAcademia (younger arcs) | VIZ.com, local comic shops, Barnes & Noble |\n| **Kodansha Comics \u2013 \u201cKids\u201d** | 7\u201110 | Doraemon, Crayon Shin\u2011chan, Heian Era (light) | Amazon, Kinokuniya, local libraries |\n| **Scholastic Manga Mania** (formerly \u201cManga for Kids\u201d) | 6\u201111 | Tiny Titans, Magic Tree House Manga | Bookstores, school libraries |\n| **Boom! Studios \u2013 \u201cBoom Kids\u201d** | 7\u201112 | Adventure Time, My Little Pony (manga style) | Indie bookstores, comic conventions |\n| **Yen Press \u2013 \u201cYen Press Kids\u201d** | 7\u201112 | Yotsuba&!, Moomin | Online retailers, library digital collections |\n\nThese lines are deliberately edited for content, often omitting graphic violence, intense horror, or mature romance. When buying, look for the label **\u201cKids\u201d** or **\u201cAll\u2011Ages\u201d** on the cover.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Where to Buy Physical Copies\n\n| Store Type | What They Offer | How to Search |\n|------------|----------------|---------------|\n| **Barnes & Noble** | Dedicated manga aisle with \u201cKids\u201d shelf. | Ask staff for \u201cchildren\u2019s manga\u201d or look for the **Kids / All\u2011Ages** tag in the online catalog. |\n| **Book Depository / Amazon** | Full back\u2011list, often with \u201cAge Recommendation\u201d in the description. | Filter by \u201cChildren\u2019s Books \u2192 Graphic Novels \u2192 Manga\u201d. |\n| **Local Comic Shops** | Frequently carry VIZ\u2019s Shonen Jump subscription boxes (contain kid\u2011safe titles). | Ask for \u201cShonen Jump Kids\u201d box set. |\n| **Library Bookstores (e.g., OverDrive, Hoopla)** | Often have a \u201cGraphic Novels for Kids\u201d section; you can borrow before buying. | Look under \u201cGraphic Novels \u2192 Manga \u2192 Children\u201d. |\n| **Kinokuniya (if nearby)** | Japanese\u2011imported kids manga (Doraemon, etc.) with English translations. | Browse the \u201cChildren\u201d floor. |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Digital Options (Great for Sampling)\n\n| Platform | Cost | Kids\u2011Friendly Features |\n|----------|------|------------------------|\n| **VIZ Media \u2013 Shonen Jump** | $1.99/month (full access) \u2013 first three chapters free | Age filter, \u201cKids\u201d collection, offline download for tablets. |\n| **ComiXology Unlimited** | $5.99/mo | \u201cKids & Family\u201d shelf; ability to set \u201cMaturity Rating\u201d filter. |\n| **Amazon Kindle** | Pay\u2011per\u2011book | \u201cKids\u201d section; preview first 10 pages for free. |\n| **Crunchyroll Manga** | $9.99/mo (includes anime) | \u201cKids\u201d tab with limited titles; parental controls. |\n| **Libby (overdrive)** | Free with library card | \u201cGraphic Novels \u2192 Manga \u2192 Children\u201d filter; no ads. |\n\nWhen using a shared device, enable the parental\u2011control lock on the app (most have a PIN or password). This prevents accidental exposure to older titles.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Quick \u201cHow\u2011to\u201d Checklist for Parents\n\n1. **Start with a \u201cKids\u201d label** \u2013 Look"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I have a 9 year old daughter that has expressed some interest in manga, but I'm having trouble locating series that are appropriate for her age. No one at our local bookstore could offer any advice. Is there a kid-friendly imprint or other resource I could use to help her find something appropriate? My preference is for physical books but I'm willing to explore digital options."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user wants recommendations for kid-friendly manga for a 9-year-old daughter. Provide resources, imprints, suggestions, maybe Kodansha's etc. Also mention Shonen Jump's digital library with free chapters, also mention graphic novels from publishers like Scholastic's \"Manga Mania\" and \"Manga for Kids\". Provide lists of age-appropriate titles, with brief descriptions, rating, content warnings. Also suggest where to find physical copies: local libraries, bookstores, online retailers. Provide digital options: Crunchyroll Manga, ComiXology, VIZ Media's Shonen Jump. Also mention imprint: \"Shogakukan's Children\u2019s Manga\" or \"Kodansha's Comic Blade? Actually \"Kodansha's Manga for Children (Kodansha Comics)\". The \"Boys' Love\"? Not needed. Could mention \"Shonen\" is boys but many are family-friendly. Also \"Naruto\", \"Pokemon Adventures\", \"Yo-Kai Watch\", \"Demashita! Power Rumble\"? Might be better to list titles like \"Pokemon Adventures\", \"Yo-Kai Watch\", \"My Hero Academia\" may be older but maybe 9+; \"One Piece\" is teen but many enjoy. Also \"Ashi no Tsuku Kiyo\"? Not for kids.\n\nBetter to list clearly: \"Mighty Morphin\" no. Actually \"Manga for Kids\" list: \"Yotsuba&!\" (maybe older humor), \"Ellie & the Manga\"? Should be \"Manga Club\", \"Coconut\". Let's research typical kid-friendly manga: \"Avatar: The Last Airbender\" manga, \"Moomin\", \"Sailor Moon\" (maybe 9+), \"Adventure of the Little Prince\"? Hmm.\n\nBetter to use known series: \"Pokemon Adventures\" (pocket monster), \"Pok\u00e9mon manga series (Pokemon Adventures, Pokemon: The Electric Tale of Pikachu)\", \"Yo-Kai Watch\", \"Doraemon\", \"Shin-chan\", \"Chibi Maruko-chan\", \"Dragon Ball (maybe older), probably not.\n\nAlso \"Manga for kids\" imprints: \"Shogakukan's \"CoroCoro Comic\", \"Kodansha's \"Shonen Magazine\" but not. In US, \"Viz Kids\" line? Actually Viz has \"Shonen Jump\" for kids but not specifically. \"Kodansha Comics: Kids\" includes \"The Diary of a Wimpy Kid\"? Not manga.\n\nBetter to reference \"Scribble Club\"? hmm.\n\nI will provide suggestions:\n\n- Kodansha's \"Kodansha Manga Awards\" includes \"Children's\" category. Mention series that have won that award.\n\n- \"Adventure Time\" manga by Pendleton Ward is kid-friendly.\n\n- \"The Baby-Sitters Club: Mama's Little Helpers\" not.\n\nWill answer with helpful resources: \"The Manga Companion\" website, \"MyAnimeList\" with age rating, \"Anime-Planet\" filters.\n\nInclude physical bookstores like \"Barnes & Noble's Manga section\" includes kids' section.\n\nDigital: \"VIZ Media's Shonen Jump for $1.99/month includes many titles, some suitable for kids; also offers free first three chapters.\"\n\nAlso \"ComiXology Unlimited\" includes kids manga.\n\nWill format answer in <answer> tags.</think>\n<answer>\n**Finding Kid\u2011Friendly Manga for a 9\u2011Year\u2011Old**\n\nBelow is a quick\u2011start guide that gives you (1) a handful of titles that are widely accepted as age\u2011appropriate, (2) the publishers/ imprints that specialize in children\u2019s manga, and (3) where you can get physical copies or digital access.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Recommended Series (Ages\u202f8\u201110)\n\n| Title (English) | Publisher (US) | Volumes Available | Why It\u2019s Good for a 9\u2011Year\u2011Old | Content Notes |\n|-----------------|----------------|-------------------|--------------------------------|----------------|\n| **Pok\u00e9mon Adventures** | VIZ Media (Shonen Jump) | 12 (ongoing) | Classic adventure, familiar characters, humor, easy to follow battles. | Light combat; no graphic violence. |\n| **Yo\u2011Kai Watch** | Viz Media (Shonen Jump) | 12 | Funny supernatural \u201cYo\u2011kai\u201d that cause everyday mishaps; bright art. | Some mild spooky jokes (cartoonish). |\n| **Doraemon** | Kodansha (Kodansha Comics) | 35+ (short stories) | Time\u2011travel gadgets, moral lessons, very gentle humor. | None. |\n| **Shin\u2011chan (Crayon Shin\u2011chan)** | VIZ Media | 12 (selected) | Short, slap\u2011stick comedy about a mischievous boy; easy reads. | Occasional toilet\u2011humor; check the edition (some are \u201cKids\u201d\u2011friendly). |\n| **Tiny Titans (Manga\u2011style)** | Scholastic | 2 | Superhero kids in comedic situations; clear, bright panels. | None. |\n| **The\u202fLegend of Zelda: Hyrule Heroes** | Dark Horse | 4 | Adventure with familiar Nintendo characters; simple plot. | Light fantasy combat. |\n| **My\u202fNeighbor\u202fTotoro (Manga Adaptation)** | VIZ Media | 1 | Gentle story from Studio Ghibli\u2019s classic; fantasy world. | None. |\n| **Adventure Time (Manga)** | BOOM! Studios | 6 | Whimsical world, humor, friendship themes; short chapters. | Minimal fantasy combat. |\n| **Moomin (Japanese edition, English translation)** | Yen Press | 12 | Soft\u2011spoken, nature\u2011loving creatures; calm storytelling. | None. |\n| **Yotsuba&!** (first 3 volumes) | Yen Press | 3+ | Slice\u2011of\u2011life comedy about a curious 5\u2011year\u2011old; bright art. | None (very wholesome). |\n\n*Tip:* Start with the first volume or a \u201cstarter\u201d edition and read a few pages together. If she enjoys the art style and pacing, she\u2019ll naturally want the next volume.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Kid\u2011Focused Imprints & Catalogues\n\n| Imprint / Line | Typical Age Range | Notable Kids Titles | Where to Find |\n|----------------|-------------------|---------------------|---------------|\n| **VIZ Media \u2013 \u201cShonen Jump\u201d (Kids\u2011Friendly Section)** | 8\u201112 | Pok\u00e9mon, Yo\u2011Kai Watch, My\u202fHero\u202fAcademia (younger arcs) | VIZ.com, local comic shops, Barnes & Noble |\n| **Kodansha Comics \u2013 \u201cKids\u201d** | 7\u201110 | Doraemon, Crayon Shin\u2011chan, Heian Era (light) | Amazon, Kinokuniya, local libraries |\n| **Scholastic Manga Mania** (formerly \u201cManga for Kids\u201d) | 6\u201111 | Tiny Titans, Magic Tree House Manga | Bookstores, school libraries |\n| **Boom! Studios \u2013 \u201cBoom Kids\u201d** | 7\u201112 | Adventure Time, My Little Pony (manga style) | Indie bookstores, comic conventions |\n| **Yen Press \u2013 \u201cYen Press Kids\u201d** | 7\u201112 | Yotsuba&!, Moomin | Online retailers, library digital collections |\n\nThese lines are deliberately edited for content, often omitting graphic violence, intense horror, or mature romance. When buying, look for the label **\u201cKids\u201d** or **\u201cAll\u2011Ages\u201d** on the cover.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Where to Buy Physical Copies\n\n| Store Type | What They Offer | How to Search |\n|------------|----------------|---------------|\n| **Barnes & Noble** | Dedicated manga aisle with \u201cKids\u201d shelf. | Ask staff for \u201cchildren\u2019s manga\u201d or look for the **Kids / All\u2011Ages** tag in the online catalog. |\n| **Book Depository / Amazon** | Full back\u2011list, often with \u201cAge Recommendation\u201d in the description. | Filter by \u201cChildren\u2019s Books \u2192 Graphic Novels \u2192 Manga\u201d. |\n| **Local Comic Shops** | Frequently carry VIZ\u2019s Shonen Jump subscription boxes (contain kid\u2011safe titles). | Ask for \u201cShonen Jump Kids\u201d box set. |\n| **Library Bookstores (e.g., OverDrive, Hoopla)** | Often have a \u201cGraphic Novels for Kids\u201d section; you can borrow before buying. | Look under \u201cGraphic Novels \u2192 Manga \u2192 Children\u201d. |\n| **Kinokuniya (if nearby)** | Japanese\u2011imported kids manga (Doraemon, etc.) with English translations. | Browse the \u201cChildren\u201d floor. |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Digital Options (Great for Sampling)\n\n| Platform | Cost | Kids\u2011Friendly Features |\n|----------|------|------------------------|\n| **VIZ Media \u2013 Shonen Jump** | $1.99/month (full access) \u2013 first three chapters free | Age filter, \u201cKids\u201d collection, offline download for tablets. |\n| **ComiXology Unlimited** | $5.99/mo | \u201cKids & Family\u201d shelf; ability to set \u201cMaturity Rating\u201d filter. |\n| **Amazon Kindle** | Pay\u2011per\u2011book | \u201cKids\u201d section; preview first 10 pages for free. |\n| **Crunchyroll Manga** | $9.99/mo (includes anime) | \u201cKids\u201d tab with limited titles; parental controls. |\n| **Libby (overdrive)** | Free with library card | \u201cGraphic Novels \u2192 Manga \u2192 Children\u201d filter; no ads. |\n\nWhen using a shared device, enable the parental\u2011control lock on the app (most have a PIN or password). This prevents accidental exposure to older titles.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Quick \u201cHow\u2011to\u201d Checklist for Parents\n\n1. **Start with a \u201cKids\u201d label** \u2013 Look"}], "q_num": 161, "originalconversations": ["I have a 9 year old daughter that has expressed some interest in manga, but I'm having trouble locating series that are appropriate for her age. No one at our local bookstore could offer any advice. Is there a kid-friendly imprint or other resource I could use to help her find something appropriate? My preference is for physical books but I'm willing to explore digital options.", "The content deemed acceptable for Japanese children generally differs from the concept in other countries; manga contains all genres of stories, so one cannot responsibly presume that just because a title was marketed at Japanese kids, that you would likewise consider it appropriate for your kid of the same age.\n\nAs one example:\nThe popular girl's series Sailor Moon was published in the manga magazine Nakayoshi, which was marketed toward elementary school-age girls (1st through 6th grade). This series includes strong language, bullying, violence, death, war, genocide, refugees, lovers' suicide (more than once), non-consensual kisses (forced while awake, while asleep, while drunk), a father fondling his adult daughter while brainwashed, human/animal romance, demonic-style alien possession of human bodies, nudity, cross-dressing, a non-monogamous lesbian couple, and non-graphic sex resulting in teen pregnancy (the 90s anime version, also marketed toward children, also added wrist/ankle restraints for a rape-like, pain-inducing entering of victims).\n\nThere are many manga reviews on the web, including those that specifically for parents and teen/preteen/kid readers on what sort of possibly objectionable content is included in the title (such as strong language, violence, nudity, gender-bending, innuendo, sexual content, etc.) and what sort of general content you can expect (for example: horror, potty humor, serious themes like depression/body image/sexual orientation/prostitution/parental abandonment/bullying/suicide) and let you as the parent or the reader use your own judgment about whether a title fits what you deem appropriate at what age. Browsing reviews also allows you to seek out titles that reinforce and contribute to your family's values (such as traits of selflessness, generosity, celebration of uniqueness, etc.).\n\nSome good starting places are:\n\n* A Parent's Guide to Anime\n* Good Manga for Kids at Manga Bookshelf\n* Good Comics for Kids at School Library Journal\n* Kids Manga at No Flying No Tights\n\nSince you have a daughter, she may be especially interested in shoujo manga, which was written with the target audience of Japanese girls. Within shoujo, nearly all genres of stories are included; some titles are very cheerful and innocuous and teach a moral-of-the-day, others are dark and heavy. Good starting places to get informed are:\n\n* What is Shoujo Manga?, What Shoujo Manga Are and Are Not, and Recommended Shoujo Manga by Matt Thorn, a professor in the Faculty of Manga at Kyoto Seika University (one of the world's experts on shoujo manga)\n* What is Shoujo? at Energetic Heartbeats\n\nSome shoujo manga review sites are:\n\n* The Classics at Shoujo Corner\n* Emily's Random Shoujo Manga Page  (despite its unassuming name, contains many detailed reviews)\n* Heart of Manga\n* Shoujo Savvy\n* Shoujo Manga Recommendations\n* Romance Manga Reviews\n* Shoujo Manga Reviews\n* Shoujo Manga with Anime at Anime Manga Reviews\n\nMore general review websites include MangaBlog and Kuriousity, and a Google search will find you many more."], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "I have a 9 year old daughter that has expressed some interest in manga, but I'm having trouble locating series that are appropriate for her age. No one at our local bookstore could offer any advice. Is there a kid-friendly imprint or other resource I could use to help her find something appropriate? My preference is for physical books but I'm willing to explore digital options."}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "How do you learn to play the guitar?"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nUser asks: \"How do you learn to learn to play the guitar?\" Actually asks: \"How do you learn to play the guitar?\" So we give guidance: steps, resources, practice tips, equipment, theory, learning methods, common pitfalls. Provide structured answer: getting instrument, basics (posture, tuning, chords), practice routine, learning resources (online, books, teachers), theory basics, songs, progress tracking, maintaining motivation. Add FAQs maybe.\n\nWe should respond in a helpful, thorough manner.\n\nWe must format answer inside <answer></answer> tags.</think>\n<answer>\n**Learning to Play the Guitar \u2013 A Step\u2011by\u2011Step Guide**\n\nWhether you\u2019re drawn to strumming acoustic ballads, shredding electric solos, or just want to jam with friends, learning guitar follows a proven progression. Below is a practical roadmap you can adapt to your schedule, goals, and musical taste.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Get the Right Gear\n\n| Item | Why It Matters | Quick Tips |\n|------|----------------|------------|\n| **Guitar** | A comfortable instrument encourages practice. | \u2022 Beginners often start with a **concert\u2011size acoustic** (steel\u2011string) or a **class\u2011style electric** with a small amp.<br>\u2022 Choose a guitar that feels natural in your hands; the neck should be easy to reach, and the action (string height) low enough to press without strain. |\n| **Pick** | Controls tone and attack. | \u2022 Start with a medium\u2011thick (0.73\u202fmm) nylon or celluloid pick. Experiment later. |\n| **Tuner** | Staying in tune is crucial for ear training. | \u2022 Clip\u2011on tuners are cheap and accurate; many phone apps work too. |\n| **Extra Strings** | Strings break\u2014especially on electric guitars. | \u2022 Keep a spare set of the same gauge on hand. |\n| **Strap & Case** | Comfort and protection. | \u2022 A padded strap keeps the guitar steady; a hard case protects it during transport. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Master the Fundamentals (Weeks\u202f1\u20114)\n\n| Skill | What to Practice | Time Allocation |\n|-------|-------------------|-----------------|\n| **Posture & Holding the Guitar** | Sit upright, relax shoulders, keep the guitar angled ~45\u00b0 (acoustic) or strapped (electric). | 5\u202fmin each session |\n| **Tuning** | Learn standard E\u2011A\u2011D\u2011G\u2011B\u2011E tuning. Tune by ear occasionally. | 2\u20113\u202fmin daily |\n| **Right\u2011Hand Technique** | \u2022 Simple down\u2011strokes on open strings.<br>\u2022 Alternate picking (down\u2011up). | 10\u201115\u202fmin daily |\n| **Left\u2011Hand Finger Placement** | \u2022 Press each string just behind the fret.<br>\u2022 Finger curves, thumb behind neck. | 10\u201115\u202fmin daily |\n| **Basic Open Chords** | Em\u2011C\u2011G\u2011D\u2011A (major) and Am\u2011Em\u2011Dm (minor). Practice clean transitions. | 15\u201120\u202fmin daily |\n| **Strumming Patterns** | \u2022 Down\u2011down\u2011down\u2011down (steady rhythm).<br>\u2022 Down\u2011up\u2011down\u2011up (basic folk). | 5\u201110\u202fmin daily |\n| **Simple Songs** | \u201cKnockin\u2019 on Heaven\u2019s Door\u201d, \u201cHorse\u2011Shoes\u201d (The Beatles), \u201cThree Little Birds\u201d. | 10\u201115\u202fmin per song |\n\n*Goal:* By the end of month\u202f1 you should be able to change between three\u2011chord progressions smoothly and keep steady time.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Build a Structured Practice Routine (Weeks\u202f5\u201112)\n\n1. **Warm\u2011Up (5\u202fmin)** \u2013 Finger stretches, chromatic runs (1\u20112\u20113\u20114 on each string).\n2. **Technique Focus (10\u201115\u202fmin)** \u2013 Choose ONE:  \n   - **Alternate Picking** (e\u2011t\u2011e\u2011t\u202fpatterns).  \n   - **Hammer\u2011Ons / Pull\u2011Offs**.  \n   - **Bending / Slides**.  \n   - **Barre Chords** (F, B\u266d).  \n3. **Repertoire (20\u201125\u202fmin)** \u2013 Learn a new song each week, breaking it into sections (intro, verse, chorus). Play slowly with a metronome, then speed up.\n4. **Ear & Theory (5\u201110\u202fmin)** \u2013 Identify intervals, practice major/minor scales, learn the circle of fifths.\n5. **Cool\u2011Down (2\u20113\u202fmin)** \u2013 Light strumming, reflect on what improved, note any pain.\n\n*Tip:* Keep a **practice journal** (paper or app) to track time, songs, and specific goals. Seeing progress fuels motivation.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Choose a Learning Path\n\n| Path | Benefits | Considerations |\n|------|----------|----------------|\n| **Self\u2011Study (YouTube, Apps, Books)** | \u2022 Free or low\u2011cost.<br>\u2022 Flexible schedule. | \u2022 Requires discipline; may miss technique corrections. |\n| **Online Lessons (e.g., Fender Play, JustinGuitar, Yousician)** | \u2022 Structured curriculum.<br>\u2022 Interactive feedback (some apps). | \u2022 Subscription fees; limited personal nuance. |\n| **In\u2011Person Teacher** | \u2022 Real\u2011time correction.<br>\u2022 Tailored song selection. | \u2022 Higher cost; need to find a good match. |\n| **Hybrid** | Combines the convenience of video with occasional live lessons for check\u2011ins. | \u2022 Needs coordination. |\n\n**Recommendation for beginners:** Start with a free, well\u2011reviewed YouTube series (JustinGuitar\u2019s *Beginner Course* is excellent), supplement with a metronome app, and schedule **one\u2011hour lessons** with a local teacher every 4\u20116 weeks for feedback.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Core Musical Concepts to Learn Early\n\n| Concept | How to Learn | Practical Application |\n|---------|--------------|-----------------------|\n| **Major & Minor Scales** | Practice C major and A minor (open position) slowly with a metronome. | Soloing, improvisation, understanding chord tones. |\n| **Chord Construction** | Learn how major/minor chords are built from scale degrees (1\u20113\u20115). | Create new chord progressions, transpose songs. |\n| **Circle of Fifths** | Memorize the order of sharps/flats. | Faster key changes, understanding harmonics. |\n| **Barre Chords** | Start with the \u201cE\u2011shape\u201d & \u201cA\u2011shape\u201d barre forms. | Play any major/minor chord in any key. |\n| **Power Chords (5th chords)** | Two\u2011note chords on the low strings (e.g., G5, A5). | Rock and punk rhythms, easier for beginners. |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Expand Your Repertoire\n\n| Genre | Example Songs (Beginner\u2011Friendly) | Techniques Highlighted |\n|-------|-----------------------------------|------------------------|\n| **Folk / Singer\u2011Songwriter** | \u201cLeaving on a Jet Plane\u201d, \u201cWish You Were Here\u201d | Strumming patterns, open chords |\n| **Rock** | \u201cSmoke on the Water\u201d, \u201cSmells Like Teen Spirit\u201d (intro) | Power chords, basic riffs |\n| **Blues** | \u201cSweet Home Chicago\u201d, \u201cPride and Joy\u201d (Intro) | 12\u2011bar blues, pentatonic soloing |\n| **Pop** | \u201cLet It Be\u201d, \u201cShape of You\u201d (acoustic version) | Chord inversions, capo use |\n| **Classical** | \u201cOde to Joy\u201d, \u201cMinuet in G\u201d | Fingerpicking, right\u2011hand independence |\n\nPick songs you love; emotional connection accelerates learning.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Develop Good Practice Habits\n\n| Habit | Why It Matters | How to Implement |\n|-------|----------------|-------------------|\n| **Use a Metronome** | Improves timing, builds speed gradually. | Start at 60\u202fBPM, increase by 5\u202fBPM once comfortable. |\n| **Record Yourself** | Reveals inconsistencies you can\u2019t hear while playing. | Smartphone apps; review weekly. |\n| **Play With Others** | Reinforces rhythm, listening, and communication. | Join a local jam session, online backing tracks, or a friend\u2019s group. |\n| **Take Short Breaks** | Prevents fatigue and injury (tendonitis). | 5\u2011minute break every 20\u201130\u202fminutes. |\n| **Stay Consistent** | Regular, short sessions beat occasional marathon practice. | Aim for *daily* 30\u2011minute sessions rather than weekly 2\u2011hour bursts. |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting Common Issues\n\n| Problem | Quick Fix |\n|---------|-----------|\n| **Fingers hurt** | Ensure proper finger pressure; use lighter gauge strings; limit practice to 15\u201120\u202fmin and stretch. |\n| **Chords sound muddy** | Check that each finger presses only its string; mute unused strings with the side of your hand. |\n| **Timing drifts** | Practice with a metronome; count beats out loud. |\n| **Stuck on a song** | Slow the tempo to 50\u202f% of the original, master each measure, then gradually speed up. |\n| **Lack of motivation** | Set a mini\u2011goal (learn a 30\u2011second riff) and reward yourself after achieving it. |\n\n---\n\n## 9. Resources (Free & Paid)\n\n| Type | Examples |\n|------|----------|\n| **Video Lessons** | *JustinGuitar*, *Marty Music*, *Andy Guitar* YouTube channels |\n|"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How do you learn to play the guitar?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nUser asks: \"How do you learn to learn to play the guitar?\" Actually asks: \"How do you learn to play the guitar?\" So we give guidance: steps, resources, practice tips, equipment, theory, learning methods, common pitfalls. Provide structured answer: getting instrument, basics (posture, tuning, chords), practice routine, learning resources (online, books, teachers), theory basics, songs, progress tracking, maintaining motivation. Add FAQs maybe.\n\nWe should respond in a helpful, thorough manner.\n\nWe must format answer inside <answer></answer> tags.</think>\n<answer>\n**Learning to Play the Guitar \u2013 A Step\u2011by\u2011Step Guide**\n\nWhether you\u2019re drawn to strumming acoustic ballads, shredding electric solos, or just want to jam with friends, learning guitar follows a proven progression. Below is