n\u2011Person Teacher** | \u2022 Real\u2011time correction.<br>\u2022 Tailored song selection. | \u2022 Higher cost; need to find a good match. |\n| **Hybrid** | Combines the convenience of video with occasional live lessons for check\u2011ins. | \u2022 Needs coordination. |\n\n**Recommendation for beginners:** Start with a free, well\u2011reviewed YouTube series (JustinGuitar\u2019s *Beginner Course* is excellent), supplement with a metronome app, and schedule **one\u2011hour lessons** with a local teacher every 4\u20116 weeks for feedback.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Core Musical Concepts to Learn Early\n\n| Concept | How to Learn | Practical Application |\n|---------|--------------|-----------------------|\n| **Major & Minor Scales** | Practice C major and A minor (open position) slowly with a metronome. | Soloing, improvisation, understanding chord tones. |\n| **Chord Construction** | Learn how major/minor chords are built from scale degrees (1\u20113\u20115). | Create new chord progressions, transpose songs. |\n| **Circle of Fifths** | Memorize the order of sharps/flats. | Faster key changes, understanding harmonics. |\n| **Barre Chords** | Start with the \u201cE\u2011shape\u201d & \u201cA\u2011shape\u201d barre forms. | Play any major/minor chord in any key. |\n| **Power Chords (5th chords)** | Two\u2011note chords on the low strings (e.g., G5, A5). | Rock and punk rhythms, easier for beginners. |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Expand Your Repertoire\n\n| Genre | Example Songs (Beginner\u2011Friendly) | Techniques Highlighted |\n|-------|-----------------------------------|------------------------|\n| **Folk / Singer\u2011Songwriter** | \u201cLeaving on a Jet Plane\u201d, \u201cWish You Were Here\u201d | Strumming patterns, open chords |\n| **Rock** | \u201cSmoke on the Water\u201d, \u201cSmells Like Teen Spirit\u201d (intro) | Power chords, basic riffs |\n| **Blues** | \u201cSweet Home Chicago\u201d, \u201cPride and Joy\u201d (Intro) | 12\u2011bar blues, pentatonic soloing |\n| **Pop** | \u201cLet It Be\u201d, \u201cShape of You\u201d (acoustic version) | Chord inversions, capo use |\n| **Classical** | \u201cOde to Joy\u201d, \u201cMinuet in G\u201d | Fingerpicking, right\u2011hand independence |\n\nPick songs you love; emotional connection accelerates learning.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Develop Good Practice Habits\n\n| Habit | Why It Matters | How to Implement |\n|-------|----------------|-------------------|\n| **Use a Metronome** | Improves timing, builds speed gradually. | Start at 60\u202fBPM, increase by 5\u202fBPM once comfortable. |\n| **Record Yourself** | Reveals inconsistencies you can\u2019t hear while playing. | Smartphone apps; review weekly. |\n| **Play With Others** | Reinforces rhythm, listening, and communication. | Join a local jam session, online backing tracks, or a friend\u2019s group. |\n| **Take Short Breaks** | Prevents fatigue and injury (tendonitis). | 5\u2011minute break every 20\u201130\u202fminutes. |\n| **Stay Consistent** | Regular, short sessions beat occasional marathon practice. | Aim for *daily* 30\u2011minute sessions rather than weekly 2\u2011hour bursts. |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting Common Issues\n\n| Problem | Quick Fix |\n|---------|-----------|\n| **Fingers hurt** | Ensure proper finger pressure; use lighter gauge strings; limit practice to 15\u201120\u202fmin and stretch. |\n| **Chords sound muddy** | Check that each finger presses only its string; mute unused strings with the side of your hand. |\n| **Timing drifts** | Practice with a metronome; count beats out loud. |\n| **Stuck on a song** | Slow the tempo to 50\u202f% of the original, master each measure, then gradually speed up. |\n| **Lack of motivation** | Set a mini\u2011goal (learn a 30\u2011second riff) and reward yourself after achieving it. |\n\n---\n\n## 9. Resources (Free & Paid)\n\n| Type | Examples |\n|------|----------|\n| **Video Lessons** | *JustinGuitar*, *Marty Music*, *Andy Guitar* YouTube channels |\n|"}], "q_num": 432, "originalconversations": ["How do you learn to play the guitar?", "There are several ways to learn how to play the guitar, including: \n\n* Taking lessons from a teacher who can help you learn and give personalized tips based on your level.\n* Using online videos and tutorials that offer free lessons and exercises for beginners.\n* Practicing independently using books or websites that teach basic techniques and songs.\n\nIt is important to remember that learning to play an instrument takes time and practice, so be patient with yourself and don't get discouraged if you don't see progress right away."], "originalsource": "authors", "originalprompt": "How do you learn to play the guitar?"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "Can you write the Python code to build and train a neural network?"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks: \"Can you write the Python code to build and train a neural network?\" This is a generic request. We need to provide code. We should comply. The user didn't specify any disallowed content. It's allowed. Provide example code using Keras/TensorFlow perhaps. Use <answer> tags. Should be safe. Provide explanation? Probably provide code within <answer>. Ensure no disallowed content. Use simple example e.g., MNIST. Provide code for building a simple feedforward network, compiling, training. Let's do that.</think>\n<answer>\nBelow is a self\u2011contained Python script that builds and trains a simple feed\u2011forward neural network on the MNIST handwritten\u2011digit dataset using **TensorFlow\u202f/Keras**.  \nYou can run it as\u2011is (after installing the required packages) or adapt it to your own data and architecture.\n\n```python\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------\n# Simple Neural Network for MNIST classification (TensorFlow/Keras)\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------\n# Required packages:\n#   pip install tensorflow numpy matplotlib\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport tensorflow as tf\nfrom tensorflow import keras\nfrom tensorflow.keras import layers\n\n# ---------------------------------\n# 1. Load and prepare the dataset\n# ---------------------------------\n# MNIST: 28x28 grayscale images of digits 0\u20119\n(x_train, y_train), (x_test, y_test) = keras.datasets.mnist.load_data()\n\n# Normalize pixel values to the range [0, 1]\nx_train = x_train.astype(\"float32\") / 255.0\nx_test  = x_test.astype(\"float32\") / 255.0\n\n# Flatten the 28x28 images into 1\u2011D vectors (required for a dense network)\nx_train = x_train.reshape(-1, 28 * 28)\nx_test  = x_test.reshape(-1, 28 * 28)\n\n# Convert integer labels to one\u2011hot vectors (optional; Keras can handle integers directly)\nnum_classes = 10\ny_train_onehot = keras.utils.to_categorical(y_train, num_classes)\ny_test_onehot  = keras.utils.to_categorical(y_test, num_classes)\n\n# ---------------------------------\n# 2. Build the model architecture\n# ---------------------------------\ndef create_model():\n    model = keras.Sequential([\n        layers.Input(shape=(28 * 28,)),               # Input layer\n        layers.Dense(256, activation=\"relu\"),         # Hidden layer 1\n        layers.Dropout(0.2),                          # Regularisation\n        layers.Dense(128, activation=\"relu\"),         # Hidden layer 2\n        layers.Dropout(0.2),\n        layers.Dense(num_classes, activation=\"softmax\")  # Output layer\n    ])\n    return model\n\nmodel = create_model()\nmodel.summary()   # Print a summary of the network\n\n# ---------------------------------\n# 3. Compile the model\n# ---------------------------------\nmodel.compile(\n    optimizer=keras.optimizers.Adam(learning_rate=0.001),\n    loss=\"categorical_crossentropy\",   # use \"sparse_categorical_crossentropy\" if you keep integer labels\n    metrics=[\"accuracy\"]\n)\n\n# ---------------------------------\n# 4. Train the model\n# ---------------------------------\nepochs = 20\nbatch_size = 128\n\nhistory = model.fit(\n    x_train,\n    y_train_onehot,\n    validation_split=0.1,          # 10\u202f% of training data used for validation\n    epochs=epochs,\n    batch_size=batch_size,\n    verbose=2\n)\n\n# ---------------------------------\n# 5. Evaluate on the test set\n# ---------------------------------\ntest_loss, test_acc = model.evaluate(x_test, y_test_onehot, verbose=0)\nprint(f\"\\nTest accuracy: {test_acc:.4f} | Test loss: {test_loss:.4f}\")\n\n# ---------------------------------\n# 6. (Optional) Plot training curves\n# ---------------------------------\ndef plot_history(hist):\n    plt.figure(figsize=(12, 5))\n\n    # Accuracy\n    plt.subplot(1, 2, 1)\n    plt.plot(hist.history[\"accuracy\"], label=\"train\")\n    plt.plot(hist.history[\"val_accuracy\"], label=\"val\")\n    plt.title(\"Model Accuracy\")\n    plt.xlabel(\"Epoch\")\n    plt.ylabel(\"Accuracy\")\n    plt.legend()\n\n    # Loss\n    plt.subplot(1, 2, 2)\n    plt.plot(hist.history[\"loss\"], label=\"train\")\n    plt.plot(hist.history[\"val_loss\"], label=\"val\")\n    plt.title(\"Model Loss\")\n    plt.xlabel(\"Epoch\")\n    plt.ylabel(\"Loss\")\n    plt.legend()\n\n    plt.tight_layout()\n    plt.show()\n\nplot_history(history)\n\n# ---------------------------------\n# 7. Save the trained model (optional)\n# ---------------------------------\nmodel.save(\"mnist_dense_model.h5\")\nprint(\"\\nModel saved to 'mnist_dense_model.h5'\")\n```\n\n### How to use the script\n1. Save it as `train_mnist.py`.\n2. Install the dependencies (`tensorflow`, `numpy`, `matplotlib`).\n3. Run `python train_mnist.py`.\n\n### Customising the network\n- **Different data**: Replace the loading/pre\u2011processing part with your own dataset (ensure `x` is a NumPy array of shape `[samples, features]` and `y` matches the output format).\n- **Architecture**: Add/replace layers (e.g., `Conv2D` for images, `LSTM` for sequences) inside the `create_model()` function.\n- **Loss / metrics**: Use `sparse_categorical_crossentropy` if you keep integer labels, or switch to regression losses such as `mse` for continuous targets.\n- **Training parameters**: Tune `epochs`, `batch_size`, optimizer, learning rate, etc., to improve performance.\n\nFeel free to adapt any part of the code to fit your specific problem!"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Can you write the Python code to build and train a neural network?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks: \"Can you write the Python code to build and train a neural network?\" This is a generic request. We need to provide code. We should comply. The user didn't specify any disallowed content. It's allowed. Provide example code using Keras/TensorFlow perhaps. Use <answer> tags. Should be safe. Provide explanation? Probably provide code within <answer>. Ensure no disallowed content. Use simple example e.g., MNIST. Provide code for building a simple feedforward network, compiling, training. Let's do that.</think>\n<answer>\nBelow is a self\u2011contained Python script that builds and trains a simple feed\u2011forward neural network on the MNIST handwritten\u2011digit dataset using **TensorFlow\u202f/Keras**.  \nYou can run it as\u2011is (after installing the required packages) or adapt it to your own data and architecture.\n\n```python\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------\n# Simple Neural Network for MNIST classification (TensorFlow/Keras)\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------\n# Required packages:\n#   pip install tensorflow numpy matplotlib\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport tensorflow as tf\nfrom tensorflow import keras\nfrom tensorflow.keras import layers\n\n# ---------------------------------\n# 1. Load and prepare the dataset\n# ---------------------------------\n# MNIST: 28x28 grayscale images of digits 0\u20119\n(x_train, y_train), (x_test, y_test) = keras.datasets.mnist.load_data()\n\n# Normalize pixel values to the range [0, 1]\nx_train = x_train.astype(\"float32\") / 255.0\nx_test  = x_test.astype(\"float32\") / 255.0\n\n# Flatten the 28x28 images into 1\u2011D vectors (required for a dense network)\nx_train = x_train.reshape(-1, 28 * 28)\nx_test  = x_test.reshape(-1, 28 * 28)\n\n# Convert integer labels to one\u2011hot vectors (optional; Keras can handle integers directly)\nnum_classes = 10\ny_train_onehot = keras.utils.to_categorical(y_train, num_classes)\ny_test_onehot  = keras.utils.to_categorical(y_test, num_classes)\n\n# ---------------------------------\n# 2. Build the model architecture\n# ---------------------------------\ndef create_model():\n    model = keras.Sequential([\n        layers.Input(shape=(28 * 28,)),               # Input layer\n        layers.Dense(256, activation=\"relu\"),         # Hidden layer 1\n        layers.Dropout(0.2),                          # Regularisation\n        layers.Dense(128, activation=\"relu\"),         # Hidden layer 2\n        layers.Dropout(0.2),\n        layers.Dense(num_classes, activation=\"softmax\")  # Output layer\n    ])\n    return model\n\nmodel = create_model()\nmodel.summary()   # Print a summary of the network\n\n# ---------------------------------\n# 3. Compile the model\n# ---------------------------------\nmodel.compile(\n    optimizer=keras.optimizers.Adam(learning_rate=0.001),\n    loss=\"categorical_crossentropy\",   # use \"sparse_categorical_crossentropy\" if you keep integer labels\n    metrics=[\"accuracy\"]\n)\n\n# ---------------------------------\n# 4. Train the model\n# ---------------------------------\nepochs = 20\nbatch_size = 128\n\nhistory = model.fit(\n    x_train,\n    y_train_onehot,\n    validation_split=0.1,          # 10\u202f% of training data used for validation\n    epochs=epochs,\n    batch_size=batch_size,\n    verbose=2\n)\n\n# ---------------------------------\n# 5. Evaluate on the test set\n# ---------------------------------\ntest_loss, test_acc = model.evaluate(x_test, y_test_onehot, verbose=0)\nprint(f\"\\nTest accuracy: {test_acc:.4f} | Test loss: {test_loss:.4f}\")\n\n# ---------------------------------\n# 6. (Optional) Plot training curves\n# ---------------------------------\ndef plot_history(hist):\n    plt.figure(figsize=(12, 5))\n\n    # Accuracy\n    plt.subplot(1, 2, 1)\n    plt.plot(hist.history[\"accuracy\"], label=\"train\")\n    plt.plot(hist.history[\"val_accuracy\"], label=\"val\")\n    plt.title(\"Model Accuracy\")\n    plt.xlabel(\"Epoch\")\n    plt.ylabel(\"Accuracy\")\n    plt.legend()\n\n    # Loss\n    plt.subplot(1, 2, 2)\n    plt.plot(hist.history[\"loss\"], label=\"train\")\n    plt.plot(hist.history[\"val_loss\"], label=\"val\")\n    plt.title(\"Model Loss\")\n    plt.xlabel(\"Epoch\")\n    plt.ylabel(\"Loss\")\n    plt.legend()\n\n    plt.tight_layout()\n    plt.show()\n\nplot_history(history)\n\n# ---------------------------------\n# 7. Save the trained model (optional)\n# ---------------------------------\nmodel.save(\"mnist_dense_model.h5\")\nprint(\"\\nModel saved to 'mnist_dense_model.h5'\")\n```\n\n### How to use the script\n1. Save it as `train_mnist.py`.\n2. Install the dependencies (`tensorflow`, `numpy`, `matplotlib`).\n3. Run `python train_mnist.py`.\n\n### Customising the network\n- **Different data**: Replace the loading/pre\u2011processing part with your own dataset (ensure `x` is a NumPy array of shape `[samples, features]` and `y` matches the output format).\n- **Architecture**: Add/replace layers (e.g., `Conv2D` for images, `LSTM` for sequences) inside the `create_model()` function.\n- **Loss / metrics**: Use `sparse_categorical_crossentropy` if you keep integer labels, or switch to regression losses such as `mse` for continuous targets.\n- **Training parameters**: Tune `epochs`, `batch_size`, optimizer, learning rate, etc., to improve performance.\n\nFeel free to adapt any part of the code to fit your specific problem!"}], "q_num": 573, "originalconversations": ["Can you write the Python code to build and train a neural network?", "There are some deep learning libraries that can help us write clean and efficient code. We use [Pytorch](https://pytorch.org/) in our example.\n\nTo install the latest Pytorch, run the following command in your terminal.\n```\npip3 install torch\n```\n\nBelow is an example script for building and training a neural network using Pytorch. The basic pipeline is as follows.\nPrepare the training and test data.\nDefine the neural network, the loss function and the optimizer.\nTrain the neural network using the training dataset for multiple epochs.\nTest the trained neural network on the test set to make predictions for unseen examples.\n\n```\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport torch.optim as optim\n# Prepare the dataset\n# Define the training data\nX_train = torch.tensor([[0,0],[2,1],[1,0],[5,5]], dtype=torch.float32)\ny_train = torch.tensor([[0],[1],[1],[0]], dtype=torch.float32)\n\n# Define the test data\nX_test = torch.tensor([[6,6],[-1,1],[1,1],[0,7]], dtype=torch.float32)\ny_test = torch.tensor([[0],[1],[0],[1]], dtype=torch.float32)\n\n\nclass Network(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n\n        # Inputs to hidden layer linear transformation\n        self.hidden = nn.Linear(2, 4)\n\n        # Output layer, 1 units\n        self.output = nn.Linear(4, 1)\n\n        # Define sigmoid activation\n        self.sigmoid = nn.Sigmoid() \n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        # Pass the input tensor through each of our operations\n        x = self.hidden(x)\n        x = self.sigmoid(x)\n        x = self.output(x)\n        X = self.sigmoid(x)\n\n        return x\n\n# Instantiate the neural network\nmodel = Network()\n\n# Define the loss function and optimizer\ncriterion = nn.BCELoss()\noptimizer = optim.Adam(model.parameters(), lr=0.01)\n\n# Train the neural network\nfor epoch in range(5):\n    # Zeros the gradients accumulated from the previous batch/step of the model\n    optimizer.zero_grad()\n    # Calculate the output using the current neural network parameters\n    outputs = model(X_train)\n    # Calculate the loss by comparing predicted outputs and gold labels\n    loss = criterion(outputs, y_train)\n    # Performs backpropagation\n    loss.backward()\n    # Updates the weights in our neural network based on the results of backpropagation\n    optimizer.step()\n\n# Evaluate the performance of the neural network on the test dataset\n# We do not need to keep gradient information during inference time\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    outputs = model(X_test)\n    predicted = (outputs > 0.5).float()\n    accuracy = (predicted == y_test).float().mean()\n\nprint(f\"Accuracy: {accuracy.item()*100:.2f}%\")\n```\n"], "originalsource": "authors", "originalprompt": "Can you write the Python code to build and train a neural network?"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "I want to write a software design document for a new product `Chateval`, which can make it easier to evaluate generative AI systems (e.g., chatgpt and claude). Chateval can (1) provide many evaluation scenarios, from text writing to code and math, and (2) support many evaluation metrics, e.g. helpfulness, relevance and factuality. It not only provides developers with optimization directions, but also helps users use generative ai products such as chatgpt more reliably. Please help me finish the document that follows the structure:  [Overview],  [Goals], [Use Cases], [API Design], [Milestones], [Open Questions], [People]."}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to produce a software design document with sections: Overview, Goals, Use Cases, API Design, Milestones, Open Questions, People. Must output within <answer></answer> tags, per system instruction.\n\nWe should write a comprehensive design doc for Chateval. Include details like architecture, components, API endpoints, data models, evaluation scenarios, metrics, etc. Provide bullet points.\n\nMake sure to wrap whole answer in <answer> tags.\n\nProceed.</think>\n<answer>\n# Chateval \u2013 Design Document  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n**Chateval** is a modular evaluation platform that streamlines the assessment of generative AI systems (e.g., ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini).  \nIt offers:\n\n1. **A catalogue of evaluation scenarios** covering writing, coding, mathematics, reasoning, creativity, and more.  \n2. **A rich set of metrics** \u2013 helpfulness, relevance, factuality, correctness, robustness, latency, cost, etc. \u2013 that can be combined, weighted, and extended.  \n3. **Actionable feedback** for developers (optimization directions, regression detection) and end\u2011users (confidence scores, usage tips).  \n\nChateval is delivered as a SaaS service with a public REST/GraphQL API, a web UI for interactive evaluation, and a plug\u2011in SDK for integration into CI pipelines, IDEs, and chatbot front\u2011ends.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Goals\n| # | Goal | Success Criteria |\n|---|------|------------------|\n| G1 | **Comprehensive Scenario Coverage** \u2013 support \u2265 30 distinct evaluation scenarios out\u2011of\u2011the\u2011box. | Scenario library includes text generation, code synthesis, math problem solving, summarization, translation, dialog, reasoning, creative writing, safety, etc. |\n| G2 | **Extensible Metric Engine** \u2013 allow users to add custom metrics without code changes. | SDK provides a \u201cMetric Plug\u2011in\u201d interface; at least 5 third\u2011party custom metrics are published within 3 months of launch. |\n| G3 | **Developer\u2011centric Feedback** \u2013 surface root\u2011cause insights (e.g., missing knowledge, prompt ambiguity). | Evaluation reports contain actionable \u201cOptimization Tips\u201d and a \u201cRegression Heatmap\u201d. |\n| G4 | **User\u2011centric Reliability** \u2013 surface confidence scores and usage recommendations for end\u2011users. | UI shows per\u2011response confidence + \u201cBest\u2011Practice\u201d suggestions; user satisfaction surveys > 80% positive. |\n| G5 | **Scalable, Low\u2011Latency Service** \u2013 handle 10k concurrent evaluations with < 500\u202fms average latency. | Load testing demonstrates target throughput and latency under peak load. |\n| G6 | **Security & Privacy** \u2013 comply with GDPR, CCPA, and allow on\u2011prem deployment for sensitive data. | Data processing agreements, encryption at rest/in\u2011transit, and an on\u2011prem Docker image are available. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Use Cases\n\n| # | Actor | Description |\n|---|-------|-------------|\n| UC1 | **AI Developer** | Runs nightly CI jobs that automatically evaluate new model checkpoints against the full scenario suite; receives a regression report with metric deltas and suggested hyper\u2011parameter tweaks. |\n| UC2 | **Product Manager** | Queries aggregate metrics (e.g., \u201cHelpfulness@Top\u20111 across code\u2011generation scenarios\u201d) to prioritize roadmap items. |\n| UC3 | **Researcher** | Defines a custom \u201cLogical Consistency\u201d metric via the SDK, runs a targeted benchmark on a new prompting technique, and downloads raw logs for analysis. |\n| UC4 | **End\u2011User (Chatbot Consumer)** | While using ChatGPT, clicks \u201cEvaluate this response\u201d \u2013 receives a confidence score, explanation of lower factuality, and a link to a more reliable source. |\n| UC5 | **Compliance Officer** | Executes a \u201cSafety & Toxicity\u201d audit across all model releases, obtains a compliance certificate, and exports audit logs for regulators. |\n| UC6 | **IDE Plugin** | As a developer writes code, the plugin calls Chateval to evaluate generated snippets for correctness, security vulnerabilities, and style adherence, showing inline feedback. |\n\n---\n\n## 4. API Design  \n\n### 4.1 Service Endpoints (REST)\n\n| Method | Path | Description | Request Body | Response |\n|--------|------|-------------|--------------|----------|\n| `POST` | `/v1/evaluations` | Submit a batch evaluation request (one or many prompts). | `EvaluationRequest` | `EvaluationJob` (jobId, statusUrl) |\n| `GET` | `/v1/evaluations/{jobId}` | Poll job status / retrieve results. | \u2013 | `EvaluationResult` (overall scores, per\u2011scenario breakdown) |\n| `GET` | `/v1/scenarios` | List available evaluation scenarios. | \u2013 | `ScenarioList` |\n| `GET` | `/v1/metrics` | List built\u2011in metrics and their definitions. | \u2013 | `MetricList` |\n| `POST` | `/v1/metrics/custom` | Register a custom metric plug\u2011in (provides Docker image URL or code zip). | `CustomMetricSpec` | `MetricRegistration` (metricId) |\n| `POST` | `/v1/feedback` | Submit user\u2011level feedback (e.g., \u201cI think this answer is wrong\u201d). | `UserFeedback` | `Ack` |\n| `GET` | `/v1/health` | Service health check. | \u2013 | `HealthStatus` |\n\n#### Data Models (JSON Schema Highlights)\n\n```json\n{\n  \"EvaluationRequest\": {\n    \"modelId\": \"string\",                     // e.g., \"openai:gpt-4o\"\n    \"scenarios\": [\"code_synthesis\", \"math_q\"], // IDs from /scenarios\n    \"metrics\": [\"helpfulness\", \"factuality\"], // IDs or custom IDs\n    \"inputs\": [\n      {\n        \"prompt\": \"string\",\n        \"metadata\": {\"sessionId\": \"string\"}\n      }\n    ],\n    \"options\": {\n      \"temperature\": 0.7,\n      \"maxTokens\": 1024,\n      \"apiKey\": \"string\"                     // optional per\u2011model auth\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### 4.2 GraphQL (optional)\n\n```graphql\nquery {\n  scenarios {\n    id name description tags\n  }\n  evaluate(\n    modelId: \"anthropic:claude-3-5-sonnet\"\n    scenarioIds: [\"code_synthesis\"]\n    metricIds: [\"helpfulness\",\"robustness\"]\n    inputs: [{prompt: \"Write a function to reverse a linked list\"}]\n  ) {\n    jobId\n    status\n    results {\n      scenario\n      metric\n      score\n      explanation\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### 4.3 SDK (Python/JS)\n\n- **Client**: `ChatevalClient(base_url, api_key)`.\n- **Metric Plug\u2011in Interface**: subclass `BaseMetric` with `evaluate(reference, prediction) -> float, dict`.\n- **Scenario Builder**: utilities to generate synthetic prompts, retrieve gold\u2011standard references from public datasets (e.g., HumanEval, MATH).\n\n### 4.4 Authentication & Authorization\n\n| Method | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| API Key | Simple token sent in `Authorization: Bearer <key>` header. |\n| OAuth2 | For enterprise tenants; scopes: `eval:write`, `eval:read`, `metrics:manage`. |\n| RBAC | Roles: `admin`, `developer`, `analyst`, `end_user`. Permissions are enforced per endpoint. |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Milestones\n\n| Phase | Timeline | Deliverables |\n|-------|----------|--------------|\n| **M0 \u2013 Foundations** | 0\u20112\u202fmo | Project repo, CI/CD pipeline, core data model, basic REST endpoint `/health`. |\n| **M1 \u2013 Scenario & Metric Core** | 2\u20114\u202fmo | 15 built\u2011in scenarios, 5 core metrics, `/scenarios`, `/metrics` endpoints, initial SDK. |\n| **M2 \u2013 Evaluation Engine** | 4\u20116\u202fmo | Batch evaluation pipeline, async job handling, result storage, `/evaluations` API, UI prototype. |\n| **M3 \u2013 Extensibility** | 6\u20118\u202fmo | Custom metric registration, plug\u2011in sandbox (Docker), SDK docs, sample custom metric (logical consistency). |\n| **M4 \u2013 Production\u2011Ready** | 8\u201110\u202fmo | Load\u2011tested service (10k concurrent), security audit, GDPR compliance, on\u2011prem Docker image, monitoring & alerting. |\n| **M5 \u2013 Beta Launch** | 10\u201112\u202fmo | Public beta with 3 partner AI developers, UI dashboard, CI integration, user feedback collection. |\n| **M6 \u2013 GA Release** | 12\u201115\u202fmo | Full documentation, pricing/quotas, enterprise SSO, marketplace for community metrics, support SLA. |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Open Questions\n\n| # | Question | Options / Considerations |\n|---|----------|--------------------------|\n| Q1 | **Metric Ground Truth** \u2013 How to source high\u2011quality references for factuality across domains? | Use curated datasets, crowd\u2011sourced verification, or LLM\u2011based self\u2011checking with human audit. |\n| Q2 | **Prompt Diversity Generation** \u2013 Should scenario generation be deterministic or stochastic? | Deterministic for reproducibility; stochastic optional via a \u201cvariation seed\u201d. |\n| Q3 | **Privacy Model** \u2013 What data retention policies for user\u2011submitted prompts? | Default 30\u2011day purge; opt\u2011in long\u2011term storage for analytics. |\n| Q4 | **Pricing Model"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I want to write a software design document for a new product `Chateval`, which can make it easier to evaluate generative AI systems (e.g., chatgpt and claude). Chateval can (1) provide many evaluation scenarios, from text writing to code and math, and (2) support many evaluation metrics, e.g. helpfulness, relevance and factuality. It not only provides developers with optimization directions, but also helps users use generative ai products such as chatgpt more reliably. Please help me finish the document that follows the structure:  [Overview],  [Goals], [Use Cases], [API Design], [Milestones], [Open Questions], [People]."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to produce a software design document with sections: Overview, Goals, Use Cases, API Design, Milestones, Open Questions, People. Must output within <answer></answer> tags, per system instruction.\n\nWe should write a comprehensive design doc for Chateval. Include details like architecture, components, API endpoints, data models, evaluation scenarios, metrics, etc. Provide bullet points.\n\nMake sure to wrap whole answer in <answer> tags.\n\nProceed.</think>\n<answer>\n# Chateval \u2013 Design Document  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n**Chateval** is a modular evaluation platform that streamlines the assessment of generative AI systems (e.g., ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini).  \nIt offers:\n\n1. **A catalogue of evaluation scenarios** covering writing, coding, mathematics, reasoning, creativity, and more.  \n2. **A rich set of metrics** \u2013 helpfulness, relevance, factuality, correctness, robustness, latency, cost, etc. \u2013 that can be combined, weighted, and extended.  \n3. **Actionable feedback** for developers (optimization directions, regression detection) and end\u2011users (confidence scores, usage tips).  \n\nChateval is delivered as a SaaS service with a public REST/GraphQL API, a web UI for interactive evaluation, and a plug\u2011in SDK for integration into CI pipelines, IDEs, and chatbot front\u2011ends.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Goals\n| # | Goal | Success Criteria |\n|---|------|------------------|\n| G1 | **Comprehensive Scenario Coverage** 