targeted benchmark on a new prompting technique, and downloads raw logs for analysis. |\n| UC4 | **End\u2011User (Chatbot Consumer)** | While using ChatGPT, clicks \u201cEvaluate this response\u201d \u2013 receives a confidence score, explanation of lower factuality, and a link to a more reliable source. |\n| UC5 | **Compliance Officer** | Executes a \u201cSafety & Toxicity\u201d audit across all model releases, obtains a compliance certificate, and exports audit logs for regulators. |\n| UC6 | **IDE Plugin** | As a developer writes code, the plugin calls Chateval to evaluate generated snippets for correctness, security vulnerabilities, and style adherence, showing inline feedback. |\n\n---\n\n## 4. API Design  \n\n### 4.1 Service Endpoints (REST)\n\n| Method | Path | Description | Request Body | Response |\n|--------|------|-------------|--------------|----------|\n| `POST` | `/v1/evaluations` | Submit a batch evaluation request (one or many prompts). | `EvaluationRequest` | `EvaluationJob` (jobId, statusUrl) |\n| `GET` | `/v1/evaluations/{jobId}` | Poll job status / retrieve results. | \u2013 | `EvaluationResult` (overall scores, per\u2011scenario breakdown) |\n| `GET` | `/v1/scenarios` | List available evaluation scenarios. | \u2013 | `ScenarioList` |\n| `GET` | `/v1/metrics` | List built\u2011in metrics and their definitions. | \u2013 | `MetricList` |\n| `POST` | `/v1/metrics/custom` | Register a custom metric plug\u2011in (provides Docker image URL or code zip). | `CustomMetricSpec` | `MetricRegistration` (metricId) |\n| `POST` | `/v1/feedback` | Submit user\u2011level feedback (e.g., \u201cI think this answer is wrong\u201d). | `UserFeedback` | `Ack` |\n| `GET` | `/v1/health` | Service health check. | \u2013 | `HealthStatus` |\n\n#### Data Models (JSON Schema Highlights)\n\n```json\n{\n  \"EvaluationRequest\": {\n    \"modelId\": \"string\",                     // e.g., \"openai:gpt-4o\"\n    \"scenarios\": [\"code_synthesis\", \"math_q\"], // IDs from /scenarios\n    \"metrics\": [\"helpfulness\", \"factuality\"], // IDs or custom IDs\n    \"inputs\": [\n      {\n        \"prompt\": \"string\",\n        \"metadata\": {\"sessionId\": \"string\"}\n      }\n    ],\n    \"options\": {\n      \"temperature\": 0.7,\n      \"maxTokens\": 1024,\n      \"apiKey\": \"string\"                     // optional per\u2011model auth\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### 4.2 GraphQL (optional)\n\n```graphql\nquery {\n  scenarios {\n    id name description tags\n  }\n  evaluate(\n    modelId: \"anthropic:claude-3-5-sonnet\"\n    scenarioIds: [\"code_synthesis\"]\n    metricIds: [\"helpfulness\",\"robustness\"]\n    inputs: [{prompt: \"Write a function to reverse a linked list\"}]\n  ) {\n    jobId\n    status\n    results {\n      scenario\n      metric\n      score\n      explanation\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### 4.3 SDK (Python/JS)\n\n- **Client**: `ChatevalClient(base_url, api_key)`.\n- **Metric Plug\u2011in Interface**: subclass `BaseMetric` with `evaluate(reference, prediction) -> float, dict`.\n- **Scenario Builder**: utilities to generate synthetic prompts, retrieve gold\u2011standard references from public datasets (e.g., HumanEval, MATH).\n\n### 4.4 Authentication & Authorization\n\n| Method | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| API Key | Simple token sent in `Authorization: Bearer <key>` header. |\n| OAuth2 | For enterprise tenants; scopes: `eval:write`, `eval:read`, `metrics:manage`. |\n| RBAC | Roles: `admin`, `developer`, `analyst`, `end_user`. Permissions are enforced per endpoint. |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Milestones\n\n| Phase | Timeline | Deliverables |\n|-------|----------|--------------|\n| **M0 \u2013 Foundations** | 0\u20112\u202fmo | Project repo, CI/CD pipeline, core data model, basic REST endpoint `/health`. |\n| **M1 \u2013 Scenario & Metric Core** | 2\u20114\u202fmo | 15 built\u2011in scenarios, 5 core metrics, `/scenarios`, `/metrics` endpoints, initial SDK. |\n| **M2 \u2013 Evaluation Engine** | 4\u20116\u202fmo | Batch evaluation pipeline, async job handling, result storage, `/evaluations` API, UI prototype. |\n| **M3 \u2013 Extensibility** | 6\u20118\u202fmo | Custom metric registration, plug\u2011in sandbox (Docker), SDK docs, sample custom metric (logical consistency). |\n| **M4 \u2013 Production\u2011Ready** | 8\u201110\u202fmo | Load\u2011tested service (10k concurrent), security audit, GDPR compliance, on\u2011prem Docker image, monitoring & alerting. |\n| **M5 \u2013 Beta Launch** | 10\u201112\u202fmo | Public beta with 3 partner AI developers, UI dashboard, CI integration, user feedback collection. |\n| **M6 \u2013 GA Release** | 12\u201115\u202fmo | Full documentation, pricing/quotas, enterprise SSO, marketplace for community metrics, support SLA. |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Open Questions\n\n| # | Question | Options / Considerations |\n|---|----------|--------------------------|\n| Q1 | **Metric Ground Truth** \u2013 How to source high\u2011quality references for factuality across domains? | Use curated datasets, crowd\u2011sourced verification, or LLM\u2011based self\u2011checking with human audit. |\n| Q2 | **Prompt Diversity Generation** \u2013 Should scenario generation be deterministic or stochastic? | Deterministic for reproducibility; stochastic optional via a \u201cvariation seed\u201d. |\n| Q3 | **Privacy Model** \u2013 What data retention policies for user\u2011submitted prompts? | Default 30\u2011day purge; opt\u2011in long\u2011term storage for analytics. |\n| Q4 | **Pricing Model"}], "q_num": 457, "originalconversations": ["I want to write a software design document for a new product `Chateval`, which can make it easier to evaluate generative AI systems (e.g., chatgpt and claude). Chateval can (1) provide many evaluation scenarios, from text writing to code and math, and (2) support many evaluation metrics, e.g. helpfulness, relevance and factuality. It not only provides developers with optimization directions, but also helps users use generative ai products such as chatgpt more reliably. Please help me finish the document that follows the structure:  [Overview],  [Goals], [Use Cases], [API Design], [Milestones], [Open Questions], [People].", "Sure, here is a software design document for your product \u2013 Chateval.\n\n[Overview]\nA major challenge facing current \"generative AI'' systems is the difficulty of determining their performance in a systematic manner, particularly for high-quality models. For example, it\u2019s hard to quantitatively discriminate between two famous chatbots: ChatGPT and Claude. Therefore, we really need an evaluation tool that can help us automate the characterization of generative AI systems.\nChateval has two main functions that can assist with the development and evaluation of generative AI systems.\n*  Support comprehensive evaluation suits: Chateval provides more than 50 different evaluation scenarios, ranging from copywriting, coding, math and safety detection. Generative AI system developers can flexibly choose what they\u2019re interested in.\n* Support rich evaluation metrics: Chateval provides a wealth of metrics that can be used to evaluate generated texts from different criteria, such as helpfulness, relevance, factuality etc.\n\n[Goals]\nDevelop an evaluation tool for generative AI so that both developers and general users can benefit from it.\n\n[Use Cases]\nThere are several potential use scenarios for Chateval:\n\n# Reveal Limitations of Generative AI systems\nChateval can be particularly valuable to developers who are working on an AI system but are uncertain of its weaknesses. With the aid of Chateval, these developers can swiftly pinpoint the system's flaws and suggest the appropriate corrective measures.\n\n# Provide Reliability Score for Users\nChateval can be utilized by users who are uncertain about the reliability (e.g. factuality) of the output generated by a generative AI product. It can offer confidence scores for various functionalities to assess the credibility of the results.\n\n[API Design]\nThis is a basic design of the API for evaluation:\n# Evaluate in terms of write_email scenario\n```\nfrom chateval import load\nscenario = load(\"../scenarios/write_email\")\npredictions = [\n    \"My name is [name], and I am currently a student in your [class name].\",\n]\nprint(scenario.evaluate(predictions))\n```\n# Evaluate Single System with Chateval Metric\n```\nfrom chateval.metrics import get_metric\ndataset = [{\"input\": \"write a movie review of Titanic\"}]\npredictions = [\n    'James Cameron\\'s 1997 epic romantic disaster film \"Titanic\" tells the '\n]\nmetric = get_metric(\"generic_likert/helpfulness\")\nresults = metric.compute(dataset, predictions)\nprint(results)\n\n```\n[Milestones]\n* 1 month: Implement crowdsourable framework for Chateval\n* 1 week: Collect benchmark datasets for evaluation scenarios\n* 2 weeks: Implement model-based and model-free evaluation metrics\n* 2 - 3 weeks: Use Chateval to evaluate existing popular chatbots\n\n[Open Questions]\n* How to design a user-friendly interface?\n* How to support more diverse chatbots\n\n[People]\n* [Name 1]\n* [Name 2]\n* [Name 3]"], "originalsource": "authors", "originalprompt": "I want to write a software design document for a new product `Chateval`, which can make it easier to evaluate generative AI systems (e.g., chatgpt and claude). Chateval can (1) provide many evaluation scenarios, from text writing to code and math, and (2) support many evaluation metrics, e.g. helpfulness, relevance and factuality. It not only provides developers with optimization directions, but also helps users use generative ai products such as chatgpt more reliably. Please help me finish the document that follows the structure:  [Overview],  [Goals], [Use Cases], [API Design], [Milestones], [Open Questions], [People]."}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "My son doesn't want to share anything with other kids, and if some kid even so much as touches his toy, he pushes the kid. He shouts and cries at the same time, and tries to express his anger by pushing and hitting the kid. I feel so embarrassed in front of other parents. \nAnd when he is at home with me and doing something wrong, I try to stop him, he tries to repeat my words and shouts at me. He is copying the behavior of others, whether it's a good act or bad...\nPlease help me how to stop him from being a bully."}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to respond with helpful advice for parent dealing with child's aggressive behavior. Must be in <answer> tags. Provide strategies, possible underlying issues, consult professional, etc. Ensure safe and supportive tone.</think>\n<answer>\nThank you for reaching out. It\u2019s understandable to feel embarrassed and worried when your child reacts aggressively toward peers and even toward you. Below are practical, evidence\u2011based steps you can take right away, as well as longer\u2011term strategies to help your son develop healthier social skills and emotional regulation.\n\n---\n\n## 1\ufe0f\u20e3 Understand What\u2019s Going On\n\n| Possible Factor | What It Looks Like | Why It Matters |\n|-----------------|-------------------|----------------|\n| **Limited emotional regulation** | He becomes overwhelmed quickly and reacts with hitting or shouting. | Young children often lack the tools to calm down before they act. |\n| **Communication skill gaps** | He repeats phrases (\u201cstop,\u201d \u201cdon\u2019t\u201d) without knowing how to express needs. | If he can\u2019t label feelings, the default is \u201cfight.\u201d |\n| **Modeling behavior** | He copies both good and bad actions he sees (parents, teachers, media). | Children learn by imitation; inconsistent adult responses can reinforce aggression. |\n| **Boundary testing / autonomy** | He pushes back when you set limits. | This is a normal developmental drive for independence that can appear as defiance. |\n| **Sensory or neuro\u2011developmental factors** (e.g., ADHD, ASD) | Impulsivity, difficulty reading social cues, hypersensitivity to touch. | If these are present, tailored strategies may be needed. |\n\n**First step:** Observe when the aggressive episodes happen (time of day, setting, who is involved, preceding events). Write brief notes for a week. This pattern will help you and any professional you consult pinpoint triggers.\n\n---\n\n## 2\ufe0f\u20e3 Immediate \u201cDe\u2011Escalation\u201d Toolkit (What to Do in the Moment)\n\n1. **Stay Calm & Safe**\n   - Take a slow, deep breath before you respond. Your calmness signals that aggression is not an effective way to get attention.\n   - Ensure all children are physically safe; gently separate them if needed.\n\n2. **Use a \u201cSafety Word\u201d or Signal**\n   - Choose a word (e.g., \u201cPause\u201d) or a visual cue (a colored card) that you teach him means \u201cstop what you\u2019re doing; we need a break.\u201d\n   - Practice it when he\u2019s calm so it becomes a shared signal.\n\n3. **Label Emotions & Offer Choices**\n   - **Label:** \u201cI see you\u2019re feeling really angry because someone touched your toy.\u201d\n   - **Validate:** \u201cIt\u2019s okay to feel angry.\u201d\n   - **Offer a choice:** \u201cYou can either (a) take three deep breaths and tell me what\u2019s wrong, or (b) we can go to a quiet spot together.\u201d  \n   Giving him a choice restores a sense of control without aggression.\n\n4. **Brief, Clear Consequence**\n   - \u201cIf you hit, we will have to stop playing for five minutes.\u201d  \n   Follow through *immediately* and *consistently*. The consequence must be **immediate, brief, and related** to the behavior.\n\n5. **Positive Reinforcement**\n   - When he uses words or the safety signal instead of hitting, praise specifically: \u201cGreat job telling us you\u2019re angry and taking a breath. I\u2019m proud of how you handled that.\u201d\n\n---\n\n## 3\ufe0f\u20e3 Daily Skill\u2011Building Routine\n\n| Skill | How to Teach | Everyday Practice |\n|-------|--------------|-------------------|\n| **Emotion Vocabulary** | Use picture cards (happy, sad, angry, scared, calm). Play \u201cWhat\u2019s this feeling?\u201d during meals or bedtime. | Label your own feelings aloud: \u201cI\u2019m feeling frustrated because the coffee spilled, so I\u2019ll take a breath.\u201d |\n| **Self\u2011Soothing (Calm\u2011Down Corner)** | Set up a small space with soft pillows, a sensory bottle, weighted blanket, or a \u201cquiet box\u201d of fidget toys. | Invite him to use it **before** he gets angry, not only after. Practice a \u201ctake\u2011three\u2011breaths\u201d routine together. |\n| **Impulse Control Games** | \u201cSimon Says,\u201d \u201cRed Light/Green Light,\u201d \u201cFreeze Dance.\u201d These turn self\u2011control into fun. | 5\u201110 minutes before transitions (e.g., leaving the park). |\n| **Problem\u2011Solving Scripts** | Role\u2011play common scenarios: *\u201cIf someone takes your truck, you can say \u2018Excuse me, I\u2019m still playing.\u2019 If they don\u2019t listen, we ask an adult.\u201d* | Rehearse during playtime; give him a \u201ctalking stick\u201d to practice phrases. |\n| **Copying Positive Behaviors** | Model calm reactions yourself. Narrate: \u201cI\u2019m feeling a little angry because I have to wait, so I\u2019ll count to five.\u201d | Include him in adult tasks that require patience (e.g., waiting in line, cooking). |\n\n---\n\n## 4\ufe0f\u20e3 Consistency Across Settings\n\n- **Home:** Keep rules and responses the same for siblings, guests, and you.\n- **Daycare/Preschool:** Communicate the same expectations and strategies with teachers. Ask them to use the same \u201cpause\u201d word or calm\u2011down corner.\n- **Playdates:** Before the visit, brief the host (and you) about the signal and consequences. Offer to bring a calm\u2011down kit.\n\n**Why consistency matters:** Children learn cause\u2011and\u2011effect. When one adult says \u201cno hitting\u201d and another ignores it, the child gets mixed messages.\n\n---\n\n## 5\ufe0f\u20e3 Positive Parenting Strategies\n\n1. **Catch Him Being Good** \u2013 Notice and comment on any cooperative or gentle behavior, even if it\u2019s small.\n2. **Use \u201cWhen/Then\u201d Statements** \u2013 \u201cWhen you use your words, then you can keep playing with the blocks.\u201d\n3. **Give Limited Choices** \u2013 \u201cDo you want to put the puzzle away now or after we finish this song?\u201d  \n   Choices reduce power struggles.\n4. **Avoid Power Struggles** \u2013 If he says \u201cNo!\u201d and you keep insisting, the interaction escalates. Offer a neutral alternative: \u201cOkay, let\u2019s try something else for a minute and come back.\u201d\n\n---\n\n## 6\ufe0f\u20e3 When to Seek Professional Help\n\n- **Persistence** \u2013 If aggressive outbursts continue daily for more than 4\u20136 weeks despite consistent strategies.\n- **Severity** \u2013 If he causes injuries, or the aggression spreads to adults.\n- **Co\u2011occurring Issues** \u2013 Noticeable attention problems, sensory sensitivities, language delays, or social misunderstandings.\n- **Parental Stress** \u2013 If you feel overwhelmed, a therapist can support both you and your child.\n\n**Who to contact:**  \n- **Pediatrician** \u2013 rule out medical or developmental concerns.  \n- **Child Psychologist or Licensed Clinical Social Worker** \u2013 for behavior therapy (e.g., Parent\u2011Child Interaction Therapy, CBT for anger).  \n- **Occupational Therapist** \u2013 if sensory processing issues are suspected.\n\nEarly intervention often prevents escalation and reduces long\u2011term risk of bullying behavior.\n\n---\n\n## 7\ufe0f\u20e3 Quick \u201cCheat Sheet\u201d for Parents\n\n| Situation | What to Say | What to Do |\n|-----------|-------------|------------|\n| **Kid touches his toy** | \u201cI see you\u2019re angry because your toy was touched.\u201d | Offer the safety word: \u201cIf you feel angry, say \u2018pause.\u2019\u201d If he still hits \u2192 brief timeout (1\u20112 min). |\n| **He shouts at you** | \u201cYou\u2019re shouting because you\u2019re upset. Let\u2019s use words.\u201d | Move to calm\u2011down corner together, model deep breaths. |\n| **After a calm moment** | \u201cI liked how you told me you were upset without hitting.\u201d | Give a specific praise and a small reward (sticker, extra story time). |\n| **Repeat misbehavior** | \u201cWe talked about using words. Let\u2019s try again.\u201d | Immediate, consistent consequence; then immediately teach the alternative phrase. |\n| **Positive behavior** | \u201cGreat job sharing your blocks with Sam!\u201d | Reinforce with high\u2011fives, verbal praise, or a token system. |\n\n---\n\n## 8\ufe0f\u20e3 Resources & Tools\n\n- **Books for Parents:**  \n  - *\u201cThe Whole-Brain Child\u201d* by Daniel Siegel & Tina Payne Bryson  \n  - *\u201cNo-Drama Discipline\u201d* by the same authors  \n  - *\u201cRaising Human Beings\u201d* by Ross Greene (focus on Collaborative Problem Solving)\n\n- **Books for Kids (age 3\u20116):**  \n  - *\u201cWhen Sophie Gets Angry \u2013 Really, Really Angry\u2026\u201d* by Molly Bang  \n  - *\u201cMy Mouth Is a Volcano!\u201d* by Julia Cook (helps kids learn to wait their turn to speak)  \n  - *\u201cHands Are Not for Hitting\u201d* by Martine Agassi\n\n- **Apps & Visual Supports:**  \n  - **Social Stories Creator** \u2013 make custom stories about sharing and calm-down strategies.  \n  - **Breathe2Relax** \u2013 simple breathing exercise for kids.  \n  - Printable *emotion charts* (found on sites like Teachers Pay Teachers or free educational blogs).\n\n---\n\n## 9\ufe0f\u20e3 A Sample Daily Plan (First 2 Weeks)\n\n| Time | Activity | Goal |\n|------|----------|------|\n| **Morning (after breakfast)** | **Emotion Check\u2011In** \u2013 look at picture cards, label your own feelings. | Build vocabulary. |\n| **Mid\u2011morning (playtime)** | Use **calm\u2011down corner** pre\u2011emptively; practice \u201cpause\u201d word. | Prevent escalation. |\n| **Lunchtime** | **Positive Praise Log** \u2013 note one thing he did well. | Reinforce good behavior. |\n|"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "My son doesn't want to share anything with other kids, and if some kid even so much as touches his toy, he pushes the kid. He shouts and cries at the same time, and tries to express his anger by pushing and hitting the kid. I feel so embarrassed in front of other parents. \nAnd when he is at home with me and doing something wrong, I try to stop him, he tries to repeat my words and shouts at me. He is copying the behavior of others, whether it's a good act or bad...\nPlease help me how to stop him from being a bully."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to respond with helpful advice for parent dealing with child's aggressive behavior. Must be in <answer> tags. Provide strategies, possible underlying issues, consult professional, etc. Ensure safe and supportive tone.</think>\n<answer>\nThank you for reaching out. It\u2019s understandable to feel embarrassed and worried when your child reacts aggressively toward peers and even toward you. Below are practical, evidence\u2011based steps you can take right away, as well as longer\u2011term strategies to help your son develop healthier social skills and emotional regulation.\n\n---\n\n## 1\ufe0f\u20e3 Understand What\u2019s Going On\n\n| Possible Factor | What It Looks Like | Why It Matters |\n|-----------------|-------------------|----------------|\n| **Limited emotional regulation** | He becomes overwhelmed quickly and reacts with hitting or shouting. | Young children often lack the tools to calm down before they