e restores a sense of control without aggression.\n\n4. **Brief, Clear Consequence**\n   - \u201cIf you hit, we will have to stop playing for five minutes.\u201d  \n   Follow through *immediately* and *consistently*. The consequence must be **immediate, brief, and related** to the behavior.\n\n5. **Positive Reinforcement**\n   - When he uses words or the safety signal instead of hitting, praise specifically: \u201cGreat job telling us you\u2019re angry and taking a breath. I\u2019m proud of how you handled that.\u201d\n\n---\n\n## 3\ufe0f\u20e3 Daily Skill\u2011Building Routine\n\n| Skill | How to Teach | Everyday Practice |\n|-------|--------------|-------------------|\n| **Emotion Vocabulary** | Use picture cards (happy, sad, angry, scared, calm). Play \u201cWhat\u2019s this feeling?\u201d during meals or bedtime. | Label your own feelings aloud: \u201cI\u2019m feeling frustrated because the coffee spilled, so I\u2019ll take a breath.\u201d |\n| **Self\u2011Soothing (Calm\u2011Down Corner)** | Set up a small space with soft pillows, a sensory bottle, weighted blanket, or a \u201cquiet box\u201d of fidget toys. | Invite him to use it **before** he gets angry, not only after. Practice a \u201ctake\u2011three\u2011breaths\u201d routine together. |\n| **Impulse Control Games** | \u201cSimon Says,\u201d \u201cRed Light/Green Light,\u201d \u201cFreeze Dance.\u201d These turn self\u2011control into fun. | 5\u201110 minutes before transitions (e.g., leaving the park). |\n| **Problem\u2011Solving Scripts** | Role\u2011play common scenarios: *\u201cIf someone takes your truck, you can say \u2018Excuse me, I\u2019m still playing.\u2019 If they don\u2019t listen, we ask an adult.\u201d* | Rehearse during playtime; give him a \u201ctalking stick\u201d to practice phrases. |\n| **Copying Positive Behaviors** | Model calm reactions yourself. Narrate: \u201cI\u2019m feeling a little angry because I have to wait, so I\u2019ll count to five.\u201d | Include him in adult tasks that require patience (e.g., waiting in line, cooking). |\n\n---\n\n## 4\ufe0f\u20e3 Consistency Across Settings\n\n- **Home:** Keep rules and responses the same for siblings, guests, and you.\n- **Daycare/Preschool:** Communicate the same expectations and strategies with teachers. Ask them to use the same \u201cpause\u201d word or calm\u2011down corner.\n- **Playdates:** Before the visit, brief the host (and you) about the signal and consequences. Offer to bring a calm\u2011down kit.\n\n**Why consistency matters:** Children learn cause\u2011and\u2011effect. When one adult says \u201cno hitting\u201d and another ignores it, the child gets mixed messages.\n\n---\n\n## 5\ufe0f\u20e3 Positive Parenting Strategies\n\n1. **Catch Him Being Good** \u2013 Notice and comment on any cooperative or gentle behavior, even if it\u2019s small.\n2. **Use \u201cWhen/Then\u201d Statements** \u2013 \u201cWhen you use your words, then you can keep playing with the blocks.\u201d\n3. **Give Limited Choices** \u2013 \u201cDo you want to put the puzzle away now or after we finish this song?\u201d  \n   Choices reduce power struggles.\n4. **Avoid Power Struggles** \u2013 If he says \u201cNo!\u201d and you keep insisting, the interaction escalates. Offer a neutral alternative: \u201cOkay, let\u2019s try something else for a minute and come back.\u201d\n\n---\n\n## 6\ufe0f\u20e3 When to Seek Professional Help\n\n- **Persistence** \u2013 If aggressive outbursts continue daily for more than 4\u20136 weeks despite consistent strategies.\n- **Severity** \u2013 If he causes injuries, or the aggression spreads to adults.\n- **Co\u2011occurring Issues** \u2013 Noticeable attention problems, sensory sensitivities, language delays, or social misunderstandings.\n- **Parental Stress** \u2013 If you feel overwhelmed, a therapist can support both you and your child.\n\n**Who to contact:**  \n- **Pediatrician** \u2013 rule out medical or developmental concerns.  \n- **Child Psychologist or Licensed Clinical Social Worker** \u2013 for behavior therapy (e.g., Parent\u2011Child Interaction Therapy, CBT for anger).  \n- **Occupational Therapist** \u2013 if sensory processing issues are suspected.\n\nEarly intervention often prevents escalation and reduces long\u2011term risk of bullying behavior.\n\n---\n\n## 7\ufe0f\u20e3 Quick \u201cCheat Sheet\u201d for Parents\n\n| Situation | What to Say | What to Do |\n|-----------|-------------|------------|\n| **Kid touches his toy** | \u201cI see you\u2019re angry because your toy was touched.\u201d | Offer the safety word: \u201cIf you feel angry, say \u2018pause.\u2019\u201d If he still hits \u2192 brief timeout (1\u20112 min). |\n| **He shouts at you** | \u201cYou\u2019re shouting because you\u2019re upset. Let\u2019s use words.\u201d | Move to calm\u2011down corner together, model deep breaths. |\n| **After a calm moment** | \u201cI liked how you told me you were upset without hitting.\u201d | Give a specific praise and a small reward (sticker, extra story time). |\n| **Repeat misbehavior** | \u201cWe talked about using words. Let\u2019s try again.\u201d | Immediate, consistent consequence; then immediately teach the alternative phrase. |\n| **Positive behavior** | \u201cGreat job sharing your blocks with Sam!\u201d | Reinforce with high\u2011fives, verbal praise, or a token system. |\n\n---\n\n## 8\ufe0f\u20e3 Resources & Tools\n\n- **Books for Parents:**  \n  - *\u201cThe Whole-Brain Child\u201d* by Daniel Siegel & Tina Payne Bryson  \n  - *\u201cNo-Drama Discipline\u201d* by the same authors  \n  - *\u201cRaising Human Beings\u201d* by Ross Greene (focus on Collaborative Problem Solving)\n\n- **Books for Kids (age 3\u20116):**  \n  - *\u201cWhen Sophie Gets Angry \u2013 Really, Really Angry\u2026\u201d* by Molly Bang  \n  - *\u201cMy Mouth Is a Volcano!\u201d* by Julia Cook (helps kids learn to wait their turn to speak)  \n  - *\u201cHands Are Not for Hitting\u201d* by Martine Agassi\n\n- **Apps & Visual Supports:**  \n  - **Social Stories Creator** \u2013 make custom stories about sharing and calm-down strategies.  \n  - **Breathe2Relax** \u2013 simple breathing exercise for kids.  \n  - Printable *emotion charts* (found on sites like Teachers Pay Teachers or free educational blogs).\n\n---\n\n## 9\ufe0f\u20e3 A Sample Daily Plan (First 2 Weeks)\n\n| Time | Activity | Goal |\n|------|----------|------|\n| **Morning (after breakfast)** | **Emotion Check\u2011In** \u2013 look at picture cards, label your own feelings. | Build vocabulary. |\n| **Mid\u2011morning (playtime)** | Use **calm\u2011down corner** pre\u2011emptively; practice \u201cpause\u201d word. | Prevent escalation. |\n| **Lunchtime** | **Positive Praise Log** \u2013 note one thing he did well. | Reinforce good behavior. |\n|"}], "q_num": 240, "originalconversations": ["My son doesn't want to share anything with other kids, and if some kid even so much as touches his toy, he pushes the kid. He shouts and cries at the same time, and tries to express his anger by pushing and hitting the kid. I feel so embarrassed in front of other parents. \nAnd when he is at home with me and doing something wrong, I try to stop him, he tries to repeat my words and shouts at me. He is copying the behavior of others, whether it's a good act or bad...\nPlease help me how to stop him from being a bully.", "Welcome to the \"Terrible Two's\"! \n\nYour son behaves typically for his age. At 2 he starts to assert himself and express his demands. It's likely that he has also learned that he gets his way when he throws a tantrum. For a 2-year old screaming, hitting or even biting is a normal way to express his anger - at least he will try and every success reinforces this.\nHe also comes across more limitations: The more he is able to do, the more often he is stopped.\n\nThe solution? It's already in your question:\nHe will copy what others do. So by all means, stay calm. No yelling, no fussing and - please! - no hitting. He has some learning (accepted behaviour like sharing) and some unlearning (no hitting or pushing) to do and needs your help. \n\n* When he throws a tantrum, either ignore him, or quietly remove him from the scene. Do not worry about others! Most likely they have had the same trouble with their kids or will some time ;-) \n* When he doesn't want to share yet - fine, he will learn. This can be practiced. Don't make him share, or he will resent it. Rather, teach him the benefits of sharing. Start with food, for example. Have a plate of cookies (or other favorite food), that's yours. He will likely demand some, but demanding doesn't get him a cookie. Asking nicely will! Explain, repeat. Then, switch roles. If he has cookies, ask him to share. If he does, praise him. Else - remind him next time when he wants your cookies that he didn't share. Share whenever possible: Having a bite from your plate, getting a piece off his. Repeat, repeat, repeat.... Same goes for objects. Ask him to \"please\" share a toy, \"play\" with it for a short moment, then return. Say \"Thank you\". He learns, that he gets the toy back. Share some of your things. (Monitor the behaviour of other children, that want his toys - make sure they ask first and give toys back, at least during this learning stage.)\n\nYou have some serious work to do, but it will be sooooo worth it! "], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "My son doesn't want to share anything with other kids, and if some kid even so much as touches his toy, he pushes the kid. He shouts and cries at the same time, and tries to express his anger by pushing and hitting the kid. I feel so embarrassed in front of other parents. \nAnd when he is at home with me and doing something wrong, I try to stop him, he tries to repeat my words and shouts at me. He is copying the behavior of others, whether it's a good act or bad...\nPlease help me how to stop him from being a bully."}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "I am a librarian at the Technion. The Technion is a public polytechnic university in Haifa, Israel. Please help me write a FAQ page for the Technion's library system. It should include questions and answers in the following categories:\n* General\n* Circulation\n* Computing & Network\n* Peripheral Equipment\n* Off-Campus Access\n* Historical Archive\n"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to write a FAQ page for Technion's library system. Include categories with questions and answers. Provide headings and a list of Q&A. Should be helpful, likely include contact info, hours, policies. Provide concise but thorough answers. Probably include bullet points. Let's produce structured HTML or markdown? Not specified; probably plain text or simple markup. Use categories with subheadings.\n\nWill include typical FAQs: opening hours, card, borrowing limits, renewals, fines, online catalogs, Wi-Fi, printing, scanning, remote access, VPN, VPN login, Off-campus access, VPN for databases, login via mytech, remote services, interlibrary loans. Historical archive: access to special collections, digitized materials, how to request, usage policies, preservation, appointments.\n\nWill be targeted to Technion staff and students.\n\nLet's produce.\n\n</think>\n**Technion Library \u2013 Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)**  \n\n*Welcome to the Technion Library system! Below you will find quick answers to the most common questions. If you need further assistance, please contact the Library Help Desk (phone\u202f+972\u20114\u2011829\u20117333, email\u202flibrary@technion.ac.il).*\n\n---\n\n## 1. General\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| **What are the library\u2019s opening hours?** | **Main Campus Library** \u2013 Sunday\u2011Thursday: 08:00\u201122:00, Friday\u2011Saturday: 09:00\u201118:00 (closed on Jewish holidays). <br>**Satellite libraries** (Engineering, Medical, Humanities, etc.) have similar hours; see the specific branch page for exact times. |\n| **Who can use the library?** | All Technion students, faculty, staff, and researchers (including alumni with a valid Technion ID) may obtain a library card and use services. External visitors may use reading rooms with an on\u2011site registration and a fee for certain resources. |\n| **How do I get a library card?** | Register online via the \u201cMy Technion\u201d portal or visit the circulation desk with your Technion ID. The card is issued instantly and can be used for borrowing, printing, and Wi\u2011Fi authentication. |\n| **Where can I find the library catalogue?** | The online catalogue (OPAC) is accessible at **[https://library.technion.ac.il/catalogue]**. It provides search across books, journals, e\u2011books, theses, and special collections. |\n| **Do you have study rooms?** | Yes. Each floor of the Main Library has reservable silent study rooms (up to 6 people). Book them via the \u201cRoom Reservation\u201d tab in the catalogue or by contacting the help desk. |\n| **Is there a mobile app?** | The Technion Library app (available for iOS & Android) lets you search the catalogue, place holds, renew items, and scan QR codes for indoor navigation. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Circulation\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| **How many items can I borrow?** | **Students:** up to 10 items (max 5 books + 5 other media). <br>**Faculty/Staff:** up to 20 items (max 10 books + 10 other media). <br>Special collections and course reserves have separate limits. |\n| **Loan periods** | **Books & printed media:** 4\u202fweeks (renewable up to 2\u202ftimes unless on hold). <br>**DVDs, CDs, equipment:** 1\u202fweek (renewable once). <br>**E\u2011books & databases:** Access controlled by license; usually 2\u202fweeks after checkout. |\n| **How do I renew items?** | Renew online via the catalogue (my\u2011loans page) or the mobile app, or in\u2011person at any circulation desk. Items with pending holds cannot be renewed. |\n| **What are the fines?** | **Books:** \u20aa1.00 per day (max \u20aa30). <br>**Multimedia:** \u20aa2.00 per day. <br>Fines are waived for first\u2011time late returns if you contact the desk within 3\u202fdays. |\n| **Can I request items from another Technion campus?** | Yes. Use the \u201cInter\u2011Campus Loan\u201d feature in the catalogue. Items are delivered to your home library within 2\u20113\u202fbusiness days. |\n| **How do I place a hold on an item?** | Click \u201cPlace Hold\u201d on the item record in the catalogue. You will receive an email and SMS (if opted\u2011in) when the item arrives. |\n| **What is a \u201cCourse Reserve\u201d?** | Faculty can place material on reserve for a specific course. Reserves can be borrowed for 24\u202fhours only and are not renewable. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Computing & Network\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| **Is Wi\u2011Fi available?** | Free Wi\u2011Fi (SSID: **Technion\u2011Library**) is available throughout all library buildings. Log in with your Technion ID and password. |\n| **Do I need a VPN for off\u2011campus resources?** | Yes. Use the Technion VPN (OpenVPN client) to access subscription databases, e\u2011books, and internal services. Detailed setup instructions are on the \u201cOff\u2011Campus Access\u201d page. |\n| **Can I use my own laptop/tablet?** | Absolutely. Power outlets are provided at most tables. Charging stations are available on each floor. |\n| **What software is pre\u2011installed on library computers?** | Windows 11, Microsoft Office 365, Adobe Acrobat, Zotero, EndNote, SPSS, MATLAB, and a selection of design/visualisation tools. |\n| **How do I print from my own device?** | Authenticate at any printer using your Technion ID card or the \u201cPrintShare\u201d mobile app. You can print up to 30 pages per day for free; additional pages are charged \u20aa0.20 per page. |\n| **Is there a secure browser for confidential research?** | Library computers run a privacy\u2011enhanced browser (Firefox with tracking protection). For sensitive data, we recommend using a VPN and the library\u2019s \u201cSecure Research Workstation\u201d (reservation required). |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Peripheral Equipment\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| **Can I scan documents?** | Yes. All floors have high\u2011resolution scanners. Scan to PDF, JPG, or TXT. Files can be emailed directly to your Technion address or saved to a USB drive. |\n| **Is there a 3\u2011D printer?** | The Makerspace (2nd floor, Main Library) provides two Ultimaker 3 printers. Booking is required via the \u201cMakerspace Reservation\u201d system. Materials are free for academic projects; a small fee applies for commercial use. |\n| **Do you have audio\u2011visual equipment?** | Meeting rooms are equipped with projectors, HDMI/USB\u2011C connections, and speaker systems. Reserve a room through the catalogue and request any additional equipment (e.g., clickers) at least 24\u202fh in advance. |\n| **Can I borrow laptops or tablets?** | Yes. The \u201cMobile Computing Lab\u201d offers loaner laptops (Windows) and tablets (iPad) for up to 5\u202fdays. Register at the help desk; you must present your library card and sign a loan agreement. |\n| **Are there any restrictions on using peripheral devices?** | Personal USB\u2011drives are allowed only in the \u201cSecure Computing Lab.\u201d For other areas, use the network shares or cloud services (OneDrive for Business, Google Drive) supplied by Technion. |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Off\u2011Campus Access\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| **How do I access the catalogue and e\u2011resources from home?** | Log in to **[https://library.technion.ac.il]** with your Technion ID and password. The system automatically redirects you to the VPN gateway for licensed databases. |\n| **Do I need a special proxy or VPN?** | Yes. Use the **Technion VPN** (OpenVPN or Cisco AnyConnect). Instructions and configuration files are on the \u201cVPN & Remote Access\u201d page. |\n| **Can I renew items remotely?** | Absolutely. Renewals can be performed through the online \u201cMy Loans\u201d portal, the mobile app, or via email (reply with your item barcode and \u201crenew\u201d). |\n| **Is there a remote chat or email support?** | Live chat is available on the library website (Monday\u2011Thursday 09:00\u201118:00). You can also email helpdesk@technion.ac.il; we aim to reply within 24\u202fhours. |\n| **Can I request articles from off\u2011site journals?** | Yes. Use the \u201cDocument Delivery\u201d service (submit a request through the catalogue). Technion\u2019s subscription licenses allow us to deliver PDFs to your Technion e\u2011mail. |\n| **What if I lose internet connectivity while using a database?** | Save your search results locally (e.g., export citations to RIS or BibTeX). The library also offers a \u201cDownload\u2011All\u201d feature for most databases. |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Historical Archive\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| **What is the Historical Archive?** | The archive houses Technion\u2019s institutional memory: theses, faculty papers, photographs, engineering drawings, oral histories, and rare books dating back to 1912. |\n| **How can I access the archive?** | On\u2011site: Book an appointment at the Archive Reading Room (tuesday & thursday 09:00\u201117:00). Off\u2011site: Digitized collections are available through the \u201cDigital Heritage\u201d portal (requires Technion login)."}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I am a librarian at the Technion. The Technion is a public polytechnic university in Haifa, Israel. Please help me write a FAQ page for the Technion's library system. It should include questions and answers in the following categories:\n* General\n* Circulation\n* Computing & Network\n* Peripheral Equipment\n* Off-Campus Access\n* Historical Archive\n"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe ne