ce (2nd floor, Main Library) provides two Ultimaker 3 printers. Booking is required via the \u201cMakerspace Reservation\u201d system. Materials are free for academic projects; a small fee applies for commercial use. |\n| **Do you have audio\u2011visual equipment?** | Meeting rooms are equipped with projectors, HDMI/USB\u2011C connections, and speaker systems. Reserve a room through the catalogue and request any additional equipment (e.g., clickers) at least 24\u202fh in advance. |\n| **Can I borrow laptops or tablets?** | Yes. The \u201cMobile Computing Lab\u201d offers loaner laptops (Windows) and tablets (iPad) for up to 5\u202fdays. Register at the help desk; you must present your library card and sign a loan agreement. |\n| **Are there any restrictions on using peripheral devices?** | Personal USB\u2011drives are allowed only in the \u201cSecure Computing Lab.\u201d For other areas, use the network shares or cloud services (OneDrive for Business, Google Drive) supplied by Technion. |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Off\u2011Campus Access\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| **How do I access the catalogue and e\u2011resources from home?** | Log in to **[https://library.technion.ac.il]** with your Technion ID and password. The system automatically redirects you to the VPN gateway for licensed databases. |\n| **Do I need a special proxy or VPN?** | Yes. Use the **Technion VPN** (OpenVPN or Cisco AnyConnect). Instructions and configuration files are on the \u201cVPN & Remote Access\u201d page. |\n| **Can I renew items remotely?** | Absolutely. Renewals can be performed through the online \u201cMy Loans\u201d portal, the mobile app, or via email (reply with your item barcode and \u201crenew\u201d). |\n| **Is there a remote chat or email support?** | Live chat is available on the library website (Monday\u2011Thursday 09:00\u201118:00). You can also email helpdesk@technion.ac.il; we aim to reply within 24\u202fhours. |\n| **Can I request articles from off\u2011site journals?** | Yes. Use the \u201cDocument Delivery\u201d service (submit a request through the catalogue). Technion\u2019s subscription licenses allow us to deliver PDFs to your Technion e\u2011mail. |\n| **What if I lose internet connectivity while using a database?** | Save your search results locally (e.g., export citations to RIS or BibTeX). The library also offers a \u201cDownload\u2011All\u201d feature for most databases. |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Historical Archive\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| **What is the Historical Archive?** | The archive houses Technion\u2019s institutional memory: theses, faculty papers, photographs, engineering drawings, oral histories, and rare books dating back to 1912. |\n| **How can I access the archive?** | On\u2011site: Book an appointment at the Archive Reading Room (tuesday & thursday 09:00\u201117:00). Off\u2011site: Digitized collections are available through the \u201cDigital Heritage\u201d portal (requires Technion login)."}], "q_num": 405, "originalconversations": ["I am a librarian at the Technion. The Technion is a public polytechnic university in Haifa, Israel. Please help me write a FAQ page for the Technion's library system. It should include questions and answers in the following categories:\n* General\n* Circulation\n* Computing & Network\n* Peripheral Equipment\n* Off-Campus Access\n* Historical Archive\n", "#General\n\n###Who is eligible to receive service?\n\nTechnion libraries are open to the general public. Academic staff, administrative staff and students at the Technion may borrow books and peripheral equipment while presenting a valid student / employee card.\n\nTechnion Email enables access to the digital library without any special registration.\n\nLibrary card access (for tracking paper format books loans and requests) is only possible for those with Technion identification (email and password).\n\n###Is there accessibility for people with disabilities?\n\nThe Central Library is accessible to people with disabilities. For accessibility information in other Technion libraries contact the relevant library.\n\n#Circulation\n\n###Is it allowed to return a book borrowed from one library to another on campus?\n\nIt is recommended to return books to the library from which they were loaned.\n\n###Is it possible to self-extend the loan period on the card after receiving an email notification to return the item?\n\nYou can try to independently extend the loan on the card. If there are still requests for the item, it must be returned on the specified due date.\n\n###Can I borrow a dictionary for an exam?\n\nThe stock of dictionaries is limited, so it is therefore advisable to check in advance the collection containing all details of its availability.\n\n#Computing & Network\n\n###I cannot remember a password and username to a network connection. What should I do?\n\nThe library is not responsible for passwords for encrypted networks and you should contact the Faculty computing manager or the Division of Computing and Information Systems:\n\u201cEduroam\u201d Network \u2013 a worldwide secure network that allows easy connection to the Wi-Fi network in academic institutions around the world with a one-time setup only. [Link to Login Instructions]\n\n###Which software is currently installed on the libraries\u2019 computers?\n\nEach library has different software installed.\n\nThese software are currently installed on the Central Library\u2019s computers.\n\nTechnion students can also install certain software (such as Visual Studio, SolidWorks, Matlab and more) free of charge.\n\n#Peripheral Equipment\n\n###Can I borrow from the library a mobile charger / a laptop / headphones / calculator?\n\nContact the relevant library regarding borrowing any peripherals equipment.\n\n###Should I pay for printing, scanning and photography services?\n\nPrinting and photography services are per pay. The charges and the technical handling of printers are under the responsibility of [Charging System]. Contact information of [Charing Company] representatives can be found at the printers\u2019 area. Scanning services are provided free of charge (copiers which are also used for scanning are indicated at the printers\u2019 area).\n\n#Off-Campus Access\n\n###How do I connect from home to the library electronic resources?\n\nHome connection requires an identification in the Technion\u2019s unified system. All information about the various access options is available on the off-campus access page.\n\n###I cannot connect from home - what should I do?\n\nPlease open a ticket at the Technion HelpDesk (Hebrew only).\n\n###I do not remember my Technion account information. Who should I turn to?\n\nAccount information can be self-recovered. If necessary, contact the Technion HelpDesk.\n\n###I have a faculty / unit account, can I use this account to connect library resources off-campus?\n\nAccess is only available with these accounts: @[domain address 1] or @[domain address 2].\n\n###Can I connect from abroad as well?\n\nSure. Access is available from anywhere and with any network, and since it is a web-based system, is also accessible from any device (computer, tablet or smartphone with Internet access).\n\n#Historical Archive\n\n###What can be found in the Technion's historical archive?\n\nThe historical archive contains:\n* Documents, letters, protocols and photocopies of newspaper articles.\n* A partial heritage of Alexander Baerwald.\n* A collection of photos and historical photographs.\n* A collection of military programs by Professor Yohanan Ratner.\n* A partial collection of maps by Gottlieb Schumacher.\n\n###Is it necessary to book in advance a visit to the Historical Archive?\n\nYes, visit by appointment only. Please contact the department\u2019s email stating the search topic and the purpose of using the materials. A visit will be coordinated during the Historical Archive opening hours, [Open Time].\n\n###Is it possible to borrow the materials from the Historical Archive?\n\nNo, the materials are not borrowable, and are not taken out of the archive\u2019s climate control room while browsing.\n\n###Do I need to pay for the service?\n\nNo, the Historical Archive does not charge for visiting or for using the materials.\n\n###Where can I find campus statistics?\n\nFor campus statistics \u2013 please contact the Communication and External Relations Unit or the Construction and Maintenance Division."], "originalsource": "authors", "originalprompt": "I am a librarian at the Technion. The Technion is a public polytechnic university in Haifa, Israel. Please help me write a FAQ page for the Technion's library system. It should include questions and answers in the following categories:\n* General\n* Circulation\n* Computing & Network\n* Peripheral Equipment\n* Off-Campus Access\n* Historical Archive\n"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "How to manage urinary incontinence in children?"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks: \"How to manage urinary incontinence in children?\" It's medical advice. According to policy, providing medical advice is allowed if it's general informational content, not specific diagnosis or treatment for a particular patient. This is fine. Provide general advice, educational. Must not request personal data. Provide of education. Should not include disallowed content. Provide answer in <answer></answer> tags as required by system. Provide bullet points, steps, types of incontinence, interventions, behavioral, medical, possible referrals.\n\nWe need to follow the style. Provide thorough answer. Use appropriate tone. Provide references? Not required but can mention typical guidelines.\n\nWe must not give diagnosis or prescribe meds without encouraging doctor contact. Just general management. Provide reassurance.\n\nWill produce answer in XML tags.</think>\n<answer>\n**Managing Urinary Incontinence in Children \u2013 A General Guide**\n\nUrinary incontinence (UI) in children is common and usually improves with time and appropriate management. The approach combines education, behavioral strategies, lifestyle modifications, and, when needed, medical or specialist intervention. Below is a step\u2011by\u2011step framework that parents, caregivers, and health\u2011care providers can use.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Understand the Types of Incontinence\n| Type | Typical Age of Onset | Key Features |\n|------|----------------------|--------------|\n| **Daytime (urinary) continence delay** | 2\u20115 years | Infrequent voiding, \u201cholding back\u201d behavior, accidents during the day |\n| **Nocturnal enuresis (bedwetting)** | 5\u20117 years (often persists to 10\u201112) | Wetting during sleep; may be primary (never dry) or secondary (dry >6 months, then relapse) |\n| **Functional incontinence** | Any age | Accident occurs due to physical/psychological barriers (e.g., constipation, ADHD, poor bathroom access) |\n| **Stress incontinence** | Rare in young children; more common after puberty | Leakage with coughing, sneezing, or activity |\n| **Urge incontinence** | Usually older children/adolescents | Sudden, strong urge to void, often with overactive bladder |\n\n---\n\n### 2. Basic Assessment (Usually by a Pediatrician)\n1. **History**  \n   - Frequency, volume, timing, and pattern of accidents.  \n   - Fluid intake (type, amount, timing).  \n   - Bowel habits (constipation is a major contributor).  \n   - Day\u2011night differences.  \n   - Psychosocial stressors (school, bullying, family changes).  \n2. **Physical Examination**  \n   - Look for abdominal distension, bladder discomfort, signs of constipation, genital anomalies.  \n3. **Simple Tests (if indicated)**  \n   - Urinalysis (rule out infection).  \n   - Ultrasound (to evaluate kidneys/bladder).  \n   - Voiding diary (3\u20135 days) \u2013 records void times, volumes, accidents, fluid intake.\n\n---\n\n### 3. First\u2011Line Management \u2013 Behavioral & Lifestyle Strategies\n| Strategy | How to Implement | Rationale |\n|----------|-----------------|-----------|\n| **Timed Voiding** | Encourage the child to use the toilet every 2\u20133\u202fhours during the day (e.g., after meals, before bedtime). Use a timer or bathroom schedule. | Prevents bladder over\u2011distention and trains reflex pathways. |\n| **Double\u2011Voiding** | After the first void, wait 1\u20132\u202fminutes and try again. | Helps empty residual urine, especially useful for nighttime. |\n| **Adequate Fluid Intake** | \u00bd\u20131\u202fL of fluid daily (adjust for age, activity, climate). Limit caffeine and sugary drinks. | Keeps urine dilute and reduces bladder irritation. |\n| **Limit Evening Fluids** | Reduce drinks 1\u20132\u202fhours before bedtime (but maintain overall daily intake). | Decreases nighttime urine production. |\n| **Reward/Positive Reinforcement** | Sticker charts, small incentives for dry nights or successful daytime voids. Keep praise non\u2011punitive. | Enhances motivation; builds confidence. |\n| **Address Constipation** | High\u2011fiber diet, adequate water, regular toilet time after meals (especially after breakfast). May need stool softeners (e.g., polyethylene glycol) under doctor guidance. | A full rectum compresses the bladder, worsening UI. |\n| **Manage Stress/Anxiety** | Open communication, school liaison if bullying, relaxation techniques before bedtime. | Emotional factors can trigger secondary enuresis. |\n| **Ensure Easy Bathroom Access** | Unlocked doors, child\u2011friendly toilets, clear pathway, especially at school or during travel. | Removes functional barriers. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. When to Add Simple Medical Aids\n| Aid | Indication | How It Works |\n|-----|------------|--------------|\n| **Enuresis Alarm (Bedwetting Alarm)** | Persistent nighttime wetting after age 5\u20116, especially primary enuresis. | Detects moisture, wakes child, conditions brain\u2013bladder connection. |\n| **Desmopressin (DDAVP)** | Nighttime enuresis with high night\u2011time urine output, after behavioral measures failed (under pediatric supervision). | Reduces urine production at night. |\n| **Anticholinergic Medications (e.g., oxybutynin, tolterodine)** | Daytime urge incontinence or overactive bladder symptoms. | Relaxes bladder muscle, increases capacity. |\n| **\u03b1\u2011Blockers (e.g., tamsulosin)** | In adolescent males with functional obstruction (rare). | Relaxes urethral smooth muscle. |\n\n*Important:* All medications require prescription and monitoring for side effects (dry mouth, constipation, hyponatremia, etc.). Use only after physician evaluation.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Role of the Specialist\nRefer to a pediatric urologist or uro\u2011gynecologist if **any** of the following are present:\n\n- Persistent UI beyond 7\u20138\u202fyears despite comprehensive behavioral therapy.\n- Sudden onset of UI after a period of dryness (secondary incontinence).\n- Suspected anatomical abnormality (e.g., ectopic ureter, bladder exstrophy).\n- Signs of urinary tract infection, hematuria, or dysuria.\n- Significant psychosocial impact (low self\u2011esteem, school problems).\n- Inadequate response to first\u2011line medications.\n\nSpecialist interventions may include:\n\n- **Urodynamic studies** (to assess bladder capacity and function).  \n- **Biofeedback or pelvic floor training** (especially for urge incontinence).  \n- **Surgical correction** (for anatomical defects).  \n\n---\n\n### 6. Tips for Parents & Caregivers\n1. **Stay Positive:** Avoid shaming or punishment. UI is not \u201cnaughtiness.\u201d  \n2. **Maintain Normal Activities:** Encourage the child to continue sports, sleepovers, and social events; adapt with waterproof mattress covers and easy\u2011change clothing.  \n3. **Collaborate with School:** Provide an individualized health plan, allow bathroom breaks, and educate teachers on the child\u2019s needs.  \n4. **Track Progress:** Use a simple chart (dry night = \u2714, wet night = \u2716) to visualize improvements.  \n5. **Seek Support:** Parent support groups or counseling can help families cope with stress.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Quick \u201cChecklist\u201d for Parents\n- \u2610 Review fluid intake and limit caffeine/sugary drinks after dinner.  \n- \u2610 Establish a timed\u2011voiding schedule (every 2\u20133\u202fhours).  \n- \u2610 Encourage double\u2011voiding before bedtime.  \n- \u2610 Monitor bowel movements; treat constipation promptly.  \n- \u2610 Use a reward system for dry periods.  \n- \u2610 Consider an enuresis alarm if nighttime wetting persists.  \n- \u2610 Schedule a pediatric visit if accidents continue beyond 7\u202fyears or if any red\u2011flag symptoms appear.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Key Take\u2011away Messages\n- **Most children outgrow UI** with consistent, non\u2011punitive strategies.  \n- **Behavioral modification** (timed voiding, fluid management, constipation control) is the foundation of treatment.  \n- **Medical options** (alarms, desmopressin, anticholinergics) are adjuncts when basic measures are insufficient.  \n- **Professional evaluation** is essential if UI is severe, refractory, or associated with other urinary or systemic symptoms.\n\n**Bottom line:** Early, supportive, and systematic management can dramatically improve a child\u2019s continence, confidence, and quality of life. If you have concerns about your child\u2019s urinary habits, consult your pediatrician for a personalized plan.  \n\n---  \n\n*Disclaimer: This information is for educational purposes only and does not replace professional medical advice. Always seek evaluation from a qualified health\u2011care provider for individual concerns.*  \n</answer>"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How to manage urinary incontinence in children?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks: \"How to manage urinary incontinence in children?\" It's medical advice. According to policy, providing medical advice is allowed if it's general informational content, not specific diagnosis or treatment for a particular patient. This is fine. Provide general advice, educational. Must not request personal data. Provide of education. Should not include disallowed content. Provide answer in <answer></answer> tags as required by system. Provide bullet points, steps, types of incontinence, interventions, behavioral, medical, possible referrals.\n\nWe need to follow the style. Provide thorough answer. Use appropriate tone. Provide references? Not required but can mention typical guidelines.\n\nWe must not give diagnosis or prescribe meds without encouraging doctor contact. Just general management. Provide reassurance.\n\nWill produce answer in XML tags.</think>\n<answer>\n**Managing Urinary