**What it is:** A public statement that the patent holder will not assert its patents against anyone *who complies with certain conditions* (often \u201cuse the technology only in an open\u2011source manner\u201d).\n- **Typical language:**  \n  > \u201cWe pledge not to assert any of our patents against any person who uses, distributes, or modifies the software under an OSI\u2011approved open\u2011source licence.\u201d\n- **Examples:**  \n  - **Microsoft\u2019s Open Patent Promise (OPP)** \u2013 non\u2011assertion for patents covering implementations of certain standards when used under an OSI licence.  \n  - **Google\u2019s Patent Pledge** \u2013 similar promise for patents covering Android\u2011related technologies.  \n- **Copyleft flavour:** By requiring *adherence to an open\u2011source licence*, the pledge indirectly forces downstream users to keep the same openness, achieving a share\u2011alike effect.\n\n**Pros:** Simple, public, no contractual overhead.  \n**Cons:**  \n  - Only a *promise not to sue*; it does not give a licence to *make, use, sell* the invention outright.  \n  - Enforcement relies on the patentee\u2019s good faith; a change of corporate policy can nullify the pledge.  \n\n### 2.2. Defensive Patent Pools with Share\u2011Alike Licensing\n\n- **What it is:** A consortium (e.g., **Open Invention Network \u2013 OIN**) gathers patents from members and grants each member a **royalty\u2011free licence** to the pool **provided they agree not to assert patents against the Linux ecosystem**.\n- **Share\u2011alike clause:** Membership is contingent on signing a *Cross\u2011License Agreement* that requires members to \u201cagree not to assert any patents against any OIN\u2011covered implementation,\u201d effectively a copyleft condition at the *pool* level.\n- **Variations:**  \n  - **Sustaining Open\u2011Source Innovation (SOSI) Patent Pool** \u2013 proposes a \u201cshare\u2011alike\u201d licence where any downstream licencee must also grant similar pool