ense\u202f2.0** includes a patent licence, but without a share\u2011alike provision; some projects have added *\u201cApache\u2011style Patent Reciprocity\u201d* as a separate addendum.\n\n**Pros:** Direct contractual control over downstream licensing.  \n**Cons:** Requires each downstream user to **sign** or **accept** the licence; may be hard to enforce across jurisdictions where contract formation is contested.\n\n### 2.4. \u201cPatent\u2011Only\u201d Copyleft Licences (Emerging Concepts)\n\n- **UC\u2011Patent License (University of California)** \u2013 a draft licence that gives royalty\u2011free use of university patents, *provided* any derivative patents are also offered royalty\u2011free to the community.\n- **CC\u2011Patents** \u2013 a Creative\u2011Commons\u2010style licence for patents (still a draft) that mirrors the \u201cShareAlike\u201d element of CC\u2011BY\u2011SA.\n\nThese are **experimental** and have not seen broad adoption, but they demonstrate the feasibility of a pure\u2011patent copyleft framework.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Practical Steps to Implement Patent Copyleft\n\n1. **Identify the patents you control** (or that you have access to via a pool or pledge).  \n2. **Choose a licensing model**:\n   - *Non\u2011assertion pledge* for the simplest public commitment.  \n   - *Defensive pool* if you can gather many patents and attract members.  \n   - *Formal licence with share\u2011alike clause* for tighter control.\n3. **Draft the licence** (or pledge) with legal counsel experienced in IP and competition law. Key clauses:\n   - **Grant** \u2013 explicit, royalty\u2011free licence to the listed claims.  \n   - **Share\u2011Alike** \u2013 downstream recipients must receive the same grant.  \n   - **Scope** \u2013 define which improvements/derivatives are covered.  \n   - **Termination** \u2013 automatic termination on breach, to preserve enforceability.\n4. **Publish the licence** in a machine\u2011readable format (e.g., SPDX identifier) and link it to the relevant software or hardware documentation.\n5. **Encourage adoption** by:\n   - Adding the licence text to source repositories (e.g., `"}], "q_num": 378, "originalconversations": ["Is there a way to achieve protections similar to \"Copyleft\" under the patent system?", "\"Copyleft\" is a specific application of copyright law (restricting the license to use copyrighted material by requiring that those obtaining it must also redistribute it for free / under specified terms).\n\nAny work under \"copyleft\" is protected by copyright law (thanks to The Berne Convention copyright is \"automatic\" in most of the world), and those copyright protections are what allow the author to dictate the terms under which the copyrighted work is used.\n\nAs far as I'm aware there is no Berne Convention equivalent in patent law: in order for you to have patent protection and the ability to dictate how your invention is used (by licensing the patent for it to impose Copyleft-style requirements on those using it) you must file for (and receive) a patent.\n\nNote that the sort of \"keeping information free\" protection the Copyleft folks would seek is inherent in US Patent Law and most other patent systems -- it is known as Prior Art.\nIn its most simplistic definition (cribbed from Wikipedia):\n\n> Prior art \u2026, in most systems of patent law, constitutes all information that has been made available to the public in any form before a given date that might be relevant to a patent's claims of originality. If an invention has been described in the prior art, a patent on that invention is not valid.\n\nSo if you have designed and publicly released something patentable (and can document the date of such a release to the satisfaction of the patent authorities) you can seek to invalidate any subsequent patent claims on the grounds that prior art exists."], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "Is there a way to achieve protections similar to \"Copyleft\" under the patent system?"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "How to make an invoice on excel?"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to respond with instructions on making an invoice in Excel. Must wrap final answer in <answer></answer> tags. Provide steps, maybe template. Use clear guide. Should be helpful.</think>\n<answer>\n**Creating a Professional Invoice in Microsoft Excel \u2013 Step\u2011by\u2011Step Guide**\n\nBelow is a complete walk\u2011through (including layout ideas, formulas, and formatting tips) so you can build a reusable invoice template that you can fill out for each client.\n\n---\n\n## 1\ufe0f\u20e3 Set Up the Worksheet\n\n1. **Open a New Workbook**  \n   - `File \u2192 New \u2192 Blank workbook`.\n\n2. **Rename the Sheet** (optional)  \n   - Double\u2011click the sheet tab \u2192 type **Invoice**.\n\n3. **Define Print Area** (so the invoice prints on one page)  \n   - Select the cells you\u2019ll use (e.g., **A1:G30**).  \n   - `Page Layout \u2192 Print Area \u2192 Set Print Area`.\n\n---\n\n## 2\ufe0f\u20e3 Layout Overview\n\n| Cell Range | Content                              |\n|------------|--------------------------------------|\n| A1:B4      | Your company logo & contact details |\n| A6:B6      | **Invoice** title (big font)         |\n| A8:B8      | Invoice number & date                |\n| A10:B10    | Bill\u2011To (client name & address)      |\n| A14:G14    | Column headers for line items       |\n| A15:Gxx    | Item rows (description, qty, price, total) |\n| Axx:Gxx    | Sub\u2011total, tax, discounts, grand total |\n| Axx:Gxx    | Payment terms & notes                |\n| Axx:Gxx    | Footer (thank\u2011you message, etc.)    |\n\n---\n\n## 3\ufe0f\u20e3 Build the Header\n\n| Cell | Content | Formatting |\n|------|---------|------------|\n| **A1** | Insert your **logo**: `Insert \u2192 Pictures`. Resize to ~80\u202f\u00d7\u202f80\u202fpx. | Align left, top. |\n| **B1** | Company name | Bold, 18\u202fpt, your brand color. |\n| **B2** | Address line 1 | Regular 11\u202fpt. |\n| **B3** | City, State ZIP | Regular 11\u202fpt. |\n| **B4** | Phone \u2022 Email \u2022 Website | Italic, 10\u202fpt, gray. |\n| **A6** | **INVOICE** | Merge A6:G6, center, 24\u202fpt, dark blue, all caps. |\n| **A8** | **Invoice #:** | Bold. |\n| **B8** | `=TEXT(RIGHT(\"0000\"&ROW(),4),\"0000\")` *(or manually type, e.g., INV\u20112025\u2011001)* | |\n| **A9** | **Date:** | Bold. |\n| **B9** | `=TODAY()` | Format as *mm/dd/yyyy*. |\n| **A10** | **Bill To:** | Bold. |\n| **A11:C13** | Client name & address (merge as needed). | Regular 11\u202fpt. |\n\n---\n\n## 4\ufe0f\u20e3 Table of Line Items\n\n1. **Column Headers (Row\u202f14)**  \n\n   | Column | Header | Width (approx.) |\n   |--------|--------|-----------------|\n   | A | **Item #** | 8 |\n   | B | **Description** | 30 |\n   | C | **Qty** | 8 |\n   | D | **Unit Price** | 12 |\n   | E | **Discount %** | 12 |\n   | F | **Tax %** | 8 |\n   | G | **Line Total** | 15 |\n\n   *Apply a light fill (e.g., light gray), bold text, and center alignment.*\n\n2. **Sample Row (Row\u202f15) \u2013 formulas**\n\n   | Cell | Formula / Content | Explanation |\n   |------|-------------------|-------------|\n   | A15 | `=ROW()-14` | Auto\u2011number items. |\n   | B15 | *type description* | e.g., \u201cConsulting services \u2013 September\u201d. |\n   | C15 | *enter quantity* | numeric. |\n   | D15 | *enter unit price* | numeric, format as Currency. |\n   | E15 | *enter discount %* | optional, default 0. |\n   | F15 | *enter tax %* | e.g., 8.25% (enter `0.0825`). |\n   | G15 | `=C15*D15*(1-E15)*(1+F15)` | Calculates line total with discount and tax. |\n\n3. **Copy Row 15 down** as many rows as you need (e.g., to row\u202f30).  \n   - Use **Ctrl+D** or drag the fill handle.  \n   - Ensure columns C\u2011F stay as numbers (no hidden text).  \n\n4. **Table Formatting**  \n   - Select the range (A14:G30).  \n   - `Home \u2192 Format as Table` \u2192 choose a style (makes alternating row colors).  \n   - Table name (e.g., **tblInvoice**) will help with formulas later.\n\n---\n\n## 5\ufe0f\u20e3 Sub\u2011Total, Tax, Discount, Grand Total\n\nAssuming the last data row is **row\u202f30** (adjust to your actual last row).\n\n| Cell | Formula / Content | Description |\n|------|-------------------|-------------|\n| **F31** | **Subtotal:** | Bold, right\u2011aligned (merge F31:G31). |\n| **G31** | `=SUM(tblInvoice[Line Total])` | Sum of all line totals. |\n| **F32** | **Tax Total:** | |\n| **G32** | `=SUMPRODUCT(tblInvoice[Qty]*tblInvoice[Unit Price]*tblInvoice[Tax %])` | Calculates tax only (ignores discount). |\n| **F33** | **Discount Total:** | |\n| **G33** | `=SUMPRODUCT(tblInvoice[Qty]*tblInvoice[Unit Price]*tblInvoice[Discount %])` | Sum of discounts (positive number). |\n| **F34** | **Grand Total:** | Larger font (e.g., 16\u202fpt), bold, fill light green. |\n| **G34** | `=G31+G32-G33` | Total amount due. |\n\n*If you already included tax & discount in the line total (as the formula above does), you can simply use:*  \n`Grand Total = G31` and omit separate tax/discount rows.\n\n---\n\n## 6\ufe0f\u20e3 Payment Terms & Notes\n\n| Cell | Content | Formatting |\n|------|---------|------------|\n| A36:G38 (merged) | **Payment Terms:** Net 30 days. Please remit payment to **Bank ABC**, Account #: 123\u2011456\u2011789. | Italic, 10\u202fpt. |\n| A40:G42 (merged) | **Notes:** Thank you for your business! If you have any questions, contact us at (555) 123\u20114567. | Regular 10\u202fpt, gray. |\n\n---\n\n## 7\ufe0f\u20e3 Footer (Optional)\n\n- Insert a small logo or a \u201cThank you!\u201d line at the very bottom.  \n- Use `Page Layout \u2192 Margins \u2192 Custom Margins` \u2192 set bottom margin to ~0.5\u202fin.\n\n---\n\n## 8\ufe0f\u20e3 Make the Template Re\u2011usable\n\n1. **Protect the Sheet** (so users only edit the input cells).  \n   - Highlight the cells **users should edit** (e.g., client info, item rows).  \n   - `Home \u2192 Format \u2192 Lock Cell` \u2192 *uncheck* \u201cLocked\u201d.  \n   - Then `Review \u2192 Protect Sheet`, set a password (optional).  \n\n2. **Create a Print\u2011Ready View**  \n   - `File \u2192 Print \u2192 Scaling \u2192 Fit Sheet on One Page`.  \n   - Check the print preview; adjust column widths if needed.\n\n3. **Save As a Template**  \n   - `File \u2192 Save As \u2192 Browse \u2192 Save as type: Excel Template (*.xltx)`.  \n   - Name it **Invoice_Template**.  \n\nNow each time you need a new invoice, just open the template, fill in the client details, add line items, and print or email the PDF.\n\n---\n\n## 9\ufe0f\u20e3 Quick\u2011Copy to PDF for Emailing\n\n1. After filling the invoice, go to `File \u2192 Export \u2192 Create PDF/XPS Document`.  \n2. Choose a file name like `Invoice_2025-001.pdf`.  \n3. Attach the PDF to your email.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udccc Handy Tips & Common Pitfalls\n\n| Tip | Why It Helps |\n|-----|--------------|\n| **Use named ranges** for key cells (`InvoiceNumber`, `InvoiceDate`) \u2013 easier to reference in other worksheets. | Improves readability of formulas. |\n| **Round tax calculations** (`ROUND(...,2)`) to avoid extra cents from floating\u2011point math. | Prevents mismatched totals. |\n| **Add a \u201cPaid/Unpaid\u201d drop\u2011down** (`Data \u2192 Data Validation`) to track status. | Simple project\u2011wide tracking. |\n| **Conditional formatting** on `Grand Total` (e.g., red if > $10,000) for quick visual alerts. | Highlights high\u2011value invoices. |\n| **Keep a master \u201cInvoice Log\u201d sheet** that pulls `Invoice #`, `Date`, `Client`, `Amount`, `Status` using formulas (`=IFERROR(...)`). | Centralized record\u2011keeping. |\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udf89 You\u2019re Done!\n\nYou now have a fully functional, professional\u2011looking invoice template built entirely in Excel. Feel free to customize colors, fonts"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How to make an invoice on excel?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to respond with instructions on making an invoice in Excel. Must wrap final answer in <answer></answer> tags. Provide steps, maybe template. Use clear guide. Should be helpful.</think>\n<answer>\n**Creating a Professional Invoice in Microsoft Excel \u2013 Step\u2011by\u2011Step Guide**\n\nBelow is a complete walk\u2011through (including layout ideas, formulas, and formatting tips) so you can build a reusable invoice template that you can fill out for each client.\n\n---\n\n## 1\ufe0f\u20e3 Set Up the Worksheet\n\n1. **Open a New Workbook**  \n   - `File \u2192 New \u2192 Blank workbook`.\n\n2. **Rename the Sheet** (optional)  \n   - Double\u2011click the sheet tab \u2192 type **Invoice**.\n\n3. **Define Print Area** (so the invoice prints on one page)  \n   - Select the cells you\u2019ll use (e.g., **A1:G30**).  \n   - `Page Layout \u2192 Print Area \u2192 Set Print Area`.\n\n---\n\n## 2\ufe0f\u20e3 Layout Overview\n\n| Cell Range | Content                              |\n|------------|--------------------------------------|\n| A1:B4      | Your company logo & contact details |\n| A6:B6      | **Invoice** title (big font)         |\n| A8:B8      | Invoice number & date                |\n| A10:B10    | Bill\u2011To (client name & address)      |\n| A14:G14    | Column headers for line items       |\n| A15:Gxx    | Item rows (description, qty, price, total) |\n| Axx:Gxx    | Sub\u2011total, tax, discounts, grand total |\n| Axx:Gxx    | Payment terms & notes                |\n| Axx:Gxx    | Footer (thank\u2011you message, etc.)    |\n\n---\n\n## 3\ufe0f\u20e3 Build the Header\n\n| Cell | Content | Formatting |\n|------|---------|------------|\n| **A1** | Insert your **logo**: `Insert \u2192 Pictures`. Resize to ~80\u202f\u00d7\u202f80\u202fpx. | Align left, top. |\n| **B1** | Company name | Bold, 18\u202fpt, your brand color. |\n| **B2** | Address line 1 | Regular 11\u202fpt. |\n| **B3** | City, State ZIP | Regular 11\u202fpt. |\n| **B4** | Phone \u2022 Email \u2022 Website | Italic, 10\u202fpt, gray. |\n| **A6** | **INVOICE** | Merge A6:G6, center, 24\u202fpt, dark blue, all caps. |\n| **A8** | **Invoice #:** | Bold. |\n| **B8** | `=TEXT(RIGHT(\"0000\"&ROW(),4),\"0000\")` *(or manually type, e.g., INV\u20112025\u2011001)* | |\n| **A9** | **Date:** | Bold. |\n| **B9** | `=TODAY()` | Format as *mm/dd/yyyy*. |\n| **A10** | **Bill To:** | Bold. |\n| **A11:C13** | Client name & address (merge as needed). | Regular 11\u202fpt. |\n\n---\n\n## 4\ufe0f\u20e3 Table of Line Items\n\n1. **Column Headers (Row\u202f14)**  \n\n   | Column | Header | Width (approx.) |\n   |--------|--------|-----------------|\n   | A | **Item #** | 8 |\n   | B | **Description** | 30 |\n   | C | **Qty** | 8 |\n   | D | **Unit Price** | 12 |\n   | E | **Discount %** | 12 |\n   | F | **Tax %** | 8 |\n   | G | **Line Total** | 15 |\n\n   *Apply a light fill (e.g., light gray), bold text, and center alignment.*\n\n2. **Sample Row (Row\u202f15) \u2013 formulas**\n\n   | Cell | Formula / Content | Explanation |\n   |------|-------------------|-------------|\n   | A15 | `=ROW()-14` | Auto\u2011number items. |\n   | B15 | *type description* | e.g., \u201cConsulting services \u2013 September\u201d. |\n   | C15 | *enter quantity* | numeric. |\n   | D15 | *enter unit price* | numeric, format as Currency. |\n   | E15 | *enter discount %* | optional, default 0. |\n   | F15 | *enter tax %* | e.g., 8.25% (enter `0.0825`). |\n   | G15 | `=C15*D15*(1-E15)*(1+F15)` | Calculates line total with discount and tax. |\n\n3. **Copy Row 15 down** as many rows as you need (e.g., to row\u202f30).  \n   - Use **Ctrl+D** or drag the fill handle.  \n   - Ensure columns C\u2011F stay as numbers (no hidden text).  \n\n4. **Table Formatting**  \n   - Select the range (A14:G30).  \n   - `Home \u2192 Format as Table` \u2192 choose a style (makes alternating row colors).  \n   - Table name (e.g., **tblInvoice**) will help with formulas later.\n\n---\n\n## 5\ufe0f\u20e3 Sub\u2011Total, Tax, Discount, Grand Total\n\nAssuming the last data row is **row\u202f30** (adjust to your actual last row).\n\n| Cell | Formula / Content | Description |\n|------|-------------------|-------------|\n| **F31** | **Subtotal:** | Bold, right\u2011aligned (merge F31:G31). |\n| **G31** | `=SUM(tblInvoice[Line Total])` | Sum of all line totals. |\n| **F32** | **Tax Total:** | |\n| **G32** | `=SUMPRODUCT(tblInvoice[Qty]*tblInvoice[Unit Price]*tblInvoice[Tax %])` | Calculates tax only (ignores discount). |\n| **F33** | **Discount Total:** | |\n| **G33** | `=SUMPRODUCT(tblInvoice[Qty]*tblInvoice[Unit Price]*tblInvoice[Discount %])` | Sum of discounts (positive number). |\n| **F34** | **Grand Total:** | Larger font (e.g., 16\u202fpt), bold, fill light green. |\n| **G34** | `=G31+G32-G33` | Total amount due. |\n\n*If you already included tax & discount in the line total (as the formula above does), you can simply use:*  \n`Grand Total = G31` and omit separate tax/discount rows.\n\n---\n\n## 6\ufe0f\u20e3 Payment Terms & Notes\n\n| Cell | Content | Formatting |\n|------|---------|------------|\n| A36:G38 (merged) | **Payment Terms:** Net 30 days. Please remit payment to **Bank ABC**, Account #: 123\u2011456\u2011789. | Italic, 10\u202fpt. |\n| A40:G42 (merged) | **Notes:** Thank you for your business! If you have any questions, contact us at (555) 123\u20114567. | Regular 10\u202fpt, gray. |\n\n---\n\n## 7\ufe0f\u20e3 Footer (Optional)\n\n- Insert a small logo or a \u201cThank you!\u201d line at the very bottom.  \n- Use `Page Layout \u2192 Margins \u2192 Custom Margins` \u2192 set bottom margin to ~0.5\u202fin.\n\n---\n\n## 8\ufe0f\u20e3 Make the Template Re\u2011usable\n\n1. **Protect the Sheet** (so users only edit the input cells).  \n   - Highlight the cells **users should edit** (e.g., client info, item rows).  \n   - `Home \u2192 Format \u2192 Lock Cell` \u2192 *uncheck* \u201cLocked\u201d.  \n   - Then `Review \u2192 Protect Sheet`, set a password (optional).  \n\n2. **Create a Print\u2011Ready View**  \n   - `File \u2192 Print \u2192 Scaling \u2192 Fit Sheet on One Page`.  \n   - Check the print preview; adjust column widths if needed.\n\n3. **Save As a Template**  \n   - `File \u2192 Save As \u2192 Browse \u2192 Save as type: Excel Template (*.xltx)`.  \n   - Name it **Invoice_Template**.  \n\nNow each time you need a new invoice, just open the template, fill in the client details, add line items, and print or email the PDF.\n\n---\n\n## 9\ufe0f\u20e3 Quick\u2011Copy to PDF for Emailing\n\n1. After filling the invoice, go to `File \u2192 Export \u2192 Create PDF/XPS Document`.  \n2. Choose a file name like `Invoice_2025-001.pdf`.  \n3. Attach the PDF to your email.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udccc Handy Tips & Common Pitfalls\n\n| Tip | Why It Helps |\n|-----|--------------|\n| **Use named ranges** for key cells (`InvoiceNumber`, `InvoiceDate`) \u2013 easier to reference in other worksheets. | Improves readability of formulas. |\n| **Round tax calculations** (`ROUND(...,2)`) to avoid extra cents from floating\u2011point math. | Prevents mismatched totals. |\n| **Add a \u201cPaid/Unpaid\u201d drop\u2011down** (`Data \u2192 Data Validation`) to track status. | Simple project\u2011wide tracking. |\n| **Conditional formatting** on `Grand Total` (e.g., red if > $10,000) for quick visual alerts. | Highlights high\u2011value invoices. |\n| **Keep a master \u201cInvoice Log\u201d sheet** that pulls `Invoice #`, `Date`, `Client`, `Amount`, `Status` using formulas (`=IFERROR(...)`). | Centralized record\u2011keeping. |\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udf89 You\u2019re Done!\n\nYou now have a fully functional, professional\u2011looking invoice template built entirely in Excel. Feel free to customize colors, fonts"}], "q_num": 643, "originalconversations": ["How to make an invoice on excel?", "## Use a template on windows\n\n1. Open Microsoft Excel. It's a green icon with a white \"X\" on it. The Microsoft Excel home page will open.\n2. Search for an invoice template. Type invoice into the search bar at the top of the page, then press \u21b5 Enter to search for invoice templates. You must be connected to the Internet in order to search for templates.\n3. Select a template. Click a template that you want to use to open it in a window.\n4. Click Create. This button is to the right of the template's preview. Doing so will open the template in Microsoft Excel.\n5. Edit the template to fit your needs. For example, most templates have \"Company\" written across the top; you would replace this heading with your company's name. To edit text on the Excel document, double-click the text item, then remove the text item or replace it with your own.\n6. Fill out the invoice. Enter any information required by your invoice template to ensure that the final total matches what you're owed. For example, some invoice templates will require you to enter an hourly rate or a fixed charge. Most invoice templates use formulas to combine your entered hourly and the number of hours worked in the \"Final Total\" box.\n7. Save your invoice. Click File in the upper-left side of the page, click Save As, double-click a save location, enter your invoice's name, and click Save. This will save your customized invoice in your selected save location. Your invoice is now ready to be sent.\n\n\n## Use a template on mac\n\n1. Open Microsoft Excel. It's a green box with a white \"X\" on it. Excel will open.\n2. Click File. This menu item is in the top-left side of the page. A drop-down menu will appear below it.\n3. Click New from Template. It's an option in the File drop-down menu. Doing so opens a new page with template options.\n4. Search for an invoice template. Type invoice into the search bar in the top-right corner of the page, then press \u23ce Return. You must be connected to the Internet in order to search for templates.\n5. Select a template. Click a template to open a preview window with the template displayed.\n6. Click Open. It's in the preview window. This will open the invoice template as a new document.\n7. Edit the template to fit your needs. For example, most templates have \"Company\" written across the top; you would replace this heading with your company's name. To edit text on the Excel document, double-click the text item, then remove the text item or replace it with your own.\n8. Fill out the invoice. Enter any information required by your invoice template to ensure that the final total matches what you're owed. For example, some invoice templates will require you to enter an hourly rate or a fixed charge. Most invoice templates use formulas to combine your entered hourly and the number of hours worked in the \"Final Total\" box.\n9. Save your invoice. Click the File menu item, click Save As, enter a name for your invoice, and click Save. Your invoice is now ready to be sent.\n\n\n## Create an invoice manually\n\n1. Open Microsoft Excel. It's a green box with a white \"X\" on it. The Microsoft Excel home page will open.\n2. Click Blank Workbook. This option is in the upper-left side of the Excel home page. A blank spreadsheet will open. On Mac, skip this step if Excel opens to a blank document.\n3. Create the invoice heading. Your heading should include the following information:\n\t* \n\t* Company name - The name of the company to whom the invoice funds will be allocated. Descriptor - The word \"Invoice\" or a description of the type of invoice it is, such as \"Price Quote\" if you're quoting a price for your services to a client instead of billing for them. Date - The date on which you're writing the invoice. Number - The invoice number. You can either use a global numbering system for all your clients or individual numbering for each client. If you choose to number for each client, you can include the client name or a form of it in the invoice number, such as \"Westwood1.\"\n4. Enter the sender and recipient addresses. This information should appear near the top of the invoice, with your information above the client's. Your contact information should include your name, your company's address, a phone number, and an email address. Your client's information should include the company name, the name of the accounts payable person, and the client address. You can also include the client's phone and email address.\n5. Enter your billing information. You can devote a column to a short description of the product or service, a column to quantity, a column to unit price or rate, and a calculated column for the total price for the quantity purchased of that item.\n6. Display the amount of the total bill. This should appear below the calculated column of individual charges and can be generated with Excel's SUM function. For example: if you have $13 of work in cell B3 and $27 of work in B4, you might put the formula =SUM(B3,B4) in cell B5 to display $40 in that cell. If you used an hourly rate (e.g., $30) in cell B3 and a number of hours (e.g., 3) in B4, you would instead write =SUM(B3\\*B4) in cell B5.\n7. Include the terms of payment. This can appear either above or below the billing information. Common payment terms are \"Due on receipt,\" \"Due within 14 days,\" \"Due within 30 days,\" or \"Due within 60 days.\" You may also want to include a memo at the bottom of the invoice covering accepted methods of payment, general information, or a thank-you to your customer for shopping with you.\n8. Save your invoice. Use a name that will distinguish the invoice from other invoices you've sent to your client if necessary. To save your invoice:\n\t* \n\t* Windows - Click File in the upper-left side of the page, click Save As, double-click a save location, enter your invoice's name, and click Save. Mac - Click the File menu item, click Save As, enter a name for your invoice, and click Save.\n"], "originalsource": "wikihow", "originalprompt": "How to make an invoice on excel?"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "How to study using a game method?"}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks: \"How to study using a game method?\" They want guidance on gamified studying. Need to respond with helpful advice, steps, techniques. Provide in <answer> tags. Should be concise but thorough. Use bullet points. Ensure the instructions are clear. Provide examples of game elements: points, levels, challenges, competition, narrative. Also how to implement with different subjects. Also tools and apps. Provide a sample plan. Probably answer accordingly.</think>\n<answer>\n**Studying with a \u201cgame method\u201d (gamified learning) turns your study sessions into a fun, goal\u2011driven experience.** Below is a step\u2011by\u2011step framework you can adapt to any subject, plus concrete ideas, tools, and a sample weekly plan.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Define the Game Structure\n| Game Element | What It Means for Studying | Quick Setup |\n|--------------|---------------------------|------------|\n| **Goal / Quest** | Clear learning objective (e.g., \u201cMaster the 10 most common French verb tenses\u201d) | Write a concise quest statement. |\n| **Levels** | Break the material into progressive chunks (easy \u2192 medium \u2192 hard). | 5\u201110 cards, each a \u201clevel\u201d. |\n| **Points / XP** | Reward each completed task (e.g., 10\u202fXP for a flash\u2011card set, 30\u202fXP for a practice test). | Use a spreadsheet or a gamified app. |\n| **Badges / Achievements** | Milestones that unlock special rewards (e.g., \u201cSpeed Reader\u201d badge for reading 500 words in <10\u202fmin). | Design 3\u20115 badges per subject. |\n| **Timer / Countdown** | Adds urgency (Pomodoro, \u201cbeat the clock\u201d). | Set 25\u2011minute intervals. |\n| **Leaderboards** | Compare progress with friends or classmates (optional). | Google Sheet or app leaderboard. |\n| **Narrative / Theme** | Story that ties everything together (e.g., you\u2019re a detective solving \u201cknowledge crimes\u201d). | Draft a 2\u2011sentence story intro. |\n| **Power\u2011Ups** | Temporary boosts (e.g., \u201cDouble XP\u201d for a weekend study marathon). | Schedule a \u201cPower\u2011Up Day\u201d each week. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Choose Your Tools\n| Tool Type | Recommended Options | How to Use |\n|----------|---------------------|-----------|\n| **Flash\u2011card & XP tracker** | Anki (with add\u2011ons), Quizlet, TinyCards, or a simple Excel sheet. | Assign XP values to each deck; auto\u2011track streaks. |\n| **Task & Quest manager** | Notion, Trello, ClickUp (custom \u201ccards\u201d as quests). | Create a board: \u201cTo\u2011Do \u2192 In\u2011Progress \u2192 Completed\u201d. |\n| **Timer & Pomodoro** | Forest, Focus Keeper, Toggl. | Use a 25/5\u2011minute cycle; reward yourself after each Pomodoro. |\n| **Leaderboard** | Google Sheets (shared), Kahoot! (team mode), Classcraft (if in class). | Update scores after each study session. |\n| **Badge creator** | Canva (design), Google Slides (simple). | Design visual badges; print or keep digital. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Build Your First \u201cStudy Game\u201d\n\n### Step\u2011by\u2011Step Example (College Biology)\n\n1. **Quest Statement**  \n   *\u201cCollect all 8 \u201cCell Organelle\u201d artifacts to become a Master Cell Explorer.\u201d*\n\n2. **Levels** (each a set of 5\u20137 topics)  \n   - Level\u202f1: Cell membrane & transport  \n   - Level\u202f2: Nucleus & DNA  \n   - \u2026  \n   - Level\u202f8: Cell signaling pathways  \n\n3. **XP System**  \n   - 10\u202fXP per flash\u2011card set (5 cards)  \n   - 30\u202fXP for a 10\u2011question quiz  \n   - 50\u202fXP for a 30\u2011minute \u201cteach\u2011back\u201d video  \n\n4. **Badges**  \n   - **\u201cSpeed Scholar\u201d** \u2013 finish a level in under 45\u202fmin.  \n   - **\u201cQuiz Master\u201d** \u2013 score \u226590\u202f% on three consecutive quizzes.  \n\n5. **Timer**  \n   - Use a 25\u2011minute Pomodoro for each flash\u2011card set.  \n\n6. **Power\u2011Up Day** (Saturday)  \n   - Double XP for every activity completed.  \n\n7. **Leaderboard** (optional)  \n   - Share scores with two study partners; weekly champion gets a coffee treat.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Daily / Weekly Workflow (General Template)\n\n| Time Block | Activity | Game Mechanics |\n|-----------|----------|----------------|\n| **Morning (15\u202fmin)** | Review yesterday\u2019s notes \u2192 5\u2011question quick quiz | +10\u202fXP each correct answer |\n| **Mid\u2011day (30\u202fmin)** | New content via video or reading | Earn \u201cKnowledge Chunk\u201d badge after 2 chunks |\n| **Afternoon (2\u00d725\u202fmin Pomodoros)** | Practice problems/flash\u2011cards | +10\u202fXP per Pomodoro, streak bonus after 4 in a row |\n| **Evening (10\u202fmin)** | Self\u2011assessment: rate confidence 1\u20115 | Unlock \u201cReflection\u201d power\u2011up if average \u22654 |\n| **Weekly (1\u202fh)** | Create a mini\u2011presentation or teach a friend | +50\u202fXP + \u201cTeacher\u201d badge |\n| **Weekend (optional)** | \u201cBoss Battle\u201d \u2013 comprehensive mock test | Double XP, bonus badge for >80\u202f% score |\n\n*Adjust durations to fit your schedule; the key is consistency and clear rewards.*\n\n---\n\n## 5. Tips for Sustainable Gamified Studying\n\n1. **Start Small** \u2013 Begin with one subject and a simple XP system; add layers later.  \n2. **Keep Scoring Simple** \u2013 Too many point categories become confusing.  \n3. **Balance Competition & Collaboration** \u2013 Friendly rivalry works, but cooperation (team quests) can boost motivation.  \n4. **Tie Rewards to Real Benefits** \u2013 Use earned XP to \u201cpurchase\u201d real treats (e.g., a 30\u2011minute break, a favorite snack, or a short episode of a show).  \n5. **Reflect Weekly** \u2013 Review what levels took too long, adjust difficulty, and celebrate achievements.  \n6. **Avoid Over\u2011Gamification** \u2013 If points feel forced, pause and simplify. The goal is to make learning enjoyable, not a pressure\u2011filled scoreboard.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Sample 2\u2011Week Study Game Plan (for Any Subject)\n\n| Day | Quest | XP Goal | Reward |\n|-----|-------|---------|--------|\n| Mon | Finish Level\u202f1 (read + flash\u2011cards) | 40\u202fXP | 15\u2011min coffee break |\n| Tue | Quiz Level\u202f1, create one\u2011sentence summary | 30\u202fXP | 10\u2011min YouTube video |\n| Wed | Power\u2011Up: Double XP on flash\u2011cards | 60\u202fXP | Small snack |\n| Thu | Teach a friend (5\u202fmin) | 50\u202fXP | Choose dinner menu |\n| Fri | Level\u202f2 content + practice problems | 45\u202fXP | 30\u2011min gaming session |\n| Sat | \u201cBoss Battle\u201d \u2013 cumulative quiz (20\u202fq) | 100\u202fXP (double) | Movie night |\n| Sun | Rest & Reflect \u2013 note difficulty areas | 10\u202fXP | No penalty \u2013 recovery day |\n| **Repeat** for Week\u202f2, increasing difficulty or adding a new badge. |\n\n---\n\n### Quick Starter Checklist\n- [ ] Write a clear **quest** for the week.  \n- [ ] Split material into **3\u20135 levels**.  \n- [ ] Assign **XP values** to tasks.  \n- [ ] Design **2\u20133 badges**.  \n- [ ] Choose a **timer app** and a **tracking sheet**.  \n- [ ] Set a **reward** for reaching the weekly XP target.  \n\nNow you have a ready\u2011to\u2011play study game! Adjust the theme, points, and rewards to match your personality, and watch motivation level up. Happy gaming\u2011learning! \n\n</answer>"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How to study using a game method?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user asks: \"How to study using a game method?\" They want guidance on gamified studying. Need to respond with helpful advice, steps, techniques. Provide in <answer> tags. Should be concise but thorough. Use bullet points. Ensure the instructions are clear. Provide examples of game elements: points, levels, challenges, competition, narrative. Also how to implement with different subjects. Also tools and apps. Provide a sample plan. Probably answer accordingly.</think>\n<answer>\n**Studying with a \u201cgame method\u201d (gamified learning) turns your study sessions into a fun, goal\u2011driven experience.** Below is a step\u2011by\u2011step framework you can adapt to any subject, plus concrete ideas, tools, and a sample weekly plan.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Define the Game Structure\n| Game Element | What It Means for Studying | Quick Setup |\n|--------------|---------------------------|------------|\n| **Goal / Quest** | Clear learning objective (e.g., \u201cMaster the 10 most common French verb tenses\u201d) | Write a concise quest statement. |\n| **Levels** | Break the material into progressive chunks (easy \u2192 medium \u2192 hard). | 5\u201110 cards, each a \u201clevel\u201d. |\n| **Points / XP** | Reward each completed task (e.g., 10\u202fXP for a flash\u2011card set, 30\u202fXP for a practice test). | Use a spreadsheet or a gamified app. |\n| **Badges / Achievements** | Milestones that unlock special rewards (e.g., \u201cSpeed Reader\u201d badge for reading 500 words in <10\u202fmin). | Design 3\u20115 badges per subject. |\n| **Timer / Countdown** | Adds urgency (Pomodoro, \u201cbeat the clock\u201d). | Set 25\u2011minute intervals. |\n| **Leaderboards** | Compare progress with friends or classmates (optional). | Google Sheet or app leaderboard. |\n| **Narrative / Theme** | Story that ties everything together (e.g., you\u2019re a detective solving \u201cknowledge crimes\u201d). | Draft a 2\u2011sentence story intro. |\n| **Power\u2011Ups** | Temporary boosts (e.g., \u201cDouble XP\u201d for a weekend study marathon). | Schedule a \u201cPower\u2011Up Day\u201d each week. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Choose Your Tools\n| Tool Type | Recommended Options | How to Use |\n|----------|---------------------|-----------|\n| **Flash\u2011card & XP tracker** | Anki (with add\u2011ons), Quizlet, TinyCards, or a simple Excel sheet. | Assign XP values to each deck; auto\u2011track streaks. |\n| **Task & Quest manager** | Notion, Trello, ClickUp (custom \u201ccards\u201d as quests). | Create a board: \u201cTo\u2011Do \u2192 In\u2011Progress \u2192 Completed\u201d. |\n| **Timer & Pomodoro** | Forest, Focus Keeper, Toggl. | Use a 25/5\u2011minute cycle; reward yourself after each Pomodoro. |\n| **Leaderboard** | Google Sheets (shared), Kahoot! (team mode), Classcraft (if in class). | Update scores after each study session. |\n| **Badge creator** | Canva (design), Google Slides (simple). | Design visual badges; print or keep digital. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Build Your First \u201cStudy Game\u201d\n\n### Step\u2011by\u2011Step Example (College Biology)\n\n1. **Quest Statement**  \n   *\u201cCollect all 8 \u201cCell Organelle\u201d artifacts to become a Master Cell Explorer.\u201d*\n\n2. **Levels** (each a set of 5\u20137 topics)  \n   - Level\u202f1: Cell membrane & transport  \n   - Level\u202f2: Nucleus & DNA  \n   - \u2026  \n   - Level\u202f8: Cell signaling pathways  \n\n3. **XP System**  \n   - 10\u202fXP per flash\u2011card set (5 cards)  \n   - 30\u202fXP for a 10\u2011question quiz  \n   - 50\u202fXP for a 30\u2011minute \u201cteach\u2011back\u201d video  \n\n4. **Badges**  \n   - **\u201cSpeed Scholar\u201d** \u2013 finish a level in under 45\u202fmin.  \n   - **\u201cQuiz Master\u201d** \u2013 score \u226590\u202f% on three consecutive quizzes.  \n\n5. **Timer**  \n   - Use a 25\u2011minute Pomodoro for each flash\u2011card set.  \n\n6. **Power\u2011Up Day** (Saturday)  \n   - Double XP for every activity completed.  \n\n7. **Leaderboard** (optional)  \n   - Share scores with two study partners; weekly champion gets a coffee treat.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Daily / Weekly Workflow (General Template)\n\n| Time Block | Activity | Game Mechanics |\n|-----------|----------|----------------|\n| **Morning (15\u202fmin)** | Review yesterday\u2019s notes \u2192 5\u2011question quick quiz | +10\u202fXP each correct answer |\n| **Mid\u2011day (30\u202fmin)** | New content via video or reading | Earn \u201cKnowledge Chunk\u201d badge after 2 chunks |\n| **Afternoon (2\u00d725\u202fmin Pomodoros)** | Practice problems/flash\u2011cards | +10\u202fXP per Pomodoro, streak bonus after 4 in a row |\n| **Evening (10\u202fmin)** | Self\u2011assessment: rate confidence 1\u20115 | Unlock \u201cReflection\u201d power\u2011up if average \u22654 |\n| **Weekly (1\u202fh)** | Create a mini\u2011presentation or teach a friend | +50\u202fXP + \u201cTeacher\u201d badge |\n| **Weekend (optional)** | \u201cBoss Battle\u201d \u2013 comprehensive mock test | Double XP, bonus badge for >80\u202f% score |\n\n*Adjust durations to fit your schedule; the key is consistency and clear rewards.*\n\n---\n\n## 5. Tips for Sustainable Gamified Studying\n\n1. **Start Small** \u2013 Begin with one subject and a simple XP system; add layers later.  \n2. **Keep Scoring Simple** \u2013 Too many point categories become confusing.  \n3. **Balance Competition & Collaboration** \u2013 Friendly rivalry works, but cooperation (team quests) can boost motivation.  \n4. **Tie Rewards to Real Benefits** \u2013 Use earned XP to \u201cpurchase\u201d real treats (e.g., a 30\u2011minute break, a favorite snack, or a short episode of a show).  \n5. **Reflect Weekly** \u2013 Review what levels took too long, adjust difficulty, and celebrate achievements.  \n6. **Avoid Over\u2011Gamification** \u2013 If points feel forced, pause and simplify. The goal is to make learning enjoyable, not a pressure\u2011filled scoreboard.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Sample 2\u2011Week Study Game Plan (for Any Subject)\n\n| Day | Quest | XP Goal | Reward |\n|-----|-------|---------|--------|\n| Mon | Finish Level\u202f1 (read + flash\u2011cards) | 40\u202fXP | 15\u2011min coffee break |\n| Tue | Quiz Level\u202f1, create one\u2011sentence summary | 30\u202fXP | 10\u2011min YouTube video |\n| Wed | Power\u2011Up: Double XP on flash\u2011cards | 60\u202fXP | Small snack |\n| Thu | Teach a friend (5\u202fmin) | 50\u202fXP | Choose dinner menu |\n| Fri | Level\u202f2 content + practice problems | 45\u202fXP | 30\u2011min gaming session |\n| Sat | \u201cBoss Battle\u201d \u2013 cumulative quiz (20\u202fq) | 100\u202fXP (double) | Movie night |\n| Sun | Rest & Reflect \u2013 note difficulty areas | 10\u202fXP | No penalty \u2013 recovery day |\n| **Repeat** for Week\u202f2, increasing difficulty or adding a new badge. |\n\n---\n\n### Quick Starter Checklist\n- [ ] Write a clear **quest** for the week.  \n- [ ] Split material into **3\u20135 levels**.  \n- [ ] Assign **XP values** to tasks.  \n- [ ] Design **2\u20133 badges**.  \n- [ ] Choose a **timer app** and a **tracking sheet**.  \n- [ ] Set a **reward** for reaching the weekly XP target.  \n\nNow you have a ready\u2011to\u2011play study game! Adjust the theme, points, and rewards to match your personality, and watch motivation level up. Happy gaming\u2011learning! \n\n</answer>"}], "q_num": 731, "originalconversations": ["How to study using a game method?", "## Play the concentration game\n\n1. Create game pieces. Make small cards using paper or something sturdier such as cardstock. Cut small squares out and be sure to make each piece the same size. Since this is a memory game, it is important that each card looks the same so you are unable to differentiate one card from the next.\n2. Write out information on game pieces. Work in pairs when writing out your review information on these cards. Write down a vocabulary word on the first card in the pair and its definition on the second. Continue writing on your cards until each review item has been covered. This game words best when reviewing things like definitions, spelling, examples, etc.\n3. Organize your cards. Flip over all your cards so that the writing is underneath. Place your cards face down and organize them into rows and columns until every game piece is situated.\n4. Flip over one card. Begin the game by choosing just one card to turn over. Read the definition or vocabulary word and choose one more card to turn over. If you flipped over the correct card, you should have a pair in which both cards are a match. When first starting out, this game is sheer luck, but after a few tries you will be able to remember where each card lies and can do your best to find its correct match.\n5. Set aside pairs. When you have found two cards that are a match, set that pair aside so that your game board becomes smaller. Continue playing until every card has been turned over and you have created matches for each game piece. When you are finished playing, you can simply shuffle your cards and redistribute them to create your game board and play again.\n\n\n## Play with card\n\n1. Obtain a deck of cards. The first thing you need to do is get an entire deck of cards. Be sure you aren\u2019t missing any because it will affect how you play the game.\n2. Create a list of review items. Using your study materials, create a list of items you want to review. This list must be numbered and can be anything from definitions to vocabulary.\n3. Pull out face cards from your deck. You want to eliminate any face cards from your deck because the only cards needed are those with numbers. You may set the face cards aside until you have finished playing. An alternative to pulling out face cards is using them to play the game. Treat these cards like wild cards and whenever you draw one you can choose to review any item from your list that you wish to review.\n4. Shuffle your cards. It is very important to shuffle your deck. By doing this, you will ensure that each review item gets chosen by mixing up the numbers within the deck.\n5. Draw cards. Place your cards in a pile after shuffling them and draw the first card off the top. You may set aside all the cards you have already drawn.\n6. Review information corresponding to the number on each card. Look at the number on the card you drew and find the corresponding number on your list of review items. Review the information for that particular number. Continue to draw cards and review the information corresponding to those cards until you have gone through the entire deck.\n\n\n## Play a guess game\n\n1. Create a list of questions. Before generating a list of game questions, you must first go through all of your study materials and review critical information. While reviewing, form questions and produce a list.\n2. Cut small pieces of paper. Once your list is created you are ready to craft several pieces of small paper. These pieces of paper will serve as the main game pieces. Use card stock or heavy-duty paper for sturdy game pieces. Use sticky notes as another option to quickly create piece.\n3. Number papers. You created your main game pieces so now you must number them according to how many questions you developed.\n4. Put papers in container. Place all of your numbered game pieces into the container and be careful not to forget any. Be sure to shake the container once you\u2019ve put all your game pieces inside to ensure the pieces are mixed sufficiently.\n5. Draw paper from container. Keep your list of questions next to you as you pull pieces out of the container. Choose randomly from the container and read the number on the piece of paper.\n6. Answer corresponding questions. Find the number on your question list that corresponds to the number on your game piece and read that question.\n7. Set aside used pieces. After answering each question, put the used game pieces aside so as not to repeat any. If you are unable to recall the correct answers to any questions you can make two piles designating one for challenging questions and one for easy questions. When you play the game for again, you can just put the challenging questions inside the container and spend less time reviewing the questions you already know.\n8. Repeat steps until container is empty. Continue draw the papers from the container until you\u2019ve used them all. To play again, simply fill up the container and start over.\n"], "originalsource": "wikihow", "originalprompt": "How to study using a game method?"}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "You are a video game critic that\u2019s been sucked into a game. After a week trapped there, you write up something that\u2019s both a survivor\u2019s journal and game review."}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user wants a piece of writing: a survivor's journal and game review from perspective of a video game critic trapped in a game for a week. Should be creative, blend journal entries (dates, feelings) and review elements (game mechanics, graphics, story, etc). The tone may be introspective, witty, with a meta perspective. The piece should be written as if it's a journal combined with a review, covering aspects like gameplay, difficulty, narrative, design, immersion, etc. Since it's a week they'd have perhaps 7 entries plus an overall review at the end. Could also intersperse rating e.g., 8/10 etc. Use first-person voice. Should capture survival challenges (like hunger, health). Exploit knowledge about typical games: maybe a survival horror, or open-world sandbox.\n\nWrite as if a critic known for analyzing games. Journal entries with dates or day number. At the end, summarizing review. Could include \"Pros\" \"Cons\". Also incorporate gameplay aspects and how they affect actual survival. Could be meta: the criticism influences the world. Write like a mix of survival log and review.\n\nOkay, produce a comprehensive piece.\n\nWill wrap it in maybe a title. Ensure it reads like a survivor's journal but also a review.\n\nProceed.</think>\n**\u201cPixelated Peril \u2013 A Week in *The Forgotten Frontier*\u201d**  \n*Survivor\u2019s Log & Critical Review*  \n\n---\n\n### Day 1 \u2013 \u201cSpawn Point\u201d\n\n> **06:07 \u2013 \u201cWoke up in a pixelated meadow\u201d**  \n> The tutorial guru greeted me with a cheery jingle and a UI that looked like it was ripped straight from a 2014 indie hit. My inventory was already capped at **12 slots** \u2013 a clear nod to \u201cforced scarcity\u201d design. I managed to collect my starter kit (a rusted axe, 5\u2011pack of canned beans, and the ever\u2011present \u201cHelp\u201d button).  \n> **First Impressions (Mechanics)** \u2013 The control scheme is tight; WASD movement with fluid analog emulation feels *hand\u2011crafted*. The tutorial is *interactive* rather than a wall of text, which is a plus.  \n\n> **Survival Note** \u2013 The UI\u2019s \u201cHunger\u201d bar flickers ominously every few minutes. I\u2019ve learned the hard way that \u201cCooking\u201d is not just a menu option; you *must* actually fire up a campfire. (You\u2019ll thank the devs for a realistic time\u2011to\u2011cook mechanic.)\n\n---\n\n### Day 2 \u2013 \u201cThe First Quest\u201d\n\n> **12:41 \u2013 \u201cBounty on a \u201cLost Sheep\u201d?\u201d**  \n> An NPC named **Grimble** handed me a quest to retrieve a lost sheep from the \u201cShrouded Woods\u201d. The quest marker was a glowing arrow that *never* led directly to the target\u2014a deliberate design to encourage exploration. The woods themselves are a gorgeous blend of low\u2011poly foliage and dynamic lighting that makes the mist feel *tangible*.  \n\n> **Review Element (World Design)** \u2013 The procedural generation is subtle: each tree placement feels intentional, yet the occasional \u201cout\u2011of\u2011place\u201d rock adds a note of unpredictability. The ambient soundtrack shifts from serene flute to low\u2011drone when you stray too far from the path \u2013 an excellent use of audio to cue danger.\n\n> **Survival Note** \u2013 Being attacked by a \u201cspiky bramble\u201d creature taught me that *shield* blocks *only* physical damage. I lost half my health, and the \u201cHealing Potion\u201d I\u2019d hoarded turned out to be a **\u201cradiant tonic\u201d** that actually *increases* stamina but does *nothing* for wounds. Lesson learned: don\u2019t trust potion labels without reading the tooltip.\n\n---\n\n### Day 3 \u2013 \u201cSystems Overload\u201d\n\n> **19:53 \u2013 \u201cInventory Full, Hack the System?\u201d**  \n> The game\u2019s **crafting system** is deep but unforgiving. Recipes are scattered across **ancient tablets** you must decipher (yes, a built\u2011in cipher). I finally unlocked the \u201cImprovised Shelter\u201d blueprint\u2014unlocked after solving a 3\u2011step puzzle involving wind direction and fire placement.  \n\n> **Critical Observation (Crafting)** \u2013 The manual approach to crafting feels like an homage to classic RPGs (think *Elder Scrolls*), but it can be a hindrance when you\u2019re starving and the UI freezes for 2 seconds each time you open the crafting menu. The developers clearly prioritized *illusion of control* over *streamlined survival*.  \n\n> **Survival Note** \u2013 Built a shelter using 2 wood planks, a stone, and a found \u201cold blanket\u201d. The shelter\u2019s **durability** degrades over time; I\u2019ve set a mental timer to rebuild before nightfall, because night brings **\u201cEcho Wraiths\u201d** that can phase through walls. The game\u2019s day/night cycle is *punishingly fast*\u2014a full day passes in 20 minutes of real time.\n\n---\n\n### Day 4 \u2013 \u201cNRG (Not Ready for Ghouls)\u201d\n\n> **02:07 \u2013 \u201cFirst Encounter with the Echo Wraith\u201d**  \n> Nightfall arrived. My makeshift lantern flickered. The wraiths are *silent* and *transparent*, appearing only when the **\u201cEcho Meter\u201d** spikes. The stealth mechanics are *heroic*: crouch, stay still, and the **silhouette** of the wraith fades if you\u2019re not moving.  \n\n> **Review (Combat & AI)** \u2013 Enemy AI is surprisingly *reactive*. Wraiths will track sound; throwing a stone (from my improvised slingshot) distracts them. This is a rare instance where *environmental interaction* directly influences combat rather than relying on button\u2011mashing. However, the lack of a **radar** or **minimap** makes navigation at night feel *deliberately blind*, bordering on frustrating.\n\n> **Survival Note** \u2013 Managed to survive by *holding my breath* (a UI mechanic that drains your stamina meter). When the stamina hits zero, you *scream*\u2014 causing the wraiths to retreat, but the noise attracts **\u201cMarauder Dogs\u201d**. The game punishes you for the very act of surviving.\n\n---\n\n### Day 5 \u2013 \u201cMeta\u2011Storytelling\u201d\n\n> **14:22 \u2013 \u201cThe Lost Journal\u201d**  \n> I found a **crumbling journal** belonging to a former player (or perhaps an NPC). It recounts their days in *The Forgotten Frontier* before the \u201cGlitch Event.\u201d The prose mirrors my own writing\u2014a *meta* nod that the developers clearly baked into the narrative. The journal hints at a **\u201cReset Portal\u201d** hidden in the old ruins.\n\n> **Critical Insight (Narrative)** \u2013 The story is *layered*: a surface questline about retrieving the sheep, a deeper lore about broken code, and a fourth\u2011wall commentary on player agency. The *dialogue trees* are robust; each choice subtly alters the environment (e.g., planting a tree that later blocks a monster\u2019s path). Yet, some branching paths feel *underutilized*, converging back to the main plot after a few minutes.  \n\n> **Survival Note** \u2013 Following the journal, I unlocked a *secret hatch* that leads to an underground bunker stocked with **canned food** and **spare batteries**. The bunker\u2019s **environmental storytelling** (graffiti, broken consoles) is a masterclass in *show, don\u2019t tell*. The only downside? The bunker is also a *spawn point* for a **Giant Burrowing Beetle** that devours any item you drop\u2014clearly a *balance* oversight.\n\n---\n\n### Day 6 \u2013 \u201cThe Glitch\u201d\n\n> **08:45 \u2013 \u201cStoryline or Bug?\u201d**  \n> A sudden *screen flicker* accompanied by a *digital chirp*. The world slipped into **low\u2011poly glitch mode**: textures vanished, collision boxes became visible, and the **HUD** displayed raw data (coordinates, memory usage). The **\u201cGlitch Event\u201d** described in the journal is *actually happening*.  \n\n> **Review (Technical & Innovation)** \u2013 This intentional \u201cglitch\u201d is a bold design risk. It forces the player to adapt to a *minimalist* version of the game, focusing on core mechanics (movement, resource management) without the polish. The developers leveraged **procedural shaders** that break gracefully, creating a unique, almost *nostalgic* aesthetic reminiscent of early 3D titles.  \n\n> However, the glitch also *crashes* the **auto\u2011saves** every 2 minutes. I lost 30 minutes of progress because the save file corrupted, an *unacceptable* quality\u2011of\u2011life bug. The **debug console** is accessible via the \u201cHelp\u201d button, but the *lack of an explicit \u201cSave\u201d option* leaves you at the mercy of the engine\u2019s whims.\n\n> **Survival Note** \u2013 In glitch mode, enemy attack patterns simplify (they become *boxes* that chase you). This made it easier to survive, but also *removed* the strategic depth that made combat enjoyable. I spent the day wandering the glitch\u2011world, noting how the **soundscape** stripped down to just a low\u2011frequency hum\u2014*eerily atmospheric*.\n\n---\n\n### Day 7 \u2013 \u201cExit Door or Exit Loop?\u201d\n\n> **23:11 \u2013 \u201cFound the Reset Portal\u201d**  \n> After deciphering the final rune (a **binary sequence** hidden behind a waterfall), the portal activated. The UI flashed \u201c**Returning to Main Menu\u2026**\u201d but instead of a soft fade, the screen went black, then displayed **\u201cTHANK YOU FOR PLAYING \u2013 0% COMPLETED.\u201d**  \n\n> **Final Review (Overall"}, "ans": null, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Be a helpful assistant. Provide your final answer in <answer></answer> XML tags."}, {"role": "user", "content": "You are a video game critic that\u2019s been sucked into a game. After a week trapped there, you write up something that\u2019s both a survivor\u2019s journal and game review."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nThe user wants a piece of writing: a survivor's journal and game review from perspective of a video game critic trapped in a game for a week. Should be creative, blend journal entries (dates, feelings) and review elements (game mechanics, graphics, story, etc). The tone may be introspective, witty, with a meta perspective. The piece should be written as if it's a journal combined with a review, covering aspects like gameplay, difficulty, narrative, design, immersion, etc. Since it's a week they'd have perhaps 7 entries plus an overall review at the end. Could also intersperse rating e.g., 8/10 etc. Use first-person voice. Should capture survival challenges (like hunger, health). Exploit knowledge about typical games: maybe a survival horror, or open-world sandbox.\n\nWrite as if a critic known for analyzing games. Journal entries with dates or day number. At the end, summarizing review. Could include \"Pros\" \"Cons\". Also incorporate gameplay aspects and how they affect actual survival. Could be meta: the criticism influences the world. Write like a mix of survival log and review.\n\nOkay, produce a comprehensive piece.\n\nWill wrap it in maybe a title. Ensure it reads like a survivor's journal but also a review.\n\nProceed.</think>\n**\u201cPixelated Peril \u2013 A Week in *The Forgotten Frontier*\u201d**  \n*Survivor\u2019s Log & Critical Review*  \n\n---\n\n### Day 1 \u2013 \u201cSpawn Point\u201d\n\n> **06:07 \u2013 \u201cWoke up in a pixelated meadow\u201d**  \n> The tutorial guru greeted me with a cheery jingle and a UI that looked like it was ripped straight from a 2014 indie hit. My inventory was already capped at **12 slots** \u2013 a clear nod to \u201cforced scarcity\u201d design. I managed to collect my starter kit (a rusted axe, 5\u2011pack of canned beans, and the ever\u2011present \u201cHelp\u201d button).  \n> **First Impressions (Mechanics)** \u2013 The control scheme is tight; WASD movement with fluid analog emulation feels *hand\u2011crafted*. The tutorial is *interactive* rather than a wall of text, which is a plus.  \n\n> **Survival Note** \u2013 The UI\u2019s \u201cHunger\u201d bar flickers ominously every few minutes. I\u2019ve learned the hard way that \u201cCooking\u201d is not just a menu option; you *must* actually fire up a campfire. (You\u2019ll thank the devs for a realistic time\u2011to\u2011cook mechanic.)\n\n---\n\n### Day 2 \u2013 \u201cThe First Quest\u201d\n\n> **12:41 \u2013 \u201cBounty on a \u201cLost Sheep\u201d?\u201d**  \n> An NPC named **Grimble** handed me a quest to retrieve a lost sheep from the \u201cShrouded Woods\u201d. The quest marker was a glowing arrow that *never* led directly to the target\u2014a deliberate design to encourage exploration. The woods themselves are a gorgeous blend of low\u2011poly foliage and dynamic lighting that makes the mist feel *tangible*.  \n\n> **Review Element (World Design)** \u2013 The procedural generation is subtle: each tree placement feels intentional, yet the occasional \u201cout\u2011of\u2011place\u201d rock adds a note of unpredictability. The ambient soundtrack shifts from serene flute to low\u2011drone when you stray too far from the path \u2013 an excellent use of audio to cue danger.\n\n> **Survival Note** \u2013 Being attacked by a \u201cspiky bramble\u201d creature taught me that *shield* blocks *only* physical damage. I lost half my health, and the \u201cHealing Potion\u201d I\u2019d hoarded turned out to be a **\u201cradiant tonic\u201d** that actually *increases* stamina but does *nothing* for wounds. Lesson learned: don\u2019t trust potion labels without reading the tooltip.\n\n---\n\n### Day 3 \u2013 \u201cSystems Overload\u201d\n\n> **19:53 \u2013 \u201cInventory Full, Hack the System?\u201d**  \n> The game\u2019s **crafting system** is deep but unforgiving. Recipes are scattered across **ancient tablets** you must decipher (yes, a built\u2011in cipher). I finally unlocked the \u201cImprovised Shelter\u201d blueprint\u2014unlocked after solving a 3\u2011step puzzle involving wind direction and fire placement.  \n\n> **Critical Observation (Crafting)** \u2013 The manual approach to crafting feels like an homage to classic RPGs (think *Elder Scrolls*), but it can be a hindrance when you\u2019re starving and the UI freezes for 2 seconds each time you open the crafting menu. The developers clearly prioritized *illusion of control* over *streamlined survival*.  \n\n> **Survival Note** \u2013 Built a shelter using 2 wood planks, a stone, and a found \u201cold blanket\u201d. The shelter\u2019s **durability** degrades over time; I\u2019ve set a mental timer to rebuild before nightfall, because night brings **\u201cEcho Wraiths\u201d** that can phase through walls. The game\u2019s day/night cycle is *punishingly fast*\u2014a full day passes in 20 minutes of real time.\n\n---\n\n### Day 4 \u2013 \u201cNRG (Not Ready for Ghouls)\u201d\n\n> **02:07 \u2013 \u201cFirst Encounter with the Echo Wraith\u201d**  \n> Nightfall arrived. My makeshift lantern flickered. The wraiths are *silent* and *transparent*, appearing only when the **\u201cEcho Meter\u201d** spikes. The stealth mechanics are *heroic*: crouch, stay still, and the **silhouette** of the wraith fades if you\u2019re not moving.  \n\n> **Review (Combat & AI)** \u2013 Enemy AI is surprisingly *reactive*. Wraiths will track sound; throwing a stone (from my improvised slingshot) distracts them. This is a rare instance where *environmental interaction* directly influences combat rather than relying on button\u2011mashing. However, the lack of a **radar** or **minimap** makes navigation at night feel *deliberately blind*, bordering on frustrating.\n\n> **Survival Note** \u2013 Managed to survive by *holding my breath* (a UI mechanic that drains your stamina meter). When the stamina hits zero, you *scream*\u2014 causing the wraiths to retreat, but the noise attracts **\u201cMarauder Dogs\u201d**. The game punishes you for the very act of surviving.\n\n---\n\n### Day 5 \u2013 \u201cMeta\u2011Storytelling\u201d\n\n> **14:22 \u2013 \u201cThe Lost Journal\u201d**  \n> I found a **crumbling journal** belonging to a former player (or perhaps an NPC). It recounts their days in *The Forgotten Frontier* before the \u201cGlitch Event.\u201d The prose mirrors my own writing\u2014a *meta* nod that the developers clearly baked into the narrative. The journal hints at a **\u201cReset Portal\u201d** hidden in the old ruins.\n\n> **Critical Insight (Narrative)** \u2013 The story is *layered*: a surface questline about retrieving the sheep, a deeper lore about broken code, and a fourth\u2011wall commentary on player agency. The *dialogue trees* are robust; each choice subtly alters the environment (e.g., planting a tree that later blocks a monster\u2019s path). Yet, some branching paths feel *underutilized*, converging back to the main plot after a few minutes.  \n\n> **Survival Note** \u2013 Following the journal, I unlocked a *secret hatch* that leads to an underground bunker stocked with **canned food** and **spare batteries**. The bunker\u2019s **environmental storytelling** (graffiti, broken consoles) is a masterclass in *show, don\u2019t tell*. The only downside? The bunker is also a *spawn point* for a **Giant Burrowing Beetle** that devours any item you drop\u2014clearly a *balance* oversight.\n\n---\n\n### Day 6 \u2013 \u201cThe Glitch\u201d\n\n> **08:45 \u2013 \u201cStoryline or Bug?\u201d**  \n> A sudden *screen flicker* accompanied by a *digital chirp*. The world slipped into **low\u2011poly glitch mode**: textures vanished, collision boxes became visible, and the **HUD** displayed raw data (coordinates, memory usage). The **\u201cGlitch Event\u201d** described in the journal is *actually happening*.  \n\n> **Review (Technical & Innovation)** \u2013 This intentional \u201cglitch\u201d is a bold design risk. It forces the player to adapt to a *minimalist* version of the game, focusing on core mechanics (movement, resource management) without the polish. The developers leveraged **procedural shaders** that break gracefully, creating a unique, almost *nostalgic* aesthetic reminiscent of early 3D titles.  \n\n> However, the glitch also *crashes* the **auto\u2011saves** every 2 minutes. I lost 30 minutes of progress because the save file corrupted, an *unacceptable* quality\u2011of\u2011life bug. The **debug console** is accessible via the \u201cHelp\u201d button, but the *lack of an explicit \u201cSave\u201d option* leaves you at the mercy of the engine\u2019s whims.\n\n> **Survival Note** \u2013 In glitch mode, enemy attack patterns simplify (they become *boxes* that chase you). This made it easier to survive, but also *removed* the strategic depth that made combat enjoyable. I spent the day wandering the glitch\u2011world, noting how the **soundscape** stripped down to just a low\u2011frequency hum\u2014*eerily atmospheric*.\n\n---\n\n### Day 7 \u2013 \u201cExit Door or Exit Loop?\u201d\n\n> **23:11 \u2013 \u201cFound the Reset Portal\u201d**  \n> After deciphering the final rune (a **binary sequence** hidden behind a waterfall), the portal activated. The UI flashed \u201c**Returning to Main Menu\u2026**\u201d but instead of a soft fade, the screen went black, then displayed **\u201cTHANK YOU FOR PLAYING \u2013 0% COMPLETED.\u201d**  \n\n> **Final Review (Overall"}], "q_num": 860, "originalconversations": ["You are a video game critic that\u2019s been sucked into a game. After a week trapped there, you write up something that\u2019s both a survivor\u2019s journal and game review.", "Day 1: I seem to have arrived on an island. I have yet to meet any other humans. I do appear to have acquired a meager shelter, and have done a small number of banal fetch quests. All significant gameplay appears to be locked behind an arbitrary clock system.\nDay 2: I have continued my work of fostering relationships with the locals. I have been provided ample sustenance by them, and am in the process of cultivating a variety of flora on which I hope to survive.\nDay 3: I have paid off my debt to the local chief, who has graciously offered to improve my living situation. I have also begun fishing in the nearby water source for fish. I am eager for protein, and fishing has quickly become my preferred method to pass the time.\nDay 4: I acquired a few more survival tools, and have started to learn carpentry. The locals have graciously begun to teach me their trades.\nDay 5: My orchard has shown signs of producing fruit. I am eager not only for a full belly, but also a full wallet.\nDay 6: I decided to take a dip in the water today. I could not swim out very far, but I found a number of critters and a few valuables I was able to sell for a tidy profit. I finally have a bed, and am working on improving my living situation further.\nDay 7: I hope to never leave my island paradise. The locals are friendlier than any human I recall meeting. The food is free and plentiful. For the first time in my life, I own a house. While I have become indebted to the local chief, he is quite reasonable, and does not demand astronomical interest. In fact, he doesn't charge interest at all. There are plenty of hobbies and crafts to distract myself with, and I feel as if my troubles have melted away with the ocean tide.\nWhile my stress melts away, I look out and smile at my new horizon."], "originalsource": "writingprompts", "originalprompt": "You are a video game critic that\u2019s been sucked into a game. After a week trapped there, you write up something that\u2019s both a survivor\u2019s journal and game review."}
{"prompt": {"role": "user", "content": "Without repeating a single exact word, write the longest fictional story you can."}, "chosen": {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\nWe need to produce a long fictional story without repeating a single exact word. That is extremely challenging: we must ensure no word is repeated. We have to write a story using each word only once. The story can be long, but we must be careful to not repeat any word. Also need to ensure not to repeat exact word, case-insensitive? Probably same word regardless of case. So we must avoid repeats.\n\nWe need to produce a story; we can use punctuation, but words are separated by spaces. So cannot repeat any token separated by spaces. Also we cannot repeat any numeric? Probably treat numbers as words.\n\nWe need to generate many words, all unique. That's massive. But we can try to craft a reasonably long story, maybe about 200-300 words, ensuring each word is unique.\n\nWe need to be careful: common words like \"the\", \"and\", \"a\", \"to\", \"in\", \"of\", \"was\", \"he\", \"she\", \"it\", \"that\", \"his\", \"her\", etc. Can't repeat any of them. So we need to avoid article repetition. Use synonyms, vary.\n\nWe must also not repeat punctuation attached to word? For example \"world.\" counts as word \"world.\" maybe distinct from \"world\"? The definition of word might strip punctuation. Safer to treat punctuation attached as part of token, but could be considered same word ignoring punctuation. Safer to avoid same base word even with punctuation.\n\nWe'll produce story where each token is unique. We'll avoid using common small words multiple times. This is difficult but doable with careful planning.\n\nApproach: Write a story using each word only once, using many distinct words. Use long sentence with varied vocabulary.\n\nWe need to keep track. Let's start writing, maintain a list of used words.\n\nWe'll start.\n\nI'll write words separated by spaces.\n\nLet's create a list. Use a systematic approach: start with a sentence.\n\nFirst word: \"Midnight\". (1)\n\nSecond: \"silhouette\". (2)\n\nThird: \"wandered\". (3)\n\nFourth: \"through\". (4)\n\nFifth: \"ancient\". (5)\n\nSixth: \"forest\". (6)\n\nSeventh: \"where\". (7)\n\nEighth: \"whispering\". (8)\n\nNinth: \"leaves\". (9)\n\nTenth: \"echoed\". (10)\n\nEleventh: \"soft\". (11)\n\nTwelfth: \"melodies\". (12)\n\nThirteenth: \"beneath\". (13)\n\nFourteenth: \"glimmering\". (14)\n\nFifteenth: \"moonlight\". (15)\n\nSixteenth: \"hollow\". (16)\n\nSeventeenth: \"cavern\". (17)\n\nEighteenth: \"suddenly\". (18)\n\nNineteenth: \"a\". (19) (can't reuse article later)\n\nTwentieth: \"luminescent\". (20)\n\nTwenty-first: \"fox\". (21)\n\nTwenty-second: \"emerged\". (22)\n\nTwenty-third: \"carrying\". (23)\n\nTwenty-fourth: \"crystal\". (24)\n\nTwenty-fifth: \"orb\". (25)\n\nTwenty-sixth: \"its\". (26) (avoid later)\n\nTwenty-seventh: \"eyes\". (27)\n\nTwenty-eighth: \"glowed\". (28)\n\nTwenty-ninth: \"emerald\". (29)\n\nThirtieth: \"fire\". (30)\n\nThirty-first: \"guiding\". (31)\n\nThirty-second: \"wanderer\". (32)\n\nThirty-third: \"deeper\". (33)\n\nThirty-fourth: \"towards\". (34)\n\nThirty-fifth: \"shimmering\". (35)\n\nThirty-sixth: \"waterfall\". (36)\n\nThirty-seventh: \"suspended\". (37)\n\nThirty-eighth: \"in\". (38) (avoid later)\n\nThirty-ninth: \"gravity\". (39)\n\nFortieth: \"defying\". (40)\n\nForty-first: \"cascade\". (41)\n\nForty-second: \"sprayed\". (42)\n\nForty-third: \"silver\". (43)\n\nForty-fourth: \"mist\". (44)\n\nForty-fifth: \"that\". (45)\n\nForty-sixth: \"kissed\". (46)\n\nForty-seventh: \"stormy\". (47)\n\nForty-eighth: \"cliffs\". (48)\n\nForty-ninth: \"below\". (49)\n\nFiftieth: \"where\". Oops \"where\" already used at word 7. Can't repeat. Need change. Use \"there\". So replace word 50 with \"there\". (50)\n\nFifty-first: \"ancient\". Already used 5. Can't repeat. Use \"forgotten\". (51)\n\nFifty-second: \"city\". (52)\n\nFifty-third: \"dusty\". (53)\n\nFifty-fourth: \"spires