f employer offering reduced wages vs redundancy, possibly concerning working hours, informal expectations, etc. Provide advice: legal framework depends on jurisdiction (likely UK? Could be US, Australia, etc). Need to ask clarification. We must not give legal advice but general info. Provide considerations: contract terms, rights to redundancy, minimum wage, hours, implied contract, at-will employment, etc. Also mention COVID-19 measures, furlough schemes. Advice to consult legal counsel.\n\nWe must format final answer inside <answer></answer>. Ensure no disallowed content. Provide thorough explanation.\n\n</think>\n"}], "q_num": 1002, "originalconversations": ["My employer's main clients are unable to pay their bills to us, as they cannot work at this time. Therefore my boss is offering me 50% wages until the coronavirus goes away (technically for part time hours, but it's been intimated that there'd be an expectation to work regular hours unofficially to help the company \"weather the storm\") or paid redundancy.  \nI have been searching for a new job since hearing this, but I want to understand if my employer's offer is fair/legal.", "I am sorry that you have been put in a hard situation. If you accept the offer, please make sure that you don't lose anything (beyond the salary). When you negotiate with the boss, reasonable terms would be a contract that says:\n\n<li>This is a temporary change of your salary. It will return to the full salary as soon as the crisis is over. </li>\n<li>Any redundancy payment will be calculated based on the original, full salary. </li>\n<li>Same terms for everyone, including management and owners. </li>\n<li>Someone with experience in these matters finds out what state benefits you might receive based on the lower salary and how to apply for them. </li>\n\nWhat's slightly to your advantage is that your tax payments and NI contributions will fall by more than 50%. Depending on your financial and family situation, you may get some more advantages, like getting child tax credits that you might not have received at the old salary. Please visit a tax calculator website and input the relevant information to compute the tax amount.\n\nUltimately you have to weigh up the pros and cons that are personal to you.", "what do I do when my boss refuses all those terms?", "The concrete actions you need to take depend on which country you are in. Generally speaking, if your boss refuses to agree to the reasonable terms mentioned earlier, you may need to consider seeking legal advice or consulting with a professional who specializes in employment law. They can help you understand your rights and options in this situation. It's important to protect your interests and ensure that you are being treated fairly and legally by your employer."], "originalsource": "multi_turn", "originalprompt": "My employer's main clients are unable to pay their bills to us, as they cannot work at this time. Therefore my boss is offering me 50% wages until the coronavirus goes away (technically for part time hours, but it's been intimated that there'd be an expectation to work regular hours unofficially to help the company \"weather the storm\") or paid redundancy.  \nI have been searching for a new job since hearing this, but I want to understand if my employer's offer is fair/legal."}
